const Map<String, Map<String, String>> embeddedSkillFiles = {
  "dart-flutter": {
    "SKILL.md":
        "---\nname: dart-flutter\ndescription: \"Writes and refactors idiomatic modern Dart 3 and Flutter code. Covers records, patterns and switch expressions, sealed classes with exhaustive switch, class modifiers (final, base, interface, sealed, mixin), extension types, async/await, Streams (single-subscription vs broadcast, async*), Isolate.run, small const widgets, keys, context.mounted after await, disposing controllers and subscriptions, state management with Riverpod (Notifier, AsyncNotifier, ref.watch, ref.read, ref.listen, autoDispose) or Bloc and Cubit (BlocBuilder vs BlocListener), go_router redirects and deep links, Pigeon platform channels, flavors with --dart-define-from-file, analysis_options lints, performance (ListView.builder, RepaintBoundary, DevTools) and tests (package:test, widget, golden, integration_test, mocktail, fakeAsync). Use when writing, refactoring or reviewing Dart or Flutter code, adding a screen, provider, bloc, route or platform channel, or setting up lints, flavors or tests.\"\nlicense: MIT\nmetadata:\n  author: Ahmad Hussain\n  version: \"1.0.0\"\n  pack: mobile-dev-kit\n---\n\n# Dart and Flutter\n\nWrite Dart and Flutter code the way a senior Flutter engineer would: Dart 3 types that make illegal states\nunrepresentable, widgets that rebuild only what changed, async code that respects the widget lifecycle,\nand one consistent state management approach per app.\n\n## When to use\n\n- Writing a new screen, widget, provider, bloc, repository, route or platform channel in a Flutter app.\n- Refactoring pre-Dart 3 code (enums plus nullable fields, `Map<String, dynamic>` everywhere, `is` chains).\n- Fixing jank, leaks, \"setState() called after dispose()\" or \"Looking up a deactivated widget's ancestor\".\n- Setting up `analysis_options.yaml`, flavors, `--dart-define-from-file`, Pigeon or the test pyramid.\n\nNot for: reviewing a whole MR (use `mobile-code-review`), a security audit (use `mobile-security`),\nor choosing an architecture from scratch (use `mobile-mvvm`, `mobile-mvi`).\n\n## Workflow\n\n1. Read the project setup before writing code.\n   - `pubspec.yaml`: SDK constraint, state management package, router, codegen (`build_runner`, `freezed`).\n   - `analysis_options.yaml`: which lint set is active.\n   - Match the existing patterns. Never add Bloc to a Riverpod app or the other way round.\n2. Model the data and state first with sealed classes, records and extension types (rules 1 to 3).\n3. Write the logic outside widgets: repository, then Notifier or Bloc. Load\n   [state management](references/state-management.md) for Riverpod and Bloc details.\n4. Write the UI as small `const` widget classes. Check every `await` in UI code for a `mounted` check,\n   and every controller, subscription and timer for a `dispose`.\n5. Native code goes through Pigeon. Load [platform channels](references/platform-channels.md).\n6. Add tests at the lowest level that proves the behaviour. Load [testing](references/testing.md).\n7. If performance is in scope, measure in profile mode first. Load [performance](references/performance.md).\n8. Run `dart format .`, `flutter analyze` and `flutter test`. Fix every warning before handing over.\n\n## Rules\n\n### 1. Closed state is a sealed class, matched with an exhaustive switch\n\nThe compiler flags every `switch` that misses a new subtype. No `default`, no stale branches.\n\n```dart\n// BAD: impossible combinations compile (isLoading && error != null)\nclass DeviceState { bool isLoading = false; Device? device; String? error; }\n\n// GOOD\nsealed class DeviceState {}\nfinal class DeviceLoading extends DeviceState {}\nfinal class DeviceReady extends DeviceState { DeviceReady(this.device); final Device device; }\nfinal class DeviceFailed extends DeviceState { DeviceFailed(this.error); final Object error; }\n\nWidget build(BuildContext context) => switch (state) {\n  DeviceLoading() => const CircularProgressIndicator(),\n  DeviceReady(:final device) => DeviceCard(device: device),\n  DeviceFailed(:final error) => ErrorView(error: error),\n};\n```\n\n- Add `when` guards for sub-cases: `Connecting(:final attempt) when attempt > 3 => ...`.\n- Do not add `_ =>` to a switch over a sealed type. It hides the next missing case.\n\n### 2. Records and patterns replace throwaway classes and unchecked maps\n\n```dart\n// BAD: unchecked casts on untrusted JSON, crash on a wrong type\nfinal lat = json['lat'] as double;\nfinal lng = json['lng'] as double;\n\n// GOOD: destructure and validate in one step\n({double lat, double lng}) parseLocation(Map<String, Object?> json) {\n  if (json case {'lat': final double lat, 'lng': final double lng}) {\n    return (lat: lat, lng: lng);\n  }\n  throw const FormatException('Invalid location');\n}\n```\n\n- Use records for multiple return values: `final (count, name) = pair();`.\n- Records are fine for private, local data. Public domain models stay named classes.\n- Use dot shorthands (`mainAxisAlignment: .center`) and null-aware elements (`[a, ?maybeB]`) when the\n  SDK constraint allows them and the codebase already uses them.\n\n### 3. Class modifiers and extension types state the API contract\n\n```dart\n// BAD: any String goes, device and user IDs are interchangeable\nFuture<void> connect(String deviceId, String userId);\n\n// GOOD: zero-cost wrappers, mixing them up is a compile error\nextension type const DeviceId(String value) {}\nextension type const UserId(String value) {}\nFuture<void> connect(DeviceId device, UserId user);\n```\n\n| Modifier | Use for |\n|---|---|\n| `final class` | Value and state classes; no subclassing or implementing outside the library |\n| `abstract interface class` | Repository and service contracts that are only implemented |\n| `base class` | Classes with invariants that subclasses must inherit, never re-implement |\n| `sealed class` | Closed hierarchies matched with `switch` |\n| `mixin class` | Rare; only when a type must be both a mixin and a class |\n\n- Extension types are compile-time only. They are not a validation or security boundary: `id as String`\n  still works. Validate in a factory if the format matters.\n\n### 4. Every stream has one owner who closes it\n\n```dart\n// BAD: single-subscription stream exposed to many listeners -> \"Stream has already been listened to\"\nfinal _controller = StreamController<ScanResult>();\n\n// GOOD: broadcast for fan-out, closed by the owner\nfinal _results = StreamController<ScanResult>.broadcast();\nStream<ScanResult> get results => _results.stream;\nFuture<void> dispose() => _results.close();\n```\n\n- Single-subscription: one listener, buffers until listened (file reads, HTTP bodies, `async*`).\n- Broadcast: many listeners, drops events nobody hears (connection state, scan results).\n- Prefer `async*` generators. Cancelling the subscription stops the generator at the next `yield`.\n- Fire-and-forget futures are explicit: `unawaited(analytics.log(event));`.\n\n### 5. CPU-heavy work leaves the UI isolate\n\n```dart\n// BAD: 5 MB JSON decoded on the UI isolate, frames drop\nfinal data = jsonDecode(body);\n\n// GOOD\nfinal data = await Isolate.run(() => jsonDecode(body) as Map<String, Object?>);\n```\n\n- The closure must not capture `BuildContext`, widgets or large objects; everything captured is copied.\n- `Isolate.run` is not available on web. Use `compute` in code that must run on web too.\n- `async` alone does not move work off the UI thread. Only isolates do.\n\n### 6. Widgets are small const classes, not helper methods\n\n```dart\n// BAD: rebuilt with the parent every time, no const, no own Element\nWidget _buildHeader() => Padding(padding: const EdgeInsets.all(16), child: Text(title));\n\n// GOOD\nclass DeviceHeader extends StatelessWidget {\n  const DeviceHeader({super.key, required this.title});\n  final String title;\n\n  @override\n  Widget build(BuildContext context) =>\n      Padding(padding: const EdgeInsets.all(16), child: Text(title));\n}\n```\n\n- `build` is pure and cheap: no futures, no controllers, no I/O.\n- Create futures once (`late final _user = repo.fetchUser();`), never inside `FutureBuilder(future: ...)` in `build`.\n- Read only what you need: `MediaQuery.sizeOf(context)` instead of `MediaQuery.of(context).size`.\n\n### 7. Keys only where identity matters\n\n```dart\n// BAD: stateful rows lose or swap state on reorder and delete\nListView.builder(itemBuilder: (context, i) => DeviceTile(device: devices[i]));\n\n// GOOD\nListView.builder(\n  itemCount: devices.length,\n  itemBuilder: (context, i) => DeviceTile(key: ValueKey(devices[i].id), device: devices[i]),\n);\n```\n\n- `ValueKey(id)` for list items that can reorder, insert or delete. Never `ValueKey(index)`.\n- `GlobalKey` only for real cross-tree access (`Form` validation, a `Navigator` key for go_router).\n  It is expensive and a widget with a `GlobalKey` must not appear twice in the tree.\n- Never create keys in `build` (`GlobalKey()` or `UniqueKey()`); the state resets on every rebuild.\n\n### 8. Check `mounted` after every `await` before using context or state\n\n```dart\n// BAD: the widget may be gone; wrong navigator or a crash\nonPressed: () async {\n  await repo.save(form);\n  context.pop();\n},\n\n// GOOD\nonPressed: () async {\n  await repo.save(form);\n  if (!context.mounted) return;\n  context.pop();\n},\n```\n\n- Inside a `State`, use `if (!mounted) return;` before `setState` or `context`.\n- In a Riverpod notifier, use `if (!ref.mounted) return;`. In a Cubit, `if (isClosed) return;`.\n- Keep `use_build_context_synchronously` enabled; it is in `flutter_lints`.\n\n### 9. Whatever a State creates, it disposes\n\n```dart\n// BAD: created in build, never disposed, text lost on rebuild\nWidget build(BuildContext context) => TextField(controller: TextEditingController());\n\n// GOOD\nfinal _controller = TextEditingController();\nlate final StreamSubscription<int> _sub;\nTimer? _poll;\n\n@override\nvoid initState() {\n  super.initState();\n  _sub = widget.battery.listen(_onLevel);\n}\n\n@override\nvoid dispose() {\n  _poll?.cancel();\n  _sub.cancel();\n  _controller.dispose();\n  super.dispose();\n}\n```\n\n- Covers `TextEditingController`, `ScrollController`, `PageController`, `FocusNode`, `AnimationController`,\n  `StreamSubscription`, `Timer`, `ChangeNotifier`s the State created.\n- Do not dispose objects passed in from outside; their owner does.\n- Avoid `late final _sub = stream.listen(...)`: it subscribes lazily, possibly for the first time in `dispose`.\n\n### 10. One state management approach per app, used the same way everywhere\n\nRiverpod:\n```dart\n// BAD: read in build never rebuilds; watch in a callback rebuilds for nothing\nfinal todos = ref.read(todosProvider);\nonPressed: () => ref.watch(todosProvider.notifier).add(todo),\n\n// GOOD\nfinal todos = ref.watch(todosProvider);                     // build: rebuild on change\nonPressed: () => ref.read(todosProvider.notifier).add(todo), // callbacks: one-off read\nref.listen(todosProvider, (prev, next) { /* snackbar, navigation */ });\n```\n\nBloc:\n```dart\n// BAD: side effect inside builder, runs on every rebuild\nBlocBuilder<LoginBloc, LoginState>(builder: (context, state) {\n  if (state is LoginSuccess) context.go('/home');\n  return const LoginForm();\n})\n\n// GOOD\nBlocListener<LoginBloc, LoginState>(\n  listenWhen: (previous, current) => current is LoginSuccess,\n  listener: (context, state) => context.go('/home'),\n  child: const LoginForm(),\n)\n```\n\n- Riverpod: screen state uses `autoDispose` (default with `@riverpod` codegen). Narrow rebuilds with\n  `ref.watch(provider.select((s) => s.count))`.\n- Bloc: `Cubit` for simple method-driven state, `Bloc` when events need transformers (debounce, restartable).\n- No business logic, API calls or repositories in widgets.\n\n### 11. Routing is declarative, auth lives in one redirect, deep link input is untrusted\n\n```dart\n// BAD: auth check copied into every screen, the protected screen flashes before it runs\n@override\nvoid initState() {\n  super.initState();\n  if (!auth.isLoggedIn) WidgetsBinding.instance.addPostFrameCallback((_) => context.go('/login'));\n}\n\n// GOOD: one redirect, re-evaluated whenever auth notifies\nGoRouter(\n  refreshListenable: auth,\n  redirect: (context, state) {\n    final atLogin = state.matchedLocation == '/login';\n    if (!auth.isLoggedIn) return atLogin ? null : '/login';\n    if (atLogin) return '/';\n    return null;\n  },\n  routes: [...],\n);\n```\n\n- `redirect` returns `null` for \"stay\". Always guard the target route to avoid redirect loops.\n- Path and query parameters arrive from outside the app via deep links. Parse and validate them; a\n  malformed ID shows an error screen, never a crash or a privileged action.\n- App Links (Android `assetlinks.json`, `autoVerify`) and Universal Links (Associated Domains\n  entitlement) are platform config. Call out every manifest, `Info.plist` and entitlement change.\n- Typed routes with `go_router_builder` are optional; adopt them app-wide or not at all.\n\n### 12. Build config comes from flavors and dart-defines, secrets do not\n\n```dart\n// BAD: shipped in the binary, readable with `strings`, obfuscation does not hide it\nconst apiKey = String.fromEnvironment('PAYMENT_SECRET_KEY');\n\n// GOOD: only non-secret config at build time\nconst apiBaseUrl = String.fromEnvironment('API_BASE_URL');\n// flutter run --flavor staging --dart-define-from-file=config/staging.json\n```\n\n- `--flavor` selects the Android product flavor and the Xcode scheme; both must exist natively.\n- `String.fromEnvironment` only works in a `const` context. Fail fast if a required value is empty.\n- Secrets that must stay secret live on a server; the app gets short-lived tokens at runtime and stores\n  them in the Keychain or Android Keystore backed storage.\n- Release builds: `--obfuscate --split-debug-info=<dir>`; keep the symbol files to symbolize crashes.\n\n### 13. Platform channels are generated with Pigeon\n\n```dart\n// BAD: stringly typed, untyped Map, typos fail at runtime\nfinal level = await const MethodChannel('device').invokeMethod('getBatery');\n\n// GOOD: pigeons/device_api.dart, generated Dart, Kotlin and Swift stay in sync\n@HostApi()\nabstract class DeviceApi {\n  DeviceInfo getDeviceInfo();\n  @async\n  bool requestPairing(String deviceId);\n}\n```\n\n- Native handlers run on the platform main thread by default. Move heavy work off it.\n- Native code validates every argument from Dart like any other external input.\n\n### 14. Lints are strict and warnings are not ignored\n\n```yaml\n# BAD: default template, never touched, casts from dynamic unchecked\ninclude: package:flutter_lints/flutter.yaml\n\n# GOOD\ninclude: package:flutter_lints/flutter.yaml\nanalyzer:\n  language:\n    strict-casts: true\n    strict-inference: true\n    strict-raw-types: true\nlinter:\n  rules:\n    - prefer_const_constructors\n    - prefer_final_locals\n    - unawaited_futures\n    - discarded_futures\n    - cancel_subscriptions\n    - close_sinks\n    - avoid_dynamic_calls\n```\n\n- `very_good_analysis` is a reasonable stricter alternative. Pick one set, do not mix.\n- `// ignore:` needs a reason comment next to it.\n- Use `package:logging` or a logger behind an interface, not `print`. Never log tokens or device IDs.\n\n### 15. Tests are deterministic and at the lowest useful level\n\n```dart\n// BAD: real time, slow and flaky\nawait Future<void>.delayed(const Duration(seconds: 2));\nexpect(cubit.state, 2);\n\n// GOOD: virtual time\nfakeAsync((async) {\n  final values = <int>[];\n  countdown(2).listen(values.add);\n  async.elapse(const Duration(seconds: 3));\n  expect(values, [2, 1, 0]);\n});\n```\n\n- Unit tests for notifiers, blocs and repositories; widget tests for screens; few `integration_test` flows.\n- `pumpAndSettle` times out on endless animations (a looping progress indicator). Use `pump(duration)`.\n\n## Anti-patterns\n\n- `setState` at the top of a screen for a value one small widget shows. Push state down or use a listenable.\n- `Widget _buildX()` helper methods in place of widget classes.\n- `!` on values from JSON, platform channels or route parameters.\n- `catch (e) {}` or catching `Error` (`StateError`, `TypeError`) to keep going; those are bugs.\n- `late` fields that may never be initialized on some path.\n- `dynamic` and `Map<String, dynamic>` leaking past the data layer. Map to typed models at the edge.\n- A provider or bloc that holds a `BuildContext`, a widget or a controller.\n- Mixing Riverpod, Bloc, Provider and GetX in one app.\n- `Opacity` animated every frame; use `FadeTransition` or `AnimatedOpacity`.\n- `RepaintBoundary` sprinkled everywhere \"for performance\" without a profile showing it helps.\n- Secrets, API keys or signing material in `--dart-define`, `.env` assets or source.\n- Golden tests run on every developer machine; font rendering differs across platforms.\n\n## Checklist\n\n- [ ] Matches the app's existing state management, router and codegen choices\n- [ ] Closed state modelled as sealed classes; switches exhaustive without `_`\n- [ ] Untrusted JSON and route parameters parsed with patterns or validated, no blind `as` or `!`\n- [ ] Every `StreamController` closed; broadcast only where there are many listeners\n- [ ] CPU-heavy work in `Isolate.run` or `compute`\n- [ ] Widgets are classes with `const` constructors; `build` has no side effects or futures\n- [ ] `ValueKey(id)` on reorderable list items; no keys created in `build`\n- [ ] `mounted` / `context.mounted` / `ref.mounted` / `isClosed` checked after each `await`\n- [ ] Controllers, focus nodes, subscriptions and timers disposed or cancelled\n- [ ] `ref.watch` in build, `ref.read` in callbacks, side effects in `ref.listen` or `BlocListener`\n- [ ] Auth redirect central, guarded against loops; deep link parameters validated\n- [ ] No secrets in dart-defines or assets; manifest, plist and entitlement changes called out\n- [ ] Platform channels generated with Pigeon; native work off the main thread\n- [ ] `flutter analyze` clean with strict lints; `dart format` applied\n- [ ] New logic covered by deterministic unit or widget tests; `flutter test` passes\n\n## Related skills\n\n- `mobile-code-review` -- review a Flutter MR against these rules\n- `mobile-security` -- secure storage, certificate pinning, deep link and logging audits\n- `mobile-mvvm`, `mobile-mvi` -- architecture behind Riverpod notifiers and Blocs\n- `mobile-design-patterns` -- repository, adapter and strategy patterns in Dart\n- `kotlin-android`, `swift-ios` -- native side of Pigeon channels and plugins\n- `mobile-ci-cd` -- flavors, signing, obfuscation symbols and golden tests in CI\n",
    "references/performance.md":
        "# Performance reference\n\nLoad this when fixing jank, slow startup, memory growth or app size, or when writing lists, images and\nanimations. Measure first; most \"optimizations\" without a profile add complexity and change nothing.\n\n## Measure\n\n1. Run on a real, preferably low-end, device in profile mode: `flutter run --profile`.\n   Debug mode is many times slower and its numbers are meaningless.\n2. Open DevTools:\n   - Performance view: frame chart, UI vs raster thread time, \"Track widget builds\".\n   - CPU profiler: hot Dart functions on the UI isolate.\n   - Memory view: heap snapshots and diffs to find leaked State objects and controllers.\n3. Find which thread is slow.\n\n| Slow thread | Usual cause | Fix direction |\n|---|---|---|\n| UI (Dart) | Too many rebuilds, heavy `build`, sync parsing | Narrow rebuilds, `const`, isolates |\n| Raster | `saveLayer` (opacity, clips, shader masks), huge images, blur | Cheaper effects, decode at size |\n\n4. Fix one thing, re-measure, keep the change only if the numbers improved.\n\n## Rebuild scope\n\n```dart\n// BAD: the whole screen rebuilds every second for one label\nsetState(() => _elapsed++);\n\n// GOOD: only the label listens\nValueListenableBuilder<int>(\n  valueListenable: _elapsed,\n  builder: (context, value, _) => Text('\$value s'),\n)\n```\n\n- `const` widgets are skipped on rebuild. Enable `prefer_const_constructors`.\n- Pass a `child` to `AnimatedBuilder`, `ValueListenableBuilder` and `Consumer` for the part that does\n  not change.\n- Select narrowly: `ref.watch(p.select((s) => s.count))`, `context.select((C c) => c.state.count)`.\n- `MediaQuery.sizeOf(context)`, `MediaQuery.paddingOf(context)` instead of `MediaQuery.of(context)`,\n  which rebuilds on any media change (keyboard, text scale, insets).\n\n## Lists\n\n```dart\n// BAD: builds all 5 000 rows up front\nListView(children: [for (final d in devices) DeviceTile(device: d)]);\n\n// GOOD: builds only visible rows\nListView.builder(\n  itemCount: devices.length,\n  itemExtent: 72, // or prototypeItem, when rows have a fixed height\n  itemBuilder: (context, i) => DeviceTile(key: ValueKey(devices[i].id), device: devices[i]),\n);\n```\n\n- `SliverList.builder` inside `CustomScrollView` for mixed scrolling content.\n- Never `shrinkWrap: true` on a long list inside another scrollable; it lays out every child.\n- Paginate network data; do not hold 10 000 models in memory to show 20.\n\n## Images\n\n```dart\n// BAD: 4000x3000 photo decoded at full size for a 48 px avatar (about 48 MB of pixels)\nImage.network(url, width: 48, height: 48);\n\n// GOOD: decoded at display size in physical pixels\nfinal dpr = MediaQuery.devicePixelRatioOf(context);\nImage.network(url, width: 48, height: 48, cacheWidth: (48 * dpr).round());\n```\n\n- `ResizeImage` does the same for any `ImageProvider`.\n- Precache hero or first-screen images with `precacheImage` when the screen is about to appear.\n- Prefer vector assets or correctly sized raster variants (`2.0x`, `3.0x`) over one huge PNG.\n\n## Animations and effects\n\n```dart\n// BAD: Opacity rebuilt every frame, saveLayer each frame\nAnimatedBuilder(\n  animation: _controller,\n  builder: (context, _) => Opacity(opacity: _controller.value, child: const Card()),\n);\n\n// GOOD: opacity handled in the render layer, child not rebuilt\nFadeTransition(opacity: _controller, child: const Card());\n```\n\n- Implicit animations (`AnimatedOpacity`, `AnimatedContainer`) for simple state changes.\n- Expensive: `Opacity` on large subtrees, `ShaderMask`, `ColorFilter`, `BackdropFilter`,\n  `Clip.antiAliasWithSaveLayer`. Use them on small areas.\n- For a translucent image, set the colour or opacity on the image or decoration instead of wrapping it.\n- `RepaintBoundary` isolates a frequently repainting widget (a progress ring, a live chart) from static\n  neighbours. Add it only when DevTools shows repaints spreading; it costs memory for an extra layer.\n\n## Work off the UI isolate\n\n- Parse large JSON, decode files, compute hashes and run crypto in `Isolate.run`.\n- Long-lived background processing (a stream of BLE packets to decode) can use a long-lived isolate with\n  `Isolate.spawn` and ports. Most apps never need it.\n- Avoid synchronous file I/O (`readAsStringSync`) on the UI isolate.\n\n## Startup\n\n- Keep `main()` small: initialize only what the first frame needs, defer the rest.\n- Do not `await` analytics, remote config or non-critical SDK init before `runApp`.\n- Measure with `flutter run --profile --trace-startup` and compare against the previous release.\n\n## Memory and leaks\n\n- Dispose controllers, cancel subscriptions and timers (see the main skill, rule 9).\n- `autoDispose` providers and `BlocProvider(create:)` scope state to the screen that needs it.\n- Image cache size is limited by `PaintingBinding.instance.imageCache`; raising it hides problems.\n- Use DevTools memory diffs: navigate into and out of a screen several times; a growing count of its\n  `State` class is a leak.\n\n## App size\n\n- `flutter build appbundle --analyze-size --target-platform android-arm64` (or `ipa`) and inspect in DevTools.\n- Remove unused packages and fonts; subset icon fonts (tree shaking of icons is on by default in release).\n- `--split-debug-info` moves symbols out of the binary; keep them for crash symbolication.\n\n## Rendering engine\n\n- Impeller is the default renderer on iOS and on modern Android devices; shader compilation jank warm-up\n  steps written for the old Skia backend are not needed there. Check the Flutter release notes before\n  copying older advice.\n",
    "references/platform-channels.md":
        "# Platform channels reference\n\nLoad this when Dart needs to call Kotlin or Swift, or native code needs to call or stream into Dart.\nUse `pigeon` (dev dependency) to generate type-safe channels. Hand-written `MethodChannel` code is only\nacceptable for a single trivial call in a legacy plugin.\n\n## Why Pigeon\n\n| Hand-written `MethodChannel` | Pigeon |\n|---|---|\n| Method names and argument keys are strings, typos fail at runtime | Generated, compile-time checked on all sides |\n| Arguments are `Map<Object?, Object?>`, casts everywhere | Typed data classes |\n| Dart and native drift apart silently | One definition file, regenerate both sides |\n\n## Definition file\n\n```dart\n// pigeons/device_api.dart -- not part of the app build, only input to the generator\nimport 'package:pigeon/pigeon.dart';\n\n@ConfigurePigeon(PigeonOptions(\n  dartOut: 'lib/src/platform/device_api.g.dart',\n  kotlinOut: 'android/app/src/main/kotlin/com/example/app/DeviceApi.g.kt',\n  kotlinOptions: KotlinOptions(package: 'com.example.app'),\n  swiftOut: 'ios/Runner/DeviceApi.g.swift',\n))\nclass DeviceInfo {\n  DeviceInfo({required this.model, required this.batteryLevel});\n  String model;\n  int batteryLevel;\n}\n\n@HostApi() // Dart calls native\nabstract class DeviceApi {\n  DeviceInfo getDeviceInfo();\n\n  @async\n  bool requestPairing(String deviceId);\n\n  @TaskQueue(type: TaskQueueType.serialBackgroundThread)\n  String hashFile(String path);\n}\n\n@FlutterApi() // native calls Dart\nabstract class DeviceEvents {\n  void onConnectionChanged(bool connected);\n}\n```\n\nGenerate, then commit the generated files so builds do not need the generator:\n\n```bash\ndart run pigeon --input pigeons/device_api.dart\n```\n\n- Dart `int` is a 64-bit integer: `Long` in Kotlin, `Int64` in Swift.\n- Change the definition, never the `.g.` files. Regenerate on every Pigeon version bump.\n\n## Android (Kotlin)\n\n```kotlin\nclass DeviceApiImpl(private val context: Context) : DeviceApi {\n    override fun getDeviceInfo(): DeviceInfo =\n        DeviceInfo(model = Build.MODEL, batteryLevel = readBatteryLevel(context).toLong())\n\n    // Generated handlers launch this on the main dispatcher: switch for blocking work\n    override suspend fun requestPairing(deviceId: String): Boolean =\n        withContext(Dispatchers.IO) { pairingManager.pair(deviceId) }\n\n    // Runs on the background task queue requested with @TaskQueue\n    override fun hashFile(path: String): String = sha256Of(File(path))\n}\n\nclass MainActivity : FlutterActivity() {\n    override fun configureFlutterEngine(flutterEngine: FlutterEngine) {\n        super.configureFlutterEngine(flutterEngine)\n        DeviceApi.setUp(flutterEngine.dartExecutor.binaryMessenger, DeviceApiImpl(applicationContext))\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- Pass `applicationContext`, never the `Activity`, to long-lived implementations.\n- Recent Pigeon versions generate `suspend fun` for `@async` methods; older versions generate a\n  `callback: (Result<T>) -> Unit` parameter. Follow the generated signature.\n\n## iOS (Swift)\n\n```swift\nfinal class DeviceApiImpl: DeviceApi {\n    func getDeviceInfo() throws -> DeviceInfo {\n        DeviceInfo(model: UIDevice.current.model, batteryLevel: Int64(currentBatteryLevel()))\n    }\n\n    // Generated code calls this from a main-actor Task: keep heavy work off the main actor\n    func requestPairing(deviceId: String) async throws -> Bool {\n        try await pairingService.pair(deviceId)\n    }\n\n    func hashFile(path: String) throws -> String { try sha256(of: URL(fileURLWithPath: path)) }\n}\n\n// AppDelegate, after GeneratedPluginRegistrant.register(with: self)\nlet controller = window?.rootViewController as! FlutterViewController\nDeviceApiSetup.setUp(binaryMessenger: controller.binaryMessenger, api: DeviceApiImpl())\n```\n\n- Throw `PigeonError(code:message:details:)` (Swift) or `FlutterError` (Kotlin) for expected failures;\n  Dart receives a `PlatformException` with that code.\n- Apps using the scene-based lifecycle register in the plugin or engine setup hook their template\n  provides; follow the generated project, not an old snippet.\n\n## Dart side\n\n```dart\n// BAD: generated API called straight from widgets, PlatformException leaks into UI\nfinal info = await DeviceApi().getDeviceInfo();\n\n// GOOD: wrapped in a repository that maps errors to domain types\nfinal class PlatformDeviceRepository implements DeviceRepository {\n  PlatformDeviceRepository(this._api);\n  final DeviceApi _api;\n\n  @override\n  Future<bool> pair(DeviceId id) async {\n    try {\n      return await _api.requestPairing(id.value);\n    } on PlatformException catch (e) {\n      throw PairingException(e.code);\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\n- The generated class is a detail of the data layer. Tests replace the repository, not the channel.\n- Native-to-Dart calls (`@FlutterApi`) are registered in Dart with the generated `setUp` and must be\n  invoked from the platform main thread on the native side.\n\n## Streams from native\n\n- For continuous data (BLE notifications, sensor readings, download progress) use `@EventChannelApi()`\n  in the definition file. Pigeon generates a Dart `Stream` function and a native stream handler class.\n- Cancel the Dart subscription when the screen or provider goes away; native `onCancel` must stop the\n  underlying work (scan, sensor listener) or it keeps draining the battery.\n- Throttle on the native side when the source emits faster than the UI can use (more than 60 per second).\n\n## Threading rules\n\n| Side | Default thread | Move off it with |\n|---|---|---|\n| Kotlin handler | Android main thread | `@TaskQueue` or `withContext(Dispatchers.IO)` |\n| Swift handler | Main thread / main actor | `@TaskQueue`, or async work on another actor |\n| Native -> Dart call | Must start on the platform main thread | Post to main before calling |\n| Dart handler | UI isolate | `Isolate.run` for heavy processing of the result |\n\nBLE callbacks arrive on binder threads (Android) or the central manager queue (iOS). Hop to the main\nthread before calling a `@FlutterApi` or emitting on an event sink.\n\n## Security\n\n- Native code treats every argument from Dart as untrusted input: validate paths, IDs, lengths.\n- Do not expose a generic \"run this command\" or \"read this file path\" API to Dart.\n- Do not send secrets, tokens or raw device identifiers through channels when an opaque handle works.\n- New permissions, `AndroidManifest.xml` entries, `Info.plist` usage strings or entitlements needed by\n  native code are listed in the MR.\n",
    "references/state-management.md":
        "# State management reference\n\nLoad this when writing or changing providers, notifiers, blocs or cubits. Examples target Riverpod 3\nand flutter_bloc 9. Pick one approach per app and follow what `pubspec.yaml` already uses.\n\n## Choosing\n\n| Need | Riverpod | Bloc |\n|---|---|---|\n| Dependency injection built in | Yes, providers are the DI graph | No, pair with `RepositoryProvider` or get_it |\n| Async loading, error, data | `AsyncNotifier` and `AsyncValue` | Your own sealed state classes |\n| Event debounce, cancel previous | Manual | `transformer:` with `bloc_concurrency` |\n| Strict event log and replay | No | Yes, every change starts from an event |\n| Boilerplate | Low, lower with codegen | Medium |\n\nBoth work. Switching mid-project costs more than either choice.\n\n## Riverpod\n\n### Provider types\n\n| Type | Use for |\n|---|---|\n| `Provider` | Dependencies: repositories, API clients, config |\n| `NotifierProvider` | Synchronous state with methods |\n| `AsyncNotifierProvider` | State loaded asynchronously, with methods to change it |\n| `FutureProvider` / `StreamProvider` | Read-only async values without methods |\n\n### AsyncNotifier\n\n```dart\nfinal todosProvider =\n    AsyncNotifierProvider.autoDispose<TodosNotifier, List<Todo>>(TodosNotifier.new);\n\nclass TodosNotifier extends AsyncNotifier<List<Todo>> {\n  @override\n  Future<List<Todo>> build() => ref.watch(todoRepositoryProvider).fetch();\n\n  Future<void> add(Todo todo) async {\n    final repo = ref.read(todoRepositoryProvider);\n    state = const AsyncLoading();\n    final result = await AsyncValue.guard(() async {\n      await repo.add(todo);\n      return repo.fetch();\n    });\n    if (!ref.mounted) return; // provider disposed while awaiting\n    state = result;\n  }\n}\n```\n\n- `build` uses `ref.watch` for dependencies so the notifier rebuilds when they change.\n- Methods use `ref.read`. Never `ref.watch` inside a method.\n- `AsyncValue.guard` turns exceptions into `AsyncError` instead of try/catch boilerplate.\n\n### Consuming\n\n```dart\nclass TodosScreen extends ConsumerWidget {\n  const TodosScreen({super.key});\n\n  @override\n  Widget build(BuildContext context, WidgetRef ref) {\n    ref.listen(todosProvider, (previous, next) {\n      if (next case AsyncError(:final error)) {\n        ScaffoldMessenger.of(context).showSnackBar(SnackBar(content: Text('\$error')));\n      }\n    });\n\n    return switch (ref.watch(todosProvider)) {\n      AsyncData(:final value) => TodoList(todos: value),\n      AsyncError(:final error) => ErrorView(error: error),\n      _ => const Center(child: CircularProgressIndicator()),\n    };\n  }\n}\n```\n\n| Call | Where | Effect |\n|---|---|---|\n| `ref.watch(p)` | `build` of widgets and providers | Rebuilds when `p` changes |\n| `ref.watch(p.select((s) => s.x))` | `build` | Rebuilds only when `x` changes |\n| `ref.read(p)` / `ref.read(p.notifier)` | Callbacks, notifier methods | One-off read, no subscription |\n| `ref.listen(p, cb)` | `build` or notifier `build` | Side effects: snackbar, navigation, analytics |\n\n- `ConsumerStatefulWidget` when the widget also owns controllers; `ref` is available on the State.\n- Use the `_ =>` branch for `AsyncValue` loading only. Data and error are matched explicitly.\n\n### Lifetime\n\n```dart\n// BAD: screen state kept forever, stale data on the next visit\nfinal searchProvider = NotifierProvider<SearchNotifier, SearchState>(SearchNotifier.new);\n\n// GOOD: disposed when the last listener goes away\nfinal searchProvider =\n    NotifierProvider.autoDispose<SearchNotifier, SearchState>(SearchNotifier.new);\n```\n\n- Generated providers (`@riverpod`) are auto-dispose by default; `@Riverpod(keepAlive: true)` opts out.\n- Keep a provider alive conditionally with `final link = ref.keepAlive();` and `link.close()` later.\n- Clean up in `ref.onDispose(...)`: close sockets, cancel timers, stop BLE scans.\n\n### Parameters (family)\n\n```dart\nfinal deviceProvider = AsyncNotifierProvider.autoDispose\n    .family<DeviceNotifier, Device, DeviceId>(DeviceNotifier.new);\n\nclass DeviceNotifier extends AsyncNotifier<Device> {\n  DeviceNotifier(this.id);\n  final DeviceId id;\n\n  @override\n  Future<Device> build() => ref.watch(deviceRepositoryProvider).byId(id);\n}\n\n// usage\nref.watch(deviceProvider(DeviceId('A1')));\n```\n\n- The parameter needs value equality (`==` and `hashCode`). Records, extension types over `String`\n  and `int`, and `final class` with `==` work. A new `List` each build creates a new provider each build.\n\n## Bloc\n\n### Cubit or Bloc\n\n```dart\n// Cubit: methods emit states directly\nclass CounterCubit extends Cubit<int> {\n  CounterCubit() : super(0);\n  void increment() => emit(state + 1);\n}\n\n// Bloc: events in, states out, with concurrency control\nclass SearchBloc extends Bloc<SearchEvent, SearchState> {\n  SearchBloc(this._api) : super(SearchIdle()) {\n    on<QueryChanged>(_onQueryChanged, transformer: restartable());\n  }\n  final SearchApi _api;\n\n  Future<void> _onQueryChanged(QueryChanged event, Emitter<SearchState> emit) async {\n    emit(SearchLoading());\n    try {\n      emit(SearchResults(await _api.search(event.query)));\n    } on Exception {\n      emit(SearchFailure());\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\n- `bloc_concurrency` transformers: `restartable()` (search as you type), `droppable()` (ignore taps while\n  busy), `sequential()` (queue writes). Default is concurrent.\n- Streams inside a handler: `await emit.forEach(stream, onData: (v) => State(v));`. It cancels when the\n  bloc closes. Do not `listen` manually in the constructor.\n- Cubit methods that await check `if (isClosed) return;` before `emit`. Emitting after close throws.\n\n### State equality\n\n```dart\n// BAD: list mutated in place, new state == old state, nothing rebuilds\nstate.items.add(item);\nemit(state);\n\n// GOOD: new immutable state\nemit(state.copyWith(items: [...state.items, item]));\n```\n\n- Bloc skips a state equal (`==`) to the current one. Give states value equality (`equatable`,\n  `freezed` or hand-written `==`) and never mutate them.\n\n### Widgets\n\n| Widget | Use for |\n|---|---|\n| `BlocProvider(create: ...)` | Creates and closes the bloc with the subtree |\n| `BlocProvider.value(value: ...)` | Passes an existing bloc; does not close it |\n| `BlocBuilder` | Builds UI from state; pure, no side effects |\n| `BlocSelector` / `context.select` | Rebuilds only when a derived value changes |\n| `BlocListener` | Side effects: navigation, snackbars, dialogs |\n| `BlocConsumer` | Builder and listener on the same bloc |\n\n```dart\n// BAD: bloc created with .value inside build, never closed\nBlocProvider.value(value: SearchBloc(api), child: const SearchView());\n\n// GOOD\nBlocProvider(create: (_) => SearchBloc(api), child: const SearchView());\n```\n\n- `context.read<T>()` in callbacks, `context.watch<T>()` or `context.select` in `build`. Same rule as Riverpod.\n- Pass `listenWhen` and `buildWhen` to cut noise instead of filtering inside the callback.\n\n## Both\n\n- State classes are immutable. Use `final` fields and `copyWith`.\n- Notifiers and blocs never hold `BuildContext`, widgets, controllers or `GlobalKey`s.\n- Navigation is a side effect: trigger it from `ref.listen` or `BlocListener`, not from the notifier.\n- Repositories are injected so tests can replace them. See [testing](testing.md).\n",
    "references/testing.md":
        "# Testing reference\n\nLoad this when adding or fixing Dart or Flutter tests. Packages: `flutter_test` (bundled), `test`,\n`mocktail`, `fake_async`, `bloc_test` for Bloc apps, `integration_test` (bundled with the SDK).\n\n## Pyramid\n\n| Level | Tool | Tests | Speed |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Unit | `package:test` or `flutter_test` | Repositories, mappers, notifiers, blocs, pure Dart | ms |\n| Widget | `testWidgets` + `WidgetTester` | One screen or widget with faked dependencies | ms to s |\n| Golden | `matchesGoldenFile` | Visual regressions of design-system widgets | s |\n| Integration | `integration_test` on device or emulator | A few critical user flows end to end | min |\n\nMost tests are unit and widget tests. Integration tests cover login, pairing, purchase: flows where a\nbreak costs real money or support tickets.\n\n## Fakes and mocks\n\n```dart\nclass MockTodoRepository extends Mock implements TodoRepository {}\nclass FakeTodo extends Fake implements Todo {}\n\nsetUpAll(() => registerFallbackValue(FakeTodo())); // needed for any() on custom types\n\nlate MockTodoRepository repo;\nsetUp(() => repo = MockTodoRepository());\n\ntest('loads todos once', () async {\n  when(() => repo.fetch()).thenAnswer((_) async => [Todo('1', 'Pair device')]);\n  // ...\n  verify(() => repo.fetch()).called(1);\n  verifyNever(() => repo.add(any()));\n});\n```\n\n- Stub futures with `thenAnswer((_) async => ...)`, never `thenReturn(Future...)`.\n- Prefer a hand-written fake (an in-memory repository) when many tests need the same behaviour. Mocks\n  are for verifying interactions.\n- Mock your own interfaces, not third-party classes such as `http.Client` or `Dio` directly. Wrap them.\n\n## Time\n\n```dart\n// BAD: real delay\nawait Future<void>.delayed(const Duration(seconds: 30));\n\n// GOOD: virtual time with package:fake_async\ntest('retries after backoff', () {\n  fakeAsync((async) {\n    final values = <int>[];\n    countdown(2).listen(values.add);\n    async.elapse(const Duration(seconds: 3));\n    expect(values, [2, 1, 0]);\n  });\n});\n```\n\n- Inside `testWidgets`, time is already fake: advance it with `tester.pump(duration)`.\n- Inject a clock (`DateTime Function() now`) instead of calling `DateTime.now()` in logic.\n\n## Riverpod\n\n```dart\ntest('notifier loads todos', () async {\n  when(() => repo.fetch()).thenAnswer((_) async => [Todo('1', 'Pair device')]);\n  final container = ProviderContainer.test(\n    overrides: [todoRepositoryProvider.overrideWithValue(repo)],\n  );\n  final sub = container.listen(todosProvider, (_, _) {}); // keeps autoDispose providers alive\n  await expectLater(container.read(todosProvider.future), completion(hasLength(1)));\n  sub.close();\n});\n```\n\n- `ProviderContainer.test` disposes the container automatically at the end of the test.\n- Widget tests wrap the tree in `ProviderScope(overrides: [...], child: ...)`.\n\n## Bloc\n\n```dart\nblocTest<SearchBloc, SearchState>(\n  'emits loading then results',\n  setUp: () => when(() => api.search('ble')).thenAnswer((_) async => ['Sensor A']),\n  build: () => SearchBloc(api),\n  act: (bloc) => bloc.add(QueryChanged('ble')),\n  expect: () => [isA<SearchLoading>(), isA<SearchResults>()],\n);\n```\n\n- With value equality on states, compare whole states: `expect: () => [SearchLoading(), SearchResults(['Sensor A'])]`.\n- Use `seed:` to start from a given state and `wait:` for debounced transformers.\n\n## Widget tests\n\n```dart\ntestWidgets('shows spinner then list', (tester) async {\n  when(() => repo.fetch()).thenAnswer((_) async => [Todo('1', 'Pair device')]);\n  await tester.pumpWidget(\n    ProviderScope(\n      overrides: [todoRepositoryProvider.overrideWithValue(repo)],\n      child: const MaterialApp(home: Scaffold(body: TodosScreen())),\n    ),\n  );\n\n  expect(find.byType(CircularProgressIndicator), findsOneWidget);\n  await tester.pump(); // let the future complete and rebuild\n  expect(find.text('Pair device'), findsOneWidget);\n});\n```\n\n| Call | Does |\n|---|---|\n| `pump()` | One frame; microtasks and completed futures are processed |\n| `pump(duration)` | Advances fake time, then one frame |\n| `pumpAndSettle()` | Pumps until no frames are scheduled; fails with a timeout on endless animations |\n| `runAsync(() => ...)` | Real async I/O (rare; files, real isolates) |\n\n- Material widgets need `MaterialApp` and usually `Scaffold` (or `Material`) above them.\n- Find by semantics a user sees (`find.text`, `find.bySemanticsLabel`, `find.byTooltip`) before `find.byKey`.\n- `tester.enterText`, `tester.tap`, then `pump`; nothing rebuilds until a frame is pumped.\n\n## Golden tests\n\n```dart\ntestWidgets('device card', (tester) async {\n  await tester.pumpWidget(const MaterialApp(home: Scaffold(body: DeviceCard(device: sampleDevice))));\n  await expectLater(find.byType(DeviceCard), matchesGoldenFile('goldens/device_card.png'));\n}, tags: 'golden');\n```\n\n- Update with `flutter test --update-goldens`; review the PNG diff in the MR.\n- Generate and compare on one pinned platform (usually the CI Linux image). Font rendering differs on\n  macOS and Windows. Skip or tag goldens locally: `flutter test --exclude-tags golden`.\n- Load real fonts in goldens, or text renders as boxes and hides layout bugs.\n\n## Integration tests\n\n```dart\n// integration_test/pairing_test.dart\nvoid main() {\n  IntegrationTestWidgetsFlutterBinding.ensureInitialized();\n\n  testWidgets('user pairs a device', (tester) async {\n    app.main();\n    await tester.pumpAndSettle();\n    await tester.tap(find.text('Add device'));\n    // ...\n  });\n}\n```\n\n- Run: `flutter test integration_test --flavor staging`.\n- Point at a test backend through `--dart-define-from-file`. Never at production with real user data.\n- Native permission dialogs are outside Flutter; pre-grant permissions in the test setup or use a\n  dedicated driver tool.\n\n## CI commands\n\n```bash\ndart format --output=none --set-exit-if-changed .\nflutter analyze\nflutter test --coverage\n```\n\n- Fail the build on analyzer warnings, not only errors.\n- Coverage is a signal, not a goal. Uncovered branches in notifiers and blocs matter more than widgets.\n",
  },
  "java-android": {
    "SKILL.md":
        "---\nname: java-android\ndescription: \"Writes, reviews and modernizes Java code in Android apps, often legacy or mixed with Kotlin. Covers Java 17 and 21 features as D8/R8 desugaring supports them (records, switch expressions) versus library APIs needing core library desugaring or a minimum API level, immutability (final fields, unmodifiable collections, records), JSpecify @NullMarked and AndroidX nullability annotations for Kotlin interop, leak-free threading (static Context, inner-class Handler, Looper.getMainLooper, deprecated AsyncTask, ExecutorService shutdown, Future cancel), LiveData and ViewModel (setValue vs postValue, getViewLifecycleOwner), ViewBinding in onDestroyView, ListAdapter and DiffUtil, try-with-resources, equals and hashCode, incremental Java to Kotlin migration (@JvmStatic, @JvmOverloads, @JvmField) and tests with JUnit, Mockito, InstantTaskExecutorRule and Espresso IdlingResource. Use when editing, reviewing or migrating .java files in an Android project.\"\nlicense: MIT\nmetadata:\n  author: Ahmad Hussain\n  version: \"1.0.0\"\n  pack: mobile-dev-kit\n---\n\n# Java on Android\n\nKeep Java Android code correct, leak-free and Kotlin-friendly while it lives, and move it to Kotlin in\nsmall safe steps when the team decides to.\n\n## When to use\n\n- Editing, fixing or reviewing `.java` files in an Android app or library.\n- Bumping the Java language level, or using a JDK API that may not exist on the app's `minSdk`.\n- Java code that Kotlin code calls (or will call) and needs nullability and interop annotations.\n- Leaks, ANRs or crashes caused by Handlers, executors, Fragments, adapters or unclosed resources.\n- Planning or executing an incremental Java-to-Kotlin migration.\n\nNot for: Kotlin-first code (use `kotlin-android`), architecture choices (use `mobile-mvvm`, `mobile-mvi`).\n\n## Workflow\n\n1. Read the module's build file: `compileOptions` source and target level, `minSdk`, and whether\n   `isCoreLibraryDesugaringEnabled` is on. These decide which features and APIs are safe.\n2. Load the reference that matches the task: [Threading](references/threading.md),\n   [Migration to Kotlin](references/migration-to-kotlin.md), [Testing](references/testing.md).\n3. If the project already builds Kotlin and the team has not said otherwise, write new classes in Kotlin.\n   Change existing Java minimally and in place.\n4. Add nullability annotations to every public signature you touch.\n5. Apply the rules below. Prefer fixing leaks and threading over stylistic rewrites.\n6. Run lint (`./gradlew lint`) for `NewApi`, threading annotation and resource-leak checks, then the tests.\n7. Keep conversion or modernization commits separate from behaviour changes.\n\n## Toolchain: what Java you can use\n\n| Feature | Java | On Android |\n|---|---|---|\n| Lambdas, method references, default and static interface methods | 8 | Desugared by D8 for every API level |\n| `var`, switch expressions, text blocks, `instanceof` patterns | 10-16 | Compile-time features, every API level |\n| Records | 16 | Supported by current AGP (D8 desugars them). Check reflection-based JSON libraries |\n| Sealed classes and interfaces | 17 | Only on recent AGP / D8. Verify before relying on them |\n| Pattern matching for `switch`, record patterns | 21 | Needs Java 21 source level and AGP / D8 support. Verify first |\n| `java.time` (API 26), `java.util.stream`, `Optional`, `java.util.function` (API 24) | library | Below those API levels only with core library desugaring |\n| Newer JDK methods (`List.of`, `List.getFirst()`, `Stream.toList()`) | library | Tied to newer API levels. Trust lint `NewApi`; desugaring covers only part |\n\nThe JDK that runs Gradle (17+ for current AGP) is separate from the language level and from the APIs\navailable at runtime. The runtime APIs come from the device's Android version.\n\n## Rules\n\n### 1. Language level is free, library APIs are not\n\n```java\n// BAD: compiles against a recent compileSdk, crashes on older devices (added to Android in API 35)\nDevice first = devices.getFirst();\n\n// GOOD\nDevice first = devices.get(0);\n```\n\n```kotlin\n// GOOD: java.time on minSdk below 26 through core library desugaring (app/build.gradle.kts)\nandroid {\n    compileOptions {\n        isCoreLibraryDesugaringEnabled = true\n        sourceCompatibility = JavaVersion.VERSION_17\n        targetCompatibility = JavaVersion.VERSION_17\n    }\n}\ndependencies { coreLibraryDesugaring(libs.desugar.jdk.libs) }\n```\n\nNever suppress `NewApi` without a `Build.VERSION.SDK_INT` check or confirmed desugaring.\n\n### 2. Immutable by default\n\n```java\n// BAD: public mutable fields, internal list shared with callers\npublic class Device {\n    public String name;\n    public List<String> tags;\n}\n\n// GOOD: record with a defensive copy (works on every API level)\npublic record Device(String id, String name, List<String> tags) {\n    public Device {\n        Objects.requireNonNull(id);\n        tags = Collections.unmodifiableList(new ArrayList<>(tags));\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- No records? `final` class, `private final` fields, no setters, copies in and unmodifiable views out.\n- `static final` constants only for truly immutable values. A `static final List` is still mutable.\n\n### 3. Nullability is declared for Kotlin callers\n\n```java\n// package-info.java: everything in the package is non-null unless marked\n@NullMarked\npackage com.example.devices;\n\nimport org.jspecify.annotations.NullMarked;\n```\n\n```java\n// BAD: Kotlin sees String! (platform type) and skips null checks, NPE at runtime\npublic String findName(String id) { return names.get(id); }\n\n// GOOD: inside @NullMarked, only nullable values are annotated\npublic @Nullable String findName(String id) { return names.get(id); }\n```\n\n- JSpecify `@Nullable` is a type-use annotation: `List<@Nullable String>`, `String @Nullable []`.\n- Packages not yet `@NullMarked` use AndroidX `@NonNull` / `@Nullable` on every public parameter and return.\n- Recent Kotlin compilers report JSpecify violations as errors, so wrong annotations break the Kotlin build.\n\n### 4. Nothing long-lived references an Activity, Fragment or View\n\n```java\n// BAD: static field keeps the Activity alive for the process lifetime\nprivate static Context context;\n\n// BAD: non-static inner class holds the outer Activity while queued or running\nprivate class Poller implements Runnable {\n    @Override public void run() { statusView.setText(read()); }\n}\n\n// GOOD: application context where a Context must be stored; work moved to a ViewModel\npublic final class StatusRepository {\n    private final Context appContext;\n    public StatusRepository(Context context) { this.appContext = context.getApplicationContext(); }\n}\n```\n\n- Anonymous classes and lambdas that use outer members capture the outer instance too.\n- `WeakReference` is a last resort for legacy callbacks, not a design.\n\n### 5. Main-thread work goes through an explicit Looper; no AsyncTask\n\n```java\n// BAD: deprecated, binds to the calling thread's Looper\nHandler handler = new Handler();\n\n// BAD: AsyncTask is deprecated (API 30); leaks the Activity and loses results on rotation\nnew LoadTask().execute();\n\n// GOOD\nprivate final Handler main = new Handler(Looper.getMainLooper());\n\n@Override protected void onDestroy() {\n    main.removeCallbacksAndMessages(null);\n    super.onDestroy();\n}\n```\n\nReplace `AsyncTask` with an `ExecutorService` in a ViewModel that publishes through `LiveData`\n(see [Threading](references/threading.md)).\n\n### 6. Every executor has an owner that shuts it down; every Future can be cancelled\n\n```java\n// BAD: new thread pool per call, never shut down\nExecutors.newFixedThreadPool(4).execute(this::sync);\n\n// GOOD: owned by the ViewModel, cancelled with it\npublic final class DevicesViewModel extends ViewModel {\n    private final ExecutorService io = Executors.newSingleThreadExecutor();\n    private @Nullable Future<?> loading;\n\n    public void load() {\n        if (loading != null) loading.cancel(true);\n        loading = io.submit(this::loadBlocking);\n    }\n\n    @Override protected void onCleared() { io.shutdownNow(); }\n}\n```\n\nIf the executor is injected and shared app-wide, cancel the `Future`s in `onCleared()` instead of shutting\nthe executor down.\n\n### 7. LiveData: right setter, read-only exposure, view lifecycle owner\n\n```java\n// BAD: setValue from a worker thread throws; mutable type exposed\npublic MutableLiveData<UiState> state = new MutableLiveData<>();\nio.execute(() -> state.setValue(load()));\n\n// GOOD\nprivate final MutableLiveData<UiState> state = new MutableLiveData<>(UiState.loading());\npublic LiveData<UiState> getState() { return state; }\nio.execute(() -> state.postValue(load()));\n```\n\n```java\n// BAD (Fragment): observers pile up after each view recreation\nviewModel.getState().observe(this, this::render);\n\n// GOOD\nviewModel.getState().observe(getViewLifecycleOwner(), this::render);\n```\n\n`postValue` keeps only the latest pending value. Use it for state, not for events that must all arrive.\n\n### 8. ViewBinding is cleared in onDestroyView\n\n```java\n// BAD: binding outlives the Fragment's view and leaks the whole view tree\nprivate FragmentDevicesBinding binding;\n\n// GOOD\nprivate @Nullable FragmentDevicesBinding binding;\n\n@Override\npublic void onViewCreated(@NonNull View view, @Nullable Bundle savedInstanceState) {\n    binding = FragmentDevicesBinding.bind(view);\n}\n\n@Override\npublic void onDestroyView() {\n    binding = null;\n    super.onDestroyView();\n}\n```\n\nUse the `Fragment(R.layout.fragment_devices)` constructor so `onCreateView` is not needed.\n\n### 9. Lists use ListAdapter and DiffUtil\n\n```java\n// BAD: full rebind, no animations, scroll state jumps\nadapter.setItems(items);\nadapter.notifyDataSetChanged();\n\n// GOOD\npublic final class DeviceAdapter extends ListAdapter<Device, DeviceAdapter.Holder> {\n    private static final DiffUtil.ItemCallback<Device> DIFF = new DiffUtil.ItemCallback<>() {\n        @Override public boolean areItemsTheSame(@NonNull Device a, @NonNull Device b) { return a.id().equals(b.id()); }\n        @Override public boolean areContentsTheSame(@NonNull Device a, @NonNull Device b) { return a.equals(b); }\n    };\n\n    public DeviceAdapter() { super(DIFF); }\n    // onCreateViewHolder / onBindViewHolder use getItem(position)\n}\n\nadapter.submitList(items); // new list instance each time; never mutate a submitted list\n```\n\n### 10. Resources are closed with try-with-resources\n\n```java\n// BAD: leaks on exception, StrictMode reports it\nInputStream in = resolver.openInputStream(uri);\nbyte[] data = readAll(in);\nin.close();\n\n// GOOD\ntry (InputStream in = resolver.openInputStream(uri)) {\n    if (in == null) throw new FileNotFoundException(uri.toString());\n    return readAll(in);\n}\n```\n\nApplies to `Cursor`, streams, `ParcelFileDescriptor`, `TypedArray` (API 31+ implements `AutoCloseable`;\ncall `recycle()` below that).\n\n### 11. equals and hashCode are overridden together\n\n```java\n// BAD: equals without hashCode breaks HashSet, HashMap and DiffUtil\n@Override public boolean equals(Object o) { return o instanceof Device d && id.equals(d.id); }\n\n// GOOD: both, over the same fields (or use a record)\n@Override public boolean equals(@Nullable Object o) {\n    return o instanceof Device d && id.equals(d.id) && name.equals(d.name);\n}\n@Override public int hashCode() { return Objects.hash(id, name); }\n```\n\n### 12. Exceptions and interrupts are never swallowed\n\n```java\n// BAD\ntry { worker.join(); } catch (InterruptedException e) { }\ntry { sync(); } catch (Exception e) { e.printStackTrace(); }\n\n// GOOD\ntry {\n    worker.join();\n} catch (InterruptedException e) {\n    Thread.currentThread().interrupt();\n    return;\n}\ntry {\n    sync();\n} catch (IOException e) {\n    state.postValue(UiState.offline());\n}\n```\n\n### 13. Interop annotations follow the direction of calls\n\n```kotlin\n// Kotlin code called from Java\nclass Units {\n    companion object {\n        @JvmStatic fun celsius(f: Double): Double = (f - 32) / 1.8 // Units.celsius(..) from Java\n        const val MAX_MTU = 517                                     // compile-time constant\n    }\n}\nclass ScanSpec @JvmOverloads constructor(val timeoutMs: Long = 10_000, val lowLatency: Boolean = false)\n```\n\n`@JvmField` exposes a non-`const` property as a field. Details and ordering are in\n[Migration to Kotlin](references/migration-to-kotlin.md).\n\n### 14. Tests are deterministic\n\n```java\n// BAD: real threads and sleeps\nviewModel.load();\nThread.sleep(500);\n\n// GOOD: inject a direct executor; InstantTaskExecutorRule runs LiveData synchronously\n@Rule public InstantTaskExecutorRule liveData = new InstantTaskExecutorRule();\n\nDevicesViewModel vm = new DevicesViewModel(fakeRepo, Runnable::run);\n```\n\nSee [Testing](references/testing.md) for Mockito, Espresso and `IdlingResource`.\n\n## Anti-patterns\n\n- Rewriting a working Java class in a review that was about a bug fix.\n- `static` fields holding `Context`, `Activity`, `View`, `Fragment`, adapters or listeners.\n- `new Handler()`, `AsyncTask`, `Executors.new...` inside an Activity or Fragment.\n- `observe(this, ...)` in a Fragment; ViewBinding fields never cleared.\n- `notifyDataSetChanged()` for list updates; mutating a list already passed to `submitList`.\n- Public signatures without nullability annotations in code Kotlin calls.\n- `catch (Exception e) { e.printStackTrace(); }` and empty `InterruptedException` handlers.\n- Raising the Java level and assuming the JDK library on the device matches.\n- Converting a file to Kotlin and changing its behaviour in the same commit.\n- Logging tokens, device identifiers or personal data with `Log.d` (escalate to `mobile-security`).\n\n## Checklist\n\n- [ ] Language level, `minSdk` and core library desugaring checked before using a JDK API\n- [ ] Lint `NewApi` clean; no blanket suppressions\n- [ ] Value types immutable (records or final fields, defensive copies)\n- [ ] Package `@NullMarked` or public signatures annotated with `@Nullable` / `@NonNull`\n- [ ] No static or long-lived references to Activity, Fragment, View or non-application Context\n- [ ] Handlers use `Looper.getMainLooper()` and are cleared; no `AsyncTask` in new code\n- [ ] Executors shut down by their owner; Futures cancelled in `onCleared()`\n- [ ] `setValue` on main thread, `postValue` from workers; `LiveData` exposed read-only\n- [ ] Fragments observe with `getViewLifecycleOwner()`; binding nulled in `onDestroyView()`\n- [ ] RecyclerView uses `ListAdapter` + `DiffUtil` with id-based `areItemsTheSame`\n- [ ] Closeables in try-with-resources\n- [ ] `equals` and `hashCode` overridden together\n- [ ] No swallowed exceptions; interrupt status restored\n- [ ] Tests use direct executors and `InstantTaskExecutorRule`; Espresso uses `IdlingResource`, not sleeps\n\n## Related skills\n\n- `kotlin-android` -- target idioms when migrating, and Kotlin-side interop rules\n- `mobile-mvvm` -- ViewModel and LiveData or StateFlow architecture\n- `mobile-design-patterns` -- Builder, Factory and Strategy where Java needs them\n- `mobile-code-review` -- review workflow and severity levels\n- `mobile-security` -- storage, logging and network rules for legacy Java code\n- `mobile-ci-cd` -- lint and test gates in the build pipeline\n",
    "references/migration-to-kotlin.md":
        "# Incremental Java to Kotlin migration\n\nLoad this when planning or doing a Java-to-Kotlin conversion in an Android codebase. The goal is a\ncodebase that builds and ships at every step, not a big-bang rewrite.\n\n## Order of work\n\n1. Make the module build Kotlin: the Kotlin Android plugin, or the built-in Kotlin support of newer AGP\n   versions. Versions go in `libs.versions.toml`.\n2. Move annotation processors that Kotlin classes will need (Room, Hilt, Glide) from `annotationProcessor`\n   to KSP. Java-only `annotationProcessor` does not see Kotlin sources.\n3. Annotate nullability in the Java code first (`@NullMarked` per package, `@Nullable` where needed).\n   The converter then produces correct nullable types instead of guesses.\n4. Write or extend tests around the class being converted, if they are missing.\n5. Convert in this order:\n   - Tests (low risk, fast feedback on Kotlin setup).\n   - Leaf classes: models, mappers, utilities with no Android dependencies.\n   - Repositories and data sources.\n   - ViewModels.\n   - Activities, Fragments and adapters last, usually together with a move to Compose or a rewrite of the screen.\n6. One class or small cluster per merge request. Conversion commit separate from cleanup and behaviour changes.\n\nDo not convert code that is scheduled for deletion, or a large class nobody can test.\n\n## Converting a file\n\n1. Use the IDE converter (\"Convert Java File to Kotlin File\" in the Code menu).\n2. Build and run tests before touching anything else. Commit.\n3. Clean up in a second commit, using the list below.\n\n## Cleanup after the converter\n\n| Converter output | Replace with |\n|---|---|\n| `!!` everywhere | Correct nullable types, `?.`, `?:`, `requireNotNull(x) { \"reason\" }` |\n| `lateinit var` for constructor-provided values | `val` constructor properties |\n| `var` fields that never change | `val` |\n| Getter / setter pairs | Properties |\n| `companion object` with only constants | Top-level `const val` or `private const val` |\n| Static utility class turned into `object` | Top-level functions or extension functions |\n| Java-style builders | Default and named arguments; delete the builder |\n| `equals` / `hashCode` / `toString` written by hand | `data class` (when all fields are part of identity) |\n| Listener interfaces with one method | `fun interface` or function types |\n| `Executor` + callback code | `suspend` functions and `Flow` (only once callers are Kotlin) |\n| `if (x != null) { x.foo() }` | `x?.foo()` or smart casts |\n\nBehaviour to double-check after conversion:\n\n- `==` in Kotlin calls `equals`. Java `==` on objects compared references.\n- Integer division, `Long` / `Int` literals and overflow in arithmetic the converter rewrote.\n- `when` without `else` over a Java enum or int constant.\n- Platform types from remaining Java code (`String!`) that the converter treated as non-null.\n\n## Keeping Java callers working\n\n```kotlin\n// Static access from Java: DeviceIds.parse(...) instead of DeviceIds.Companion.parse(...)\nclass DeviceIds {\n    companion object {\n        @JvmStatic fun parse(raw: String): DeviceId? = ...\n        const val PREFIX = \"dev-\"         // primitives and String: const val\n        @JvmField val DEFAULT_TAGS = listOf(\"new\") // other types: @JvmField exposes a field\n    }\n}\n\n// Default arguments: Java sees only the full signature without @JvmOverloads\nclass ScanSpec @JvmOverloads constructor(\n    val timeoutMs: Long = 10_000,\n    val lowLatency: Boolean = false,\n)\n\n// Checked exceptions Java callers must catch\n@Throws(IOException::class)\nfun readConfig(file: File): Config = ...\n```\n\n```kotlin\n// Bytes.kt: name the class generated for top-level functions (must precede the package line)\n@file:JvmName(\"Bytes\")\n\npackage com.example.ble\n\nfun ByteArray.toHex(): String = joinToString(\"\") { \"%02x\".format(it) } // Java: Bytes.toHex(array)\n```\n\n- Callbacks for Java callers: `fun interface` instead of Kotlin function types. A Java lambda for\n  `(String) -> Unit` must `return Unit.INSTANCE`.\n- `suspend` functions are awkward from Java. While Java callers remain, add a Java-facing wrapper that\n  returns a `Future` or takes a callback, and delete it with the last Java caller.\n- `internal` members get mangled names in Java. Do not rely on calling them from Java.\n- `object` singletons are `Foo.INSTANCE` from Java; `@JvmStatic` on their members avoids that.\n- Sealed classes and `data class` work from Java, but Java callers do not get Kotlin's exhaustive `when`\n  checks. Java branches over them end with a default that throws.\n\n## Java code that Kotlin calls\n\n- Every public signature carries nullability: JSpecify `@NullMarked` packages or AndroidX `@Nullable`\n  / `@NonNull`. Without them Kotlin sees platform types and null checks disappear.\n- Getters and setters named `getX` / `setX` / `isX` appear as properties in Kotlin.\n- Java `List` returns are seen as `(Mutable)List` in Kotlin; return unmodifiable collections so Kotlin's\n  read-only view is honest.\n- Lombok-generated members are not visible to Kotlin in the same module without the Kotlin Lombok compiler\n  plugin. Delombok or convert Lombok classes early.\n\n## Tracking progress\n\n- Measure the Java / Kotlin line ratio per module, not repo-wide, and convert module by module so a module\n  can eventually drop Java-specific setup.\n- Lint and detekt or ktlint run on converted files from the first commit, so style debt does not accumulate.\n- Avoid long-lived migration branches. Small merges to the main line keep conflicts manageable.\n",
    "references/testing.md":
        "# Testing Java Android code\n\nLoad this when writing or fixing tests for Java classes: ViewModels with `LiveData`, repositories with\nexecutors, and Espresso UI tests.\n\n## Setup\n\n| Test type | Runs on | Typical libraries |\n|---|---|---|\n| Local unit (`src/test`) | Desktop JVM | JUnit 4 or 5, Mockito, `androidx.arch.core:core-testing`, Robolectric when framework classes are needed |\n| Instrumented (`src/androidTest`) | Device or emulator | AndroidX Test (`AndroidJUnit4`), Espresso, `mockito-android`, Hilt testing |\n\n- JUnit 4 is the default for both. JUnit 5 (Jupiter) for local tests requires the Gradle test task to use the\n  JUnit Platform. Instrumented tests stay on JUnit 4 unless a third-party plugin is added.\n- `InstantTaskExecutorRule` is a JUnit 4 rule. With Jupiter, keep LiveData tests on JUnit 4 through the\n  vintage engine, or write an equivalent extension.\n- Dependency versions live in `libs.versions.toml`.\n\n## ViewModel with LiveData and an executor\n\nProduction code takes its executor as a constructor parameter, so tests can run work synchronously.\n\n```java\n/** Runs every task on the calling thread. AbstractExecutorService.submit wraps tasks and calls execute. */\nfinal class DirectExecutorService extends AbstractExecutorService {\n    private volatile boolean shutdown;\n\n    @Override public void execute(Runnable command) { command.run(); }\n    @Override public void shutdown() { shutdown = true; }\n    @Override public List<Runnable> shutdownNow() { shutdown = true; return Collections.emptyList(); }\n    @Override public boolean isShutdown() { return shutdown; }\n    @Override public boolean isTerminated() { return shutdown; }\n    @Override public boolean awaitTermination(long timeout, TimeUnit unit) { return true; }\n}\n```\n\n```java\npublic class DevicesViewModelTest {\n\n    @Rule public InstantTaskExecutorRule liveData = new InstantTaskExecutorRule();\n\n    private final DeviceRepository repo = mock(DeviceRepository.class);\n\n    @Test\n    public void load_success_emitsLoaded() throws Exception {\n        List<Device> devices = Collections.singletonList(new Device(\"1\", \"Gateway\", Collections.emptyList()));\n        when(repo.devices()).thenReturn(devices);\n        DevicesViewModel vm = new DevicesViewModel(repo, new DirectExecutorService());\n\n        vm.load();\n\n        assertEquals(UiState.loaded(devices), vm.getState().getValue());\n    }\n\n    @Test\n    public void load_ioError_emitsError() throws Exception {\n        when(repo.devices()).thenThrow(new IOException(\"offline\"));\n        DevicesViewModel vm = new DevicesViewModel(repo, new DirectExecutorService());\n\n        vm.load();\n\n        assertEquals(UiState.error(), vm.getState().getValue());\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- With `InstantTaskExecutorRule`, `postValue` delivers immediately, so `getValue()` is up to date.\n- To assert a sequence of states, record them with an observer:\n\n```java\nList<UiState> seen = new ArrayList<>();\nObserver<UiState> observer = seen::add;\nvm.getState().observeForever(observer);\ntry {\n    vm.load();\n    assertEquals(Arrays.asList(UiState.loading(), UiState.loaded(devices)), seen);\n} finally {\n    vm.getState().removeObserver(observer);\n}\n```\n\n`UiState` needs `equals` / `hashCode` (a record works) for these assertions.\n\n## Mockito\n\n- `mockito-core` for local tests, `mockito-android` for instrumented tests. Mockito 5 uses the inline mock\n  maker by default, so final classes and records can be mocked; prefer not to.\n- Fakes beat mocks for repositories and data sources: a small in-memory implementation is easier to read\n  and does not break on refactors.\n- Mock only what the class under test talks to directly. Never mock value types.\n- `verify(repo).refresh()` for interactions that are the behaviour under test; otherwise assert on state.\n- `ArgumentCaptor` for arguments built inside the class under test.\n\n## Framework classes in local tests\n\n- Code that touches `Context`, resources or `SharedPreferences` runs under Robolectric with\n  `@RunWith(AndroidJUnit4.class)` and `unitTests.isIncludeAndroidResources = true` in the Gradle test options.\n- Better still, keep framework calls at the edges so most logic needs no Android classes at all.\n\n## Espresso\n\n```java\n@RunWith(AndroidJUnit4.class)\npublic class DevicesScreenTest {\n\n    @Rule public ActivityScenarioRule<MainActivity> activity = new ActivityScenarioRule<>(MainActivity.class);\n\n    @Test\n    public void refresh_showsDevices() {\n        onView(withId(R.id.refresh)).perform(click());\n        onView(withText(\"Gateway\")).check(matches(isDisplayed()));\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- Espresso waits for the main thread and `AsyncTask` pool only. Work on your own executors needs an\n  `IdlingResource`, or Espresso asserts before the data arrives.\n- Never `Thread.sleep` in UI tests. It is slow on fast devices and flaky on slow ones.\n\n```java\n// Debug or test source set: counts in-flight background work\npublic final class BackgroundWork {\n    public static final CountingIdlingResource IDLING = new CountingIdlingResource(\"background-work\");\n    private BackgroundWork() {}\n}\n\n// Around asynchronous work in the code under test\nBackgroundWork.IDLING.increment();\nio.execute(() -> {\n    try {\n        state.postValue(UiState.loaded(repo.devices()));\n    } catch (IOException e) {\n        state.postValue(UiState.error());\n    } finally {\n        BackgroundWork.IDLING.decrement();\n    }\n});\n\n// Test\n@Before public void registerIdling() { IdlingRegistry.getInstance().register(BackgroundWork.IDLING); }\n@After public void unregisterIdling() { IdlingRegistry.getInstance().unregister(BackgroundWork.IDLING); }\n```\n\n- Cleaner alternative with Hilt: replace the production executor module in tests (`@TestInstallIn` or\n  `@UninstallModules`) with one whose executor Espresso can track.\n- Lists: `RecyclerViewActions` from `espresso-contrib` to scroll to or click items by position or matcher.\n- Fragments in isolation: `FragmentScenario` from `fragment-testing`. Hilt fragments need a Hilt-enabled\n  host activity in the debug source set.\n\n## Hilt in instrumented tests\n\n```java\n@HiltAndroidTest\npublic class SyncFlowTest {\n    @Rule public HiltAndroidRule hilt = new HiltAndroidRule(this);\n\n    @Before public void setUp() { hilt.inject(); }\n}\n```\n\nThe instrumentation runner must start `HiltTestApplication` (a custom `AndroidJUnitRunner` that overrides\n`newApplication`).\n\n## What to test first in legacy Java\n\n1. Pure logic hidden in Activities and Fragments: extract it into plain classes, then unit test.\n2. Parsers and mappers (DTOs, byte frames, CRC checks) with table-driven tests including malformed input.\n3. ViewModel state transitions for success, error and cancellation.\n4. One Espresso smoke test per critical flow, not per screen detail.\n",
    "references/threading.md":
        "# Threading in Java Android code\n\nLoad this when Java code starts background work, touches the main thread, handles callbacks from\nsystem services (Bluetooth, location, sensors) or replaces `AsyncTask`.\n\n## Quick scan\n\n| Pattern | Why | Fix |\n|---|---|---|\n| `new Thread(...).start()` in UI code | Unowned, no cancellation, leaks the Activity | Owned executor in a ViewModel or repository |\n| `AsyncTask` | Deprecated, leaks, loses results on rotation | `ExecutorService` + `LiveData` |\n| `new Handler()` | Deprecated, implicit Looper | `new Handler(Looper.getMainLooper())` |\n| `Executors.new...` without `shutdown()` | Thread leak | Owner shuts it down, or inject a shared one |\n| `future.get()` on the main thread | ANR | Callback, `LiveData`, or `get` only on a worker |\n| `CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(task)` with no executor | Runs on the common ForkJoin pool | Pass an explicit executor |\n| Work inside a `BluetoothGattCallback` method | Blocks a binder thread | Hand off to a single-thread executor |\n| Check-then-act on a shared `HashMap` | Race | `ConcurrentHashMap.computeIfAbsent` or a lock |\n\n## Replacing AsyncTask\n\n```java\n// BAD\nprivate class LoadTask extends AsyncTask<Void, Void, List<Device>> {\n    @Override protected List<Device> doInBackground(Void... v) { return repo.devices(); }\n    @Override protected void onPostExecute(List<Device> d) { adapter.submitList(d); }\n}\n\n// GOOD: ViewModel owns the work, the Fragment only observes\n@HiltViewModel\npublic final class DevicesViewModel extends ViewModel {\n    private final DeviceRepository repo;\n    private final ExecutorService io;\n    private final MutableLiveData<UiState> state = new MutableLiveData<>(UiState.loading());\n    private @Nullable Future<?> pending;\n\n    @Inject\n    public DevicesViewModel(DeviceRepository repo, @IoExecutor ExecutorService io) {\n        this.repo = repo;\n        this.io = io;\n    }\n\n    public LiveData<UiState> getState() { return state; }\n\n    public void load() {\n        if (pending != null) pending.cancel(true);\n        pending = io.submit(() -> {\n            try {\n                state.postValue(UiState.loaded(repo.devices()));\n            } catch (IOException e) {\n                state.postValue(UiState.error());\n            }\n        });\n    }\n\n    @Override protected void onCleared() {\n        if (pending != null) pending.cancel(true); // shared executor: cancel work, do not shut it down\n    }\n}\n```\n\n`@IoExecutor` is a project-defined qualifier. Tests pass an `ExecutorService` that runs tasks synchronously\n(see [Testing](testing.md)).\n\n## Executors\n\n- One shared, injected pool for IO (`@Singleton` in DI). Per-feature pools only when work must be serialized,\n  usually `Executors.newSingleThreadExecutor()`.\n- Owners that create an executor call `shutdown()` (finish queued work) or `shutdownNow()` (interrupt) when\n  their lifecycle ends.\n- `Future.cancel(true)` only interrupts. Blocking code must respond: check\n  `Thread.currentThread().isInterrupted()` in loops and do not swallow `InterruptedException`.\n- Main-thread executor without a Context dependency on the call site: `ContextCompat.getMainExecutor(context)`.\n- Name threads with a `ThreadFactory` so stack traces and profilers show what each pool is for.\n\n```java\nThreadFactory named = r -> new Thread(r, \"ble-io\");\nExecutorService bleIo = Executors.newSingleThreadExecutor(named);\n```\n\n## Futures\n\n- `CompletableFuture` (API 24+) chains with explicit executors:\n\n```java\nCompletableFuture\n    .supplyAsync(repo::loadConfig, io)\n    .thenApplyAsync(ConfigMapper::toDomain, io)\n    .whenComplete((config, error) -> state.postValue(error == null ? UiState.ready(config) : UiState.error()));\n```\n\n- Libraries that expect `ListenableFuture` (WorkManager, CameraX) can be bridged with\n  `CallbackToFutureAdapter` from `androidx.concurrent:concurrent-futures`.\n\n## Main thread and Handlers\n\n```java\n// BAD: inner class Handler keeps the Activity alive while messages are queued\nprivate final Handler handler = new Handler() {\n    @Override public void handleMessage(Message msg) { title.setText(\"done\"); }\n};\n\n// GOOD\nprivate final Handler main = new Handler(Looper.getMainLooper());\nprivate final Runnable refresh = this::refresh; // field, so it can be removed\n\n@Override protected void onStart() {\n    super.onStart();\n    main.postDelayed(refresh, 1_000);\n}\n\n@Override protected void onStop() {\n    main.removeCallbacks(refresh);\n    super.onStop();\n}\n```\n\n- Annotate methods with `@MainThread`, `@WorkerThread` or `@AnyThread` (`androidx.annotation`); lint checks calls\n  between them.\n\n## Shared state\n\n- Immutable snapshots shared between threads are safe without locks. Prefer replacing a reference over\n  mutating shared collections.\n- `volatile` for a single flag or reference written by one thread and read by others.\n- `AtomicInteger`, `AtomicReference` for counters and compare-and-set updates.\n- `synchronized` blocks for invariants spanning several fields. Never call out to unknown code while holding a lock.\n\n```java\n// BAD: two threads both miss and both create\nif (!cache.containsKey(id)) cache.put(id, load(id));\n\n// GOOD\nSession session = sessions.computeIfAbsent(id, this::open); // ConcurrentHashMap\n```\n\n## System callbacks (Bluetooth, location, sensors)\n\n- `BluetoothGattCallback` methods run on binder threads, not the main thread. Do not block them, and do not\n  touch views or call `setValue` from them.\n- Hand each callback to one single-thread executor, so state updates and the next GATT operation are\n  serialized in callback order.\n- Android GATT allows one outstanding operation per connection. Queue reads and writes; issue the next one\n  only after the previous callback.\n- Copy byte arrays received in callbacks before handing them to another thread if the source may be reused.\n\n```java\nprivate final ExecutorService gattThread = Executors.newSingleThreadExecutor();\n\n@Override\npublic void onCharacteristicChanged(@NonNull BluetoothGatt gatt,\n                                    @NonNull BluetoothGattCharacteristic characteristic,\n                                    @NonNull byte[] value) {\n    byte[] copy = value.clone();\n    gattThread.execute(() -> frames.onFrame(copy)); // CRC check and parsing happen off the binder thread\n}\n```\n\nThe `value` parameter variant of `onCharacteristicChanged` is the API 33+ callback; older devices call the\ndeprecated variant, so implement both when `minSdk` is below 33.\n\n## Background work that must survive the screen\n\n- Deferrable or guaranteed work: WorkManager. A Java `Worker.doWork()` runs synchronously on WorkManager's\n  background executor; return `Result.retry()` for transient failures.\n- Long-running user-visible work: a foreground service with the correct `foregroundServiceType`\n  (required when targeting Android 14+).\n\n## Debug checks\n\n```java\nif (BuildConfig.DEBUG) {\n    StrictMode.setThreadPolicy(new StrictMode.ThreadPolicy.Builder().detectAll().penaltyLog().build());\n    StrictMode.setVmPolicy(new StrictMode.VmPolicy.Builder().detectAll().penaltyLog().build());\n}\n```\n\nStrictMode flags disk and network access on the main thread and leaked closeables. LeakCanary in debug\nbuilds catches retained Activities and Fragments.\n",
  },
  "kotlin-android": {
    "SKILL.md":
        "---\nname: kotlin-android\ndescription: \"Writes and refactors idiomatic Kotlin for Android apps: Kotlin 2.x and the K2 compiler, null safety without !!, sealed interfaces, @JvmInline value classes for typed IDs, immutable data classes for UI state, coroutines with structured concurrency, injected dispatchers and correct CancellationException handling, Flow, StateFlow and SharedFlow with stateIn, update and collectAsStateWithLifecycle, Jetpack Compose (state hoisting, rememberSaveable, derivedStateOf, stability and strong skipping, effects, lazy list keys), Hilt, Room, KSP, kotlinx.serialization, Gradle Kotlin DSL with libs.versions.toml, the Compose compiler plugin and R8. Covers JUnit, MockK, Turbine, coroutine and Compose UI tests, detekt and ktlint. Flags Android 15 and 16 changes: edge-to-edge, predictive back, foreground service types. Use when writing Kotlin ViewModels, composables, repositories, DAOs, DI modules or build.gradle.kts files in an Android project.\"\nlicense: MIT\nmetadata:\n  author: Ahmad Hussain\n  version: \"1.0.0\"\n  pack: mobile-dev-kit\n---\n\n# Kotlin for Android\n\nWrite Kotlin the way a senior Android engineer would in a current codebase: null-safe, immutable by default,\nstructured concurrency everywhere, Compose that skips work it does not need to do, and a build that is\nreproducible from the version catalog. Prefer the boring, documented API over a clever one.\n\n## When to use\n\n- Writing new Kotlin code in an Android app: ViewModels, use cases, repositories, composables, DAOs, workers.\n- Refactoring Java, callback or RxJava code to coroutines and Flow.\n- Migrating XML screens to Jetpack Compose, kapt to KSP, Groovy Gradle to Kotlin DSL.\n- Fixing recomposition, lifecycle, coroutine-leak or nullability bugs.\n- Updating code for a new `targetSdk` (edge-to-edge, predictive back, foreground service rules).\n\nNot for: architecture choice (use `mobile-mvvm`, `mobile-mvi`), a security audit (use `mobile-security`),\nlegacy Java maintenance (use `java-android`), or reviewing someone else's diff (use `mobile-code-review`).\n\n## Workflow\n\n1. Read the project before writing code.\n   - `gradle/libs.versions.toml`: Kotlin, AGP, Compose BOM, coroutines, Hilt, Room versions.\n   - Module `build.gradle.kts`: `minSdk`, `targetSdk`, `compileSdk`, KSP or kapt, Compose enabled.\n   - Existing conventions: package layout, base classes, dispatcher qualifiers, error types, test style.\n2. Match the existing stack. Do not add a library the project does not use without saying so.\n   Do not change `minSdk`, `targetSdk` or `compileSdk` without asking.\n3. Load the reference for the area being touched:\n   [Coroutines and Flow](references/coroutines-flow.md), [Compose](references/compose.md),\n   [Hilt and Room](references/hilt-room.md), [Build and tooling](references/build.md),\n   [Platform changes](references/platform.md), [Testing](references/testing.md).\n4. Model first: domain types, UI state, events, errors as sealed or data types. Then data layer, then\n   ViewModel, then UI.\n5. Write the code following the Rules below. Keep functions small, `private` by default, and public API\n   explicit about types.\n6. Add or update unit tests for new domain and data logic, and a Compose test for new interactive UI.\n7. Run the build, the tests and the linters (`./gradlew detekt ktlintCheck testDebugUnitTest` or the\n   project's equivalents). Report failures honestly; do not declare done on a red build.\n8. Summarize what changed, any new dependency, and any manifest, permission or SDK impact.\n\n## Baseline stack\n\n| Area | Default | Avoid in new code |\n|---|---|---|\n| Language | Kotlin 2.x, K2 compiler | Kotlin 1.x language versions, synthetic view imports |\n| UI | Jetpack Compose + Material 3 | New XML layouts, `findViewById` |\n| State | `StateFlow` + immutable data class | `LiveData` in new ViewModels, mutable public state |\n| Async | Coroutines + Flow | `AsyncTask`, raw threads, new RxJava |\n| DI | Hilt with constructor injection | Manual singletons, service locators |\n| Persistence | Room (suspend + Flow), DataStore | `SharedPreferences` for new code |\n| Serialization | kotlinx.serialization | Reflection-based parsers without keep rules |\n| Codegen | KSP | kapt where a KSP processor exists |\n| Build | Kotlin DSL + `libs.versions.toml` | Hardcoded versions in module files |\n\n## Rules\n\n### 1. No `!!`. Fail with a message or handle the null\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: NullPointerException with no context\nval id = savedStateHandle.get<String>(\"id\")!!\n\n// GOOD: invariant violation explains itself\nval id = requireNotNull(savedStateHandle.get<String>(\"id\")) { \"id nav argument missing\" }\n\n// GOOD: absence is a normal case\nval name = user?.displayName ?: context.getString(R.string.anonymous)\n```\n\n- `checkNotNull` for state invariants, `requireNotNull` for arguments. Java platform types are nullable.\n- `lateinit` only for framework-injected fields (`@Inject lateinit var` in an entry point), never for data.\n\n### 2. State is immutable; variants are sealed\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: mutable fields, impossible states (loading and error at once)\nclass ProfileState { var loading = false; var error: String? = null; var user: User? = null }\n\n// GOOD\nsealed interface ProfileUiState {\n    data object Loading : ProfileUiState\n    data class Content(val user: User, val isRefreshing: Boolean = false) : ProfileUiState\n    data class Error(val reason: UiError) : ProfileUiState\n}\n```\n\n- `when` over a sealed type has no `else`, so a new variant is a compile error, not a silent fallthrough.\n- Use `val` and read-only collection types in data classes. Change state with `copy`.\n- Guard conditions in `when` (stable since Kotlin 2.2) replace nested `if` inside a branch:\n  `is ProfileUiState.Content if it.isRefreshing -> ...`.\n\n### 3. Typed IDs use value classes\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: easy to swap arguments, compiler cannot help\nfun assign(deviceId: String, userId: String)\nassign(user.id, device.id) // compiles, wrong\n\n// GOOD: zero-cost wrapper on the JVM\n@JvmInline value class DeviceId(val value: String)\n@JvmInline value class UserId(val value: String)\nfun assign(deviceId: DeviceId, userId: UserId)\n```\n\n- Keep value classes in the domain layer. Map to primitives at DTO and entity boundaries unless the\n  library supports them (kotlinx.serialization does).\n- Validate in `init` when the wrapper carries an invariant: `init { require(value.isNotBlank()) }`.\n\n### 4. Every coroutine has an owner. No `GlobalScope`\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: outlives the screen, cannot be cancelled or tested\nGlobalScope.launch { repository.sync() }\n\n// GOOD: tied to the ViewModel\nviewModelScope.launch { repository.sync() }\n\n// GOOD: work that must outlive a screen uses an injected application scope or WorkManager\nclass SyncTrigger @Inject constructor(@ApplicationScope private val scope: CoroutineScope)\n```\n\n- Repositories and use cases expose `suspend` functions or `Flow`. They do not launch and return `Unit`.\n- `runBlocking` only in `main` functions and legacy bridges, never on the main thread.\n\n### 5. Suspend functions are main-safe; dispatchers are injected\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: hardcoded dispatcher, caller must remember to switch threads\nclass FileStore { fun read(): ByteArray = file.readBytes() }\n\n// GOOD\nclass FileStore @Inject constructor(\n    @IoDispatcher private val io: CoroutineDispatcher,\n) {\n    suspend fun read(): ByteArray = withContext(io) { file.readBytes() }\n}\n```\n\n- The function that does the blocking work switches context. Callers never wrap it again.\n- `Dispatchers.Default` for CPU work, IO dispatcher for blocking IO. Main is only for UI.\n\n### 6. `CancellationException` is always rethrown\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: runCatching and catch-all both swallow cancellation\nval result = runCatching { api.fetch() }\ntry { api.fetch() } catch (e: Exception) { log(e) }\n\n// GOOD\ntry {\n    api.fetch()\n} catch (e: CancellationException) {\n    throw e\n} catch (e: IOException) {\n    _state.update { it.copy(error = UiError.Network) }\n}\n```\n\nCatch the narrowest exception that can be handled. Use `ensureActive()` in long loops.\n\n### 7. Use `supervisorScope` when children fail independently\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: one failed widget cancels all the others\nsuspend fun loadDashboard() = coroutineScope {\n    val a = async { api.weather() }\n    val b = async { api.news() }\n    Dashboard(a.await(), b.await())\n}\n\n// GOOD: each child failure is handled at its await\nsuspend fun loadDashboard() = supervisorScope {\n    val a = async { api.weather() }\n    val b = async { api.news() }\n    Dashboard(weather = a.awaitOrNull(), news = b.awaitOrNull())\n}\n```\n\n`awaitOrNull` is a local helper that catches the expected exception types and rethrows cancellation.\nUse `coroutineScope` when the result is all-or-nothing.\n\n### 8. Mutable state is private; updates are atomic\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: public mutable state and a read-modify-write race\nval state = MutableStateFlow(UiState())\nfun select(id: ItemId) { state.value = state.value.copy(selected = id) }\n\n// GOOD\nprivate val _state = MutableStateFlow(UiState())\nval state: StateFlow<UiState> = _state.asStateFlow()\nfun select(id: ItemId) = _state.update { it.copy(selected = id) }\n```\n\n### 9. Cold flows become state once, and UI collects with the lifecycle\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: new upstream per collector, keeps running in the background\nval items: Flow<List<Item>> get() = repository.observeItems()\nval items by viewModel.items.collectAsState(initial = emptyList())\n\n// GOOD\nval items: StateFlow<List<Item>> = repository.observeItems()\n    .stateIn(viewModelScope, SharingStarted.WhileSubscribed(5_000), emptyList())\n\nval items by viewModel.items.collectAsStateWithLifecycle()\n```\n\n- `WhileSubscribed(5_000)` keeps the upstream alive across configuration changes, stops it in background.\n- Views and Fragments collect inside `repeatOnLifecycle(Lifecycle.State.STARTED)`.\n- Details on `flowOn`, `catch`, `combine` and SharedFlow: [Coroutines and Flow](references/coroutines-flow.md).\n\n### 10. Composables are stateless; state is hoisted\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: ViewModel passed deep, no modifier, not previewable\n@Composable\nfun DeviceRow(vm: DevicesViewModel, id: DeviceId) { /* reads vm.state, calls vm.connect */ }\n\n// GOOD\n@Composable\nfun DeviceRow(\n    device: DeviceUi,\n    onConnect: (DeviceId) -> Unit,\n    modifier: Modifier = Modifier,\n) {\n    Row(modifier.clickable { onConnect(device.id) }) { Text(device.name) }\n}\n```\n\n- Only the screen-level composable touches the ViewModel. Below it: state in, lambdas out.\n- `modifier: Modifier = Modifier` is the first optional parameter and is applied to the root only.\n- `rememberSaveable` for user input that must survive rotation and process death.\n\n### 11. Parameters are stable so composables can skip\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: List is an interface the compiler treats as unstable; a new list instance\n// every emission forces recomposition of every row\ndata class FeedUi(val posts: List<PostUi>)\n\n// GOOD\n@Immutable\ndata class FeedUi(val posts: ImmutableList<PostUi>)\n// built with posts.map(::toUi).toImmutableList()\n```\n\n- Strong skipping is the default since Kotlin 2.0.20: unstable parameters are compared by instance,\n  stable ones by `equals`. Recreating objects on every emission still defeats skipping.\n- Do not sprinkle `@Stable` to silence reports. Annotate only types that keep the contract.\n\n### 12. Effects are keyed by what they read\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: keeps the first deviceId forever\nLaunchedEffect(Unit) { viewModel.connect(deviceId) }\n\n// GOOD: restarts when deviceId changes\nLaunchedEffect(deviceId) { viewModel.connect(deviceId) }\n\n// GOOD: long-lived effect that must call the latest lambda without restarting\nval currentOnTimeout by rememberUpdatedState(onTimeout)\nLaunchedEffect(Unit) { delay(30_000); currentOnTimeout() }\n```\n\n- Listeners and observers registered in composition go in `DisposableEffect` with `onDispose`.\n- No side effects in the composable body. More in [Compose](references/compose.md).\n\n### 13. Dependencies come through the constructor\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: Activity context in a singleton, hidden dependency\nobject Prefs { lateinit var context: Context }\n\n// GOOD\n@HiltViewModel\nclass SettingsViewModel @Inject constructor(\n    private val settings: SettingsRepository,\n    private val savedStateHandle: SavedStateHandle,\n) : ViewModel()\n\n@Singleton\nclass SettingsRepository @Inject constructor(\n    @ApplicationContext private val context: Context,\n)\n```\n\n- Bind interfaces with `@Binds` in an abstract module; `@Provides` only for third-party types.\n- Scope annotations match lifetime. Details: [Hilt and Room](references/hilt-room.md).\n\n### 14. Room DAOs are suspend or Flow, and schemas migrate\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: blocking DAO, and user data wiped on the next schema change\n@Query(\"SELECT * FROM device\") fun all(): List<DeviceEntity>\nRoom.databaseBuilder(ctx, AppDb::class.java, \"app.db\").fallbackToDestructiveMigration()\n\n// GOOD\n@Query(\"SELECT * FROM device ORDER BY name\") fun observeAll(): Flow<List<DeviceEntity>>\n@Upsert suspend fun upsert(device: DeviceEntity)\n\n@Database(entities = [DeviceEntity::class], version = 3, exportSchema = true)\nabstract class AppDb : RoomDatabase()\n```\n\n- Every version bump ships a `Migration` or `AutoMigration` and a migration test.\n\n### 15. KSP, version catalog, and the Compose compiler plugin\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: kapt and hardcoded versions\nplugins { kotlin(\"kapt\") }\ndependencies { kapt(\"com.google.dagger:hilt-compiler:2.51\") }\n\n// GOOD\nplugins {\n    alias(libs.plugins.ksp)\n    alias(libs.plugins.hilt)\n    alias(libs.plugins.kotlin.compose)\n}\ndependencies { ksp(libs.hilt.compiler) }\n```\n\n- `libs.plugins.kotlin.compose` maps to `org.jetbrains.kotlin.plugin.compose`, versioned with Kotlin.\n- Release builds enable R8 and add keep rules for reflection. Details: [Build and tooling](references/build.md).\n\n### 16. Code is ready for the current `targetSdk`\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: content under the status bar when targeting SDK 35+, and onBackPressed is deprecated\n// and no longer called when targeting SDK 36 with predictive back\nsetContent { LazyColumn { items(rows, key = { it.id }) { RowItem(it) } } }\noverride fun onBackPressed() { if (editing) cancelEdit() else super.onBackPressed() }\n\n// GOOD\nenableEdgeToEdge()\nsetContent {\n    Scaffold { innerPadding ->\n        LazyColumn(contentPadding = innerPadding) { items(rows, key = { it.id }) { RowItem(it) } }\n    }\n    BackHandler(enabled = editing) { cancelEdit() }\n}\n```\n\nForeground service types, large-screen orientation rules and more: [Platform changes](references/platform.md).\n\n### 17. New logic ships with deterministic tests\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: real dispatcher and sleep, slow and flaky\n@Test fun loads() { vm.load(); Thread.sleep(1000); assertEquals(3, vm.state.value.items.size) }\n\n// GOOD: virtual time, Turbine for the flow\n@Test fun loads() = runTest {\n    coEvery { repo.items() } returns listOf(a, b, c)\n    val vm = ItemsViewModel(repo)\n    vm.state.test {\n        assertEquals(ItemsUiState.Loading, awaitItem())\n        assertEquals(3, (awaitItem() as ItemsUiState.Content).items.size)\n    }\n}\n```\n\nMain dispatcher rule, fakes vs mocks and Compose tests: [Testing](references/testing.md).\n\n## Anti-patterns\n\n- `!!` \"because it cannot be null here\". If it truly cannot, the type should say so.\n- `GlobalScope`, `runBlocking` on the main thread, or a `CoroutineScope(...)` field nobody cancels.\n- `runCatching` or `catch (e: Exception)` around suspend calls without rethrowing cancellation.\n- `MutableStateFlow` or `MutableList` exposed from a ViewModel.\n- One-off events kept in state that the UI never acknowledges, so the last navigation or toast replays after\n  rotation. Clear the event once handled (see `mobile-mvvm`).\n- ViewModels importing `android.view`, `Context` (non-application), or Compose UI types.\n- Passing a ViewModel into leaf composables, or `hiltViewModel()` called below the screen level.\n- `LaunchedEffect(Unit)` reading a parameter that changes.\n- `derivedStateOf` wrapping a value that changes as often as its input. It adds cost, not savings.\n- Lazy lists without `key`, or keyed by index.\n- New kapt processors, versions hardcoded in module files, `kotlinOptions {}` instead of `compilerOptions {}`.\n- `fallbackToDestructiveMigration()` in release builds.\n- Secrets, tokens or API keys in `BuildConfig`, `strings.xml` or source. Escalate to `mobile-security`.\n- Logging device identifiers, emails or tokens with `Log.d`. They are personal data.\n- Silently bumping SDK levels or adding manifest permissions without calling it out.\n\n## Checklist\n\n- [ ] Project versions, SDK levels and conventions read before writing code\n- [ ] No `!!`; nullable inputs handled or failed with `requireNotNull` and a message\n- [ ] UI state is an immutable data class or sealed interface; `when` is exhaustive without `else`\n- [ ] Typed IDs are value classes where mix-ups are possible\n- [ ] Every coroutine has an owner scope; no `GlobalScope`\n- [ ] Dispatchers injected; blocking work wrapped in `withContext` at the source\n- [ ] `CancellationException` rethrown in every catch around suspend code\n- [ ] Mutable flows private; updates use `update {}`\n- [ ] Cold flows converted with `stateIn(..., WhileSubscribed(5_000), ...)`; UI uses `collectAsStateWithLifecycle`\n- [ ] Composables stateless below the screen; `modifier` parameter first optional and applied to root\n- [ ] Composable parameters stable; immutable collections where lists are passed\n- [ ] Effects keyed correctly; `rememberUpdatedState` for long-lived lambdas; listeners in `DisposableEffect`\n- [ ] Lazy lists have stable `key` and `contentType` for mixed rows\n- [ ] Hilt constructor injection; `@ApplicationContext` only; scopes match lifetimes\n- [ ] Room DAOs suspend or Flow; migrations and `exportSchema = true`\n- [ ] KSP instead of kapt; new dependencies in `libs.versions.toml`\n- [ ] R8 keep rules for reflection or serialization added and release build verified\n- [ ] Edge-to-edge insets and predictive back handled for the current `targetSdk`\n- [ ] Unit tests for new domain and data logic; tests and linters pass\n\n## Related skills\n\n- `mobile-mvvm`, `mobile-mvi` -- where ViewModels, state and events fit in the architecture\n- `mobile-design-patterns` -- repository, use case, adapter and factory patterns in Kotlin\n- `mobile-code-review` -- reviewing Kotlin changes against these rules\n- `mobile-security` -- storage, network, crypto and logging rules for the same code\n- `mobile-ci-cd` -- running detekt, ktlint, tests and release builds in CI\n- `java-android` -- legacy Java code and Java-Kotlin interop\n",
    "references/build.md":
        "# Build and tooling reference\n\nLoad this when editing `build.gradle.kts`, `settings.gradle.kts`, `libs.versions.toml`, R8 rules, or lint\nconfiguration.\n\n## Version catalog\n\n~~~toml\n# gradle/libs.versions.toml\n[versions]\nkotlin = \"<kotlin>\"\nksp = \"<ksp>\"\nagp = \"<agp>\"\nhilt = \"<hilt>\"\nroom = \"<room>\"\ncompose-bom = \"<bom>\"\n\n[libraries]\ncompose-bom = { group = \"androidx.compose\", name = \"compose-bom\", version.ref = \"compose-bom\" }\ncompose-material3 = { group = \"androidx.compose.material3\", name = \"material3\" }\nhilt-android = { group = \"com.google.dagger\", name = \"hilt-android\", version.ref = \"hilt\" }\nhilt-compiler = { group = \"com.google.dagger\", name = \"hilt-compiler\", version.ref = \"hilt\" }\nroom-runtime = { group = \"androidx.room\", name = \"room-runtime\", version.ref = \"room\" }\nroom-ktx = { group = \"androidx.room\", name = \"room-ktx\", version.ref = \"room\" }\nroom-compiler = { group = \"androidx.room\", name = \"room-compiler\", version.ref = \"room\" }\n\n[plugins]\nandroid-application = { id = \"com.android.application\", version.ref = \"agp\" }\nkotlin-compose = { id = \"org.jetbrains.kotlin.plugin.compose\", version.ref = \"kotlin\" }\nkotlin-serialization = { id = \"org.jetbrains.kotlin.plugin.serialization\", version.ref = \"kotlin\" }\nksp = { id = \"com.google.devtools.ksp\", version.ref = \"ksp\" }\nhilt = { id = \"com.google.dagger.hilt.android\", version.ref = \"hilt\" }\nroom = { id = \"androidx.room\", version.ref = \"room\" }\n~~~\n\n- Use the project's existing versions. Do not invent or bump versions without saying so.\n- Compose libraries take their version from the BOM: `implementation(platform(libs.compose.bom))`.\n- The Compose compiler plugin and the serialization plugin are versioned with Kotlin. Since Kotlin 2.0 there is\n  no separate `kotlinCompilerExtensionVersion` to manage.\n- Check KSP compatibility with the Kotlin version whenever either one is upgraded.\n\n## Module build file\n\n```kotlin\nplugins {\n    alias(libs.plugins.android.application)\n    alias(libs.plugins.kotlin.compose)\n    alias(libs.plugins.kotlin.serialization)\n    alias(libs.plugins.ksp)\n    alias(libs.plugins.hilt)\n    alias(libs.plugins.room)\n}\n\nandroid {\n    namespace = \"com.example.app\"\n    compileSdk = 36\n    defaultConfig {\n        minSdk = 26\n        targetSdk = 36\n    }\n    buildFeatures { compose = true }\n    buildTypes {\n        release {\n            isMinifyEnabled = true\n            isShrinkResources = true\n            proguardFiles(getDefaultProguardFile(\"proguard-android-optimize.txt\"), \"proguard-rules.pro\")\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nkotlin {\n    jvmToolchain(17)\n}\n\nroom {\n    schemaDirectory(\"\$projectDir/schemas\")\n}\n\ndependencies {\n    implementation(platform(libs.compose.bom))\n    implementation(libs.compose.material3)\n    implementation(libs.hilt.android)\n    ksp(libs.hilt.compiler)\n    implementation(libs.room.runtime)\n    implementation(libs.room.ktx)\n    ksp(libs.room.compiler)\n}\n```\n\n- SDK numbers above are an example. Never change the project's `compileSdk`, `targetSdk` or `minSdk`\n  without asking; a `targetSdk` bump opts into behavior changes (see [Platform changes](platform.md)).\n- Compiler options go in `kotlin { compilerOptions { } }`. `kotlinOptions {}` is deprecated.\n- Recent AGP versions include built-in Kotlin support, so a separate `org.jetbrains.kotlin.android` plugin may\n  not be needed. Follow what the project's AGP version requires rather than adding plugins by habit.\n- Shared configuration across many modules belongs in convention plugins (`build-logic`), not copied blocks.\n\n## kapt to KSP\n\n- Replace `kapt(...)` with `ksp(...)` for Room, Hilt (Dagger), Moshi codegen and other processors that ship a\n  KSP implementation. Remove the kapt plugin once nothing uses it.\n- KSP is faster and works with K2. kapt runs in a compatibility mode and slows every build.\n- Keep any processor that has no KSP version on kapt, and say so.\n\n## Compose compiler options\n\n```kotlin\ncomposeCompiler {\n    reportsDestination = layout.buildDirectory.dir(\"compose_compiler\")\n    metricsDestination = layout.buildDirectory.dir(\"compose_compiler\")\n}\n```\n\n- Enable reports in a local or CI diagnostic build, not permanently in every build.\n- Strong skipping is on by default; do not add flags to enable it.\n- A stability configuration file (also set in `composeCompiler {}`) marks external types as stable. Use it\n  only for types that really are immutable.\n\n## R8\n\n- `isMinifyEnabled = true` and `isShrinkResources = true` for release. Test the release build, not only debug.\n- Keep rules are needed for code reached only by reflection, JNI, or class names held in strings.\n  Classes referenced from the manifest and XML layouts are kept by AGP-generated rules.\n\n```proguard\n# Reflection-based JSON models (Gson and similar): keep field names\n-keepclassmembers class com.example.app.data.remote.dto.** { <fields>; }\n\n# Classes loaded by name\n-keep class com.example.app.plugin.** implements com.example.app.plugin.Plugin { <init>(); }\n```\n\n- kotlinx.serialization uses generated serializers, not reflection, and its artifacts ship R8 rules for\n  standard usage. Verify the release build when serializers are looked up dynamically.\n- Retrofit, OkHttp, Room and Hilt ship consumer rules. Do not copy old blanket rules for them.\n- `-keep class com.example.** { *; }` disables shrinking for the whole app. Never add it to fix one crash.\n- `@Keep` (androidx.annotation) on a single class is acceptable for a narrow reflection case.\n- Upload the mapping file (`build/outputs/mapping/release/mapping.txt`) with each release for readable\n  stack traces.\n\n## Static analysis\n\n- detekt for code smells and complexity; ktlint (directly or through Spotless) for formatting.\n- Add the Compose lint rule set for detekt or ktlint to catch missing `modifier` parameters, unstable\n  parameters, and effects without keys.\n- Commit a baseline when adopting on legacy code; never grow the baseline for new code.\n- Run both in CI on every merge request, alongside `lint` and unit tests.\n- Android Lint warnings about new API usage, missing permissions and `RECEIVER_EXPORTED` flags are real\n  defects, not noise.\n\n## Secrets and build config\n\n- No API keys, tokens or signing passwords in `build.gradle.kts`, `BuildConfig`, `gradle.properties` in VCS,\n  or `strings.xml`. Anything in the APK can be extracted.\n- Signing configs read from CI secrets or a local untracked file. See `mobile-ci-cd` and\n  `mobile-security`.\n- `buildConfig = true` only in modules that actually read `BuildConfig`.\n\n## Native libraries\n\n- Apps with `.so` files (own or from dependencies) must support 16 KB memory page sizes for current Google\n  Play submissions targeting Android 15+. Check with the APK Analyzer and rebuild native code with a current\n  NDK.\n",
    "references/compose.md":
        "# Jetpack Compose reference\n\nLoad this when writing or changing composables, screen state wiring, effects, lists or previews.\n\n## Screen and content split\n\n```kotlin\n@Composable\nfun DevicesRoute(\n    onOpenDevice: (DeviceId) -> Unit,\n    viewModel: DevicesViewModel = hiltViewModel(),\n) {\n    val state by viewModel.state.collectAsStateWithLifecycle()\n    DevicesScreen(\n        state = state,\n        onQueryChange = viewModel::onQueryChange,\n        onDeviceClick = onOpenDevice,\n    )\n}\n\n@Composable\nfun DevicesScreen(\n    state: DevicesUiState,\n    onQueryChange: (String) -> Unit,\n    onDeviceClick: (DeviceId) -> Unit,\n    modifier: Modifier = Modifier,\n) { /* pure UI, previewable, testable without a ViewModel */ }\n```\n\n- The route composable is the only one that knows about the ViewModel and navigation.\n- Parameter order: required data, event lambdas, `modifier`, other optional parameters, trailing content\n  lambda.\n- Event lambdas are named for what happened (`onDeviceClick`), not what the parent will do.\n\n## State\n\n| Need | API |\n|---|---|\n| Survive recomposition | `remember { }` |\n| Survive rotation and process death | `rememberSaveable { }` (Bundle-able types, `@Parcelize`, or a `Saver`) |\n| Value computed from other state that changes less often than its inputs | `remember { derivedStateOf { } }` |\n| Recompute when an input changes | `remember(input) { }` |\n| Turn Compose state into a Flow | `snapshotFlow { }` |\n| Load into state from a suspend source | `produceState` |\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: recomposes on every scroll pixel\nval showButton = listState.firstVisibleItemIndex > 0\n\n// GOOD: recomposes only when the boolean flips\nval showButton by remember { derivedStateOf { listState.firstVisibleItemIndex > 0 } }\n\n// BAD: derivedStateOf adds overhead, output changes as often as input\nval fullName by remember { derivedStateOf { \"\$first \$last\" } }\n\n// GOOD\nval fullName = \"\$first \$last\"\n```\n\n- `remember` keys include every input of the calculation, or the cached value goes stale.\n- Read fast-changing state as late as possible: lambda modifiers (`Modifier.offset { }`,\n  `Modifier.graphicsLayer { }`, `drawBehind { }`) read in layout or draw, skipping composition.\n\n## Stability and skipping\n\n- Strong skipping is on by default since Kotlin 2.0.20. Restartable composables skip when every argument\n  is equal: stable types by `equals`, unstable types by instance (`===`).\n- Lambdas are remembered automatically under strong skipping. Do not wrap them in `remember` by hand.\n- What still breaks skipping: creating new list or object instances on every emission or recomposition,\n  and mapping inside the composable body.\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: new list each recomposition, child never skips\nDeviceList(devices = state.devices.filter { it.online })\n\n// GOOD: filter in the ViewModel, pass an ImmutableList\nDeviceList(devices = state.onlineDevices)\n```\n\n- `kotlinx.collections.immutable`: `ImmutableList`, `persistentListOf()`, `toImmutableList()`.\n- `@Immutable`: all public properties are `val` and deeply immutable.\n- `@Stable`: mutable, but changes are observable through Compose state and `equals` is consistent.\n- Types from modules without the Compose compiler (domain, third-party) are unstable. Either map to a UI\n  model in the UI module or list them in a stability configuration file in the `composeCompiler {}` block.\n- Measure before optimizing: Layout Inspector recomposition counts and the compiler reports\n  (`composeCompiler { reportsDestination = ...; metricsDestination = ... }`).\n\n## Side effects\n\n| API | Use |\n|---|---|\n| `LaunchedEffect(key)` | Suspend work tied to composition, restarted when keys change |\n| `rememberCoroutineScope()` | Launch from event callbacks (click, swipe) |\n| `DisposableEffect(key)` | Register and unregister listeners, observers, callbacks |\n| `rememberUpdatedState(value)` | Latest value inside a long-lived effect without restarting it |\n| `SideEffect` | Publish Compose state to non-Compose code after every successful recomposition |\n| `LifecycleStartEffect`, `LifecycleResumeEffect` | Work paired with lifecycle start/stop or resume/pause |\n\n```kotlin\n@Composable\nfun ConnectionObserver(deviceId: DeviceId, onStateChange: (LinkState) -> Unit) {\n    val currentOnStateChange by rememberUpdatedState(onStateChange)\n    val monitor = LocalLinkMonitor.current\n    DisposableEffect(deviceId, monitor) {\n        val listener = LinkListener { currentOnStateChange(it) }\n        monitor.register(deviceId, listener)\n        onDispose { monitor.unregister(deviceId, listener) }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- Never call ViewModel functions, start navigation or log analytics directly in the composable body.\n- `LaunchedEffect(Unit)` and `LaunchedEffect(true)` are only correct when the effect reads nothing that\n  can change.\n\n## Lazy lists\n\n```kotlin\nLazyColumn(\n    modifier = modifier,\n    contentPadding = contentPadding,\n) {\n    item(key = \"header\", contentType = \"header\") { Header() }\n    items(\n        items = state.messages,\n        key = { it.id.value },\n        contentType = { it.kind },\n    ) { message ->\n        MessageRow(message, Modifier.animateItem())\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- `key` is stable and unique (database or server id, never index). It preserves item state and scroll\n  position across updates.\n- `contentType` lets the list reuse compositions between rows of the same type.\n- Keys must be Bundle-able types (String, Int, Long, Parcelable) if they are saved with the list state.\n- No nested scrollable in the same direction without a fixed height.\n\n## Modifiers\n\n- Modifier order matters: `Modifier.padding(16.dp).clickable { }` has a smaller touch area than\n  `Modifier.clickable { }.padding(16.dp)`.\n- Apply the incoming `modifier` once, to the root, first in the chain:\n  `modifier.fillMaxWidth().padding(...)`.\n- Do not create `Modifier` chains in a loop body when they are constant; hoist them.\n\n## Accessibility and semantics\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: tappable icon, no label, 24dp target\nIcon(Icons.Default.Refresh, null, Modifier.clickable { onRefresh() })\n\n// GOOD\nIconButton(onClick = onRefresh) {\n    Icon(Icons.Default.Refresh, contentDescription = stringResource(R.string.refresh))\n}\n```\n\n- `Modifier.semantics(mergeDescendants = true) { }` for rows read as one item.\n- `Modifier.testTag(\"...\")` only where text or content description cannot identify a node.\n- Text sizes in `sp`, no fixed heights on text containers.\n\n## Previews\n\n```kotlin\n@PreviewLightDark\n@PreviewFontScale\n@Composable\nprivate fun DevicesScreenPreview(\n    @PreviewParameter(DevicesStateProvider::class) state: DevicesUiState,\n) {\n    AppTheme { DevicesScreen(state = state, onQueryChange = {}, onDeviceClick = {}) }\n}\n```\n\n- Previews take state, not a ViewModel. If a screen cannot be previewed, it is doing too much.\n- Keep previews `private` and in the same file or a `debug` source set.\n\n## Navigation\n\n- Pass IDs between destinations, never whole objects. Load the object in the destination ViewModel.\n- Navigation callbacks are lambdas from the route composable. Screens do not hold a `NavController`.\n- With Navigation 3, the back stack is state the app owns; with Navigation 2.8+, routes are type-safe\n  `@Serializable` classes and the ViewModel reads arguments from `SavedStateHandle`.\n",
    "references/coroutines-flow.md":
        "# Coroutines and Flow reference\n\nLoad this when writing or changing suspend functions, scopes, dispatchers, Flow pipelines or ViewModel state.\n\n## Scopes and ownership\n\n| Work | Scope |\n|---|---|\n| Screen-bound loading, user actions | `viewModelScope` (runs on `Dispatchers.Main.immediate`) |\n| UI-only work in a composable | `rememberCoroutineScope()` or `LaunchedEffect` |\n| Activity or Fragment UI collection | `lifecycleScope` + `repeatOnLifecycle(Lifecycle.State.STARTED)` |\n| Must finish even if the screen closes (short) | Injected application scope |\n| Must survive process death, constraints, retries | WorkManager (`CoroutineWorker`) |\n\nApplication scope, provided once:\n\n```kotlin\n@Qualifier @Retention(AnnotationRetention.BINARY) annotation class ApplicationScope\n@Qualifier @Retention(AnnotationRetention.BINARY) annotation class IoDispatcher\n@Qualifier @Retention(AnnotationRetention.BINARY) annotation class DefaultDispatcher\n\n@Module\n@InstallIn(SingletonComponent::class)\nobject CoroutinesModule {\n    @Provides @IoDispatcher fun io(): CoroutineDispatcher = Dispatchers.IO\n    @Provides @DefaultDispatcher fun default(): CoroutineDispatcher = Dispatchers.Default\n\n    @Provides @Singleton @ApplicationScope\n    fun appScope(@DefaultDispatcher dispatcher: CoroutineDispatcher): CoroutineScope =\n        CoroutineScope(SupervisorJob() + dispatcher)\n}\n```\n\n- `SupervisorJob` so one failed child does not cancel the whole app scope.\n- A class that creates its own `CoroutineScope(...)` must also own a `close()` or `cancel()` path.\n\n## Structured concurrency\n\n```kotlin\n// Parallel, all-or-nothing: first failure cancels the siblings and is rethrown\nsuspend fun loadProfile(id: UserId): Profile = coroutineScope {\n    val user = async { userApi.get(id) }\n    val stats = async { statsApi.get(id) }\n    Profile(user.await(), stats.await())\n}\n\n// Fan-out over a list\nsuspend fun uploadAll(files: List<File>) = coroutineScope {\n    files.map { async { uploader.upload(it) } }.awaitAll()\n}\n\n// Bounded parallelism for many items\nprivate val uploads = io.limitedParallelism(4)\n```\n\n- `supervisorScope` when children fail independently; handle each child's failure at its `await` or\n  inside the child.\n- `CoroutineExceptionHandler` works only on root coroutines (`launch` in a scope). It does nothing on\n  `async` or on child coroutines.\n- `withTimeout` throws `TimeoutCancellationException`; `withTimeoutOrNull` returns null. Prefer the latter\n  when timeout is an expected outcome.\n- Long CPU loops call `ensureActive()` or `yield()` so cancellation can take effect.\n- `NonCancellable` only for short cleanup in `finally`:\n\n```kotlin\ntry {\n    session.stream()\n} finally {\n    withContext(NonCancellable) { session.close() }\n}\n```\n\n## Error handling\n\n```kotlin\n// A suspend-safe replacement for runCatching\nsuspend inline fun <T> suspendRunCatching(block: () -> T): Result<T> = try {\n    Result.success(block())\n} catch (e: CancellationException) {\n    throw e\n} catch (e: Exception) {\n    Result.failure(e)\n}\n```\n\n- Map exceptions to a domain error type in the data layer. The UI renders `UiError`, not `IOException`.\n- Do not mix `Result`/sealed results and thrown exceptions for the same failure in one layer.\n\n## Cold vs hot\n\n| Type | Starts | Holds value | Use for |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `flow {}`, Room `Flow`, `callbackFlow` | On each collect | No | Data streams from a source |\n| `StateFlow` | Always active | Latest value, conflated, `distinctUntilChanged` | UI state |\n| `SharedFlow` | Always active | Configurable replay | Broadcast events to many collectors |\n| `Channel` | n/a | Buffered until received | Single-consumer events |\n\n## ViewModel state patterns\n\n```kotlin\n@HiltViewModel\nclass DevicesViewModel @Inject constructor(\n    repository: DeviceRepository,\n    private val connect: ConnectDeviceUseCase,\n) : ViewModel() {\n\n    private val query = MutableStateFlow(\"\")\n    private val transient = MutableStateFlow(Transient())\n\n    val state: StateFlow<DevicesUiState> = combine(\n        repository.observeDevices(),\n        query,\n        transient,\n    ) { devices, q, t ->\n        DevicesUiState(\n            devices = devices.filter { it.name.contains(q, ignoreCase = true) }\n                .map { it.toUi() }\n                .toImmutableList(),\n            query = q,\n            connectingId = t.connectingId,\n        )\n    }\n        .catch { emit(DevicesUiState(error = UiError.Storage)) }\n        .stateIn(viewModelScope, SharingStarted.WhileSubscribed(5_000), DevicesUiState())\n\n    fun onQueryChange(value: String) { query.value = value }\n\n    fun onConnect(id: DeviceId) {\n        viewModelScope.launch {\n            transient.update { it.copy(connectingId = id) }\n            try {\n                connect(id)\n            } finally {\n                transient.update { it.copy(connectingId = null) }\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- `stateIn` once per property, not in a getter.\n- `combine` emits only after every source has emitted once. Give sources an initial value.\n- `catch` handles upstream errors only. An exception in `collect` is not caught by it.\n- After `catch`, the flow completes. For a stream that must continue, handle errors inside a\n  `flatMapLatest` or `retryWhen`.\n\n## Operators that matter\n\n```kotlin\n// flowOn changes the context of everything ABOVE it\nrepository.rawReadings()\n    .map { parse(it) }          // runs on io\n    .flowOn(io)\n    .filter { it.isValid }      // runs on the collector's context\n\n// Search: cancel the previous request on each new query\nquery\n    .debounce(300)\n    .distinctUntilChanged()\n    .flatMapLatest { q -> repository.search(q) }\n```\n\n- `flatMapLatest` requires `@OptIn(ExperimentalCoroutinesApi::class)`; `debounce` requires\n  `@OptIn(FlowPreview::class)`.\n- `conflate()` or `sample()` (`FlowPreview`) for high-frequency sensor or BLE notifications that the UI cannot render at\n  full rate.\n- `distinctUntilChanged()` is redundant on a `StateFlow`.\n\n## Wrapping callbacks\n\n```kotlin\n// Caller holds BLUETOOTH_SCAN; ScanFailedException is an app-defined type\nfun BluetoothLeScanner.scanFlow(filters: List<ScanFilter>, settings: ScanSettings): Flow<ScanResult> =\n    callbackFlow {\n        val callback = object : ScanCallback() {\n            override fun onScanResult(callbackType: Int, result: ScanResult) {\n                trySend(result) // called on a binder thread, never assume main\n            }\n            override fun onScanFailed(errorCode: Int) {\n                close(ScanFailedException(errorCode))\n            }\n        }\n        startScan(filters, settings, callback)\n        awaitClose { stopScan(callback) }\n    }\n```\n\n- `awaitClose` is mandatory. Without it the flow completes immediately and the listener leaks.\n- `trySend` from callbacks; `send` only inside the producer coroutine.\n- Pick a buffer policy deliberately: `.buffer(capacity, BufferOverflow.DROP_OLDEST)` for latest-wins data.\n- One-shot callbacks use `suspendCancellableCoroutine` and unregister in `invokeOnCancellation`.\n\n## One-off events\n\nPrefer modelling the event as state the UI consumes and acknowledges:\n\n```kotlin\ndata class EditUiState(val saved: Boolean = false)\n// UI: LaunchedEffect(state.saved) { if (state.saved) { onSaved(); viewModel.onSavedHandled() } }\n```\n\nIf a `Channel` is used, expose it with `receiveAsFlow()` and collect it in `LaunchedEffect` or\n`repeatOnLifecycle`, so events are not delivered to a stopped UI.\n\n## Views and Fragments\n\n```kotlin\nviewLifecycleOwner.lifecycleScope.launch {\n    viewLifecycleOwner.repeatOnLifecycle(Lifecycle.State.STARTED) {\n        launch { viewModel.state.collect(::render) }\n        launch { viewModel.events.collect(::handle) }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- Fragments use `viewLifecycleOwner`, not the Fragment itself.\n- `launchWhenStarted` and `launchWhenResumed` are deprecated. Replace with `repeatOnLifecycle`.\n",
    "references/hilt-room.md":
        "# Hilt and Room reference\n\nLoad this when adding injected classes, DI modules, entities, DAOs, migrations or serialization models.\n\n## Hilt setup\n\n```kotlin\n@HiltAndroidApp\nclass App : Application()\n\n@AndroidEntryPoint\nclass MainActivity : ComponentActivity()\n\n@HiltViewModel\nclass DeviceViewModel @Inject constructor(\n    private val savedStateHandle: SavedStateHandle,\n    private val repository: DeviceRepository,\n) : ViewModel()\n```\n\n- Constructor injection for every class the app owns. Modules exist only for interfaces and third-party types.\n- Compose obtains ViewModels with `hiltViewModel()` at the route level only.\n\n## Modules\n\n```kotlin\n// Interface to implementation: @Binds in an abstract module, no body to maintain\n@Module\n@InstallIn(SingletonComponent::class)\nabstract class DataModule {\n    @Binds abstract fun deviceRepository(impl: OfflineFirstDeviceRepository): DeviceRepository\n}\n\n// Third-party types: @Provides\n@Module\n@InstallIn(SingletonComponent::class)\nobject NetworkModule {\n    @Provides @Singleton\n    fun json(): Json = Json { ignoreUnknownKeys = true }\n\n    @Provides @Singleton\n    fun database(@ApplicationContext context: Context): AppDb =\n        Room.databaseBuilder(context, AppDb::class.java, \"app.db\")\n            .addMigrations(MIGRATION_1_2, MIGRATION_2_3)\n            .build()\n\n    @Provides\n    fun deviceDao(db: AppDb): DeviceDao = db.deviceDao()\n}\n```\n\n## Scopes\n\n| Annotation | Component | Lives as long as |\n|---|---|---|\n| `@Singleton` | `SingletonComponent` | The process |\n| `@ActivityRetainedScoped` | `ActivityRetainedComponent` | Activity, across configuration changes |\n| `@ViewModelScoped` | `ViewModelComponent` | One ViewModel |\n| `@ActivityScoped` | `ActivityComponent` | One Activity instance |\n\n- Unscoped is the default and the right choice for stateless classes. Scope only when the instance holds\n  shared state or is expensive to create.\n- A `@Singleton` must never hold an Activity, View, or screen state.\n- `@ApplicationContext` for any `Context` in a singleton. Never inject an Activity context into it.\n- Use `@Qualifier` annotations for two bindings of the same type (dispatchers, scopes, base URLs).\n- `Lazy<T>` or `Provider<T>` to defer expensive construction off the startup path.\n\n## Room entities and DAOs\n\n```kotlin\n@Entity(tableName = \"device\", indices = [Index(\"serial\", unique = true)])\ndata class DeviceEntity(\n    @PrimaryKey val id: String,\n    val serial: String,\n    val name: String,\n    @ColumnInfo(defaultValue = \"0\") val lastSeenEpochMs: Long,\n)\n\n@Dao\ninterface DeviceDao {\n    @Query(\"SELECT * FROM device ORDER BY name\")\n    fun observeAll(): Flow<List<DeviceEntity>>\n\n    @Query(\"SELECT * FROM device WHERE id = :id\")\n    suspend fun byId(id: String): DeviceEntity?\n\n    @Upsert\n    suspend fun upsert(devices: List<DeviceEntity>)\n\n    @Query(\"DELETE FROM device\")\n    suspend fun clear()\n\n    @Transaction\n    suspend fun replaceAll(devices: List<DeviceEntity>) {\n        clear()\n        upsert(devices)\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- `Flow` queries for observed data, `suspend` for one-shot reads and writes. Room runs them off the main\n  thread; no `withContext` needed around DAO calls.\n- Flow queries re-emit on any write to the observed tables. Add `distinctUntilChanged()` when the UI only\n  cares about actual changes.\n- Multi-step writes in one `@Transaction`.\n- Entities stay in the data layer. Map to domain models (with value-class IDs) in the repository.\n- Never `allowMainThreadQueries()` outside tests.\n\n## Migrations\n\n```kotlin\n@Database(\n    entities = [DeviceEntity::class],\n    version = 3,\n    exportSchema = true,\n    autoMigrations = [AutoMigration(from = 1, to = 2)],\n)\nabstract class AppDb : RoomDatabase() {\n    abstract fun deviceDao(): DeviceDao\n}\n\nval MIGRATION_2_3 = object : Migration(2, 3) {\n    override fun migrate(db: SupportSQLiteDatabase) {\n        db.execSQL(\"ALTER TABLE device ADD COLUMN lastSeenEpochMs INTEGER NOT NULL DEFAULT 0\")\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- `exportSchema = true` and the schema directory committed to version control. Auto-migrations and\n  migration tests need the exported JSON.\n- Schema location with the Room Gradle plugin: `room { schemaDirectory(\"\$projectDir/schemas\") }`.\n- Renames and deletes in auto-migrations need an `AutoMigrationSpec` (`@RenameColumn`, `@DeleteColumn`).\n- `fallbackToDestructiveMigration` deletes user data. Do not ship it in release builds.\n- Each migration has a test with `MigrationTestHelper` (see [Testing](testing.md)).\n\n## kotlinx.serialization\n\n```kotlin\n@Serializable\ndata class DeviceDto(\n    val id: String,\n    @SerialName(\"display_name\") val name: String,\n    val firmware: String? = null,\n)\n\n// One configured Json instance, injected\nval json = Json {\n    ignoreUnknownKeys = true   // tolerate new server fields\n    explicitNulls = false      // omit nulls when encoding\n}\n```\n\n- Default values make fields optional on decode. A missing non-default field throws.\n- DTOs are separate from entities and domain models. Map at the edge.\n- Retrofit uses the official kotlinx.serialization converter with the same injected `Json` instance.\n- Payloads with device identifiers are personal data: no full-body logging in release builds.\n",
    "references/platform.md":
        "# Platform changes reference\n\nLoad this when touching Activities, window insets, back handling, services, permissions, or when the\nproject's `targetSdk` changes. Only behavior tied to `targetSdk` is listed; bumping it opts the app in.\n\nFor an SDK level not listed here, read the official behavior-changes page for that release before changing\ncode. Do not assume.\n\n## Edge-to-edge (targetSdk 35+)\n\n- On Android 15+ devices, apps targeting SDK 35 are drawn edge-to-edge. Status and navigation bar color APIs\n  no longer have an effect.\n- The `windowOptOutEdgeToEdgeEnforcement` opt-out is deprecated and ignored when targeting SDK 36. Do not rely\n  on it.\n- Call `enableEdgeToEdge()` in `onCreate` before `setContent` so older versions behave the same way.\n\nCompose:\n\n```kotlin\noverride fun onCreate(savedInstanceState: Bundle?) {\n    enableEdgeToEdge()\n    super.onCreate(savedInstanceState)\n    setContent { AppTheme { AppRoot() } }\n}\n\n@Composable\nfun SettingsScreen(modifier: Modifier = Modifier) {\n    Scaffold(modifier = modifier, topBar = { TopAppBar(title = { Text(\"Settings\") }) }) { innerPadding ->\n        LazyColumn(contentPadding = innerPadding) { /* ... */ }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- Material 3 `Scaffold`, `TopAppBar` and `NavigationBar` consume insets. Pass `innerPadding` to scrolling\n  content as `contentPadding` so items scroll under the bars but end above them.\n- Without a `Scaffold`: `Modifier.safeDrawingPadding()`, `Modifier.windowInsetsPadding(WindowInsets.safeDrawing)`,\n  and `Modifier.imePadding()` for text fields above the keyboard.\n- Backgrounds draw behind the bars; touch targets and text stay inside the insets.\n\nViews:\n\n```kotlin\nViewCompat.setOnApplyWindowInsetsListener(binding.list) { view, insets ->\n    val bars = insets.getInsets(WindowInsetsCompat.Type.systemBars())\n    view.updatePadding(bottom = bars.bottom)\n    insets\n}\n```\n\n## Predictive back\n\n- `onBackPressed()` is deprecated. For apps targeting SDK 36 on Android 16+, predictive back is on by default\n  and `onBackPressed()` is no longer called, nor is `KEYCODE_BACK` dispatched.\n- Compose: `BackHandler(enabled = condition) { }`. For a custom animation driven by the gesture,\n  `PredictiveBackHandler { progress -> }` collects the back events.\n- Views: `onBackPressedDispatcher.addCallback(owner) { }` and toggle `isEnabled` as state changes.\n\n```kotlin\n// GOOD: enabled only while there is something to intercept\nBackHandler(enabled = state.hasUnsavedChanges) { showDiscardDialog = true }\n```\n\n- A callback that is always enabled breaks back-to-home. Enable it only while it has work to do.\n- Navigation libraries (Navigation Compose, Navigation 3) already handle back for their back stack.\n\n## Foreground services\n\n- Targeting SDK 34+: every foreground service declares `android:foregroundServiceType` in the manifest,\n  holds the matching `FOREGROUND_SERVICE_<TYPE>` permission, and passes the type when starting.\n\n```kotlin\nServiceCompat.startForeground(\n    this,\n    NOTIFICATION_ID,\n    notification,\n    ServiceInfo.FOREGROUND_SERVICE_TYPE_CONNECTED_DEVICE,\n)\n```\n\n- A BLE gateway service usually fits `connectedDevice`. Pick the type that matches what the service actually\n  does; a wrong type is a Play policy problem, not only a crash risk.\n- Targeting SDK 35+: `dataSync` and `mediaProcessing` services have a daily time limit. Implement\n  `Service.onTimeout(startId, fgsType)` and stop the service there.\n- Targeting SDK 35+: some types (including `dataSync`, `camera`, `microphone`, `mediaPlayback`) can no longer\n  be started from a `BOOT_COMPLETED` receiver.\n- Deferrable or retryable work belongs in WorkManager. Prefer it over a foreground service whenever the user\n  does not need to see ongoing work.\n\n## Large screens and orientation (targetSdk 36+)\n\n- On displays with smallest width 600dp or more, manifest orientation, resizability and aspect-ratio\n  restrictions are ignored for apps targeting SDK 36. Games are excluded.\n- Code must handle any size and orientation: state in ViewModels and `rememberSaveable`, layouts adapting to\n  window size classes, no hardcoded portrait assumptions in camera or scanning screens.\n\n## Other target-dependent rules still often missed\n\n- Runtime-registered broadcast receivers (targetSdk 34+) pass `RECEIVER_EXPORTED` or `RECEIVER_NOT_EXPORTED`.\n  Use `ContextCompat.registerReceiver` for backward compatibility.\n- `PendingIntent` (targetSdk 31+) must specify `FLAG_IMMUTABLE` or `FLAG_MUTABLE`. Default to immutable.\n- Bluetooth (targetSdk 31+): `BLUETOOTH_SCAN` and `BLUETOOTH_CONNECT` are runtime permissions. Add\n  `android:usesPermissionFlags=\"neverForLocation\"` to `BLUETOOTH_SCAN` only if scan results are never used to\n  derive location.\n- Notifications (targetSdk 33+): `POST_NOTIFICATIONS` is a runtime permission. Request it in context.\n- Exported components (`exported=\"true\"`) are an attack surface. See `mobile-security`.\n\n## Manifest and permission changes\n\n- Every new permission, service type, or exported component is called out explicitly in the change summary.\n- Request runtime permissions at the point of use with `rememberLauncherForActivityResult(\n  ActivityResultContracts.RequestPermission())` or `RequestMultiplePermissions()`, and handle denial.\n",
    "references/testing.md":
        "# Testing reference\n\nLoad this when writing unit tests for ViewModels, use cases, repositories and flows, or UI tests for\ncomposables and Room migrations.\n\n## What to test where\n\n| Code | Test type | Source set |\n|---|---|---|\n| Use cases, mappers, validators | Plain JUnit | `test` |\n| ViewModels, repositories, flows | JUnit + `kotlinx-coroutines-test` + Turbine | `test` |\n| Composables (behavior) | Compose UI test (instrumented, or JVM with Robolectric) | `androidTest` or `test` |\n| Room DAOs and migrations | Instrumented with an in-memory DB and `MigrationTestHelper` | `androidTest` |\n\n## Main dispatcher rule\n\n`viewModelScope` uses `Dispatchers.Main`, which does not exist on the JVM. Replace it per test:\n\n```kotlin\nclass MainDispatcherRule(\n    val testDispatcher: TestDispatcher = StandardTestDispatcher(),\n) : TestWatcher() {\n    override fun starting(description: Description) = Dispatchers.setMain(testDispatcher)\n    override fun finished(description: Description) = Dispatchers.resetMain()\n}\n```\n\n- With `StandardTestDispatcher`, launched coroutines run only when the scheduler advances\n  (`advanceUntilIdle()`, `runCurrent()`, or a suspending call such as `awaitItem()`).\n- `UnconfinedTestDispatcher` runs eagerly. Use it when a test needs `stateIn` collectors to start immediately,\n  not as a default to hide ordering problems.\n- Inject the same scheduler into code under test:\n  `Repo(io = StandardTestDispatcher(testScheduler))` inside `runTest`, so virtual time is shared.\n\n## ViewModel test with a fake and Turbine\n\n```kotlin\nclass DevicesViewModelTest {\n    @get:Rule val mainRule = MainDispatcherRule()\n\n    private val repository = FakeDeviceRepository()\n\n    @Test\n    fun `filters devices by query`() = runTest {\n        repository.emit(listOf(device(\"Gateway A\"), device(\"Sensor B\")))\n        val vm = DevicesViewModel(repository, ConnectDeviceUseCase(repository))\n\n        vm.state.test {\n            assertEquals(DevicesUiState(), awaitItem()) // stateIn initial value\n            assertEquals(2, awaitItem().devices.size)\n            vm.onQueryChange(\"gate\")\n            assertEquals(listOf(\"Gateway A\"), awaitItem().devices.map { it.name })\n            cancelAndIgnoreRemainingEvents()\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- The first `awaitItem()` on a `StateFlow` may be the initial value. Assert it or skip it explicitly; do not\n  rely on timing.\n- `expectNoEvents()` to prove nothing else was emitted; `awaitError()` for failing flows.\n- `turbineScope { }` with `testIn(backgroundScope)` when asserting several flows together.\n\n## Fakes vs mocks\n\n- Prefer fakes for repositories and data sources: a small class backed by a `MutableStateFlow`. They test\n  behavior and survive refactors.\n- Use MockK for collaborators that are hard to fake, or to verify an interaction that is the behavior itself.\n\n```kotlin\nprivate val api = mockk<DeviceApi>()\n\n@Test\nfun `maps network failure to domain error`() = runTest {\n    coEvery { api.devices() } throws IOException(\"offline\")\n    val repo = NetworkDeviceRepository(api, io = StandardTestDispatcher(testScheduler))\n\n    val result = repo.refresh()\n\n    assertEquals(RefreshResult.Failure(DeviceError.Network), result)\n    coVerify(exactly = 1) { api.devices() }\n}\n```\n\n- `coEvery` / `coVerify` for suspend functions; `every` / `verify` for regular ones.\n- `relaxed = true` hides missing stubs. Use it only for collaborators whose return value does not matter.\n- Do not mock types you own just to return data. Write a fake.\n- Do not mock data classes or value classes.\n\n## Time and delays\n\n```kotlin\n@Test\nfun `retries after backoff`() = runTest {\n    val api = FlakyApi(failures = 1)\n    val repo = Repo(api, io = StandardTestDispatcher(testScheduler))\n\n    val result = async { repo.loadWithRetry() }\n    advanceTimeBy(1_000)\n    runCurrent()\n\n    assertEquals(expected, result.await())\n}\n```\n\n- `runTest` skips `delay` with virtual time. Never `Thread.sleep` in a test.\n- `runTest` waits for every child coroutine. A collector that never completes makes the test fail on timeout,\n  which surfaces leaked work; launch such collectors in `backgroundScope`.\n\n## Compose UI tests\n\n```kotlin\nclass DevicesScreenTest {\n    @get:Rule val composeRule = createComposeRule()\n\n    @Test\n    fun clickingDeviceReportsId() {\n        var clicked: DeviceId? = null\n        composeRule.setContent {\n            AppTheme {\n                DevicesScreen(\n                    state = DevicesUiState(devices = persistentListOf(deviceUi(\"Gateway A\"))),\n                    onQueryChange = {},\n                    onDeviceClick = { clicked = it },\n                )\n            }\n        }\n\n        composeRule.onNodeWithText(\"Gateway A\").performClick()\n\n        assertEquals(DeviceId(\"gateway-a\"), clicked)\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- Test the stateless screen with plain state and lambdas. No ViewModel, no Hilt, no network.\n- Find nodes by semantics: `onNodeWithText`, `onNodeWithContentDescription`, `onNodeWithTag` as a last resort.\n- Assertions: `assertIsDisplayed()`, `assertIsEnabled()`, `assertTextEquals()`, `assertCountEquals()` on\n  `onAllNodes...`.\n- `createAndroidComposeRule<MainActivity>()` only when the test needs the Activity (resources, Hilt entry\n  point). With Hilt, add `@HiltAndroidTest` and `HiltAndroidRule` ordered before the compose rule.\n- The rule waits for idle. Use `waitUntil { }` for asynchronous conditions instead of sleeps.\n\n## Room\n\n```kotlin\n@get:Rule\nval helper = MigrationTestHelper(\n    InstrumentationRegistry.getInstrumentation(),\n    AppDb::class.java,\n)\n\n@Test\nfun migrate2To3() {\n    helper.createDatabase(TEST_DB, 2).use { db ->\n        db.execSQL(\"INSERT INTO device (id, serial, name) VALUES ('1', 'S1', 'A')\")\n    }\n    helper.runMigrationsAndValidate(TEST_DB, 3, true, MIGRATION_2_3)\n}\n```\n\n- DAO tests use `Room.inMemoryDatabaseBuilder(...)` and close the database in `@After`.\n- Migration tests need the exported schema JSON files available to the test APK.\n\n## Test hygiene\n\n- Test names describe behavior: `` `emits error when token expired` ``.\n- One behavior per test; arrange, act, assert separated by blank lines.\n- No shared mutable state between tests; build fresh fakes per test.\n- New domain and data logic ships with tests in the same change.\n",
  },
  "mobile-ci-cd": {
    "SKILL.md":
        "---\nname: mobile-ci-cd\ndescription: \"Designs, writes and hardens CI/CD pipelines for Android, iOS and Flutter apps on GitHub Actions and GitLab CI. Covers Gradle with gradle/actions/setup-gradle caching, Android lint, detekt, ktlint, unit tests and coverage gates (Kover, xccov, lcov), release signing with the keystore decoded from a base64 secret into the runner temp dir, AAB upload to Google Play with fastlane supply, iOS on macOS runners with xcodebuild, SPM caching, fastlane match, gym and pilot using an App Store Connect API key, Flutter with subosito/flutter-action, flavors, --obfuscate and --split-debug-info, Firebase App Distribution and TestFlight. Enforces least-privilege permissions, SHA-pinned actions, concurrency groups, protected environments, no secrets on fork pull requests, and build numbers from the run number. Use when asked to set up, fix, speed up or review a mobile pipeline, workflow YAML, .gitlab-ci.yml, Fastfile or store release automation.\"\nlicense: MIT\nmetadata:\n  author: Ahmad Hussain\n  version: \"1.0.0\"\n  pack: mobile-dev-kit\n---\n\n# Mobile CI/CD\n\nBuild pipelines that a senior mobile engineer would trust with the signing keys: fast feedback on every\npull request, reproducible signed release builds from tags, and no path for untrusted code to reach a\nsecret or a store account.\n\n## When to use\n\n- The user asks to set up, fix, speed up or review CI for an Android, iOS, Flutter or KMP app.\n- A workflow file (`.github/workflows/*.yml`), `.gitlab-ci.yml`, `Fastfile`, `Matchfile` or Gradle\n  signing config is being added or changed.\n- Release automation: Play Console upload, TestFlight, Firebase App Distribution, version numbering.\n- A security review of an existing pipeline (secret exposure, unpinned actions, broad token permissions).\n\nNot for: app-level security such as storage or network hardening (use `mobile-security`), or writing the\nmerge request that ships the pipeline (use `mobile-merge-request`).\n\n## Workflow\n\n1. Inventory the project before writing YAML.\n   - Platforms, build system, flavors or schemes, module layout, existing lint and test tasks.\n   - Existing CI files, `Gemfile` / `Fastfile`, `libs.versions.toml`, `Package.resolved`, `pubspec.lock`.\n   - Where releases go today (Play track, TestFlight, Firebase App Distribution) and who approves them.\n2. Agree the pipeline shape with the user (see table below). Ask before choosing store tracks,\n   distribution groups or approval rules; do not guess them.\n3. Load the matching reference and adapt its workflow instead of writing from memory:\n   [Android on GitHub Actions](references/android-github-actions.md),\n   [iOS on GitHub Actions](references/ios-github-actions.md),\n   [Flutter on GitHub Actions](references/flutter-github-actions.md),\n   [GitLab CI](references/gitlab-ci.md).\n4. Write the verify job first (lint, static analysis, unit tests, coverage gate). It runs on every PR and\n   never sees a secret.\n5. Write the release job second: tag or manual trigger only, protected `environment:`, signing from\n   secrets into the runner temp dir, upload to the store or distribution service.\n6. Harden: top-level `permissions: contents: read`, SHA-pinned actions, concurrency group, timeouts,\n   secrets passed through `env:` only, untrusted event data never interpolated into `run:`.\n7. Validate locally where possible: `actionlint` for syntax and expression errors, `zizmor` for\n   workflow security findings, and a dry run on a branch with the release job disabled.\n8. Deliver the files plus the list of required secrets and the manual setup steps (see Output format).\n   Never ask the user to paste secret values into the conversation.\n\n## Pipeline shape\n\n| Trigger | Jobs | Secrets |\n|---|---|---|\n| `pull_request` | verify (lint, detekt/ktlint/SwiftLint/analyze, unit tests, coverage gate), debug build | None |\n| `push` to `main` | verify, QA build to Firebase App Distribution or TestFlight internal | Distribution only, via `environment:` |\n| `push` tag `v*` or `workflow_dispatch` | verify, signed release build, store upload | Signing and store, via `environment:` with required reviewers |\n\nRun iOS jobs only when iOS files change (`paths:` filters). macOS runner minutes cost several times more\nthan Linux minutes.\n\n## Rules\n\n### 1. Every action is pinned to a full commit SHA\n\nTags are mutable. A compromised tag runs attacker code with your secrets.\n\n```yaml\n# BAD: tag can be moved to a malicious commit\n- uses: actions/checkout@v5\n- uses: some-org/upload-thing@main\n\n# GOOD: immutable commit, human-readable version in the comment\n# (the SHA below is a placeholder, resolve the real one before use)\n- uses: actions/checkout@<full-40-char-commit-sha> # vX.Y.Z\n```\n\n- Resolve a tag to its commit: `git ls-remote https://github.com/actions/checkout 'refs/tags/vX.Y.Z*'`\n  (use the `^{}` line for annotated tags) or `gh api repos/actions/checkout/commits/vX.Y.Z --jq .sha`.\n- Keep pins fresh with Dependabot, which updates the SHA and the comment together:\n\n```yaml\n# .github/dependabot.yml\nversion: 2\nupdates:\n  - package-ecosystem: github-actions\n    directory: /\n    schedule:\n      interval: weekly\n```\n\n- Container images are pinned by digest (`image@sha256:<digest>`), tools by exact version\n  (`Gemfile.lock`, `firebase-tools@<x.y.z>`).\n\n### 2. The token gets the least privilege, granted per job\n\n```yaml\n# BAD: default token may have write access to the whole repo\non: pull_request\njobs:\n  test: ...\n\n# GOOD: read-only by default, escalate only where needed\npermissions:\n  contents: read\n\njobs:\n  distribute:\n    permissions:\n      contents: read\n      id-token: write   # OIDC to a cloud provider, nothing else\n```\n\n- Set `persist-credentials: false` on `actions/checkout` unless a later step pushes with the token.\n\n### 3. Secrets never reach untrusted code\n\nSecrets are not passed to `pull_request` runs from forks. Do not \"fix\" that by switching triggers.\n\n```yaml\n# BAD: runs fork code with secrets and a write token\non: pull_request_target\njobs:\n  build:\n    steps:\n      - uses: actions/checkout@<sha> # vX.Y.Z\n        with:\n          ref: \${{ github.event.pull_request.head.sha }}\n      - run: ./gradlew bundleRelease\n\n# GOOD: PRs build unsigned; signing happens only on trusted refs\non:\n  pull_request:\n  push:\n    tags: ['v*']\njobs:\n  release:\n    if: startsWith(github.ref, 'refs/tags/v')\n    environment: production\n```\n\n- `workflow_run` and `issue_comment` triggers carry the same risk: secrets plus someone else's input.\n\n### 4. Release jobs run behind a protected environment\n\n- Store and signing secrets are environment secrets, not repository secrets.\n- The environment has required reviewers and a deployment branch or tag rule (`v*` tags, `main`).\n\n```yaml\n# BAD: any workflow on any branch can read the Play key\nenv:\n  PLAY_JSON: \${{ secrets.PLAY_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_JSON }}   # repository secret, workflow level\n\n# GOOD: only this job, only after approval, only on allowed refs\njobs:\n  release:\n    environment: play-production\n    steps:\n      - run: bundle exec fastlane android release\n        env:\n          PLAY_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_JSON: \${{ secrets.PLAY_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_JSON }}\n```\n\n### 5. Signing material is decoded into the runner temp dir, never committed\n\n```yaml\n# BAD: keystore committed as app/release.jks, password in gradle.properties,\n# both visible to every fork and every past clone forever\n- run: ./gradlew bundleRelease\n\n# GOOD: decoded at build time, outside the workspace\n- name: Decode upload keystore\n  env:\n    KEYSTORE_BASE64: \${{ secrets.ANDROID_UPLOAD_KEYSTORE_BASE64 }}\n  run: |\n    echo \"\$KEYSTORE_BASE64\" | base64 --decode > \"\$RUNNER_TEMP/upload.jks\"\n    echo \"ANDROID_KEYSTORE_PATH=\$RUNNER_TEMP/upload.jks\" >> \"\$GITHUB_ENV\"\n```\n\n- Gradle reads path and passwords from environment variables (see the Android reference).\n- With Play App Signing, CI holds only the upload key. A leaked upload key can be reset in Play Console;\n  a leaked app signing key cannot.\n- iOS: certificates and profiles come from `fastlane match` in `readonly` mode into a temporary keychain\n  created by `setup_ci`. Never export a `.p12` into the repo.\n- `.gitignore` covers `*.jks`, `*.keystore`, `*.p12`, `*.p8`, `*.mobileprovision`, `google-services.json`\n  if it is environment specific, and any `*-service-account.json`.\n\n### 6. Secrets and untrusted input go through `env:`, never inline\n\n```yaml\n# BAD: secret ends up in the process list and possibly the log; PR title is a script injection\n- run: |\n    set -x\n    curl -H \"Authorization: Bearer \${{ secrets.API_TOKEN }}\" ...\n    echo \"Building \${{ github.event.pull_request.title }}\"\n\n# GOOD\n- env:\n    API_TOKEN: \${{ secrets.API_TOKEN }}\n    PR_TITLE: \${{ github.event.pull_request.title }}\n  run: |\n    curl -H \"Authorization: Bearer \$API_TOKEN\" ...\n    echo \"Building \$PR_TITLE\"\n```\n\n- No `set -x`, `env`, `printenv` or `--debug` flags in steps that hold secrets.\n- Mask values derived from a secret: `echo \"::add-mask::\$DERIVED\"`.\n- `--dart-define` values and `BuildConfig` fields are compiled into the app. They are configuration,\n  never secrets.\n\n### 7. One run per ref; cancel PR runs, never cancel releases\n\n```yaml\n# BAD: every push queues another full run, and a new tag can kill an upload half way\nconcurrency:\n  group: ci\n  cancel-in-progress: true\n\n# GOOD\nconcurrency:\n  group: \${{ github.workflow }}-\${{ github.ref }}\n  cancel-in-progress: \${{ github.event_name == 'pull_request' }}\n```\n\n- Every job has `timeout-minutes`. A hung simulator should not burn six hours of macOS minutes.\n\n### 8. Caches are keyed on lockfiles and written only from trusted branches\n\n```yaml\n# BAD: hand-rolled Gradle cache, keyed on nothing useful, written by every PR\n- uses: actions/cache@<sha> # vX.Y.Z\n  with:\n    path: ~/.gradle\n    key: gradle\n\n# GOOD: setup-gradle caches and only writes from the default branch by default\n- uses: gradle/actions/setup-gradle@<sha> # vX.Y.Z\n```\n\n- iOS: cache `SourcePackages` (passed via `-clonedSourcePackagesDirPath`) keyed on\n  `hashFiles('**/Package.resolved')`, and resolve with `-disableAutomaticPackageResolution` so CI uses\n  the committed versions.\n- Flutter: `subosito/flutter-action` with `cache: true`. Release jobs restore caches read-only.\n\n### 9. Version numbers come from CI and only go up\n\n```yaml\n# BAD: versionCode hand-edited in build.gradle.kts, forgotten, rejected by Play\nversionCode = 42\n\n# BAD: GitHub expressions have no arithmetic, this is not a number\nrun: ./gradlew bundleRelease -PversionCode=\${{ github.run_number + 1000 }}\n\n# GOOD: offset computed in the shell, passed as a Gradle property\n- run: echo \"VERSION_CODE=\$(( GITHUB_RUN_NUMBER + \${VERSION_CODE_OFFSET:-0} ))\" >> \"\$GITHUB_ENV\"\n  env:\n    VERSION_CODE_OFFSET: \${{ vars.VERSION_CODE_OFFSET }}\n- run: ./gradlew bundleRelease -PversionCode=\"\$VERSION_CODE\"\n```\n\n- iOS: pass `CURRENT_PROJECT_VERSION=\$BUILD_NUMBER` to the build so `CFBundleVersion` follows the run.\n- Flutter: `--build-number=\"\$VERSION_CODE\"` sets both Android `versionCode` and iOS `CFBundleVersion`.\n- `run_number` is per workflow and does not change on re-run. Set an offset above the highest number\n  already uploaded when migrating, and never rename the release workflow file without bumping the offset.\n- Marketing version (`versionName`, `CFBundleShortVersionString`, `--build-name`) comes from the tag.\n\n### 10. Quality gates fail the build before anything is signed\n\n```yaml\n# BAD: lint runs but never fails; release job does not depend on tests\n- run: ./gradlew lint || true\nrelease:\n  runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n\n# GOOD\nverify:\n  steps:\n    - run: ./gradlew ktlintCheck detekt lint testDebugUnitTest koverVerify --continue\nrelease:\n  needs: verify\n```\n\n- Coverage thresholds live in the build (`koverVerify` rules) or a short script over `xccov` / `lcov`\n  output, and the threshold is a number in the repo, not in someone's head.\n- Lint baselines are fine for legacy debt; new issues fail the build. `verify` is a required check on `main`.\n- Upload test reports with `if: always()` so failures can be diagnosed.\n\n### 11. Obfuscation symbols and mapping files are kept per build\n\n```yaml\n# BAD: obfuscated release with nothing to symbolicate crashes\n- run: flutter build appbundle --obfuscate --split-debug-info=build/symbols\n# (build/symbols discarded with the runner)\n\n# GOOD: symbols archived and sent to the crash reporter\n- run: flutter build appbundle --release --obfuscate --split-debug-info=build/symbols --build-number=\"\$VERSION_CODE\"\n- uses: actions/upload-artifact@<sha> # vX.Y.Z\n  with:\n    name: symbols-\${{ env.VERSION_CODE }}\n    path: build/symbols\n    retention-days: 90\n```\n\n- Android R8 `mapping.txt`, iOS `dSYM`s and Flutter `--split-debug-info` output are archived with the\n  build number in the name, and uploaded to Crashlytics or the crash reporter in use.\n- Retention for crash symbols matches how long the release is supported.\n\n### 12. Apple authentication uses an App Store Connect API key\n\n```ruby\n# BAD: Apple ID password and 2FA session cookies in CI\nENV[\"FASTLANE_PASSWORD\"] = \"...\"\nENV[\"FASTLANE_SESSION\"] = \"...\"\n\n# GOOD: .p8 API key from secrets, scoped by role, revocable\napi_key = app_store_connect_api_key(\n  key_id: ENV.fetch(\"ASC_KEY_ID\"),\n  issuer_id: ENV.fetch(\"ASC_ISSUER_ID\"),\n  key_content: ENV.fetch(\"ASC_KEY_P8_BASE64\"),\n  is_key_content_base64: true\n)\nupload_to_testflight(api_key: api_key, skip_waiting_for_build_processing: true)\n```\n\n- Lowest role that covers the lane; a dedicated CI key, never a personal one.\n- `match(readonly: true)` in CI. Creating or renewing certificates is a manual, reviewed operation.\n\n### 13. Prefer short-lived credentials over stored keys\n\n- Where the target supports it (Google Cloud and Firebase, AWS, Azure), use OIDC from the workflow\n  (`id-token: write` plus the provider's auth action) instead of a long-lived JSON key.\n- Where a stored key is unavoidable (Play service account JSON, App Store Connect `.p8`), scope it to one\n  app, grant the minimum console role, and record a rotation date.\n\n## Anti-patterns\n\n- `pull_request_target` or `workflow_run` that checks out and builds PR code with secrets available.\n- Third-party actions referenced by tag or branch, or `curl ... | bash` installers in release jobs.\n- Workflow-level `permissions: write-all` or no `permissions:` block at all.\n- Keystores, `.p12`, `.p8`, `google-services.json` with production keys, or passwords committed \"temporarily\".\n- Signing release builds on every PR \"to make sure signing works\".\n- `continue-on-error: true` or `|| true` on lint and test steps.\n- Release jobs that re-run the whole verify pipeline in parallel instead of `needs:` on it.\n- Uploading to the production track directly from CI without a staged rollout or a human approval.\n- Caches shared between PR and release jobs without lockfile keys.\n- Debug output (`set -x`, `--verbose`, `--debug`) left on in jobs that handle secrets.\n- Hand-maintained version numbers that drift between Android, iOS and the tag.\n- One giant job doing lint, tests, build and upload, so a flaky test blocks diagnosis of everything else.\n\n## Checklist\n\nSecure pipeline:\n- [ ] Top-level `permissions: contents: read`; any extra scope granted per job with a comment\n- [ ] Every third-party action pinned to a full commit SHA with a version comment; Dependabot enabled\n- [ ] Container images pinned by digest; CLI tools pinned by exact version or lockfile\n- [ ] No `pull_request_target`, `workflow_run` or comment-triggered job builds untrusted code with secrets\n- [ ] Signing and store secrets are environment secrets behind required reviewers and ref rules\n- [ ] Keystore and keys decoded into `\$RUNNER_TEMP`, never written into the workspace or committed\n- [ ] Secrets and event data reach scripts only through `env:`; no `set -x` or debug flags near them\n- [ ] `persist-credentials: false` on checkout unless the job pushes\n- [ ] App Store Connect API key used; no Apple ID password or session; `match` is `readonly`\n- [ ] OIDC used where the provider supports it; remaining stored keys scoped and rotation-dated\n\nPipeline quality:\n- [ ] Verify job runs lint, static analysis, unit tests and a coverage threshold, and fails on violations\n- [ ] Release job `needs:` the verify job and runs only on tags, `main` or manual dispatch\n- [ ] Concurrency group per workflow and ref; `cancel-in-progress` only for PRs\n- [ ] `timeout-minutes` on every job; `paths:` filters on expensive macOS jobs\n- [ ] Caches keyed on lockfiles; release jobs do not write caches from PR content\n- [ ] versionCode and build number derived from the run number with a documented offset\n- [ ] R8 mapping, dSYMs and Flutter split debug info archived per build and uploaded to the crash reporter\n- [ ] Test and lint reports uploaded with `if: always()`\n- [ ] `actionlint` and `zizmor` pass on the workflow files\n\n## Output format\n\nDeliver files, then a setup sheet. Do not invent secret values; name them and say how to create them.\n\n~~~markdown\n## CI/CD: <project> -- <platforms>\n\n### Files\n- `.github/workflows/android.yml` -- verify on PR, Firebase QA build on main, Play internal on tag\n- `fastlane/Fastfile` -- lanes `android release`, `ios beta`\n- `app/build.gradle.kts` -- signing config and versionCode from Gradle properties (diff below)\n\n```yaml\n<full workflow file>\n```\n\n### Required secrets\n| Name | Scope | What it is | How to create |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| ANDROID_UPLOAD_KEYSTORE_BASE64 | env `play-production` | Upload keystore, base64 | `base64 -i upload.jks` (macOS) or `base64 -w0 upload.jks` (Linux) |\n| PLAY_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_JSON | env `play-production` | Play Console service account key | Google Cloud service account, invited in Play Console with release permission for this app only |\n| ASC_KEY_ID / ASC_ISSUER_ID / ASC_KEY_P8_BASE64 | env `testflight` | App Store Connect API key | Users and Access > Integrations, lowest role that can upload |\n\n### Repository variables\n| Name | Example | Purpose |\n|---|---|---|\n| VERSION_CODE_OFFSET | 1000 | Keeps versionCode above previously uploaded builds |\n\n### Manual setup\n1. Create environments `play-production`, `testflight` with required reviewers and tag rule `v*`.\n2. Add the secrets above as environment secrets.\n3. Branch protection on `main`: require the `verify` check.\n4. Enable Dependabot for `github-actions`.\n5. <store-side steps: Play App Signing enrolled, first AAB uploaded manually, TestFlight groups created>\n\n### Verification\n- `actionlint` and `zizmor` output\n- Which jobs were run on a branch, and which were not run because they need secrets\n\n### Open questions\n- <tracks, groups, approvers or thresholds that need the owner's decision>\n~~~\n\n## Related skills\n\n- `mobile-security` -- secret storage, MASVS and supply-chain review beyond the pipeline\n- `mobile-merge-request` -- describing pipeline changes and required manual steps in the MR\n- `mobile-code-review` -- reviewing Gradle, Fastfile and workflow changes alongside app code\n- `kotlin-android`, `swift-ios`, `dart-flutter` -- build configuration conventions per platform\n",
    "references/android-github-actions.md":
        "# Android on GitHub Actions\n\nLoad this when the pipeline builds a native Android app (Kotlin or Java, Gradle Kotlin DSL). Adapt module\nnames, flavors, tasks and package names to the project.\n\nPinning convention used in every file below: `@<sha>` is a placeholder for the full 40-character commit\nSHA of the release named in the comment. Resolve it before committing (see Rule 1 in SKILL.md). Versions\nin comments were valid at the time of writing; use the latest release when pinning.\n\n## Workflow: `.github/workflows/android.yml`\n\n```yaml\nname: Android\n\non:\n  pull_request:\n  push:\n    branches: [main]\n    tags: ['v*']\n  workflow_dispatch:\n\npermissions:\n  contents: read\n\nconcurrency:\n  group: \${{ github.workflow }}-\${{ github.ref }}\n  cancel-in-progress: \${{ github.event_name == 'pull_request' }}\n\njobs:\n  verify:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    timeout-minutes: 30\n    steps:\n      - uses: actions/checkout@<sha> # v5\n        with:\n          persist-credentials: false\n\n      - uses: gradle/actions/wrapper-validation@<sha> # v4\n\n      - uses: actions/setup-java@<sha> # v5\n        with:\n          distribution: temurin\n          java-version: '21'\n\n      - uses: gradle/actions/setup-gradle@<sha> # v4\n\n      - name: Static analysis\n        run: ./gradlew ktlintCheck detekt lint --continue\n\n      - name: Unit tests and coverage gate\n        run: ./gradlew testDebugUnitTest koverVerify --continue\n\n      - name: Upload reports\n        if: always()\n        uses: actions/upload-artifact@<sha> # v4\n        with:\n          name: verify-reports\n          path: |\n            **/build/reports/\n            **/build/test-results/\n          retention-days: 14\n\n  instrumented:\n    needs: verify\n    if: github.event_name == 'push' && github.ref == 'refs/heads/main'\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    timeout-minutes: 45\n    strategy:\n      fail-fast: false\n      matrix:\n        api-level: [29, 35]\n    steps:\n      - uses: actions/checkout@<sha> # v5\n        with:\n          persist-credentials: false\n      - uses: actions/setup-java@<sha> # v5\n        with:\n          distribution: temurin\n          java-version: '21'\n      - uses: gradle/actions/setup-gradle@<sha> # v4\n      - name: Enable KVM\n        run: |\n          echo 'KERNEL==\"kvm\", GROUP=\"kvm\", MODE=\"0666\", OPTIONS+=\"static_node=kvm\"' | sudo tee /etc/udev/rules.d/99-kvm4all.rules\n          sudo udevadm control --reload-rules\n          sudo udevadm trigger --name-match=kvm\n      - uses: reactivecircus/android-emulator-runner@<sha> # v2\n        with:\n          api-level: \${{ matrix.api-level }}\n          arch: x86_64\n          target: google_apis\n          script: ./gradlew connectedDebugAndroidTest\n\n  firebase-qa:\n    needs: verify\n    if: github.event_name == 'push' && github.ref == 'refs/heads/main'\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    timeout-minutes: 30\n    environment: firebase-qa\n    permissions:\n      contents: read\n      id-token: write # OIDC to Google Cloud, no stored JSON key\n    steps:\n      - uses: actions/checkout@<sha> # v5\n        with:\n          persist-credentials: false\n      - uses: actions/setup-java@<sha> # v5\n        with:\n          distribution: temurin\n          java-version: '21'\n      - uses: gradle/actions/setup-gradle@<sha> # v4\n        with:\n          cache-read-only: true\n\n      - name: Compute versionCode\n        env:\n          VERSION_CODE_OFFSET: \${{ vars.VERSION_CODE_OFFSET }}\n        run: echo \"VERSION_CODE=\$(( GITHUB_RUN_NUMBER + \${VERSION_CODE_OFFSET:-0} ))\" >> \"\$GITHUB_ENV\"\n\n      - name: Build QA APK\n        run: ./gradlew assembleQa -PversionCode=\"\$VERSION_CODE\"\n\n      - uses: google-github-actions/auth@<sha> # v2\n        with:\n          workload_identity_provider: \${{ vars.GCP_WORKLOAD_IDENTITY_PROVIDER }}\n          service_account: \${{ vars.GCP_DISTRIBUTION_SERVICE_ACCOUNT }}\n\n      - name: Distribute to testers\n        env:\n          FIREBASE_APP_ID: \${{ vars.FIREBASE_ANDROID_APP_ID }}\n        run: |\n          npx --yes firebase-tools@<x.y.z> appdistribution:distribute \\\n            app/build/outputs/apk/qa/app-qa.apk \\\n            --app \"\$FIREBASE_APP_ID\" \\\n            --groups \"qa\" \\\n            --release-notes \"main @ \${GITHUB_SHA::8}, build \$VERSION_CODE\"\n\n  release:\n    needs: verify\n    if: startsWith(github.ref, 'refs/tags/v')\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    timeout-minutes: 45\n    environment: play-production # required reviewers + tag rule v*\n    steps:\n      - uses: actions/checkout@<sha> # v5\n        with:\n          persist-credentials: false\n      - uses: actions/setup-java@<sha> # v5\n        with:\n          distribution: temurin\n          java-version: '21'\n      - uses: gradle/actions/setup-gradle@<sha> # v4\n        with:\n          cache-read-only: true\n      - uses: ruby/setup-ruby@<sha> # v1\n        with:\n          bundler-cache: true\n\n      - name: Compute versions\n        env:\n          VERSION_CODE_OFFSET: \${{ vars.VERSION_CODE_OFFSET }}\n        run: |\n          echo \"VERSION_CODE=\$(( GITHUB_RUN_NUMBER + \${VERSION_CODE_OFFSET:-0} ))\" >> \"\$GITHUB_ENV\"\n          echo \"VERSION_NAME=\${GITHUB_REF_NAME#v}\" >> \"\$GITHUB_ENV\"\n\n      - name: Decode upload keystore\n        env:\n          KEYSTORE_BASE64: \${{ secrets.ANDROID_UPLOAD_KEYSTORE_BASE64 }}\n        run: |\n          echo \"\$KEYSTORE_BASE64\" | base64 --decode > \"\$RUNNER_TEMP/upload.jks\"\n          echo \"ANDROID_KEYSTORE_PATH=\$RUNNER_TEMP/upload.jks\" >> \"\$GITHUB_ENV\"\n\n      - name: Build signed AAB\n        env:\n          ANDROID_KEYSTORE_PASSWORD: \${{ secrets.ANDROID_KEYSTORE_PASSWORD }}\n          ANDROID_KEY_ALIAS: \${{ secrets.ANDROID_KEY_ALIAS }}\n          ANDROID_KEY_PASSWORD: \${{ secrets.ANDROID_KEY_PASSWORD }}\n        run: ./gradlew bundleRelease -PversionCode=\"\$VERSION_CODE\" -PversionName=\"\$VERSION_NAME\"\n\n      - name: Archive AAB and R8 mapping\n        uses: actions/upload-artifact@<sha> # v4\n        with:\n          name: release-\${{ env.VERSION_CODE }}\n          path: |\n            app/build/outputs/bundle/release/app-release.aab\n            app/build/outputs/mapping/release/mapping.txt\n          retention-days: 90\n\n      - name: Upload to Play internal track\n        env:\n          PLAY_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_JSON: \${{ secrets.PLAY_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_JSON }}\n        run: bundle exec fastlane android internal\n```\n\nNotes:\n- `instrumented` and `firebase-qa` run on `main` only; the PR path stays fast and secret-free.\n- The `qa` build type is assumed to exist and to be signed with a non-production key (or the debug key).\n  Firebase App Distribution accepts AABs only when the Firebase project is linked to Play, so APK is the\n  simpler default for QA.\n- `environment:` on `firebase-qa` and `release` holds the secrets and variables those jobs need.\n- If the Play account cannot use OIDC with the upload tool, the JSON key is an environment secret, not a\n  repository secret.\n\n## Gradle: signing and version from CI (`app/build.gradle.kts`)\n\n```kotlin\nandroid {\n    defaultConfig {\n        versionCode = providers.gradleProperty(\"versionCode\").orNull?.toInt() ?: 1\n        versionName = providers.gradleProperty(\"versionName\").orNull ?: \"0.0.0-dev\"\n    }\n\n    signingConfigs {\n        create(\"release\") {\n            val keystorePath = providers.environmentVariable(\"ANDROID_KEYSTORE_PATH\").orNull\n            if (keystorePath != null) {\n                storeFile = file(keystorePath)\n                storePassword = providers.environmentVariable(\"ANDROID_KEYSTORE_PASSWORD\").get()\n                keyAlias = providers.environmentVariable(\"ANDROID_KEY_ALIAS\").get()\n                keyPassword = providers.environmentVariable(\"ANDROID_KEY_PASSWORD\").get()\n            }\n        }\n    }\n\n    buildTypes {\n        release {\n            isMinifyEnabled = true\n            isShrinkResources = true\n            signingConfig = signingConfigs.getByName(\"release\")\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- No passwords in `gradle.properties`, `local.properties` or the build file.\n- A local `bundleRelease` without the environment variables fails at signing. That is intended: release\n  builds come from CI.\n- `versionCode` must stay below 2100000000. Keep the offset small.\n\n## Coverage threshold (Kover)\n\n```kotlin\n// build.gradle.kts of the module (or the root, if Kover merges modules)\nkover {\n    reports {\n        verify {\n            rule {\n                minBound(80)\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- Exclude generated code (Hilt, Room, Compose previews, DI modules) through Kover filters so the number\n  measures real logic.\n- Raise the threshold over time; never lower it to make a PR pass without a recorded decision.\n- JaCoCo with a `JacocoCoverageVerification` task is an equivalent alternative if the project already uses it.\n\n## Fastlane: Play upload (`fastlane/Fastfile`)\n\n```ruby\ndefault_platform(:android)\n\nplatform :android do\n  lane :internal do\n    upload_to_play_store(\n      package_name: \"com.example.app\",\n      track: \"internal\",\n      aab: ENV.fetch(\"AAB_PATH\", \"app/build/outputs/bundle/release/app-release.aab\"),\n      json_key_data: ENV.fetch(\"PLAY_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_JSON\"),\n      skip_upload_metadata: true,\n      skip_upload_images: true,\n      skip_upload_screenshots: true,\n      release_status: \"completed\"\n    )\n  end\n\n  lane :promote_production do\n    upload_to_play_store(\n      package_name: \"com.example.app\",\n      track: \"internal\",\n      track_promote_to: \"production\",\n      rollout: \"0.1\",\n      json_key_data: ENV.fetch(\"PLAY_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_JSON\"),\n      skip_upload_changelogs: true,\n      skip_upload_metadata: true,\n      skip_upload_images: true,\n      skip_upload_screenshots: true\n    )\n  end\nend\n```\n\n- `upload_to_play_store` is the action behind `fastlane supply`.\n- Use `release_status: \"draft\"` while the app has never been published; Play rejects completed releases\n  for draft apps.\n- Commit `Gemfile` and `Gemfile.lock` so the fastlane version is pinned.\n- The Play service account is invited in Play Console with release permissions for this app only, not\n  admin on the developer account.\n- Production promotion is a separate, manually triggered lane with a staged rollout, not part of the tag job.\n\n## Secrets and variables\n\n| Name | Kind | Scope |\n|---|---|---|\n| `ANDROID_UPLOAD_KEYSTORE_BASE64` | secret | env `play-production` |\n| `ANDROID_KEYSTORE_PASSWORD`, `ANDROID_KEY_ALIAS`, `ANDROID_KEY_PASSWORD` | secret | env `play-production` |\n| `PLAY_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_JSON` | secret | env `play-production` |\n| `VERSION_CODE_OFFSET` | variable | repository |\n| `GCP_WORKLOAD_IDENTITY_PROVIDER`, `GCP_DISTRIBUTION_SERVICE_ACCOUNT`, `FIREBASE_ANDROID_APP_ID` | variable | env `firebase-qa` |\n\nCreate the keystore secret with `base64 -i upload.jks | pbcopy` (macOS) or `base64 -w0 upload.jks` (Linux)\nand paste it into the environment secret form. Delete any local plaintext copies of passwords afterwards.\n",
    "references/flutter-github-actions.md":
        "# Flutter on GitHub Actions\n\nLoad this when the pipeline builds a Flutter app for Android and iOS. Platform-specific signing and store\nupload follow the [Android](android-github-actions.md) and [iOS](ios-github-actions.md) references; this\nfile covers what changes for Flutter.\n\nPinning convention: `@<sha>` is a placeholder for the full 40-character commit SHA of the release named\nin the comment. Resolve it before committing. Versions in comments were valid at the time of writing.\n\n## Assumptions\n\n- Flavors `staging` and `prod`, with entry points `lib/main_staging.dart` and `lib/main_prod.dart`, and\n  matching Android product flavors and iOS schemes. Ask the owner if the project differs.\n- The Flutter version is pinned in `pubspec.yaml` (`environment: flutter:`) or in the workflow, and\n  `pubspec.lock` is committed.\n- Android Gradle reads signing from environment variables exactly as in the Android reference. Do not set\n  `versionCode` in Gradle; Flutter passes `--build-number` through.\n\n## Workflow: `.github/workflows/flutter.yml`\n\n```yaml\nname: Flutter\n\non:\n  pull_request:\n  push:\n    branches: [main]\n    tags: ['v*']\n  workflow_dispatch:\n\npermissions:\n  contents: read\n\nconcurrency:\n  group: \${{ github.workflow }}-\${{ github.ref }}\n  cancel-in-progress: \${{ github.event_name == 'pull_request' }}\n\njobs:\n  verify:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    timeout-minutes: 20\n    steps:\n      - uses: actions/checkout@<sha> # v5\n        with:\n          persist-credentials: false\n\n      - uses: subosito/flutter-action@<sha> # v2\n        with:\n          channel: stable\n          flutter-version-file: pubspec.yaml\n          cache: true\n\n      - run: flutter pub get --enforce-lockfile\n      - run: dart format --output=none --set-exit-if-changed .\n      - run: flutter analyze --fatal-infos\n      - run: flutter test --coverage\n\n      - name: Coverage gate\n        env:\n          MIN_COVERAGE: '70'\n        run: |\n          awk -F: -v min=\"\$MIN_COVERAGE\" '\n            /^LF:/ { found += \$2 }\n            /^LH:/ { hit += \$2 }\n            END {\n              pct = found ? hit * 100 / found : 0\n              printf \"Line coverage: %.1f%%\\n\", pct\n              exit (pct < min)\n            }' coverage/lcov.info\n\n  android:\n    needs: verify\n    if: startsWith(github.ref, 'refs/tags/v')\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    timeout-minutes: 40\n    environment: android-release # required reviewers + tag rule v*\n    permissions:\n      contents: read\n      id-token: write # OIDC for Firebase App Distribution\n    strategy:\n      fail-fast: false\n      matrix:\n        include:\n          - flavor: staging\n            target: apk\n          - flavor: prod\n            target: appbundle\n    steps:\n      - uses: actions/checkout@<sha> # v5\n        with:\n          persist-credentials: false\n      - uses: actions/setup-java@<sha> # v5\n        with:\n          distribution: temurin\n          java-version: '21'\n      - uses: subosito/flutter-action@<sha> # v2\n        with:\n          channel: stable\n          flutter-version-file: pubspec.yaml\n          cache: true\n\n      - name: Compute versions\n        env:\n          VERSION_CODE_OFFSET: \${{ vars.VERSION_CODE_OFFSET }}\n        run: |\n          echo \"VERSION_CODE=\$(( GITHUB_RUN_NUMBER + \${VERSION_CODE_OFFSET:-0} ))\" >> \"\$GITHUB_ENV\"\n          echo \"VERSION_NAME=\${GITHUB_REF_NAME#v}\" >> \"\$GITHUB_ENV\"\n\n      - name: Decode upload keystore\n        env:\n          KEYSTORE_BASE64: \${{ secrets.ANDROID_UPLOAD_KEYSTORE_BASE64 }}\n        run: |\n          echo \"\$KEYSTORE_BASE64\" | base64 --decode > \"\$RUNNER_TEMP/upload.jks\"\n          echo \"ANDROID_KEYSTORE_PATH=\$RUNNER_TEMP/upload.jks\" >> \"\$GITHUB_ENV\"\n\n      - name: Build\n        env:\n          ANDROID_KEYSTORE_PASSWORD: \${{ secrets.ANDROID_KEYSTORE_PASSWORD }}\n          ANDROID_KEY_ALIAS: \${{ secrets.ANDROID_KEY_ALIAS }}\n          ANDROID_KEY_PASSWORD: \${{ secrets.ANDROID_KEY_PASSWORD }}\n          FLAVOR: \${{ matrix.flavor }}\n          TARGET: \${{ matrix.target }}\n        run: |\n          flutter build \"\$TARGET\" --release \\\n            --flavor \"\$FLAVOR\" \\\n            -t \"lib/main_\${FLAVOR}.dart\" \\\n            --dart-define-from-file=\"config/\${FLAVOR}.json\" \\\n            --obfuscate \\\n            --split-debug-info=\"build/symbols/android-\${FLAVOR}\" \\\n            --build-number=\"\$VERSION_CODE\" \\\n            --build-name=\"\$VERSION_NAME\"\n\n      - name: Keep binaries, symbols and mapping\n        uses: actions/upload-artifact@<sha> # v4\n        with:\n          name: android-\${{ matrix.flavor }}-\${{ env.VERSION_CODE }}\n          path: |\n            build/app/outputs/flutter-apk/*.apk\n            build/app/outputs/bundle/\${{ matrix.flavor }}Release/*.aab\n            build/app/outputs/mapping/\${{ matrix.flavor }}Release/mapping.txt\n            build/symbols/android-\${{ matrix.flavor }}\n          retention-days: 90\n\n      # staging: testers via Firebase App Distribution\n      - if: matrix.flavor == 'staging'\n        uses: google-github-actions/auth@<sha> # v2\n        with:\n          workload_identity_provider: \${{ vars.GCP_WORKLOAD_IDENTITY_PROVIDER }}\n          service_account: \${{ vars.GCP_DISTRIBUTION_SERVICE_ACCOUNT }}\n      - if: matrix.flavor == 'staging'\n        env:\n          FIREBASE_APP_ID: \${{ vars.FIREBASE_ANDROID_STAGING_APP_ID }}\n        run: |\n          npx --yes firebase-tools@<x.y.z> appdistribution:distribute \\\n            build/app/outputs/flutter-apk/app-staging-release.apk \\\n            --app \"\$FIREBASE_APP_ID\" --groups \"qa\"\n\n      # prod: Play internal track, lane as in the Android reference with the Flutter AAB path\n      - if: matrix.flavor == 'prod'\n        uses: ruby/setup-ruby@<sha> # v1\n        with:\n          bundler-cache: true\n          working-directory: android\n      - if: matrix.flavor == 'prod'\n        working-directory: android\n        env:\n          PLAY_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_JSON: \${{ secrets.PLAY_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_JSON }}\n          AAB_PATH: \${{ github.workspace }}/build/app/outputs/bundle/prodRelease/app-prod-release.aab\n        run: bundle exec fastlane android internal\n```\n\nMatrix notes:\n- A job-level `if:` is evaluated before the matrix expands and cannot read `matrix`. Filter per entry\n  with step-level `if: matrix.flavor == '...'`, or split into separate jobs.\n- `fail-fast: false` so a staging failure does not cancel an in-flight prod upload.\n- `upload-artifact` warns, not fails, on path patterns that match nothing, so one path list can serve\n  both targets.\n- For a staging build on every push to `main`, add a second job with the same steps and only the\n  staging entry; keep prod on tags.\n\n## iOS job\n\n```yaml\n  ios:\n    needs: verify\n    if: startsWith(github.ref, 'refs/tags/v')\n    runs-on: macos-15\n    timeout-minutes: 60\n    environment: testflight\n    steps:\n      - uses: actions/checkout@<sha> # v5\n        with:\n          persist-credentials: false\n      - uses: subosito/flutter-action@<sha> # v2\n        with:\n          channel: stable\n          flutter-version-file: pubspec.yaml\n          cache: true\n      - uses: ruby/setup-ruby@<sha> # v1\n        with:\n          bundler-cache: true\n          working-directory: ios\n\n      - name: Compute build number\n        env:\n          BUILD_NUMBER_OFFSET: \${{ vars.BUILD_NUMBER_OFFSET }}\n        run: echo \"BUILD_NUMBER=\$(( GITHUB_RUN_NUMBER + \${BUILD_NUMBER_OFFSET:-0} ))\" >> \"\$GITHUB_ENV\"\n\n      - name: Signing, build and upload\n        working-directory: ios\n        env:\n          ASC_KEY_ID: \${{ secrets.ASC_KEY_ID }}\n          ASC_ISSUER_ID: \${{ secrets.ASC_ISSUER_ID }}\n          ASC_KEY_P8_BASE64: \${{ secrets.ASC_KEY_P8_BASE64 }}\n          MATCH_PASSWORD: \${{ secrets.MATCH_PASSWORD }}\n          MATCH_GIT_BASIC_AUTHORIZATION: \${{ secrets.MATCH_GIT_BASIC_AUTHORIZATION }}\n        run: bundle exec fastlane ios beta\n\n      - name: Keep symbols\n        uses: actions/upload-artifact@<sha> # v4\n        with:\n          name: ios-prod-\${{ env.BUILD_NUMBER }}\n          path: |\n            build/symbols/ios-prod\n            build/ios/archive/*.xcarchive/dSYMs\n          retention-days: 90\n```\n\n`ios/fastlane/Fastfile`:\n\n```ruby\ndefault_platform(:ios)\n\nplatform :ios do\n  lane :beta do\n    setup_ci\n    api_key = app_store_connect_api_key(\n      key_id: ENV.fetch(\"ASC_KEY_ID\"),\n      issuer_id: ENV.fetch(\"ASC_ISSUER_ID\"),\n      key_content: ENV.fetch(\"ASC_KEY_P8_BASE64\"),\n      is_key_content_base64: true\n    )\n    match(type: \"appstore\", readonly: true, api_key: api_key)\n\n    Dir.chdir(\"../..\") do\n      sh(\n        \"flutter\", \"build\", \"ipa\", \"--release\",\n        \"--flavor\", \"prod\",\n        \"-t\", \"lib/main_prod.dart\",\n        \"--dart-define-from-file=config/prod.json\",\n        \"--obfuscate\",\n        \"--split-debug-info=build/symbols/ios-prod\",\n        \"--build-number=#{ENV.fetch('BUILD_NUMBER')}\",\n        \"--export-options-plist=ios/ExportOptions.plist\"\n      )\n    end\n\n    ipa = Dir[File.join(File.expand_path(\"../..\"), \"build/ios/ipa/*.ipa\")].first\n    UI.user_error!(\"No IPA produced\") if ipa.nil?\n    upload_to_testflight(api_key: api_key, ipa: ipa, skip_waiting_for_build_processing: true)\n  end\nend\n```\n\n- Plain Ruby in a Fastfile runs with `ios/fastlane` as the working directory, while actions resolve\n  paths from `ios/`. Absolute paths (as above) avoid mixing the two up.\n- `ios/ExportOptions.plist` sets `method` to App Store distribution, manual signing, and maps the bundle\n  id to the `match AppStore <bundle id>` profile.\n- The Xcode project's Release configuration for the `prod` scheme uses manual signing with that profile.\n\n## Obfuscation symbols\n\n- `--obfuscate` requires `--split-debug-info`. The directory holds the symbol files needed to read Dart\n  stack traces; without them, production crashes are unreadable.\n- Archive them per flavor and build number, and upload them to the crash reporter. For Crashlytics:\n  `firebase crashlytics:symbols:upload --app=<firebase-app-id> build/symbols/<dir>`.\n- Symbolicate a stored trace manually with\n  `flutter symbolize -i <stack-trace-file> -d <symbols-dir>/<file>.symbols`.\n- Never ship the symbols directory inside the app or commit it.\n\n## Configuration and secrets\n\n- `--dart-define` and `--dart-define-from-file` values are compiled into the binary and are readable by\n  anyone with the APK or IPA. Use them for environment URLs and feature flags, never for API secrets.\n- `config/<flavor>.json` files hold non-secret configuration and can be committed. Anything secret stays\n  server side.\n- Secrets and variables are the union of the Android and iOS tables, scoped to the same environments.\n",
    "references/gitlab-ci.md":
        "# GitLab CI\n\nLoad this when the project uses GitLab CI instead of GitHub Actions. The rules in SKILL.md apply\nunchanged; this file maps them to GitLab keywords and shows a short Android pipeline. iOS and Flutter\njobs follow the same shape on a macOS runner and reuse the fastlane lanes from the\n[iOS](ios-github-actions.md) and [Flutter](flutter-github-actions.md) references.\n\n## Concept mapping\n\n| GitHub Actions | GitLab CI |\n|---|---|\n| `uses: owner/action@<sha>` | `image: <image>@sha256:<digest>`, `include: project: ... ref: <commit-sha>` |\n| `permissions:` | `CI_JOB_TOKEN` allowlist and project token access settings |\n| Environment secrets with required reviewers | Protected and masked CI/CD variables, protected environments with deployment approvals |\n| Secrets withheld from fork PRs | Protected variables are exposed only to pipelines on protected branches and tags |\n| `concurrency` + `cancel-in-progress` | `interruptible: true` + auto-cancel redundant pipelines; `resource_group` for deploys |\n| `github.run_number` | `CI_PIPELINE_IID` (per project, increasing) |\n| OIDC `id-token: write` | `id_tokens:` keyword |\n| `actions/upload-artifact` | `artifacts:` with `expire_in` |\n| `paths:` filter | `rules: - changes:` |\n\n## Variables\n\n- Signing and store credentials are CI/CD variables that are protected (only on protected refs) and\n  masked (or masked and hidden). Mark `v*` tags as protected tags so release pipelines receive them.\n- Binary files (keystore, `.p8`) are stored base64-encoded and decoded at runtime. A File-type variable\n  can hold text files such as the Play service account JSON; the variable then contains a path.\n- Merge request pipelines from forks run in the fork project by default. A parent-project member can\n  choose to run them in the parent project, which exposes its non-protected variables; review the fork's\n  code, including `.gitlab-ci.yml`, before doing that.\n\n## Pipeline: `.gitlab-ci.yml` (Android)\n\n```yaml\nworkflow:\n  rules:\n    - if: \$CI_PIPELINE_SOURCE == \"merge_request_event\"\n    - if: \$CI_COMMIT_BRANCH == \$CI_DEFAULT_BRANCH\n    - if: \$CI_COMMIT_TAG =~ /^v/\n\nstages: [verify, build, deploy]\n\ndefault:\n  image: <android-sdk-image>@sha256:<digest> # placeholder: JDK + Android SDK image, pinned by digest\n  interruptible: true\n\nvariables:\n  GRADLE_USER_HOME: \$CI_PROJECT_DIR/.gradle\n\n.gradle-cache: &gradle-cache\n  key:\n    files:\n      - gradle/wrapper/gradle-wrapper.properties\n      - gradle/libs.versions.toml\n  paths:\n    - .gradle/caches/\n    - .gradle/wrapper/\n  policy: pull\n\nverify:\n  stage: verify\n  timeout: 30m\n  cache:\n    - <<: *gradle-cache   # read-only for MRs and tags\n  rules:\n    - if: \$CI_COMMIT_BRANCH != \$CI_DEFAULT_BRANCH\n  script:\n    - ./gradlew ktlintCheck detekt lint testDebugUnitTest koverVerify --continue\n  artifacts:\n    when: always\n    expire_in: 14 days\n    reports:\n      junit: \"**/build/test-results/**/TEST-*.xml\"\n    paths:\n      - \"**/build/reports/\"\n\nverify-main:\n  extends: verify\n  cache:\n    - <<: *gradle-cache\n      policy: pull-push   # only the default branch writes the cache\n  rules:\n    - if: \$CI_COMMIT_BRANCH == \$CI_DEFAULT_BRANCH\n\nrelease:\n  stage: build\n  timeout: 45m\n  interruptible: false\n  needs: [verify]\n  rules:\n    - if: \$CI_COMMIT_TAG =~ /^v/\n  environment:\n    name: play-production   # protected environment with deployment approval\n  resource_group: play-store\n  cache:\n    - <<: *gradle-cache\n  script:\n    - export VERSION_CODE=\$(( CI_PIPELINE_IID + \${VERSION_CODE_OFFSET:-0} ))\n    - export ANDROID_KEYSTORE_PATH=\"\$(mktemp -d)/upload.jks\"\n    - echo \"\$ANDROID_UPLOAD_KEYSTORE_BASE64\" | base64 -d > \"\$ANDROID_KEYSTORE_PATH\"\n    - ./gradlew bundleRelease -PversionCode=\"\$VERSION_CODE\" -PversionName=\"\${CI_COMMIT_TAG#v}\"\n    - bundle install\n    - bundle exec fastlane android internal\n  artifacts:\n    expire_in: 90 days\n    paths:\n      - app/build/outputs/bundle/release/app-release.aab\n      - app/build/outputs/mapping/release/mapping.txt\n```\n\nNotes:\n- Only `verify-main` writes the Gradle cache, so merge request code cannot poison what release jobs\n  restore. `cache:key:files` accepts at most two files.\n- The release job assumes Ruby and Bundler are available in the image; otherwise use a separate,\n  digest-pinned image for the upload step.\n- Enable \"Auto-cancel redundant pipelines\" in the project CI/CD settings so `interruptible: true` jobs are\n  cancelled by newer pipelines on the same ref. The release job sets `interruptible: false`.\n- `resource_group` serialises store uploads so two tags never upload at the same time.\n- Shell expansion works in `script:` lines, so arithmetic for `VERSION_CODE` is done there.\n- `ANDROID_KEYSTORE_PASSWORD`, `ANDROID_KEY_ALIAS`, `ANDROID_KEY_PASSWORD` and\n  `PLAY_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_JSON` are protected, masked variables scoped to the `play-production` environment.\n- Never enable `CI_DEBUG_TRACE` on jobs with protected variables; it prints every variable.\n\n## iOS and Flutter on GitLab\n\n```yaml\nios-testflight:\n  stage: deploy\n  tags: [macos]   # placeholder: tag of a GitLab-hosted or self-hosted macOS runner\n  timeout: 60m\n  interruptible: false\n  needs: [verify]\n  rules:\n    - if: \$CI_COMMIT_TAG =~ /^v/\n  environment:\n    name: testflight\n  resource_group: testflight\n  script:\n    - export BUILD_NUMBER=\$(( CI_PIPELINE_IID + \${BUILD_NUMBER_OFFSET:-0} ))\n    - export MARKETING_VERSION=\"\${CI_COMMIT_TAG#v}\"\n    - bundle install\n    - bundle exec fastlane ios beta\n  artifacts:\n    expire_in: 90 days\n    paths:\n      - build/*.dSYM.zip\n```\n\n- Self-hosted macOS runners keep state between jobs. `setup_ci` uses a temporary keychain, but also clean\n  derived data and never leave decoded keys on disk.\n- Use `rules: - changes:` to run macOS jobs only when iOS or shared code changed.\n",
    "references/ios-github-actions.md":
        "# iOS on GitHub Actions\n\nLoad this when the pipeline builds a native iOS app (Swift, Xcode project or workspace, SPM dependencies).\nAdapt workspace, scheme, bundle id and simulator names to the project.\n\nPinning convention: `@<sha>` is a placeholder for the full 40-character commit SHA of the release named\nin the comment. Resolve it before committing. Versions in comments were valid at the time of writing.\n\n## Before writing the workflow\n\n- Check the runner image's installed Xcode versions and simulators in the `actions/runner-images`\n  documentation for the chosen macOS label. Do not assume a path or device name exists.\n- App Store uploads must be built with the minimum Xcode and SDK Apple currently requires. Pick the\n  runner label and Xcode version from that, not from what is cheapest.\n- Release signing uses manual signing in the Release configuration with the `match` profile names, or\n  `match` updates the project with `update_code_signing_settings`. Decide which before writing lanes.\n- Any `Info.plist`, entitlement or capability change needed for CI (for example a new app group) is\n  called out to the owner, because the provisioning profiles must be regenerated.\n\n## Workflow: `.github/workflows/ios.yml`\n\n```yaml\nname: iOS\n\non:\n  pull_request:\n    paths:\n      - 'ios/**'\n      - 'fastlane/**'\n      - 'Gemfile.lock'\n      - '.github/workflows/ios.yml'\n  push:\n    tags: ['v*']\n  workflow_dispatch:\n\npermissions:\n  contents: read\n\nconcurrency:\n  group: \${{ github.workflow }}-\${{ github.ref }}\n  cancel-in-progress: \${{ github.event_name == 'pull_request' }}\n\nenv:\n  XCODE_VERSION: '<xcode-version-on-runner>' # placeholder, for example the value listed in the image README\n  WORKSPACE: ios/App.xcworkspace\n  SCHEME: App\n\njobs:\n  test:\n    runs-on: macos-15 # choose the image that ships the required Xcode\n    timeout-minutes: 40\n    steps:\n      - uses: actions/checkout@<sha> # v5\n        with:\n          persist-credentials: false\n\n      - name: Select Xcode\n        run: sudo xcode-select -s \"/Applications/Xcode_\${XCODE_VERSION}.app\"\n\n      - uses: actions/cache@<sha> # v4\n        with:\n          path: SourcePackages\n          key: spm-\${{ runner.os }}-\${{ env.XCODE_VERSION }}-\${{ hashFiles('**/Package.resolved') }}\n          restore-keys: spm-\${{ runner.os }}-\${{ env.XCODE_VERSION }}-\n\n      - name: Lint\n        run: swiftlint lint --strict # assumes SwiftLint is provided (SPM plugin, Mint or Homebrew)\n\n      - name: Unit tests\n        run: |\n          set -o pipefail\n          xcodebuild test \\\n            -workspace \"\$WORKSPACE\" \\\n            -scheme \"\$SCHEME\" \\\n            -destination 'platform=iOS Simulator,name=<simulator-on-runner>,OS=latest' \\\n            -clonedSourcePackagesDirPath SourcePackages \\\n            -disableAutomaticPackageResolution \\\n            -enableCodeCoverage YES \\\n            -resultBundlePath \"\$RUNNER_TEMP/Tests.xcresult\" \\\n            CODE_SIGNING_ALLOWED=NO\n\n      - name: Coverage gate\n        env:\n          MIN_COVERAGE: '0.70'\n        run: |\n          COVERAGE=\$(xcrun xccov view --report --json \"\$RUNNER_TEMP/Tests.xcresult\" | jq '.lineCoverage')\n          echo \"Line coverage: \$COVERAGE\"\n          awk -v c=\"\$COVERAGE\" -v min=\"\$MIN_COVERAGE\" 'BEGIN { exit (c + 0 < min + 0) }'\n\n      - name: Upload result bundle\n        if: failure()\n        uses: actions/upload-artifact@<sha> # v4\n        with:\n          name: test-results\n          path: \${{ runner.temp }}/Tests.xcresult\n          retention-days: 7\n\n  testflight:\n    needs: test\n    if: startsWith(github.ref, 'refs/tags/v')\n    runs-on: macos-15\n    timeout-minutes: 60\n    environment: testflight # required reviewers + tag rule v*\n    steps:\n      - uses: actions/checkout@<sha> # v5\n        with:\n          persist-credentials: false\n\n      - name: Select Xcode\n        run: sudo xcode-select -s \"/Applications/Xcode_\${XCODE_VERSION}.app\"\n\n      - uses: actions/cache/restore@<sha> # v4\n        with:\n          path: SourcePackages\n          key: spm-\${{ runner.os }}-\${{ env.XCODE_VERSION }}-\${{ hashFiles('**/Package.resolved') }}\n\n      - uses: ruby/setup-ruby@<sha> # v1\n        with:\n          bundler-cache: true\n\n      - name: Compute build number\n        env:\n          BUILD_NUMBER_OFFSET: \${{ vars.BUILD_NUMBER_OFFSET }}\n        run: |\n          echo \"BUILD_NUMBER=\$(( GITHUB_RUN_NUMBER + \${BUILD_NUMBER_OFFSET:-0} ))\" >> \"\$GITHUB_ENV\"\n          echo \"MARKETING_VERSION=\${GITHUB_REF_NAME#v}\" >> \"\$GITHUB_ENV\"\n\n      - name: Build and upload to TestFlight\n        env:\n          ASC_KEY_ID: \${{ secrets.ASC_KEY_ID }}\n          ASC_ISSUER_ID: \${{ secrets.ASC_ISSUER_ID }}\n          ASC_KEY_P8_BASE64: \${{ secrets.ASC_KEY_P8_BASE64 }}\n          MATCH_PASSWORD: \${{ secrets.MATCH_PASSWORD }}\n          MATCH_GIT_BASIC_AUTHORIZATION: \${{ secrets.MATCH_GIT_BASIC_AUTHORIZATION }}\n        run: bundle exec fastlane ios beta\n\n      - name: Archive dSYMs\n        uses: actions/upload-artifact@<sha> # v4\n        with:\n          name: dsyms-\${{ env.BUILD_NUMBER }}\n          path: build/*.dSYM.zip\n          retention-days: 90\n```\n\nNotes:\n- The release job uses `actions/cache/restore` only, so a PR cannot poison what the release build links.\n- `-disableAutomaticPackageResolution` makes the build fail if `Package.resolved` is out of date instead\n  of silently resolving new versions.\n- `paths:` keeps Android-only PRs off macOS runners. If the iOS check is required on `main`, add a\n  lightweight always-running job with the same name or the PR will wait for a check that never starts.\n\n## Fastlane (`fastlane/Fastfile`)\n\n```ruby\ndefault_platform(:ios)\n\nplatform :ios do\n  lane :beta do\n    setup_ci # temporary keychain on CI, no changes to the login keychain\n\n    api_key = app_store_connect_api_key(\n      key_id: ENV.fetch(\"ASC_KEY_ID\"),\n      issuer_id: ENV.fetch(\"ASC_ISSUER_ID\"),\n      key_content: ENV.fetch(\"ASC_KEY_P8_BASE64\"),\n      is_key_content_base64: true\n    )\n\n    match(type: \"appstore\", readonly: true, api_key: api_key)\n\n    build_app(\n      workspace: \"ios/App.xcworkspace\",\n      scheme: \"App\",\n      configuration: \"Release\",\n      export_method: \"app-store\",\n      cloned_source_packages_path: \"SourcePackages\",\n      output_directory: \"build\",\n      xcargs: \"CURRENT_PROJECT_VERSION=#{ENV.fetch('BUILD_NUMBER')} MARKETING_VERSION=#{ENV.fetch('MARKETING_VERSION')}\"\n    )\n\n    upload_to_testflight(\n      api_key: api_key,\n      skip_waiting_for_build_processing: true\n    )\n  end\nend\n```\n\n`fastlane/Matchfile`:\n\n```ruby\ngit_url(\"https://github.com/<org>/<certificates-repo>.git\")\nstorage_mode(\"git\")\ntype(\"appstore\")\napp_identifier([\"com.example.app\"])\n```\n\n- `MATCH_GIT_BASIC_AUTHORIZATION` is base64 of `user:token` for a token that can only read the\n  certificates repository. A deploy key or a cloud storage backend for `match` is equally fine.\n- `MATCH_PASSWORD` decrypts the repository. Losing it means regenerating certificates; store it in a\n  password manager as well as in CI.\n- `CURRENT_PROJECT_VERSION` and `MARKETING_VERSION` overrides work when `Info.plist` uses\n  `\$(CURRENT_PROJECT_VERSION)` and `\$(MARKETING_VERSION)`, which is the default for current Xcode templates.\n  Older projects with hardcoded values need that change first.\n- Build numbers must increase for each upload of the same marketing version. Set `BUILD_NUMBER_OFFSET`\n  above the highest build already in App Store Connect when migrating.\n- Upload dSYMs to the crash reporter in the same lane (for example `upload_symbols_to_crashlytics`) when\n  the project uses one.\n\n## App Store Connect API key\n\n- Created in App Store Connect under Users and Access, Integrations. The `.p8` file can be downloaded only once.\n- Store as `ASC_KEY_P8_BASE64` (`base64 -i AuthKey_XXXX.p8`), with `ASC_KEY_ID` and `ASC_ISSUER_ID`, as\n  environment secrets of `testflight`.\n- Use a team key with the lowest role that can upload and manage TestFlight for the lanes in use. Revoke\n  it when the pipeline is retired or the key may have leaked.\n- Never use `FASTLANE_USER` / `FASTLANE_PASSWORD`, app-specific passwords or `FASTLANE_SESSION` in CI.\n\n## Firebase App Distribution (ad hoc builds)\n\n- Needs an `adhoc` (or enterprise) profile from `match(type: \"adhoc\", readonly: true)` and tester devices\n  registered in the profile. TestFlight internal testing is usually simpler for iOS QA.\n- Upload with the Firebase CLI (`firebase appdistribution:distribute <ipa> --app <id> --groups <group>`)\n  after authenticating with OIDC via `google-github-actions/auth`, as in the Android reference.\n\n## Secrets and variables\n\n| Name | Kind | Scope |\n|---|---|---|\n| `ASC_KEY_ID`, `ASC_ISSUER_ID`, `ASC_KEY_P8_BASE64` | secret | env `testflight` |\n| `MATCH_PASSWORD`, `MATCH_GIT_BASIC_AUTHORIZATION` | secret | env `testflight` |\n| `BUILD_NUMBER_OFFSET` | variable | repository |\n",
  },
  "mobile-code-review": {
    "SKILL.md":
        "---\nname: mobile-code-review\ndescription: \"Reviews mobile code and diffs in Kotlin, Java, Swift and Dart for Android, iOS and Flutter apps. Checks correctness, concurrency (coroutine scopes and dispatchers, Swift actors, Sendable and @MainActor, Dart isolates), lifecycle and memory leaks (Context leaks, retain cycles, uncancelled StreamSubscriptions), UI performance (Jetpack Compose stability and recomposition, SwiftUI identity, Flutter rebuilds and const widgets), architecture boundaries, error handling, tests, accessibility and i18n. Produces severity-ranked findings (Blocker, Major, Minor, Nit) with file:line, reason and fix snippet, then an Approve or Request changes verdict. Use when asked to review a pull request, merge request, diff, branch, commit or file in an Android, iOS, Kotlin Multiplatform or Flutter codebase, or before merging mobile changes.\"\nlicense: MIT\nmetadata:\n  author: Ahmad Hussain\n  version: \"1.0.0\"\n  pack: mobile-dev-kit\n---\n\n# Mobile code review\n\nReview mobile code the way a senior Android, iOS and Flutter engineer would: find the defects that will crash,\nleak, hang or mislead users in production, explain why each one matters, and show the fix.\n\n## When to use\n\n- A pull request, merge request, branch, commit range or single file needs review.\n- The user asks \"review this\", \"is this safe to merge\", \"what is wrong with this code\".\n- Before merging changes in Kotlin, Java, Swift, Objective-C interop or Dart code.\n- After the agent itself wrote mobile code and should check it before handing it over.\n\nNot for: a full security audit (use `mobile-security`), or designing an architecture from scratch\n(use `mobile-mvvm`, `mobile-mvi`).\n\n## Workflow\n\n1. Establish scope.\n   - Diff against the merge base, not the tip of main: `git diff --merge-base origin/main`.\n   - Read the MR description and linked ticket so findings are judged against intent.\n2. Identify platforms and load the matching reference before reviewing:\n   [Kotlin](references/kotlin.md), [Java](references/java.md), [Swift](references/swift.md),\n   [Dart and Flutter](references/dart.md).\n3. Read every changed file in full, plus direct callers and callees of changed functions.\n   A hunk out of context hides most lifecycle and threading bugs.\n4. Pass over the change once per dimension, in this order:\n   correctness, concurrency, lifecycle and memory, security and privacy, UI performance,\n   architecture, error handling, tests, accessibility, i18n.\n5. Verify each candidate finding before reporting it.\n   - Trace the code path. Check it is not already handled elsewhere (base class, interceptor, wrapper).\n   - If it cannot be confirmed from the code, report it as a question, not a defect.\n6. Assign a severity (see below) and write a minimal fix snippet for every Blocker and Major.\n7. Give the verdict. Do not edit files unless the user asks for fixes.\n\n## Severity levels\n\n| Severity | Meaning | Merge impact |\n|---|---|---|\n| Blocker | Crash, data loss or corruption, security or privacy breach, broken build, store rejection | Must fix before merge |\n| Major | Wrong behaviour on a realistic path, leak, race, ANR or hang, swallowed error, missing test for new logic, broken layer boundary | Fix before merge, or defer only with a ticket |\n| Minor | Maintainability, small inefficiency, unclear naming that misleads, missing edge-case test | Should fix, may be a follow-up |\n| Nit | Preference or polish. Never blocks | Optional |\n\nRules for severity:\n- Rate by consequence and likelihood, not by how easy the fix is.\n- Do not report what the formatter or linter already enforces.\n- One root cause is one finding. List extra locations inside it.\n\n## Rules\n\n### 1. Coroutines and tasks are scoped to an owner and cancellable\n\nWork outlives its screen when launched in a global scope. It leaks memory and writes to dead UI.\n\nKotlin:\n```kotlin\n// BAD\nGlobalScope.launch { repo.sync() }\n\n// GOOD\nviewModelScope.launch { repo.sync() }\n```\n\nSwift:\n```swift\n// BAD: unstructured task keeps running after the view disappears\n.onAppear { Task { await model.load() } }\n\n// GOOD: cancelled automatically when the view disappears\n.task { await model.load() }\n```\n\nDart:\n```dart\n// BAD\n_sub = stream.listen(_onData); // never cancelled\n\n// GOOD\n@override\nvoid dispose() {\n  _sub?.cancel();\n  super.dispose();\n}\n```\n\nJava:\n```java\n// BAD: executor never shut down, callbacks hit a destroyed Activity\nexecutor.execute(() -> textView.setText(load()));\n\n// GOOD: work in a ViewModel, result exposed as LiveData, executor shut down in onCleared()\nexecutor.execute(() -> result.postValue(load()));\n```\n\n### 2. Cancellation is never swallowed\n\nKotlin:\n```kotlin\n// BAD: runCatching also catches CancellationException and breaks structured concurrency\nval user = runCatching { api.user() }.getOrNull()\n\n// GOOD\nval user = try {\n    api.user()\n} catch (e: IOException) {\n    null\n}\n```\n\nSwift:\n```swift\n// BAD: loop ignores cancellation\nfor id in ids { await upload(id) }\n\n// GOOD\nfor id in ids {\n    try Task.checkCancellation()\n    await upload(id)\n}\n```\n\n### 3. UI state is touched only on the main thread or actor\n\nKotlin:\n```kotlin\n// BAD: hardcoded dispatcher, untestable and easy to misuse\nwithContext(Dispatchers.IO) { dao.insert(item) }\n\n// GOOD: injected dispatcher\nclass Repo(private val io: CoroutineDispatcher) {\n    suspend fun save(item: Item) = withContext(io) { dao.insert(item) }\n}\n```\n\nSwift:\n```swift\n// BAD: UI state mutated from an arbitrary executor\nfinal class FeedModel: ObservableObject { @Published var items: [Item] = [] }\n\n// GOOD\n@MainActor @Observable\nfinal class FeedModel { var items: [Item] = [] }\n```\n\nDart:\n```dart\n// BAD: 5 MB JSON parsed on the UI isolate, drops frames\nfinal data = jsonDecode(body);\n\n// GOOD\nfinal data = await Isolate.run(() => jsonDecode(body));\n```\n\n### 4. No long-lived references to short-lived objects\n\nKotlin:\n```kotlin\n// BAD: Activity leaked for the lifetime of the process\nobject Analytics { lateinit var context: Context }\n\n// GOOD\nclass Analytics @Inject constructor(@ApplicationContext private val context: Context)\n```\n\nSwift:\n```swift\n// BAD: self -> timer -> closure -> self\ntimer = Timer.scheduledTimer(withTimeInterval: 1, repeats: true) { _ in self.tick() }\n\n// GOOD\ntimer = Timer.scheduledTimer(withTimeInterval: 1, repeats: true) { [weak self] _ in self?.tick() }\n```\n\nDart:\n```dart\n// BAD: controller created in build, never disposed\nWidget build(BuildContext context) => TextField(controller: TextEditingController());\n\n// GOOD: owned by the State object, disposed in dispose()\nlate final _controller = TextEditingController();\n```\n\n### 5. Async UI code re-checks lifecycle after every suspension point\n\nDart:\n```dart\n// BAD\nawait repo.save();\nNavigator.of(context).pop();\n\n// GOOD\nawait repo.save();\nif (!context.mounted) return;\nNavigator.of(context).pop();\n```\n\nKotlin (Compose):\n```kotlin\n// BAD: collects while the app is in the background\nval state by viewModel.state.collectAsState()\n\n// GOOD\nval state by viewModel.state.collectAsStateWithLifecycle()\n```\n\n### 6. UI recomposes and rebuilds only what changed\n\nKotlin (Compose):\n```kotlin\n// BAD: no key, item state jumps on reorder\nLazyColumn { items(messages) { MessageRow(it) } }\n\n// GOOD\nLazyColumn { items(messages, key = { it.id }) { MessageRow(it) } }\n```\n\nSwift (SwiftUI):\n```swift\n// BAD: index identity, wrong rows animate on insert\nForEach(items.indices, id: \\.self) { i in Row(item: items[i]) }\n\n// GOOD: Item is Identifiable with a stable id\nForEach(items) { item in Row(item: item) }\n```\n\nDart (Flutter):\n```dart\n// BAD: whole screen rebuilds on every tick\nsetState(() => _seconds++);\n\n// GOOD: only the label listens\nValueListenableBuilder<int>(\n  valueListenable: _seconds,\n  builder: (context, value, _) => Text('\$value'),\n)\n```\n\n### 7. Errors are handled where they can be acted on, and never silently dropped\n\nKotlin:\n```kotlin\n// BAD\ntry { upload() } catch (e: Exception) { }\n\n// GOOD\ntry {\n    upload()\n} catch (e: IOException) {\n    _state.update { it.copy(error = UiError.Network) }\n}\n```\n\nSwift:\n```swift\n// BAD\nlet data = try? await api.fetch()\n\n// GOOD\ndo {\n    state = .loaded(try await api.fetch())\n} catch {\n    state = .failed(error)\n}\n```\n\n### 8. Layer boundaries hold\n\n- ViewModels and domain code do not import `android.view`, `UIKit` views or `BuildContext`.\n- Domain models are not network DTOs or database entities. Map at the data-layer edge.\n- UI does not call repositories or APIs directly.\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: ViewModel holds a View and returns a Retrofit DTO\nclass ProfileViewModel(private val view: TextView, private val api: Api)\n\n// GOOD\nclass ProfileViewModel(private val getProfile: GetProfileUseCase) : ViewModel()\n```\n\n### 9. New logic ships with tests, and tests are deterministic\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: real delay, flaky and slow\nThread.sleep(500)\n\n// GOOD: virtual time, runs instantly\n@Test fun retriesAfterBackoff() = runTest {\n    val repo = Repo(api = failingOnceApi, io = StandardTestDispatcher(testScheduler))\n    val result = async { repo.load() }\n    advanceTimeBy(500)\n    assertEquals(expected, result.await())\n}\n```\n\nSwift: use `await` on the operation under test, not `sleep`. Dart: use `fakeAsync` or `tester.pump(duration)`.\n\n### 10. Accessibility and i18n are part of correctness\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD\nIcon(Icons.Default.Delete, contentDescription = null) // actionable icon, no label\n\n// GOOD\nIcon(Icons.Default.Delete, contentDescription = stringResource(R.string.delete))\n```\n\n```swift\n// BAD: concatenated String is shown verbatim, never localized, no plural rules\nText(\"You have \" + String(count) + \" messages\")\n\n// GOOD: localized key with a plural variation in the String Catalog\nText(\"You have \\(count) messages\", comment: \"Inbox count\")\n```\n\n- Touch targets at least 48dp (Android) or 44pt (iOS).\n- Text scales with system font size. No fixed heights on text containers.\n- Use start/end, not left/right, so RTL layouts work.\n- Never build sentences by concatenating translated fragments.\n\n## Anti-patterns\n\n- Reviewing only the diff hunks without opening the full file.\n- Reporting style issues the linter already catches.\n- \"Consider refactoring\" with no reason and no snippet.\n- Twenty Nits hiding one Blocker. Order by severity, cap Nits at five.\n- Guessing at a bug the code does not show. Ask instead.\n- Approving with an open Blocker or Major because \"it was already like that\". Pre-existing issues in\n  touched lines are in scope; elsewhere, mention them once as out of scope.\n- Rewriting the author's whole function in the fix snippet. Show the smallest change.\n\n## Checklist\n\n- [ ] Read the MR description, ticket and every changed file in full\n- [ ] Coroutines, Tasks, Futures and Streams have an owner and are cancelled\n- [ ] Cancellation is propagated (no catch-all over suspending or async code)\n- [ ] UI state mutated only on main thread or `@MainActor`\n- [ ] No Activity, Context, View or BuildContext stored beyond its lifecycle\n- [ ] Closures capturing `self` checked for cycles; controllers and subscriptions disposed\n- [ ] Lists have stable keys or identity; no heavy work in composition, body or build\n- [ ] No swallowed errors; user-facing failure state exists\n- [ ] No secrets, tokens or personal data in code or logs (escalate to `mobile-security`)\n- [ ] Layer boundaries respected; DTOs mapped at the data layer\n- [ ] New logic has deterministic tests\n- [ ] Accessibility labels, touch targets, dynamic text size\n- [ ] User-visible strings localized, plurals handled, RTL safe\n- [ ] Every Blocker and Major has a fix snippet\n\n## Output format\n\n~~~markdown\n## Review: <MR title or scope>\n\nScope: <files / commits reviewed>. Platforms: <Android | iOS | Flutter>.\n\n### Findings\n\n1. [Blocker] path/to/File.kt:42 -- <one-line issue>\n   Why: <consequence for users or the codebase>\n   Fix:\n   ```kotlin\n   <minimal snippet>\n   ```\n\n2. [Major] path/to/View.swift:88 -- <issue>\n   Why: ...\n   Fix: ...\n\n### Questions\n- path/to/file.dart:17 -- <thing that could not be confirmed from the code>\n\n### Clean\n- <dimensions checked with no findings, for example \"Concurrency: no issues\">\n\n### Verdict\nRequest changes -- 1 Blocker, 1 Major.\n~~~\n\nVerdict rule: any Blocker or Major means Request changes. Otherwise Approve, listing Minor and Nit items as optional.\n\n## Related skills\n\n- `mobile-security` -- deep MASVS audit when the change touches storage, network, auth or crypto\n- `kotlin-android`, `java-android`, `swift-ios`, `dart-flutter` -- language rules the review applies\n- `mobile-mvvm`, `mobile-mvi`, `mobile-design-patterns` -- architecture expectations\n- `mobile-merge-request` -- MR description, commits and reviewer etiquette\n",
    "references/dart.md":
        "# Dart and Flutter review reference\n\nLoad this when the change contains `.dart` files. Assume Dart 3 with sound null safety.\n\n## Quick scan\n\n| Pattern | Why | Usual severity |\n|---|---|---|\n| `context` used after `await` without a `mounted` check | Uses a dead element, wrong navigator, crash | Major |\n| `setState` after an `await` without `if (!mounted) return;` | Exception on disposed State | Major |\n| `.listen(` with the subscription not stored and cancelled | Leak, callbacks on disposed widget | Major |\n| `TextEditingController(`, `AnimationController(`, `ScrollController(`, `FocusNode(` without `dispose` | Leak | Major |\n| `FutureBuilder(future: fetch()` in `build` | Refetches on every rebuild | Major |\n| `!` on values from network, platform or JSON | Crash | Major |\n| `catch (e) {}` / `catch (_) {}` | Swallowed error | Major |\n| Widget-returning helper methods (`Widget _buildHeader()`) in large widgets | Loses const and rebuild granularity | Minor |\n| `ListView(children: list.map(...).toList())` for long lists | Builds every item up front | Major for unbounded lists |\n| Missing `const` on constructors and widgets | Extra rebuild work | Nit (the linter usually catches it) |\n| `print(` / `debugPrint(` with tokens, emails, device IDs | Personal data in logs | Major, see `mobile-security` |\n\n## Async and lifecycle\n\n```dart\n// BAD\nFuture<void> _save() async {\n  await repo.save(form);\n  ScaffoldMessenger.of(context).showSnackBar(const SnackBar(content: Text('Saved')));\n}\n\n// GOOD\nFuture<void> _save() async {\n  await repo.save(form);\n  if (!mounted) return; // inside State; use context.mounted when only a BuildContext is available\n  ScaffoldMessenger.of(context).showSnackBar(const SnackBar(content: Text('Saved')));\n}\n```\n\n- Every controller, `FocusNode`, `StreamSubscription`, `Timer` and `AnimationController` created by a\n  `State` is disposed or cancelled in `dispose()`.\n- `StreamController`s are closed by their owner.\n- Fire-and-forget futures are explicit: wrap in `unawaited(...)` and enable the `unawaited_futures` or\n  `discarded_futures` lint.\n\n## Concurrency\n\n- CPU-heavy work (large JSON, image processing, crypto) runs in `Isolate.run` or `compute`.\n- Data sent to isolates is sendable; closures passed to `Isolate.run` do not capture large objects or\n  `BuildContext`.\n\n## Widgets and performance\n\n- `build` is pure and cheap: no network calls, no controller creation, no future creation.\n\n```dart\n// BAD: new future each rebuild, request fires repeatedly\n@override\nWidget build(BuildContext context) => FutureBuilder(future: api.fetchUser(), builder: _builder);\n\n// GOOD: future created once\nlate final Future<User> _user = api.fetchUser();\n\n@override\nWidget build(BuildContext context) => FutureBuilder(future: _user, builder: _builder);\n```\n\n- Split large widgets into small `StatelessWidget` classes with `const` constructors rather than private\n  methods returning widgets.\n- Keys: `ValueKey(item.id)` on stateful list items that can reorder. `GlobalKey` only when cross-tree access\n  is really needed.\n- Long or unbounded lists use `ListView.builder` / `SliverList.builder`.\n- Large images are decoded at display size (`cacheWidth` / `cacheHeight` or `ResizeImage`).\n- State is scoped as low as possible; a `setState` at the top of a screen rebuilds everything below it.\n\n## State management\n\n- Riverpod: `ref.watch` in `build`, `ref.read` in callbacks, `ref.listen` for side effects such as\n  navigation and snackbars. Providers that hold screen state use `autoDispose` unless they must persist.\n- Bloc: no `emit` after the handler completes or after `close`; long streams use `emit.forEach` /\n  `emit.onEach`. UI side effects go through `BlocListener`, not `BlocBuilder`.\n- Business logic does not live in widgets.\n\n## Errors and null safety\n\n- Catch specific types (`on SocketException`, `on FormatException`, domain exceptions). Never catch `Error`\n  subclasses such as `StateError` or `TypeError` to keep going; they indicate bugs.\n- JSON parsing validates types instead of `as` casts on untrusted data.\n- Sealed classes plus exhaustive `switch` for result and state types; the compiler then flags missing cases.\n\n## Platform integration\n\n- Platform channels use Pigeon or a typed wrapper; channel names and method names are not stringly duplicated.\n- Native side handlers run on the platform main thread unless a background task queue is configured;\n  heavy native work is moved off it.\n- New permissions, `Info.plist` keys or `AndroidManifest.xml` entries are called out in the MR.\n\n## Accessibility and i18n\n\n- Icon-only buttons have a `tooltip` or `Semantics(label: ...)`.\n- Text uses theme styles and respects text scaling; no fixed-height containers around text.\n- Strings come from `AppLocalizations` (gen-l10n) with ICU plurals, not string concatenation.\n- `EdgeInsetsDirectional` and `AlignmentDirectional` for RTL support.\n\n## Testing\n\n- Unit tests with `package:test`; mocks with `mocktail`; prefer fakes for repositories.\n- Widget tests use `pump(duration)` for timed behaviour; `pumpAndSettle` never returns with infinite\n  animations such as a looping progress indicator.\n- Golden tests run on one pinned platform in CI because font rendering differs.\n- Time-dependent logic uses `fakeAsync` or an injected clock.\n",
    "references/java.md":
        "# Java (Android) review reference\n\nLoad this when the change contains `.java` files. Java on Android is usually legacy code, so focus on leaks,\nthreading and Kotlin interop rather than asking for a rewrite.\n\n## Quick scan\n\n| Pattern | Why | Usual severity |\n|---|---|---|\n| `static Context`, `static Activity`, `static View` | Leaks the Activity for the process lifetime | Major |\n| Non-static inner `Handler`, `Runnable`, `AsyncTask`, listener in an Activity | Implicit outer reference leaks the Activity | Major |\n| `new Handler()` with no Looper | Deprecated, binds to whatever thread calls it | Minor |\n| `AsyncTask` | Deprecated since API 30, leaks and loses results on rotation | Major in new code |\n| `Executors.new...` with no `shutdown()` | Thread leak | Major |\n| `catch (InterruptedException e) {}` | Interrupt status lost | Major |\n| `catch (Exception e) {}` / `e.printStackTrace()` | Swallowed error | Major |\n| `notifyDataSetChanged()` | Full rebind, no animations | Minor |\n| `observe(this, ...)` in a Fragment | Duplicate observers after view recreation | Major |\n| `equals` without `hashCode` (or the reverse) | Broken in sets, maps, DiffUtil | Major |\n| `Stream`, `Cursor` or `InputStream` not closed | Resource leak | Major |\n\n## Leaks and lifecycle\n\n```java\n// BAD: inner class holds MainActivity, message queue holds the Handler\nprivate final Handler handler = new Handler() {\n    @Override public void handleMessage(Message msg) { textView.setText(\"done\"); }\n};\n\n// GOOD: no outer reference, explicit Looper, callbacks removed in onDestroy\nprivate final Handler handler = new Handler(Looper.getMainLooper());\n\n@Override protected void onDestroy() {\n    handler.removeCallbacksAndMessages(null);\n    super.onDestroy();\n}\n```\n\n- Fragments observe with `getViewLifecycleOwner()` and null the binding in `onDestroyView()`.\n- Listeners registered in `onStart` / `onResume` are removed in the matching `onStop` / `onPause`.\n\n## Threading\n\n- Background work belongs in a ViewModel or repository using an `ExecutorService` that is shut down in\n  `onCleared()`, not in the Activity.\n- `LiveData.setValue` on the main thread only; `postValue` from background threads. `postValue` coalesces:\n  if called twice quickly only the last value is delivered. Flag it when every value matters.\n- Shared mutable fields accessed from multiple threads are `volatile`, guarded by `synchronized`, or use\n  `java.util.concurrent.atomic` types. Check-then-act on a `ConcurrentHashMap` still needs `computeIfAbsent`\n  or similar.\n\n```java\n// BAD: interrupt swallowed, thread cannot be stopped cleanly\ntry { Thread.sleep(1000); } catch (InterruptedException e) { }\n\n// GOOD\ntry {\n    Thread.sleep(1000);\n} catch (InterruptedException e) {\n    Thread.currentThread().interrupt();\n    return;\n}\n```\n\n## Resources\n\n```java\n// BAD\nCursor c = db.query(...);\nwhile (c.moveToNext()) { ... }\n\n// GOOD\ntry (Cursor c = db.query(...)) {\n    while (c.moveToNext()) { ... }\n}\n```\n\n## Value objects and collections\n\n- `equals` and `hashCode` are overridden together, over the same fields.\n- `DiffUtil.ItemCallback.areItemsTheSame` compares ids; `areContentsTheSame` compares content\n  (usually via `equals`).\n- Getters do not expose internal mutable collections:\n\n```java\n// BAD\npublic List<Item> getItems() { return items; }\n\n// GOOD\npublic List<Item> getItems() { return Collections.unmodifiableList(items); }\n```\n\n## Kotlin interop\n\n- Public API used from Kotlin carries nullability annotations. Prefer JSpecify\n  (`org.jspecify.annotations.Nullable`, `@NullMarked` on the package) or AndroidX `@Nullable` / `@NonNull`.\n  Without them Kotlin sees platform types and null checks disappear.\n- Static utilities that Kotlin callers use often are candidates for migration, but migration is out of scope\n  for a review unless the MR is about it.\n\n## Testing\n\n- JUnit 4 or 5 with Mockito for unit tests; `InstantTaskExecutorRule` when LiveData is involved.\n- Espresso tests use `IdlingResource` or test executors, never `Thread.sleep`.\n",
    "references/kotlin.md":
        "# Kotlin and Android review reference\n\nLoad this when the change contains `.kt` or `.kts` files. Each item lists what to look for and the usual severity.\n\n## Quick scan\n\nSearch the diff for these first. Each hit needs a closer look, not an automatic finding.\n\n| Pattern | Why | Usual severity |\n|---|---|---|\n| `GlobalScope`, `CoroutineScope(` in a class with no `cancel()` | Unowned work, leaks | Major |\n| `runBlocking` outside tests and `main` | Blocks the calling thread, ANR on main | Major |\n| `runCatching {` around suspend calls | Swallows `CancellationException` | Major |\n| `catch (e: Exception)` / `catch (e: Throwable)` in suspend code | Same, unless it rethrows cancellation | Major |\n| `Dispatchers.IO` / `Dispatchers.Default` hardcoded in a class | Untestable, hidden threading | Minor |\n| `!!` | Crash on unexpected null | Major if input is external |\n| `lateinit var` read before guaranteed init | `UninitializedPropertyAccessException` | Major |\n| `collectAsState()` | Collects while app is backgrounded | Minor |\n| `launchWhenStarted`, `launchWhenResumed` | Deprecated, suspends instead of cancelling | Minor |\n| `fallbackToDestructiveMigration` | Deletes user data on schema change | Blocker in release builds |\n| `allowMainThreadQueries` | Jank and ANR | Major |\n| `registerReceiver(` without an export flag | Crash when targeting Android 14+ | Blocker |\n| `Log.d(` / `println(` with tokens, emails, device IDs | Personal data in logs | Major, see `mobile-security` |\n\n## Coroutines and Flow\n\n- Every `launch` and `async` runs in a scope tied to a lifecycle: `viewModelScope`, `lifecycleScope`,\n  or an injected application scope for work that must outlive a screen.\n- Prefer `suspend` functions over functions that launch internally and return `Unit`. The caller owns the scope.\n- Injected dispatchers, so tests can replace them.\n- `CancellationException` is rethrown. A generic `catch` in suspend code must rethrow it:\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD\ntry { repo.refresh() } catch (e: Exception) { log(e) }\n\n// GOOD\ntry {\n    repo.refresh()\n} catch (e: CancellationException) {\n    throw e\n} catch (e: IOException) {\n    log(e)\n}\n```\n\n- Mutable state is private; the public type is read-only.\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: anyone can push state, and read-modify-write races\nval state = MutableStateFlow(UiState())\nfun select(id: String) { state.value = state.value.copy(selected = id) }\n\n// GOOD\nprivate val _state = MutableStateFlow(UiState())\nval state: StateFlow<UiState> = _state.asStateFlow()\nfun select(id: String) = _state.update { it.copy(selected = id) }\n```\n\n- Cold flows exposed to UI are converted once, not per collector:\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: new upstream per collector, restarts on rotation\nval items: Flow<List<Item>> get() = repo.observe()\n\n// GOOD\nval items: StateFlow<List<Item>> = repo.observe()\n    .stateIn(viewModelScope, SharingStarted.WhileSubscribed(5_000), emptyList())\n```\n\n- `flowOn` changes the upstream context only. Check it is placed after the operators that need it.\n- One-off events: prefer modelling them as state the UI consumes and acknowledges. If a `Channel` is used,\n  check it is collected with `repeatOnLifecycle` so events are not delivered to a stopped UI.\n- Fragments collect with `viewLifecycleOwner.lifecycleScope` plus `repeatOnLifecycle(Lifecycle.State.STARTED)`,\n  never `lifecycleScope` alone.\n\n## Jetpack Compose\n\n- Composables do not perform side effects in the body. Use `LaunchedEffect`, `DisposableEffect`,\n  or event callbacks.\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: runs on every recomposition\n@Composable fun Screen(vm: ScreenViewModel) {\n    vm.load()\n}\n\n// GOOD\n@Composable fun Screen(vm: ScreenViewModel) {\n    LaunchedEffect(Unit) { vm.load() }\n}\n```\n\n- `LaunchedEffect` keys match the inputs the effect reads. `LaunchedEffect(Unit)` that reads a changing\n  parameter is a bug; use the parameter as key or `rememberUpdatedState`.\n- `remember` keys include every input of the calculation, otherwise the cached value is stale.\n- High-frequency state (scroll, animation) is read in a lambda modifier or `derivedStateOf`:\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: recomposes on every scroll pixel\nval showFab = listState.firstVisibleItemIndex > 0\n\n// GOOD\nval showFab by remember { derivedStateOf { listState.firstVisibleItemIndex > 0 } }\n```\n\n- Stability: with strong skipping (default since Kotlin 2.0.20), unstable parameters are compared by\n  instance. A list rebuilt on every recomposition defeats skipping. Hoist it, `remember` it, or use\n  `kotlinx.collections.immutable`.\n- Lazy lists use `key` and, for mixed rows, `contentType`.\n- Reusable composables take `modifier: Modifier = Modifier` as the first optional parameter and apply it\n  to the root element only.\n- Composables below the screen level receive state and lambdas, not the ViewModel.\n- `rememberSaveable` for user input that must survive process death and rotation.\n\n## Lifecycle and Android components\n\n- No `Activity`, `Fragment`, `View` or `Context` (other than application context) in singletons, companion\n  objects or ViewModels.\n- Fragment view bindings are cleared in `onDestroyView`.\n- Broadcast receivers registered at runtime use `ContextCompat.registerReceiver` with\n  `RECEIVER_EXPORTED` or `RECEIVER_NOT_EXPORTED`, and are unregistered.\n- Foreground services declare `android:foregroundServiceType` and the matching permission (required when\n  targeting Android 14+). Check the type matches what the service actually does.\n- Deferrable background work uses WorkManager, not a service or a coroutine in `Application`.\n- New permissions in the manifest are justified in the MR and requested at the point of use.\n\n## Hilt and dependency injection\n\n- `@ApplicationContext` for context in singletons.\n- Scope annotations match lifetime: no `@Singleton` holding screen state.\n- No service locator calls (`EntryPoints.get`) inside business logic.\n\n## Room and persistence\n\n- DAO functions are `suspend` or return `Flow`.\n- Every schema version bump has a `Migration` or `AutoMigration`, and `exportSchema = true`.\n- Transactions wrap multi-step writes (`@Transaction` or `withTransaction`).\n\n## Error handling and nullability\n\n- `!!` only where the invariant is local and obvious; otherwise `requireNotNull(x) { \"reason\" }` or a\n  safe call with a fallback.\n- Network and IO results are mapped to a domain error type the UI can render, not raw exceptions.\n- `Result` or sealed result types are not mixed with thrown exceptions in the same layer.\n\n## Testing\n\n- `runTest` for coroutines; `StandardTestDispatcher(testScheduler)` injected; `Dispatchers.setMain` in a\n  rule or extension when `viewModelScope` is used.\n- Turbine (`flow.test { ... }`) for Flow assertions instead of `toList()` with timing hacks.\n- MockK: `coEvery` / `coVerify` for suspend functions. Prefer fakes for repositories.\n- Compose UI tests find nodes by semantics (text, content description, test tag), not by position.\n\n## Build and dependencies\n\n- New dependencies are added to `libs.versions.toml`, not hardcoded in module build files.\n- KSP preferred over kapt for new annotation processors.\n- R8 keep rules added for new reflection, serialization or JNI code.\n- `minSdk`, `targetSdk` or `compileSdk` changes are called out explicitly in the MR.\n",
    "references/swift.md":
        "# Swift and iOS review reference\n\nLoad this when the change contains `.swift` files. Assume Swift 6 language mode with strict concurrency\nunless the project settings say otherwise.\n\n## Quick scan\n\n| Pattern | Why | Usual severity |\n|---|---|---|\n| `!` force unwrap, `try!`, `as!` | Crash on unexpected input | Major, Blocker on external data |\n| Implicitly unwrapped `var x: T!` outside IBOutlets | Crash if read early | Minor |\n| Escaping closure using `self` without `[weak self]`, stored by `self` | Retain cycle | Major |\n| `unowned self` | Crash if the owner is gone first | Major unless lifetime is guaranteed |\n| `@unchecked Sendable` | Silences data-race checking | Major unless a lock guards every field |\n| `nonisolated(unsafe)` | Same | Major unless justified |\n| `DispatchQueue.main.async` inside `async` code | Mixes models, loses isolation checking | Minor |\n| `Task.detached` | Drops actor context and priority | Minor, needs a reason |\n| `DispatchSemaphore.wait()` / `.sync` on main | Deadlock or hang | Blocker |\n| `@ObservedObject var model = Model()` | Model recreated on every parent redraw | Major |\n| `ForEach(..., id: \\.self)` over non-unique values | Wrong rows update, crashes on duplicates | Major |\n| `AnyView` in lists | Defeats diffing | Minor |\n| `print(` with tokens, emails, device IDs | Personal data in logs | Major, see `mobile-security` |\n\n## Concurrency\n\n- UI-facing models are `@MainActor`. View models built with `@Observable` are usually `@MainActor final class`.\n- Types crossing isolation boundaries are `Sendable`. Prefer value types; make reference types final with\n  immutable state or protect them with an actor.\n- Actor reentrancy: state read before an `await` may have changed after it.\n\n```swift\n// BAD: two concurrent calls both see nil and both download\nactor ImageCache {\n    var images: [URL: Image] = [:]\n    func image(for url: URL) async throws -> Image {\n        if let cached = images[url] { return cached }\n        let image = try await download(url)\n        images[url] = image\n        return image\n    }\n}\n\n// GOOD: cache the in-flight task, not just the result\nactor ImageCache {\n    private var tasks: [URL: Task<Image, Error>] = [:]\n    func image(for url: URL) async throws -> Image {\n        if let task = tasks[url] { return try await task.value }\n        let task = Task { try await download(url) }\n        tasks[url] = task\n        return try await task.value\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- Long-running `for await` loops inside a `Task` stored by a class capture `self` until the loop ends,\n  so `deinit` never runs. Use `[weak self]` inside the loop body, or tie the work to `.task` in the view.\n\n```swift\n// BAD: self is retained by the task forever, deinit never cancels it\ntask = Task {\n    for await value in stream { self.handle(value) }\n}\n\n// GOOD\ntask = Task { [weak self] in\n    for await value in stream {\n        guard let self else { return }\n        self.handle(value)\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- Cancellation is checked in long loops (`try Task.checkCancellation()` or `Task.isCancelled`).\n- `withTaskGroup` / `withThrowingTaskGroup` instead of manually spawning many `Task {}` and joining.\n\n## Memory\n\n- Delegates are `weak var delegate: SomeDelegate?` with a class-constrained protocol.\n- Combine: `sink` closures capture `self` weakly; `AnyCancellable` stored and released with the owner.\n- Block-based `NotificationCenter` observers are removed, or replaced by\n  `for await _ in NotificationCenter.default.notifications(named: ...)` inside `.task`.\n\n## SwiftUI\n\n- Ownership: `@State private var model = Model()` for `@Observable` models the view creates;\n  `@StateObject` for legacy `ObservableObject`. Passed-in models are plain properties (`@Observable`) or\n  `@ObservedObject` (legacy).\n- `body` is cheap and pure: no formatting with new `DateFormatter()` per call, no I/O, no sorting large arrays.\n- Async work starts in `.task` or `.task(id:)`, which cancels on disappear or id change.\n- Identity: `ForEach` uses stable `Identifiable` ids. `.id(x)` on a view resets its state when `x` changes;\n  check that is intended.\n- `NavigationStack` with a typed path for programmatic navigation; no deprecated `NavigationView` in new code.\n- Accessibility: images that convey meaning have `.accessibilityLabel`; decorative images use\n  `Image(decorative:)`; custom controls set `.accessibilityAddTraits(.isButton)`; text uses Dynamic Type\n  styles, not fixed sizes.\n\n## Errors and safety\n\n- `try?` only where failure is truly irrelevant; otherwise `do / catch` with a user-visible state.\n- Typed throws (`throws(MyError)`) where the error set is closed and callers switch over it.\n- `guard let` early exits instead of nested `if let` pyramids.\n- `Codable` models tolerate missing optional fields from the server (`decodeIfPresent` or optional properties).\n\n## Testing\n\n- Swift Testing (`@Test`, `#expect`, `#require`) for new tests; XCTest is fine in existing suites.\n- Async tests `await` the operation. In XCTest async contexts use `await fulfillment(of:timeout:)`,\n  not `wait(for:)`.\n- No `sleep` in tests. Inject clocks (`any Clock<Duration>`) or schedulers.\n- Protocols at boundaries (network, storage) so fakes can be injected.\n\n## Project and dependencies\n\n- New SPM packages pinned to a version range, `Package.resolved` committed for apps.\n- New `Info.plist` keys, entitlements, background modes or privacy usage strings are called out in the MR.\n- New required-reason API usage is reflected in `PrivacyInfo.xcprivacy`.\n",
  },
  "mobile-design-patterns": {
    "SKILL.md":
        "---\nname: mobile-design-patterns\ndescription: \"Chooses, implements and reviews design patterns in Kotlin, Java, Swift and Dart for Android, iOS and Flutter apps: Repository, Factory, Builder versus named and default arguments, Singleton versus dependency injection (Hilt, Riverpod, initializer injection), Strategy, Observer with Kotlin Flow and StateFlow, Swift Combine, AsyncSequence and Observation, Dart Stream, Adapter, Decorator, Facade, State machine with sealed states and explicit transitions (BLE connection state), Command (serialized GATT queue, undo), Coordinator and Router navigation, Result and typed errors (Kotlin sealed types, Swift typed throws, Dart sealed classes), and Delegation (Kotlin by, weak Swift delegates). Includes a problem-to-pattern selection table and over-engineering warnings. Use when picking a pattern, refactoring god classes or flag-driven state, wrapping SDKs, modelling lifecycles, or when a design feels over-abstracted.\"\nlicense: MIT\nmetadata:\n  author: Ahmad Hussain\n  version: \"1.0.0\"\n  pack: mobile-dev-kit\n---\n\n# Mobile design patterns\n\nPick the smallest structure that solves a real problem in Android, iOS or Flutter code, implement it\nidiomatically for the language, and remove patterns that only add indirection.\n\n## When to use\n\n- Structuring a new feature, data source, SDK wrapper, device protocol or navigation flow.\n- Refactoring long `when` / `switch` chains, boolean state flags, god classes or copy-pasted wrappers.\n- Reviewing code full of `*Manager`, `*Factory`, `*Builder`, `*Impl` names and deciding which ones earn their place.\n- Modelling a lifecycle (BLE connection, upload, onboarding, checkout) as an explicit state machine.\n- Deciding between thrown exceptions and typed results at a layer boundary.\n\nNot for: whole-app architecture (use `mobile-mvvm`, `mobile-mvi`, `mobile-mvc`) or security design\n(use `mobile-security`).\n\n## Workflow\n\n1. Write the problem in one sentence before naming a pattern: \"X changes independently of Y because Z\".\n   No sentence, no pattern.\n2. Check whether the language already solves it: named and default arguments, `by` delegation, function\n   types, sealed types, `Flow` / `AsyncSequence` / `Stream`, protocol extensions. Prefer the language feature.\n3. Find the problem in the selection table and read the matching rule, including its \"Skip when\" line.\n4. Load the language reference for full implementations: [Kotlin](references/kotlin.md),\n   [Swift](references/swift.md), [Dart](references/dart.md), [Java](references/java.md).\n5. Implement the smallest version. Add an interface or protocol only for a second implementation,\n   a test seam or a module boundary.\n6. Wire collaborators through constructor injection, never through globals.\n7. Unit test what the pattern isolates: transitions, strategies, mappers, decorators, command ordering.\n8. When reviewing, name the cost of a misused pattern (extra files, indirection, test burden) and show the\n   simpler alternative.\n\n## Pattern selection\n\n| Problem | Pattern | Mobile example | Skip when |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| UI must not know if data comes from network, database or cache | Repository | Room or Core Data as source of truth, API refreshes it | One read-only source, no mapping, no caching |\n| Concrete type depends on runtime input | Factory | Exporter per format, message type from JSON | Only one type, or DI already builds it |\n| Many optional parameters | Named / default args (Builder only in Java) | Notification spec, request options | Kotlin, Swift, Dart: almost always skip Builder |\n| One shared instance | DI scope (`@Singleton`, app container) | HTTP client, database, BLE manager | Never hand-roll a mutable global |\n| Interchangeable algorithm | Strategy, often just a function | Retry backoff, sort order, image compression | Only one algorithm and no test need |\n| React to changing values | Observer via platform streams | Connection state, database queries | One-shot value: use a suspend / async function |\n| Foreign API does not fit your interface | Adapter | Callback SDK to suspend / async, DTO to domain | You own both sides: change the interface |\n| Add caching, logging, retry or metrics without editing a class | Decorator | Timed or caching repository | It belongs on every call: put it in the class or an HTTP interceptor |\n| Multi-step subsystem used from many places | Facade | `DeviceSession.open()` hides connect, MTU, discovery, notifications | It starts holding UI state: split it |\n| Behaviour depends on phase; some transitions are illegal | State machine | BLE connection, upload, onboarding | Two states a single `Boolean` models correctly |\n| Operations must be queued, serialized, retried or undone | Command | GATT operation queue, undoable edits | Fire-and-forget call with no ordering needs |\n| Flow logic spread across screens | Coordinator / Router | Onboarding, deep links, auth gate | One push from list to detail |\n| Expected failures are part of the API | Result / sealed errors / typed throws | Network, validation, pairing errors | Programmer errors: let them crash |\n| Reuse behaviour or call back to an owner | Delegation | Kotlin `by`, `CBCentralManagerDelegate` | Inheritance is not involved and a lambda suffices |\n\n## Rules\n\n### 1. An abstraction needs a reason\n\nKotlin:\n```kotlin\n// BAD: interface + Impl with one implementation, no fake, same module\ninterface DateFormatter { fun format(i: Instant): String }\nclass DateFormatterImpl : DateFormatter { ... }\n\n// GOOD: a plain class until a second implementation, a test fake or a module boundary appears\nclass DateFormatter(private val zone: ZoneId) { fun format(i: Instant): String = ... }\n```\n\nValid reasons for an interface or protocol: a fake in ViewModel tests, a domain module that must not see\nthe data module, multiple real implementations, a platform split (KMP `expect` / `actual`).\n\n### 2. Repository: one source of truth, domain types out\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: ViewModel talks to Retrofit and Room directly and exposes DTOs\nclass DevicesViewModel(private val api: DeviceApi, private val dao: DeviceDao) : ViewModel()\n\n// GOOD: database is the source of truth, network only refreshes it, mapping happens here\nclass DeviceRepository @Inject constructor(private val api: DeviceApi, private val dao: DeviceDao) {\n    fun observeDevices(): Flow<List<Device>> = dao.observeAll().map { rows -> rows.map { it.toDomain() } }\n    suspend fun refresh() = dao.upsertAll(api.devices().map { it.toEntity() })\n}\n```\n\nSkip when: one repository per table that forwards DAO calls 1:1. Group by feature or aggregate instead.\n\n### 3. Factory: one place decides which type to build\n\nKotlin:\n```kotlin\n// BAD: construction logic duplicated at every call site\nval exporter = if (format == \"pdf\") PdfExporter(fonts) else CsvExporter(separator = ';')\n\n// GOOD\nclass ExporterFactory @Inject constructor(private val fonts: FontProvider) {\n    fun create(format: ExportFormat): Exporter = when (format) {\n        ExportFormat.Pdf -> PdfExporter(fonts)\n        ExportFormat.Csv -> CsvExporter(separator = ';')\n    }\n}\n```\n\nDart (factory constructor with pattern matching on untrusted JSON):\n```dart\nsealed class Message {\n  const Message();\n  factory Message.fromJson(Map<String, Object?> json) => switch (json) {\n        {'type': 'text', 'body': String body} => TextMessage(body),\n        {'type': 'image', 'url': String url} => ImageMessage(Uri.parse(url)),\n        _ => throw FormatException('Unknown message type: \${json['type']}'),\n      };\n}\n```\n\nSkip when: there is one concrete type, or the DI container already provides it. No factory of factories.\n\n### 4. Builder: use named and default arguments instead\n\nKotlin:\n```kotlin\n// BAD: hand-written builder in a language with named arguments\nval spec = NotificationSpec.Builder().title(\"Low battery\").channel(\"alerts\").build()\n\n// GOOD\ndata class NotificationSpec(val title: String, val channel: String = \"default\", val silent: Boolean = false)\nval spec = NotificationSpec(title = \"Low battery\", channel = \"alerts\")\n```\n\nSwift: a struct with defaulted `var` properties gets a memberwise initializer with default arguments.\nDart: `const NotificationSpec({required this.title, this.channel = 'default'})` plus `copyWith`.\nJava: a Builder is the right tool; see [Java](references/java.md).\n\n### 5. Singleton: scope it in DI, do not hand-roll it\n\nKotlin:\n```kotlin\n// BAD: global mutable state, leaks Context, cannot be replaced in tests\nobject Session { lateinit var context: Context; var token: String? = null }\n\n// GOOD\n@Singleton\nclass SessionStore @Inject constructor(@ApplicationContext private val context: Context)\n```\n\nSwift:\n```swift\n// BAD: hidden dependency\nfinal class FeedModel { func load() async throws { items = try await APIClient.shared.feed() } }\n\n// GOOD: injected, `.shared` may be the default only at the composition root\nfinal class FeedModel {\n    private let api: FeedAPI\n    init(api: FeedAPI) { self.api = api }\n}\n```\n\nFine to keep: stateless Kotlin `object` utilities and `data object` cases of sealed types.\n\n### 6. Strategy: often just a function\n\nKotlin:\n```kotlin\n// BAD: algorithm switches on a mode flag\nfun delayFor(attempt: Int, mode: String): Long = if (mode == \"exp\") 500L shl attempt else 1_000L\n\n// GOOD: the policy is a value the caller injects\nfun interface BackoffPolicy { fun delayMillis(attempt: Int): Long }\n\nval exponential = BackoffPolicy { attempt -> 500L shl attempt.coerceAtMost(6) }\nval fixed = BackoffPolicy { 1_000L }\nclass Uploader(private val backoff: BackoffPolicy)\n```\n\nSwift: `let backoff: (Int) -> Duration`. Dart: `typedef BackoffPolicy = Duration Function(int attempt);`.\nSkip when: only one algorithm exists and tests do not need to swap it.\n\n### 7. Observer: use the platform stream type, not listener lists\n\nKotlin:\n```kotlin\n// BAD: listeners never removed, callbacks on arbitrary threads\nprivate val listeners = mutableListOf<(ConnState) -> Unit>()\n\n// GOOD\nprivate val _state = MutableStateFlow<ConnState>(ConnState.Disconnected)\nval state: StateFlow<ConnState> = _state.asStateFlow()\n```\n\nSwift: `@Observable` for UI models, `AsyncStream` for single-consumer event streams, Combine\n`CurrentValueSubject` when several subscribers need the latest value.\nDart: `Stream` from a `StreamController.broadcast()` closed by its owner, or `ValueNotifier` for UI.\nSkip when: the value is produced once. Return it from a suspend or async function.\n\n### 8. Adapter: wrap foreign APIs at the edge\n\nKotlin:\n```kotlin\n// BAD: SDK callback types leak into the ViewModel\nprinter.print(job, object : PrintCallback { ... })\n\n// GOOD: one adapter turns the callback API into a cancellable suspend function\nsuspend fun LegacyPrinter.printAwait(job: PrintJob): PrintResult =\n    suspendCancellableCoroutine { cont ->\n        val handle = print(job, object : PrintCallback {\n            override fun onDone(result: PrintResult) = cont.resume(result)\n            override fun onError(error: Throwable) = cont.resumeWithException(error)\n        })\n        cont.invokeOnCancellation { handle.cancel() }\n    }\n```\n\nSwift: `withCheckedThrowingContinuation`, resumed exactly once. Dart: a `Completer`.\nDTO-to-domain mappers are adapters too; keep them in the data layer.\n\n### 9. Decorator: add cross-cutting behaviour by wrapping\n\nKotlin:\n```kotlin\n// BAD: timing code copy-pasted into every method, or a subclass of the concrete client\n// GOOD: delegate everything, override only what changes\nclass TimedDeviceApi(private val inner: DeviceApi, private val metrics: Metrics) : DeviceApi by inner {\n    override suspend fun firmwareInfo(id: String): FirmwareInfo {\n        val mark = TimeSource.Monotonic.markNow()\n        try { return inner.firmwareInfo(id) } finally { metrics.record(\"firmwareInfo\", mark.elapsedNow()) }\n    }\n}\n```\n\nSkip when: the concern is HTTP-level (use an OkHttp `Interceptor` or `URLSession` configuration), or the\nstack of decorators has an order nobody documented.\n\n### 10. Facade: hide a multi-step subsystem behind intent-level calls\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: every screen repeats connect -> request MTU -> discover services -> enable notifications\n// GOOD\nclass DeviceSession @Inject constructor(private val gatt: GattClient, private val queue: GattQueue) {\n    suspend fun open(address: String): Result<Unit> = ...   // runs the whole sequence in order\n    fun telemetry(): Flow<Telemetry> = ...\n    suspend fun close() = ...\n}\n```\n\nSkip when: the facade starts holding UI state, analytics and persistence. Split it by capability.\n\n### 11. State machine: sealed states, explicit transitions\n\nKotlin:\n```kotlin\n// BAD: flags that can contradict each other (connected && connecting)\nvar isConnecting = false; var isConnected = false; var lastError: Throwable? = null\n\n// GOOD: states carry their own data; one pure function decides transitions\nsealed interface ConnState {\n    data object Disconnected : ConnState\n    data class Connecting(val attempt: Int) : ConnState\n    data class Connected(val mtu: Int) : ConnState\n    data class Failed(val error: ConnError) : ConnState\n}\n\nfun reduce(state: ConnState, event: ConnEvent): ConnState = when (state) {\n    is ConnState.Connecting -> when (event) {\n        is ConnEvent.LinkUp -> ConnState.Connected(event.mtu)\n        is ConnEvent.LinkLost -> if (state.attempt < 3) ConnState.Connecting(state.attempt + 1) else ConnState.Failed(event.error)\n        else -> state // illegal here: ignore and log\n    }\n    // ... one branch per state\n}\n\n// GATT callbacks arrive on binder threads: funnel every event through one atomic update\nfun onEvent(event: ConnEvent) = _state.update { reduce(it, event) }\n```\n\nSwift: `enum ConnectionState` with associated values and a `mutating func handle(_ event:)` inside an actor.\nDart: `sealed class` plus an exhaustive `switch`. Full versions with tests are in the references.\nSkip when: two states that one `Boolean` models without contradictions.\n\n### 12. Command: operations as values, executed in order\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: Android GATT allows one outstanding operation; the second call is rejected while the first runs\ngatt.writeCharacteristic(config, payload, BluetoothGattCharacteristic.WRITE_TYPE_DEFAULT)\ngatt.readCharacteristic(status)\n\n// GOOD: commands queued and run one at a time by a single consumer\nsealed interface GattCommand {\n    class Write(val characteristic: UUID, val value: ByteArray) : GattCommand\n    class Read(val characteristic: UUID) : GattCommand\n}\n\nclass GattQueue(scope: CoroutineScope, private val executor: GattExecutor) {\n    private val commands = Channel<GattCommand>(Channel.UNLIMITED)\n    init { scope.launch { for (command in commands) executor.executeAndAwait(command) } }\n    fun enqueue(command: GattCommand) { commands.trySend(command) }\n}\n```\n\nAlso fits: undo / redo (`apply` and `revert`), offline actions persisted and replayed.\nSkip when: calls are independent and fire-and-forget.\n\n### 13. Coordinator / Router: views raise intents, one owner decides the flow\n\nSwift:\n```swift\n// BAD: each view builds the next screen and its dependencies\nNavigationLink { PairingView(service: PairingService(api: .shared)) } label: { Text(\"Continue\") }\n\n// GOOD: typed routes, one owner of the path\nenum OnboardingRoute: Hashable { case permissions, pairing, done }\n\n@MainActor @Observable\nfinal class OnboardingCoordinator {\n    var path: [OnboardingRoute] = []\n    func permissionsGranted() { path.append(.pairing) }\n    func paired() { path.append(.done) }\n}\n```\n\nKotlin (Compose): screens take callbacks (`onDeviceClick: (String) -> Unit`); the nav host owns the back\nstack. Dart: `go_router` with `redirect` for auth gates. Skip when: the app has a handful of linear screens.\n\n### 14. Expected errors are typed results; bugs still crash\n\nKotlin:\n```kotlin\n// BAD: every failure collapses to null; runCatching also swallows CancellationException\nsuspend fun pair(id: String): Device? = runCatching { api.pair(id) }.getOrNull()\n\n// GOOD\nsealed interface PairResult {\n    data class Paired(val device: Device) : PairResult\n    data object Timeout : PairResult\n    data class Rejected(val reason: String) : PairResult\n}\n```\n\nSwift: `func pair(id: String) async throws(PairingError) -> Device` when the error set is closed.\nDart: `sealed class Result<T>` with `Ok` and `Err`, matched with an exhaustive `switch`.\nDo not mix thrown exceptions and result types in the same layer.\n\n### 15. Delegation: compose behaviour, keep back-references weak\n\nKotlin:\n```kotlin\n// BAD: inheriting a concrete class to reuse one method\nclass AuditedStore : PrefsStore() { override fun put(k: String, v: String) { audit(k); super.put(k, v) } }\n\n// GOOD: class delegation through an interface\nclass AuditedStore(private val inner: KeyValueStore, private val audit: Audit) : KeyValueStore by inner {\n    override fun put(key: String, value: String) { audit.record(key); inner.put(key, value) }\n}\n```\n\nSwift:\n```swift\n// BAD: strong delegate, owner <-> child retain cycle\nvar delegate: ScannerDelegate?\n\n// GOOD: class-constrained protocol, weak reference\nprotocol ScannerDelegate: AnyObject { func scanner(_ s: Scanner, didFind device: Device) }\nweak var delegate: ScannerDelegate?\n```\n\n## Anti-patterns\n\n- `interface Foo` + `FooImpl` with one implementation and no fake, in the same module.\n- A pattern chosen before the problem was stated. \"We might need it later\" is not a reason.\n- `*Manager` and `*Helper` classes that mix networking, persistence and UI state: a god object, not a facade.\n- Builders in Kotlin, Swift or Dart; hand-rolled singletons with `lateinit` or `static var shared` state.\n- Use cases that only forward one repository call, added \"for consistency\".\n- Boolean flags modelling a lifecycle, or a state machine where transitions happen in many places.\n- Listener lists, `NotificationCenter` or event buses used where a typed stream would do.\n- Generic `BaseRepository<T>`, `BaseViewModel<S, E, A>` hierarchies that every feature must bend to.\n- Strategy hierarchies for an algorithm that has exactly one implementation.\n- Wrapping every third-party type \"in case we switch libraries\" when nothing is planned.\n- Decorator chains or command queues with undocumented ordering assumptions.\n\n## Checklist\n\n- [ ] Problem stated in one sentence before the pattern was chosen\n- [ ] Language feature considered first (named args, `by`, function types, sealed types, streams)\n- [ ] Every interface or protocol has a second implementation, a test fake or a module boundary\n- [ ] Repositories expose domain types and a single source of truth\n- [ ] Shared instances come from DI scopes, not mutable globals\n- [ ] Observables use `Flow` / `AsyncSequence` / Combine / `Stream`, and subscriptions are scoped\n- [ ] Foreign APIs adapted at the edge; callbacks resume exactly once and support cancellation\n- [ ] State machines: sealed states, one transition function, illegal transitions handled, events serialized\n- [ ] Commands that touch serialized resources (GATT, files) run one at a time\n- [ ] Navigation decisions live in one owner per flow\n- [ ] Expected errors typed; cancellation not swallowed; programmer errors not caught\n- [ ] Swift delegates are `weak` with `AnyObject` protocols\n- [ ] Unit tests cover transitions, strategies, mappers and decorator behaviour\n\n## Related skills\n\n- `mobile-mvvm`, `mobile-mvi`, `mobile-mvc` -- the architecture these patterns plug into\n- `kotlin-android`, `java-android`, `swift-ios`, `dart-flutter` -- language idioms behind each implementation\n- `mobile-code-review` -- reviewing pattern misuse as part of a change\n- `mobile-security` -- when a pattern wraps storage, crypto or network trust decisions\n",
    "references/dart.md":
        "# Dart and Flutter pattern implementations\n\nLoad this when implementing or reviewing patterns in Dart. Assumes Dart 3 (sealed classes, records,\npatterns, class modifiers) and Flutter. DI examples use Riverpod; the patterns are the same with\n`get_it` or plain constructor injection.\n\n## Repository\n\n```dart\n// Interface because widget and notifier tests override it with a fake.\nabstract interface class DeviceRepository {\n  Stream<List<Device>> watchDevices();\n  Future<void> refresh();\n}\n\nfinal class OfflineFirstDeviceRepository implements DeviceRepository {\n  OfflineFirstDeviceRepository(this._api, this._dao);\n\n  final DeviceApi _api;\n  final DeviceDao _dao;\n\n  @override\n  Stream<List<Device>> watchDevices() =>\n      _dao.watchAll().map((rows) => [for (final row in rows) row.toDomain()]);\n\n  @override\n  Future<void> refresh() async {\n    final dtos = await _api.devices();\n    await _dao.upsertAll([for (final dto in dtos) dto.toRow()]);\n  }\n}\n```\n\n- The UI listens to the local database stream; `refresh()` only writes into it.\n- JSON DTOs and database rows never reach widgets.\n\n## Factory\n\n```dart\nsealed class Message {\n  const Message();\n\n  factory Message.fromJson(Map<String, Object?> json) => switch (json) {\n        {'type': 'text', 'body': String body} => TextMessage(body),\n        {'type': 'image', 'url': String url} => ImageMessage(Uri.parse(url)),\n        _ => throw FormatException('Unknown message type: \${json['type']}'),\n      };\n}\n\nfinal class TextMessage extends Message {\n  const TextMessage(this.body);\n  final String body;\n}\n\nfinal class ImageMessage extends Message {\n  const ImageMessage(this.url);\n  final Uri url;\n}\n```\n\n- Map patterns check key presence and value types, so malformed JSON fails at the edge instead of\n  with a cast error deep in the UI.\n- Named constructors (`Device.placeholder()`) cover simple alternative constructions without a factory.\n\n## Builder replaced by named parameters\n\n```dart\nfinal class ScanOptions {\n  const ScanOptions({\n    this.timeout = const Duration(seconds: 10),\n    this.serviceFilter,\n    this.lowLatency = false,\n  });\n\n  final Duration timeout;\n  final String? serviceFilter;\n  final bool lowLatency;\n\n  ScanOptions copyWith({Duration? timeout, String? serviceFilter, bool? lowLatency}) => ScanOptions(\n        timeout: timeout ?? this.timeout,\n        serviceFilter: serviceFilter ?? this.serviceFilter,\n        lowLatency: lowLatency ?? this.lowLatency,\n      );\n}\n\nconst options = ScanOptions(lowLatency: true);\n```\n\n- A hand-written `copyWith` cannot reset a nullable field to `null`. Code generation such as `freezed`\n  handles that, or use a sentinel.\n\n## Singleton replaced by DI scopes\n\n```dart\nfinal httpClientProvider = Provider<http.Client>((ref) {\n  final client = http.Client();\n  ref.onDispose(client.close);\n  return client;\n});\n\nfinal deviceRepositoryProvider = Provider<DeviceRepository>(\n  (ref) => OfflineFirstDeviceRepository(ref.watch(deviceApiProvider), ref.watch(deviceDaoProvider)),\n);\n\n// Test\nfinal container = ProviderContainer(\n  overrides: [deviceRepositoryProvider.overrideWithValue(FakeDeviceRepository())],\n);\naddTearDown(container.dispose);\n```\n\n- No top-level mutable variables holding clients or tokens.\n- `late final` globals initialized in `main()` are a service locator in disguise; tests cannot replace them.\n\n## Strategy\n\n```dart\ntypedef BackoffPolicy = Duration Function(int attempt);\n\nDuration fixedBackoff(int attempt) => const Duration(seconds: 1);\nDuration exponentialBackoff(int attempt) => Duration(milliseconds: 500 * (1 << (attempt > 6 ? 6 : attempt)));\n\nfinal class Uploader {\n  Uploader({BackoffPolicy backoff = exponentialBackoff}) : _backoff = backoff;\n  final BackoffPolicy _backoff;\n}\n```\n\nA `typedef` of a function type covers most strategies. Use an interface when the strategy has several methods.\n\n## Observer\n\n```dart\nfinal class ConnectionMonitor {\n  final _controller = StreamController<ConnState>.broadcast();\n  ConnState _state = const Disconnected();\n\n  ConnState get state => _state;\n  Stream<ConnState> get states => _controller.stream;\n\n  void _emit(ConnState next) {\n    _state = next;\n    _controller.add(next);\n  }\n\n  Future<void> dispose() => _controller.close();\n}\n```\n\n- Broadcast streams do not replay the latest value. Expose a `state` getter, or start the stream with it.\n- Listeners store the `StreamSubscription` and cancel it in `dispose()`.\n- In widgets prefer the state-management tool's observer (`ref.watch`, `BlocBuilder`) or `ValueListenableBuilder`\n  over raw `StreamBuilder` plumbing.\n\n## Adapter\n\n```dart\n// Callback API -> Future.\nFuture<PrintReceipt> printReceipt(LegacyPrinter printer, PrintJob job) {\n  final completer = Completer<PrintReceipt>();\n  printer.submit(job, onDone: completer.complete, onError: completer.completeError);\n  return completer.future;\n}\n\n// Listener API -> Stream, registered only while someone listens.\nStream<bool> adapterEnabled(BluetoothAdapterWrapper adapter) {\n  late final StreamController<bool> controller;\n  void listener(bool enabled) => controller.add(enabled);\n  controller = StreamController<bool>(\n    onListen: () => adapter.addListener(listener),\n    onCancel: () => adapter.removeListener(listener),\n  );\n  return controller.stream;\n}\n```\n\n- Guard against the callback firing twice: `if (!completer.isCompleted) completer.complete(value);`.\n- Platform channels are adapters too: generate them with Pigeon instead of hand-written method names.\n\n## Decorator\n\n```dart\nfinal class TimedDeviceApi implements DeviceApi {\n  TimedDeviceApi(this._inner, this._metrics);\n\n  final DeviceApi _inner;\n  final Metrics _metrics;\n\n  @override\n  Future<FirmwareInfo> firmwareInfo(String id) async {\n    final watch = Stopwatch()..start();\n    try {\n      return await _inner.firmwareInfo(id);\n    } finally {\n      _metrics.record('firmwareInfo', watch.elapsed);\n    }\n  }\n\n  // Dart has no automatic forwarding: every other member is forwarded by hand.\n  @override\n  Future<List<DeviceDto>> devices() => _inner.devices();\n}\n```\n\nFor HTTP concerns (auth headers, logging, retry) use the client's interceptor mechanism (for example `dio`\ninterceptors) rather than decorating every API class.\n\n## Facade\n\n```dart\nfinal class DeviceSession {\n  DeviceSession(this._ble, this._queue, this._machine);\n\n  final BleClient _ble;\n  final CommandQueue _queue;\n  final ConnectionMachine _machine;\n\n  Future<void> open(String deviceId) async {\n    _machine.send(const Connect());\n    await _ble.connect(deviceId);\n    final mtu = await _queue.enqueue(() => _ble.requestMtu(deviceId, 247));\n    await _queue.enqueue(() => _ble.discoverServices(deviceId));\n    await _queue.enqueue(() => _ble.enableNotifications(deviceId, telemetryCharacteristic));\n    _machine.send(LinkUp(mtu));\n  }\n}\n```\n\n`BleClient` is the app's own adapter over whichever BLE plugin the project uses. MTU requests are an\nAndroid concept; on iOS the plugin typically reports the negotiated value instead.\n\n## State machine (connection)\n\n```dart\nenum ConnError { timeout, rejected, bluetoothOff }\n\nsealed class ConnState { const ConnState(); }\nfinal class Disconnected extends ConnState { const Disconnected(); }\nfinal class Connecting extends ConnState { const Connecting(this.attempt); final int attempt; }\nfinal class Connected extends ConnState { const Connected(this.mtu); final int mtu; }\nfinal class Disconnecting extends ConnState { const Disconnecting(); }\nfinal class Failed extends ConnState { const Failed(this.error); final ConnError error; }\n\nsealed class ConnEvent { const ConnEvent(); }\nfinal class Connect extends ConnEvent { const Connect(); }\nfinal class LinkUp extends ConnEvent { const LinkUp(this.mtu); final int mtu; }\nfinal class LinkLost extends ConnEvent { const LinkLost(this.error); final ConnError error; }\nfinal class Disconnect extends ConnEvent { const Disconnect(); }\nfinal class Closed extends ConnEvent { const Closed(); }\n\nConnState reduce(ConnState state, ConnEvent event, {int maxAttempts = 3}) => switch ((state, event)) {\n      (Disconnected() || Failed(), Connect()) => const Connecting(1),\n      (Connecting(), LinkUp(:final mtu)) => Connected(mtu),\n      (Connecting(:final attempt), LinkLost(:final error)) =>\n        attempt < maxAttempts ? Connecting(attempt + 1) : Failed(error),\n      (Connected(), LinkLost()) => const Connecting(1),\n      (Connecting() || Connected(), Disconnect()) => const Disconnecting(),\n      (Disconnecting(), Closed() || LinkLost()) => const Disconnected(),\n      _ => state, // illegal transition: ignored, logged by the caller\n    };\n\nfinal class ConnectionMachine {\n  final _states = StreamController<ConnState>.broadcast();\n  ConnState state = const Disconnected();\n  Stream<ConnState> get states => _states.stream;\n\n  void send(ConnEvent event) {\n    final next = reduce(state, event);\n    if (identical(next, state)) return;\n    state = next;\n    _states.add(next);\n  }\n}\n```\n\n- An isolate is single-threaded, so `send` cannot race. It must stay synchronous: an `await` inside it\n  would let another event interleave.\n- These classes do not override `==`. Tests use matchers, or add `==` / `hashCode` (or `equatable`, `freezed`).\n\n```dart\ntest('link lost while connected starts reconnect', () {\n  final next = reduce(const Connected(185), const LinkLost(ConnError.timeout));\n  expect(next, isA<Connecting>().having((s) => s.attempt, 'attempt', 1));\n});\n```\n\n## Command\n\n```dart\nfinal class CommandQueue {\n  Future<void> _tail = Future.value();\n\n  // Runs commands strictly one after another; a failing command does not block the next.\n  Future<T> enqueue<T>(Future<T> Function() command) {\n    final result = _tail.then((_) => command());\n    _tail = result.then<void>((_) {}, onError: (Object _, StackTrace __) {});\n    return result;\n  }\n}\n```\n\n- Add a timeout per command (`command().timeout(...)`) when the underlying operation can hang.\n- Undo / redo: commands with `apply` and `revert`, two stacks, redo cleared on every new command.\n\n## Router (go_router)\n\n```dart\nGoRouter buildRouter(AuthState auth) => GoRouter(\n      initialLocation: '/devices',\n      refreshListenable: auth,\n      redirect: (context, state) {\n        final loggingIn = state.matchedLocation == '/login';\n        if (!auth.isLoggedIn) return loggingIn ? null : '/login';\n        if (loggingIn) return '/devices';\n        return null;\n      },\n      routes: [\n        GoRoute(path: '/login', builder: (context, state) => const LoginScreen()),\n        GoRoute(\n          path: '/devices',\n          builder: (context, state) => const DeviceListScreen(),\n          routes: [\n            GoRoute(\n              path: ':id',\n              builder: (context, state) => DeviceDetailScreen(id: state.pathParameters['id']!),\n            ),\n          ],\n        ),\n      ],\n    );\n```\n\n- Auth gating and deep-link handling live in `redirect`, not in each screen.\n- Screens take callbacks or call `context.go(...)` with paths built in one place, not string literals\n  scattered across widgets.\n\n## Result type\n\n```dart\nsealed class Result<T> {\n  const Result();\n}\n\nfinal class Ok<T> extends Result<T> {\n  const Ok(this.value);\n  final T value;\n}\n\nfinal class Err<T> extends Result<T> {\n  const Err(this.error);\n  final AppError error;\n}\n\nString label(Result<Device> r) => switch (r) {\n      Ok(:final value) => value.name,\n      Err(:final error) => error.message,\n    };\n```\n\n- Return `Result` for expected failures (network, validation, pairing). Let `Error` subclasses\n  (`StateError`, `ArgumentError`) propagate; they are bugs.\n\n## Delegation\n\nDart has no `by` keyword. Options, in order of preference:\n\n- Composition with explicit forwarding (as in the decorator above).\n- Callbacks passed to the constructor for owner notifications, instead of a delegate interface.\n- `mixin` for small reusable behaviour that needs no state from a specific superclass.\n\n```dart\nmixin Disposables {\n  final _subscriptions = <StreamSubscription<Object?>>[];\n  void track(StreamSubscription<Object?> s) => _subscriptions.add(s);\n  Future<void> disposeAll() => Future.wait(_subscriptions.map((s) => s.cancel()));\n}\n```\n",
    "references/java.md":
        "# Java pattern implementations (Android)\n\nLoad this when implementing or reviewing patterns in Java Android code. Java lacks named arguments,\nclass delegation and (on many toolchains) pattern matching for `switch`, so some patterns that are\nover-engineering in Kotlin are the right tool here. Examples use Java 17 language features that D8\ndesugars for all API levels (records, `instanceof` patterns, switch expressions).\n\n## Builder (justified in Java)\n\n```java\npublic final class NotificationSpec {\n    private final String title;\n    private final String channel;\n    private final boolean silent;\n\n    private NotificationSpec(Builder b) {\n        this.title = b.title;\n        this.channel = b.channel;\n        this.silent = b.silent;\n    }\n\n    public static Builder builder(String title) { return new Builder(title); }\n\n    public static final class Builder {\n        private final String title;        // required values go in the builder constructor\n        private String channel = \"default\";\n        private boolean silent;\n\n        private Builder(String title) { this.title = Objects.requireNonNull(title); }\n\n        public Builder channel(String channel) { this.channel = channel; return this; }\n        public Builder silent(boolean silent) { this.silent = silent; return this; }\n        public NotificationSpec build() { return new NotificationSpec(this); }\n    }\n}\n\nNotificationSpec spec = NotificationSpec.builder(\"Low battery\").channel(\"alerts\").build();\n```\n\n- Two or three fields: a `record` or a constructor is enough. Builders pay off with many optional fields.\n- When the class moves to Kotlin, delete the builder and use default arguments plus `@JvmOverloads`\n  for remaining Java callers.\n\n## Singleton through DI (Hilt)\n\n```java\n@Singleton\npublic final class SessionStore {\n    private final Context context;\n\n    @Inject\n    public SessionStore(@ApplicationContext Context context) { this.context = context; }\n}\n\n@Module\n@InstallIn(SingletonComponent.class)\npublic abstract class DataModule {\n    @Binds\n    abstract DeviceRepository bindDeviceRepository(DefaultDeviceRepository impl);\n}\n```\n\n```java\n// BAD: global lazy singleton, not thread-safe, keeps whatever Context the first caller passed\npublic static Analytics getInstance(Context c) {\n    if (instance == null) instance = new Analytics(c);\n    return instance;\n}\n```\n\n## Factory\n\n```java\npublic final class Exporters {\n    private Exporters() {}\n\n    public static Exporter forFormat(ExportFormat format, FontProvider fonts) {\n        return switch (format) {\n            case PDF -> new PdfExporter(fonts);\n            case CSV -> new CsvExporter(';');\n        };\n    }\n}\n```\n\nA switch expression over an enum is exhaustive at compile time; a new enum constant breaks the build\ninstead of falling through silently.\n\n## Strategy\n\n```java\n@FunctionalInterface\npublic interface BackoffPolicy {\n    long delayMillis(int attempt);\n\n    BackoffPolicy FIXED = attempt -> 1_000L;\n    BackoffPolicy EXPONENTIAL = attempt -> 500L << Math.min(attempt, 6);\n}\n\npublic final class Uploader {\n    private final BackoffPolicy backoff;\n    public Uploader(BackoffPolicy backoff) { this.backoff = backoff; }\n}\n```\n\nKotlin callers can pass a lambda directly because the interface has a single abstract method.\n\n## Observer (LiveData)\n\n```java\npublic final class ConnectionViewModel extends ViewModel {\n    private final MutableLiveData<ConnState> state = new MutableLiveData<>(new ConnState.Disconnected());\n\n    public LiveData<ConnState> getState() { return state; } // read-only type outward\n}\n\n// Fragment\nviewModel.getState().observe(getViewLifecycleOwner(), this::render);\n```\n\n- `postValue` from background threads keeps only the latest value. Fine for state, wrong for events.\n\n## Adapter\n\n```java\n// DTO -> domain at the data-layer edge.\nfinal class DeviceMapper {\n    private DeviceMapper() {}\n\n    static @Nullable Device toDomain(DeviceDto dto) {\n        if (dto.serial == null) return null;\n        return new Device(dto.serial, dto.name != null ? dto.name : \"\", dto.firmware);\n    }\n}\n```\n\n## Decorator\n\n```java\npublic final class TimedDeviceApi implements DeviceApi {\n    private final DeviceApi inner;\n    private final Metrics metrics;\n\n    public TimedDeviceApi(DeviceApi inner, Metrics metrics) {\n        this.inner = inner;\n        this.metrics = metrics;\n    }\n\n    @Override\n    public FirmwareInfo firmwareInfo(String id) throws IOException {\n        long start = SystemClock.elapsedRealtime();\n        try {\n            return inner.firmwareInfo(id);\n        } finally {\n            metrics.record(\"firmwareInfo\", SystemClock.elapsedRealtime() - start);\n        }\n    }\n\n    @Override\n    public List<DeviceDto> devices() throws IOException { return inner.devices(); } // manual forwarding\n}\n```\n\n## State machine (connection)\n\n```java\npublic interface ConnState {\n    record Disconnected() implements ConnState {}\n    record Connecting(int attempt) implements ConnState {}\n    record Connected(int mtu) implements ConnState {}\n    record Failed(ConnError error) implements ConnState {}\n}\n\npublic interface ConnEvent {\n    record Connect() implements ConnEvent {}\n    record LinkUp(int mtu) implements ConnEvent {}\n    record LinkLost(ConnError error) implements ConnEvent {}\n    record Disconnect() implements ConnEvent {}\n}\n\npublic final class ConnectionReducer {\n    private static final int MAX_ATTEMPTS = 3;\n\n    private ConnectionReducer() {}\n\n    public static ConnState reduce(ConnState state, ConnEvent event) {\n        if (state instanceof ConnState.Disconnected || state instanceof ConnState.Failed) {\n            return event instanceof ConnEvent.Connect ? new ConnState.Connecting(1) : state;\n        }\n        if (state instanceof ConnState.Connecting c) {\n            if (event instanceof ConnEvent.LinkUp up) return new ConnState.Connected(up.mtu());\n            if (event instanceof ConnEvent.LinkLost lost) {\n                return c.attempt() < MAX_ATTEMPTS ? new ConnState.Connecting(c.attempt() + 1) : new ConnState.Failed(lost.error());\n            }\n            if (event instanceof ConnEvent.Disconnect) return new ConnState.Disconnected();\n            return state;\n        }\n        if (state instanceof ConnState.Connected) {\n            if (event instanceof ConnEvent.LinkLost) return new ConnState.Connecting(1);\n            if (event instanceof ConnEvent.Disconnect) return new ConnState.Disconnected();\n            return state;\n        }\n        throw new IllegalStateException(\"Unhandled state \" + state);\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- Records give `equals` / `hashCode`, so tests compare states directly.\n- If the project's AGP and D8 support sealed types, declare `sealed interface ConnState permits ...`.\n  Pattern matching for `switch` is a Java 21 feature; confirm toolchain support before using it.\n- GATT callbacks arrive on binder threads. Apply events on one thread: a single-thread executor, or a\n  `synchronized` method that reduces and then calls `postValue`.\n\n## Command (serialized GATT queue)\n\n```java\npublic final class GattQueue {\n    private final ExecutorService worker = Executors.newSingleThreadExecutor();\n    private final GattExecutor executor;\n\n    public GattQueue(GattExecutor executor) { this.executor = executor; }\n\n    // One command at a time: executeBlocking issues the call and waits (with a timeout) for its callback.\n    public Future<byte[]> submit(GattCommand command) {\n        return worker.submit(() -> executor.executeBlocking(command, 5, TimeUnit.SECONDS));\n    }\n\n    public void close() { worker.shutdownNow(); }\n}\n```\n\n- `executeBlocking` typically uses a `CountDownLatch` or `CompletableFuture` completed from the\n  `BluetoothGattCallback`. Always wait with a timeout.\n- The owner calls `close()` when the connection ends, or the worker thread leaks.\n\n## Delegation\n\nJava has no class delegation. Prefer composition with explicit forwarding (as in the decorator) over\nextending a concrete class. For callbacks to an owner, pass a listener interface and clear it when the\nowner's lifecycle ends:\n\n```java\npublic void setListener(@Nullable ScanListener listener) { this.listener = listener; }\n\n@Override public void onDestroyView() {\n    scanner.setListener(null);\n    super.onDestroyView();\n}\n```\n",
    "references/kotlin.md":
        "# Kotlin pattern implementations\n\nLoad this when implementing or reviewing patterns in Kotlin (Android or KMP). Assumes coroutines, Flow and\nHilt. Adapt the DI annotations if the project uses Koin or manual injection.\n\n## Repository (offline-first)\n\n```kotlin\n// Interface only because ViewModel tests use a fake and the domain module must not see Room or Retrofit.\ninterface DeviceRepository {\n    fun observeDevices(): Flow<List<Device>>\n    suspend fun refresh(): RefreshResult\n}\n\nclass OfflineFirstDeviceRepository @Inject constructor(\n    private val api: DeviceApi,\n    private val dao: DeviceDao,\n    @IoDispatcher private val io: CoroutineDispatcher,\n) : DeviceRepository {\n\n    override fun observeDevices(): Flow<List<Device>> =\n        dao.observeAll().map { rows -> rows.map(DeviceEntity::toDomain) }\n\n    override suspend fun refresh(): RefreshResult = withContext(io) {\n        try {\n            dao.upsertAll(api.devices().map(DeviceDto::toEntity))\n            RefreshResult.Updated\n        } catch (e: IOException) {\n            RefreshResult.Offline\n        }\n    }\n}\n\n@Module\n@InstallIn(SingletonComponent::class)\nabstract class DataModule {\n    @Binds abstract fun deviceRepository(impl: OfflineFirstDeviceRepository): DeviceRepository\n}\n```\n\n- The UI observes the database; `refresh()` only writes into it. No second in-memory copy that can drift.\n- `HttpException` from Retrofit is not an `IOException`; map it separately if the server can reject requests.\n\n## Factory\n\n```kotlin\n// Factory function named after the type, the Kotlin convention (like MutableStateFlow(...)).\nfun BackoffPolicy(config: RetryConfig): BackoffPolicy = when (config.mode) {\n    RetryMode.Fixed -> BackoffPolicy { config.baseMillis }\n    RetryMode.Exponential -> BackoffPolicy { attempt -> config.baseMillis shl attempt.coerceAtMost(6) }\n}\n\n// Companion factory for parsing; the constructor stays private so invalid instances cannot exist.\n@JvmInline\nvalue class SerialNumber private constructor(val value: String) {\n    companion object {\n        private val format = Regex(\"[A-Z0-9]{12}\")\n        fun parse(raw: String): SerialNumber? = raw.trim().uppercase().takeIf(format::matches)?.let(::SerialNumber)\n    }\n}\n```\n\nAssisted injection when a ViewModel or worker needs runtime arguments plus injected dependencies:\nuse Hilt `@AssistedInject` / `@AssistedFactory` rather than a hand-written factory class.\n\n## Builder replaced by named and default arguments\n\n```kotlin\ndata class ScanSettingsSpec(\n    val timeout: Duration = 10.seconds,\n    val serviceFilter: UUID? = null,\n    val lowLatency: Boolean = false,\n)\n\nval spec = ScanSettingsSpec(serviceFilter = TELEMETRY_SERVICE, lowLatency = true)\nval longer = spec.copy(timeout = 30.seconds)\n```\n\n- `require(...)` in `init` validates combinations a builder would have checked in `build()`.\n- A type-safe DSL (`buildList { }`, `buildString { }`, a custom `@DslMarker` builder) is justified for\n  nested structures such as test fixtures or menus, not for flat option bags.\n- Java callers of a class with defaults need `@JvmOverloads` on the constructor.\n\n## Singleton through DI\n\n```kotlin\n@Module\n@InstallIn(SingletonComponent::class)\nobject NetworkModule {\n    @Provides @Singleton\n    fun okHttp(): OkHttpClient = OkHttpClient.Builder().build()\n}\n\n@Singleton\nclass BleManager @Inject constructor(@ApplicationContext private val context: Context)\n```\n\n- Scope matches lifetime: `@Singleton` for process-wide, `@ActivityRetainedScoped` or `@ViewModelScoped`\n  for screen-level state. Never put screen state in a `@Singleton`.\n- An application `CoroutineScope` for work that outlives screens is also injected, not created ad hoc.\n\n## Strategy\n\n```kotlin\nfun interface ChecksumStrategy { fun compute(bytes: ByteArray): Long }\n\nclass FrameValidator(private val checksum: ChecksumStrategy) {\n    fun isValid(payload: ByteArray, expected: Long): Boolean = checksum.compute(payload) == expected\n}\n\n// java.util.zip.CRC32 is available on every API level.\nval crc32 = ChecksumStrategy { bytes -> CRC32().apply { update(bytes) }.value }\n```\n\nWhich checksum a device protocol uses is defined by the firmware spec. Do not guess it.\n\n## Observer\n\n```kotlin\nclass ConnectionMonitor @Inject constructor(private val bluetooth: BluetoothAdapterWrapper) {\n\n    // Hot state: latest value always available, deduplicated.\n    private val _state = MutableStateFlow<ConnState>(ConnState.Disconnected)\n    val state: StateFlow<ConnState> = _state.asStateFlow()\n\n    // Adapting a listener API into a cold Flow: unregister in awaitClose.\n    fun adapterEnabled(): Flow<Boolean> = callbackFlow {\n        val listener = AdapterListener { enabled -> trySend(enabled) }\n        bluetooth.register(listener)\n        awaitClose { bluetooth.unregister(listener) }\n    }.distinctUntilChanged()\n}\n```\n\n- `StateFlow` for state, `SharedFlow` for broadcasts where every subscriber needs every value.\n- Expose cold flows to the UI through `stateIn(viewModelScope, SharingStarted.WhileSubscribed(5_000), initial)`.\n- Collect in UI with `collectAsStateWithLifecycle()` or `repeatOnLifecycle`.\n\n## Adapter\n\n```kotlin\n// DTO -> domain at the data-layer edge. The domain type never sees nullable wire fields.\nfun DeviceDto.toDomain(): Device? {\n    val serial = SerialNumber.parse(serial ?: return null) ?: return null\n    return Device(serial = serial, name = name.orEmpty(), firmware = firmware ?: \"unknown\")\n}\n\n// Callback API -> suspend. Resume exactly once; a resume after cancellation is ignored.\nsuspend fun LocationClient.currentLocation(): Location =\n    suspendCancellableCoroutine { cont ->\n        val request = requestSingle(\n            onResult = { cont.resume(it) },\n            onError = { cont.resumeWithException(it) },\n        )\n        cont.invokeOnCancellation { request.cancel() }\n    }\n```\n\n## Decorator\n\n```kotlin\nclass CachingForecastSource(\n    private val inner: ForecastSource,\n    private val time: TimeSource = TimeSource.Monotonic,\n    private val ttl: Duration = 10.minutes,\n) : ForecastSource by inner {\n\n    private val mutex = Mutex()\n    private var cached: Pair<TimeMark, Forecast>? = null\n\n    override suspend fun forecast(): Forecast = mutex.withLock {\n        cached?.takeIf { it.first.elapsedNow() < ttl }?.second\n            ?: inner.forecast().also { cached = time.markNow() to it }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- `TimeSource` is injected so tests pass a `TestTimeSource` and advance it with `+=`.\n- The `Mutex` makes concurrent callers share one fetch instead of racing.\n\n## Facade\n\n```kotlin\nclass DeviceSession @Inject constructor(\n    private val client: GattClient,\n    private val queue: GattQueue,\n    private val machine: ConnectionStateMachine,\n) {\n    suspend fun open(address: String, desiredMtu: Int): OpenResult {\n        machine.onEvent(ConnEvent.Connect)\n        val link = client.connect(address) ?: return OpenResult.Unreachable\n        val mtu = (queue.execute(GattCommand.RequestMtu(desiredMtu)) as? GattResponse.Mtu)?.value\n            ?: return OpenResult.Failed\n        queue.execute(GattCommand.DiscoverServices)\n        queue.execute(GattCommand.EnableNotifications(TELEMETRY_CHARACTERISTIC))\n        machine.onEvent(ConnEvent.LinkUp(mtu = mtu))\n        return OpenResult.Ready(link)\n    }\n}\n```\n\nThe facade sequences calls; it does not own UI state or persistence.\n\n## State machine (connection)\n\n```kotlin\nsealed interface ConnState {\n    data object Disconnected : ConnState\n    data class Connecting(val attempt: Int) : ConnState\n    data class Connected(val mtu: Int) : ConnState\n    data object Disconnecting : ConnState\n    data class Failed(val error: ConnError) : ConnState\n}\n\nsealed interface ConnEvent {\n    data object Connect : ConnEvent\n    data class LinkUp(val mtu: Int) : ConnEvent\n    data class LinkLost(val error: ConnError) : ConnEvent\n    data object Disconnect : ConnEvent\n    data object Closed : ConnEvent\n}\n\nfun reduce(state: ConnState, event: ConnEvent, maxAttempts: Int = 3): ConnState = when (state) {\n    ConnState.Disconnected, is ConnState.Failed -> when (event) {\n        ConnEvent.Connect -> ConnState.Connecting(attempt = 1)\n        else -> state\n    }\n    is ConnState.Connecting -> when (event) {\n        is ConnEvent.LinkUp -> ConnState.Connected(event.mtu)\n        is ConnEvent.LinkLost ->\n            if (state.attempt < maxAttempts) ConnState.Connecting(state.attempt + 1) else ConnState.Failed(event.error)\n        ConnEvent.Disconnect -> ConnState.Disconnecting\n        ConnEvent.Connect, ConnEvent.Closed -> state\n    }\n    is ConnState.Connected -> when (event) {\n        is ConnEvent.LinkLost -> ConnState.Connecting(attempt = 1)\n        ConnEvent.Disconnect -> ConnState.Disconnecting\n        ConnEvent.Connect, is ConnEvent.LinkUp, ConnEvent.Closed -> state\n    }\n    ConnState.Disconnecting -> when (event) {\n        ConnEvent.Closed, is ConnEvent.LinkLost -> ConnState.Disconnected\n        else -> state\n    }\n}\n\nclass ConnectionStateMachine @Inject constructor(private val log: Logger) {\n    private val _state = MutableStateFlow<ConnState>(ConnState.Disconnected)\n    val state: StateFlow<ConnState> = _state.asStateFlow()\n\n    // Safe from GATT binder threads: update is an atomic compare-and-set over a pure function.\n    fun onEvent(event: ConnEvent) {\n        val (old, new) = _state.updateAndGetPair { reduce(it, event) }\n        if (old == new) log.debug(\"Ignored \$event in \$old\")\n    }\n}\n\nprivate inline fun <T> MutableStateFlow<T>.updateAndGetPair(transform: (T) -> T): Pair<T, T> {\n    while (true) {\n        val old = value\n        val new = transform(old)\n        if (compareAndSet(old, new)) return old to new\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- `reduce` is pure, so it is trivially unit tested and safe to retry inside `compareAndSet`.\n- Side effects (reconnect timers, `gatt.close()`) run in a collector of `state`, not inside `reduce`.\n- Prefer listing events explicitly over `else` where the compiler should flag new events.\n\n```kotlin\nclass ReduceTest {\n    @Test fun `link lost while connected starts reconnect`() {\n        assertEquals(ConnState.Connecting(1), reduce(ConnState.Connected(mtu = 247), ConnEvent.LinkLost(ConnError.Timeout)))\n    }\n\n    @Test fun `gives up after max attempts`() {\n        assertEquals(\n            ConnState.Failed(ConnError.Timeout),\n            reduce(ConnState.Connecting(3), ConnEvent.LinkLost(ConnError.Timeout), maxAttempts = 3),\n        )\n    }\n}\n```\n\n## Command (GATT queue with results and timeout)\n\n```kotlin\nsealed interface GattCommand {\n    class Read(val characteristic: UUID) : GattCommand\n    class Write(val characteristic: UUID, val value: ByteArray) : GattCommand\n    class RequestMtu(val mtu: Int) : GattCommand\n    data object DiscoverServices : GattCommand\n    class EnableNotifications(val characteristic: UUID) : GattCommand\n}\n\nsealed interface GattResponse {\n    class Value(val bytes: ByteArray) : GattResponse\n    data class Mtu(val value: Int) : GattResponse\n    data object Done : GattResponse\n}\n\nclass GattQueue(scope: CoroutineScope, private val executor: GattExecutor) {\n\n    private class Pending(val command: GattCommand, val result: CompletableDeferred<GattResponse>)\n\n    private val pending = Channel<Pending>(Channel.UNLIMITED)\n\n    init {\n        scope.launch {\n            for (p in pending) {\n                // withTimeoutOrNull: a plain withTimeout throws a CancellationException subtype.\n                val response = try {\n                    withTimeoutOrNull(5.seconds) { executor.executeAndAwait(p.command) }\n                } catch (e: GattException) {\n                    p.result.completeExceptionally(e)\n                    continue\n                }\n                if (response != null) p.result.complete(response) else p.result.completeExceptionally(GattTimeoutException(p.command))\n            }\n        }\n    }\n\n    suspend fun execute(command: GattCommand): GattResponse {\n        val p = Pending(command, CompletableDeferred())\n        pending.send(p)\n        return p.result.await()\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- `Write` holds a `ByteArray`, so it is a plain class: a `data class` would compare arrays by reference.\n- `GattExecutor.executeAndAwait` issues the platform call and suspends until the matching\n  `BluetoothGattCallback` fires (bridged with `suspendCancellableCoroutine` or a `CompletableDeferred`).\n- Payload size, write type and retry policy come from the firmware protocol document.\n\nUndo / redo variant:\n\n```kotlin\ninterface EditCommand {\n    fun apply(doc: Document): Document\n    fun revert(doc: Document): Document\n}\n\nclass History(private var doc: Document) {\n    private val undo = ArrayDeque<EditCommand>()\n    private val redo = ArrayDeque<EditCommand>()\n    fun run(c: EditCommand) { doc = c.apply(doc); undo.addLast(c); redo.clear() }\n    fun undo() { undo.removeLastOrNull()?.let { doc = it.revert(doc); redo.addLast(it) } }\n}\n```\n\n## Router (Compose)\n\n```kotlin\n@Composable\nfun DeviceListScreen(state: DeviceListState, onDeviceClick: (String) -> Unit) { ... }\n\n// The nav host owns the back stack; screens only raise intents.\n// With Navigation 3 the back stack is a list of typed keys you own, rendered by NavDisplay.\nDeviceListScreen(state = state, onDeviceClick = { id -> backStack.add(DeviceDetailKey(id)) })\n```\n\n- ViewModels do not hold a `NavController`. Navigation triggered by a result is modelled as state the\n  screen reacts to, or a callback from the screen after it observes success.\n- Deep links and auth gating are decided in one place (the nav host or a small router class).\n\n## Result types\n\n```kotlin\nsealed interface Outcome<out T, out E> {\n    data class Ok<T>(val value: T) : Outcome<T, Nothing>\n    data class Err<E>(val error: E) : Outcome<Nothing, E>\n}\n\ninline fun <T, E, R> Outcome<T, E>.map(f: (T) -> R): Outcome<R, E> = when (this) {\n    is Outcome.Ok -> Outcome.Ok(f(value))\n    is Outcome.Err -> this\n}\n```\n\n- `kotlin.Result` is fine inside a layer, but its error is an untyped `Throwable` and `runCatching`\n  catches `CancellationException`. At layer boundaries prefer a sealed domain result.\n- Exceptions for programmer errors (`require`, `check`, `error`) are not wrapped.\n\n## Delegation\n\n```kotlin\n// Class delegation: override only what changes.\nclass ReadOnlySettings(private val inner: Settings) : Settings by inner {\n    override fun set(key: String, value: String) = throw UnsupportedOperationException(\"read-only\")\n}\n\n// Property delegation: lifecycle-aware or lazy values without boilerplate.\nprivate val viewModel: DevicesViewModel by viewModels()\nprivate val parser by lazy(LazyThreadSafetyMode.NONE) { FrameParser() } // main-thread only\n```\n\n- `by inner` forwards calls made on the wrapper only. If `inner` calls its own methods internally,\n  the override is not used (unlike inheritance). Check this before relying on it.\n",
    "references/swift.md":
        "# Swift pattern implementations\n\nLoad this when implementing or reviewing patterns in Swift for iOS. Assumes Swift 6 language mode,\nasync/await, `@Observable` (iOS 17+) and SwiftUI. Older targets can swap `@Observable` for\n`ObservableObject` without changing the patterns.\n\n## Repository\n\n```swift\n// Protocol because view models are tested with a fake and the feature module must not see URLSession.\nprotocol DeviceRepository: Sendable {\n    func devices() async throws -> [Device]\n    func refresh() async throws\n}\n\nactor CachedDeviceRepository: DeviceRepository {\n    private let api: DeviceAPI\n    private let store: DeviceStore           // disk cache, the source of truth\n    private var refreshTask: Task<Void, Error>?\n\n    init(api: DeviceAPI, store: DeviceStore) {\n        self.api = api\n        self.store = store\n    }\n\n    func devices() async throws -> [Device] {\n        try await store.load().map(Device.init(record:))\n    }\n\n    // Concurrent callers share one in-flight refresh (actor reentrancy safe).\n    func refresh() async throws {\n        if let refreshTask { return try await refreshTask.value }\n        let task = Task { try await store.save(api.devices().map(DeviceRecord.init(dto:))) }\n        refreshTask = task\n        defer { refreshTask = nil }\n        try await task.value\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- DTOs (`Decodable`) and persistence records stay in the data layer; the protocol returns domain types.\n\n## Factory\n\n```swift\nenum ExportFormat { case pdf, csv }\n\nprotocol Exporter { func export(_ report: Report) throws -> Data }\n\nstruct ExporterFactory {\n    let fonts: FontProvider\n\n    func make(_ format: ExportFormat) -> any Exporter {\n        switch format {\n        case .pdf: PDFExporter(fonts: fonts)\n        case .csv: CSVExporter(separator: \";\")\n        }\n    }\n}\n\n// Failable static factory for validated values.\nstruct SerialNumber: Hashable, Sendable {\n    let value: String\n    private init(_ value: String) { self.value = value }\n\n    static func parse(_ raw: String) -> SerialNumber? {\n        let s = raw.trimmingCharacters(in: .whitespaces).uppercased()\n        guard s.count == 12, s.allSatisfy({ \$0.isASCII && (\$0.isLetter || \$0.isNumber) }) else { return nil }\n        return SerialNumber(s)\n    }\n}\n```\n\n## Builder replaced by memberwise initializers\n\n```swift\nstruct ScanOptions: Sendable {\n    var timeout: Duration = .seconds(10)\n    var serviceFilter: CBUUID? = nil\n    var allowDuplicates = false\n}\n\nlet options = ScanOptions(serviceFilter: telemetryService) // defaulted members may be omitted\nvar longer = options\nlonger.timeout = .seconds(30)                               // value semantics replace copy()\n```\n\n- Memberwise initializers are internal. Public types need an explicit `public init` with default arguments.\n- Result builders (`@resultBuilder`) are for nested declarative structures, not option bags.\n\n## Singleton replaced by injection\n\n```swift\n@main\nstruct GatewayApp: App {\n    // Composition root: the only place that knows concrete types.\n    @State private var devices = DevicesModel(repository: CachedDeviceRepository(api: .live, store: .disk))\n\n    var body: some Scene {\n        WindowGroup { DevicesView().environment(devices) }\n    }\n}\n\nstruct DevicesView: View {\n    @Environment(DevicesModel.self) private var model\n    var body: some View { List(model.items) { DeviceRow(device: \$0) } }\n}\n```\n\n- `static let shared` is acceptable for truly stateless or system-owned services, and only referenced at the\n  composition root, never inside view models.\n- Tests construct `DevicesModel(repository: FakeDeviceRepository())` directly.\n\n## Strategy\n\n```swift\nstruct BackoffPolicy: Sendable {\n    let delay: @Sendable (_ attempt: Int) -> Duration\n\n    static let fixed = BackoffPolicy { _ in .seconds(1) }\n    static let exponential = BackoffPolicy { attempt in .milliseconds(500 * (1 << min(attempt, 6))) }\n}\n\nfinal class Uploader {\n    private let backoff: BackoffPolicy\n    init(backoff: BackoffPolicy = .exponential) { self.backoff = backoff }\n}\n```\n\nA protocol is the better fit when the strategy has several methods or its own state.\n\n## Observer\n\n```swift\n// UI state: Observation framework.\n@MainActor @Observable\nfinal class DevicesModel {\n    private(set) var items: [Device] = []\n    private let repository: any DeviceRepository\n    init(repository: any DeviceRepository) { self.repository = repository }\n    func load() async { items = (try? await repository.devices()) ?? [] } // real code maps errors to state\n}\n\n// Event stream with a single consumer: AsyncStream.\nfinal class TelemetryFeed: Sendable {\n    let readings: AsyncStream<Reading>\n    private let continuation: AsyncStream<Reading>.Continuation\n\n    init() {\n        (readings, continuation) = AsyncStream.makeStream(of: Reading.self, bufferingPolicy: .bufferingNewest(64))\n    }\n\n    func publish(_ reading: Reading) { continuation.yield(reading) }\n    deinit { continuation.finish() }\n}\n\n// Several subscribers that each need the latest value: Combine.\nfinal class BatteryMonitor {\n    private let subject = CurrentValueSubject<Int?, Never>(nil)\n    var level: AnyPublisher<Int?, Never> { subject.eraseToAnyPublisher() }\n    func update(_ level: Int) { subject.send(level) }\n}\n```\n\n- One `AsyncStream` is meant for one consumer; two loops iterating it split the elements between them.\n- Combine subscribers keep their `AnyCancellable` and capture `self` weakly in `sink`.\n- Consume streams in `.task { for await r in feed.readings { ... } }` so the loop cancels with the view.\n\n## Adapter\n\n```swift\n// Completion-handler API -> async. Resume exactly once on every path.\nextension LegacyPrinter {\n    func printReceipt(_ job: PrintJob) async throws -> PrintReceipt {\n        try await withCheckedThrowingContinuation { continuation in\n            submit(job) { result in continuation.resume(with: result) }\n        }\n    }\n}\n\n// Delegate API -> ordered AsyncStream. CBCentralManager keeps its delegate weakly; the owner retains this object.\nfinal class CentralEvents: NSObject, CBCentralManagerDelegate {\n    let events: AsyncStream<CentralEvent>\n    private let continuation: AsyncStream<CentralEvent>.Continuation\n    private var central: CBCentralManager?\n\n    override init() {\n        (events, continuation) = AsyncStream.makeStream(of: CentralEvent.self)\n        super.init()\n        central = CBCentralManager(delegate: self, queue: DispatchQueue(label: \"ble.central\"))\n    }\n\n    func centralManagerDidUpdateState(_ central: CBCentralManager) {\n        continuation.yield(.powered(central.state == .poweredOn))\n    }\n\n    func centralManager(_ central: CBCentralManager, didConnect peripheral: CBPeripheral) {\n        continuation.yield(.connected(peripheral.identifier))\n    }\n\n    func centralManager(_ central: CBCentralManager, didDisconnectPeripheral peripheral: CBPeripheral, error: (any Error)?) {\n        continuation.yield(.disconnected(peripheral.identifier))\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- Add cancellation with `withTaskCancellationHandler` when the wrapped API can be cancelled.\n- Yielding into one stream keeps callback order. Spawning a `Task { await ... }` per callback does not.\n\n## Decorator\n\n```swift\nstruct TimedDeviceAPI: DeviceAPI {\n    let inner: any DeviceAPI\n    let metrics: Metrics\n\n    func firmwareInfo(id: Device.ID) async throws -> FirmwareInfo {\n        let start = ContinuousClock.now\n        defer { metrics.record(\"firmwareInfo\", ContinuousClock.now - start) }\n        return try await inner.firmwareInfo(id: id)\n    }\n\n    // Swift has no automatic forwarding: every other requirement is forwarded by hand.\n    func devices() async throws -> [DeviceDTO] { try await inner.devices() }\n}\n```\n\nLarge protocols make decorators expensive in Swift. Keep the decorated protocol small.\n\n## Facade\n\n```swift\nactor DeviceSession {\n    private let central: CentralClient\n    private let machine: ConnectionStateMachine\n\n    init(central: CentralClient, machine: ConnectionStateMachine) {\n        self.central = central\n        self.machine = machine\n    }\n\n    func open(_ id: UUID) async throws(SessionError) -> PeripheralLink {\n        await machine.send(.connect)\n        do {\n            let link = try await central.connect(id)\n            try await link.discoverServices([telemetryService])\n            try await link.enableNotifications(for: telemetryCharacteristic)\n            await machine.send(.linkUp(mtu: link.maximumWriteLength))\n            return link\n        } catch {\n            throw SessionError.setupFailed(String(describing: error))\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n`maximumWriteLength` here stands for a wrapper over `CBPeripheral.maximumWriteValueLength(for:)`.\n\n## State machine (connection)\n\n```swift\nenum ConnectionError: Error, Equatable, Sendable { case timeout, rejected, poweredOff }\n\nenum ConnectionState: Equatable, Sendable {\n    case disconnected\n    case connecting(attempt: Int)\n    case connected(mtu: Int)\n    case disconnecting\n    case failed(ConnectionError)\n}\n\nenum ConnectionEvent: Sendable {\n    case connect, disconnect, closed\n    case linkUp(mtu: Int)\n    case linkLost(ConnectionError)\n}\n\nextension ConnectionState {\n    func next(on event: ConnectionEvent, maxAttempts: Int = 3) -> ConnectionState {\n        switch (self, event) {\n        case (.disconnected, .connect), (.failed, .connect):\n            .connecting(attempt: 1)\n        case (.connecting, .linkUp(let mtu)):\n            .connected(mtu: mtu)\n        case (.connecting(let attempt), .linkLost(let error)):\n            attempt < maxAttempts ? .connecting(attempt: attempt + 1) : .failed(error)\n        case (.connected, .linkLost):\n            .connecting(attempt: 1)\n        case (.connecting, .disconnect), (.connected, .disconnect):\n            .disconnecting\n        case (.disconnecting, .closed), (.disconnecting, .linkLost):\n            .disconnected\n        default:\n            self // illegal transition: ignored, logged by the caller\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nactor ConnectionStateMachine {\n    private(set) var state: ConnectionState = .disconnected\n    let states: AsyncStream<ConnectionState>\n    private let continuation: AsyncStream<ConnectionState>.Continuation\n\n    init() {\n        (states, continuation) = AsyncStream.makeStream(of: ConnectionState.self, bufferingPolicy: .bufferingNewest(1))\n    }\n\n    func send(_ event: ConnectionEvent) {\n        let next = state.next(on: event)\n        guard next != state else { return }\n        state = next\n        continuation.yield(next)\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- Feed delegate events in order: one task iterates `CentralEvents.events` and calls `await machine.send(...)`.\n- `next(on:)` is pure; test it without Bluetooth:\n\n```swift\n@Test func linkLostWhileConnectedReconnects() {\n    #expect(ConnectionState.connected(mtu: 185).next(on: .linkLost(.timeout)) == .connecting(attempt: 1))\n}\n```\n\n## Command\n\n```swift\nfinal class CommandQueue<Command: Sendable>: Sendable {\n    private let continuation: AsyncStream<Command>.Continuation\n    private let worker: Task<Void, Never>\n\n    init(execute: @escaping @Sendable (Command) async -> Void) {\n        let (stream, continuation) = AsyncStream.makeStream(of: Command.self)\n        self.continuation = continuation\n        worker = Task { for await command in stream { await execute(command) } }\n    }\n\n    func enqueue(_ command: Command) { continuation.yield(command) }\n\n    deinit {\n        continuation.finish()\n        worker.cancel()\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- CoreBluetooth queues individual read and write requests itself. On iOS a command queue is about\n  app-level sequences (write config, wait for acknowledgement, then read status) and undo, not about the stack.\n- Writes without response use flow control: check `canSendWriteWithoutResponse` and resume on\n  `peripheralIsReady(toSendWriteWithoutResponse:)`.\n- Undo in document-style apps: `UndoManager` from the environment rather than a custom stack.\n\n## Coordinator / Router\n\n```swift\nenum OnboardingRoute: Hashable { case permissions, pairing, done }\n\n@MainActor @Observable\nfinal class OnboardingCoordinator {\n    var path: [OnboardingRoute] = []\n    func start() { path.append(.permissions) }\n    func permissionsGranted() { path.append(.pairing) }\n    func paired() { path.append(.done) }\n    func finish() { path.removeAll() }\n}\n\nstruct OnboardingFlow: View {\n    @State private var coordinator = OnboardingCoordinator()\n\n    var body: some View {\n        NavigationStack(path: \$coordinator.path) {\n            WelcomeView(onContinue: { coordinator.start() })\n                .navigationDestination(for: OnboardingRoute.self) { route in\n                    switch route {\n                    case .permissions: PermissionsView(onGranted: { coordinator.permissionsGranted() })\n                    case .pairing: PairingView(onPaired: { coordinator.paired() })\n                    case .done: DoneView(onFinish: { coordinator.finish() })\n                    }\n                }\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- Child views receive closures, not the coordinator, so they stay previewable and reusable.\n- UIKit apps use the classic coordinator owning a `UINavigationController`; keep child coordinators in an\n  array and remove them when their flow finishes, or they leak.\n\n## Result and typed throws\n\n```swift\nenum PairingError: Error { case timeout, rejected(reason: String), bluetoothOff }\n\nfunc pair(id: Device.ID) async throws(PairingError) -> Device { ... }\n\nfunc onPairTapped() async {\n    do throws(PairingError) {\n        state = .paired(try await pair(id: selected))\n    } catch {\n        switch error { // error is PairingError, exhaustive switch\n        case .timeout: state = .retryable\n        case .rejected(let reason): state = .failed(reason)\n        case .bluetoothOff: state = .needsBluetooth\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- Typed throws only where the error set is closed and callers switch over it; otherwise `throws` (any Error).\n- `Result<Success, Failure>` when an outcome must be stored or passed around, not for normal control flow.\n\n## Delegation\n\n```swift\nprotocol ScannerDelegate: AnyObject {\n    func scanner(_ scanner: Scanner, didFind device: Device)\n}\n\nfinal class Scanner {\n    weak var delegate: ScannerDelegate?\n}\n```\n\n- Prefer closures or `AsyncStream` for new APIs with one callback; delegates suit many related callbacks.\n- `unowned` only when the delegate provably outlives the delegator; otherwise `weak`.\n",
  },
  "mobile-merge-request": {
    "SKILL.md":
        "---\nname: mobile-merge-request\ndescription: \"Guides the GitLab merge request and GitHub pull request workflow for Android, iOS and Flutter teams: branch naming, Conventional Commits (feat, fix, scope, breaking changes with ! and BREAKING CHANGE footer), small focused and stacked MRs, self-review, rebase vs merge, git push --force-with-lease, fixup commits with git rebase --autosquash, git range-diff, squash policy, and safe conflict resolution for lockfiles (Podfile.lock, Package.resolved, pubspec.lock), generated code, Xcode project.pbxproj and libs.versions.toml. Writes MR or PR descriptions with summary, ticket, changes, Android and iOS screenshots, testing, risk and rollback. Covers reviewer etiquette, comment prefixes (nit:, question:) and resolving threads. Use when creating a branch, writing commit messages, opening, describing, splitting, rebasing or reviewing an MR or PR, resolving merge conflicts, or answering review comments.\"\nlicense: MIT\nmetadata:\n  author: Ahmad Hussain\n  version: \"1.0.0\"\n  pack: mobile-dev-kit\n---\n\n# Mobile merge request\n\nGet mobile changes from a branch into main with a clean history, a description a reviewer can act on,\nand no lost work along the way. Applies to GitLab merge requests and GitHub pull requests alike;\n\"MR\" below means either.\n\n## When to use\n\n- Creating a branch, writing or cleaning up commit messages before review.\n- Opening an MR or PR, writing or improving its title and description.\n- Splitting a large change into smaller or stacked MRs.\n- Rebasing onto main, resolving conflicts, or force-pushing after review fixes.\n- Reviewing someone else's MR, or replying to review comments on your own.\n- Checking MR hygiene: branch name, commit messages, size, description, checklist.\n\nNot for: reviewing the code itself for bugs (use `mobile-code-review`), or pipeline configuration\n(use `mobile-ci-cd`).\n\n## Workflow\n\n1. Gather facts before writing anything.\n   - Branch and target: `git branch --show-current`, `git fetch origin`.\n   - Commits: `git log --oneline origin/main..HEAD`.\n   - Size and files: `git diff --stat --merge-base origin/main`.\n   - Ticket key from the branch name or the user. Never invent a ticket key; leave a placeholder.\n2. Check hygiene against the Rules below: branch name, commit messages, size, unrelated changes,\n   generated files, secrets in the diff.\n3. If the history needs cleanup, propose the commands (fixup, autosquash, rebase) and wait for approval.\n   The agent never rewrites history, force-pushes or merges without explicit user consent.\n4. Self-review the full diff as a reviewer would. Remove debug logs, commented-out code, stray TODOs.\n5. Run the project's build, lint and tests for every affected platform. Report failures honestly.\n6. Write the MR title and description from [the MR template](references/mr-template.md).\n   Fill only what the diff and the user support; mark unknowns as `TODO` for the author.\n7. For conflicts, follow [conflict resolution](references/conflicts.md). Re-run build and tests after.\n8. For exact command sequences (stacking, retargeting, recovery), use\n   [git commands](references/git-commands.md).\n\n## Rules\n\n### 1. Branch names carry type, owner and ticket\n\n```text\n# BAD\nahmad-changes\nfix\nfeature/new-stuff\n\n# GOOD\nfeature/AH/PROJ-123-ble-reconnect-backoff\nfix/AH/PROJ-456-crash-on-empty-scan\nchore/AH/PROJ-789-bump-agp\n```\n\n- Pattern: `<type>/<initials>/<TICKET>-<kebab-short-title>`. Types: `feature`, `fix`, `chore`,\n  `refactor`, `docs`, `test`, `release`, `hotfix`.\n- Lowercase after the ticket key, no spaces, under about 60 characters.\n- Follow the repository's own convention if it already has one.\n\n```bash\ngit fetch origin\ngit switch -c feature/AH/PROJ-123-ble-reconnect-backoff origin/main\n```\n\n### 2. Commits follow Conventional Commits\n\n```text\n# BAD\nfixed stuff\nWIP\nUpdate MainActivity.kt\n\n# GOOD\nfix(ble): retry GATT connect with exponential backoff\nfeat(onboarding): add QR pairing screen\nrefactor(settings): move formatting out of SettingsViewController\n```\n\n- Format: `<type>(<optional scope>): <imperative summary>`, summary under about 72 characters, no period.\n- Types: `feat`, `fix`, `refactor`, `perf`, `test`, `docs`, `build`, `ci`, `chore`, `style`, `revert`.\n- Body explains why, not what the diff already shows. Wrap at 72.\n- Footers: `Refs: PROJ-123`, `BREAKING CHANGE: <what breaks and how to migrate>`.\n\nBreaking changes:\n\n```text\n# BAD: breaking change hidden in a fix\nfix(api): rename deviceId field\n\n# GOOD\nfeat(api)!: rename deviceId to gatewayId in telemetry payload\n\nBREAKING CHANGE: backend must accept gatewayId; app versions before 3.2 send deviceId.\nRefs: PROJ-321\n```\n\n- No tool-generated footers, sign-offs or co-author trailers unless the project requires them.\n\n### 3. One concern per MR, small enough to review in one sitting\n\n```text\n# BAD: one MR\nfeat: QR pairing + Gradle 9 upgrade + rename package + fix crash in settings\n\n# GOOD: four MRs\nchore(build): upgrade Gradle wrapper\nrefactor: rename package com.acme.old to com.acme.gateway\nfeat(onboarding): add QR pairing screen\nfix(settings): crash on empty device name\n```\n\n- Rough guidance: under about 400 changed lines of hand-written code. Lockfiles, generated code and\n  snapshots do not count but must be listed.\n- Mechanical changes (renames, formatting, dependency bumps) go in their own MR, never mixed with logic.\n- If it cannot be small, stack it (rule 4) or put it behind a feature flag and land it in slices.\n\n### 4. Stacked MRs target their parent, then get retargeted\n\n```bash\n# BAD: second MR targets main and shows the first MR's commits too\ngit switch -c feature/AH/PROJ-124-pairing-ui feature/AH/PROJ-123-pairing-api\n# ...opened against main\n\n# GOOD: target the parent branch; after the parent merges, rebase and retarget to main\ngit switch -c feature/AH/PROJ-124-pairing-ui feature/AH/PROJ-123-pairing-api\n# open MR: feature/AH/PROJ-124-pairing-ui -> feature/AH/PROJ-123-pairing-api\n```\n\n- Say \"Depends on !123\" (GitLab) or \"Depends on #123\" (GitHub) at the top of the description.\n- Keep stacks shallow: two or three levels.\n- After the parent is squash-merged, replay only your own commits with `git rebase --onto` (see\n  [git commands](references/git-commands.md)) and retarget the MR to main.\n\n### 5. Rebase your own branch; never rewrite shared branches\n\n```bash\n# BAD\ngit push --force                  # overwrites whatever is on the remote, including others' commits\ngit rebase origin/main            # on main, develop or a branch a colleague also pushes to\n\n# GOOD: own branch only, refuses if the remote moved since your last fetch\ngit fetch origin\ngit rebase origin/main\ngit push --force-with-lease\n```\n\n- Shared branches (`main`, `develop`, `release/*`, a branch two people push to) only get merge commits\n  or new commits, never rewrites.\n- Do not rebase in the middle of an active review round without telling reviewers; it resets their\n  \"changes since last review\" view on some platforms. Prefer fixup commits until approval.\n\n### 6. Review fixes are fixup commits, squashed before merge\n\n```bash\n# BAD: noise that lands in main\ngit commit -m \"address review comments\"\ngit commit -m \"fix typo\"\n\n# GOOD\ngit commit --fixup=a1b2c3d\n# after approval, fold fixups into their targets\ngit rebase -i --autosquash origin/main\ngit range-diff origin/main @{u} HEAD   # confirm only intended changes before pushing\ngit push --force-with-lease\n```\n\n### 7. Squash policy is explicit\n\n- One logical change per MR: squash-merge. The MR title becomes the commit, so it must be a valid\n  Conventional Commit.\n- Several atomic, well-written commits (for example a refactor then a feature): rebase-merge or\n  fast-forward, no squash, so `git bisect` and `git revert` stay precise.\n- Never squash an MR that mixes a dependency bump with a behaviour change; split it instead.\n\n```text\n# BAD squash-merge title\nFeature/AH/PROJ-123 ble reconnect backoff\n\n# GOOD\nfix(ble): retry GATT connect with exponential backoff (PROJ-123)\n```\n\n### 8. Conflicts are resolved by understanding both sides, then verified by a build\n\n```bash\n# BAD: pick a side blindly for every file\ngit checkout --theirs .\ngit add . && git rebase --continue\n\n# GOOD: inspect, resolve per file, regenerate what is generated, then build and test\ngit status\ngit diff --name-only --diff-filter=U\n# resolve each file; regenerate lockfiles and generated code instead of hand-merging\ngit add <file>\ngit rebase --continue\n./gradlew assembleDebug testDebugUnitTest\n```\n\n- During a rebase `--ours` is the branch being rebased onto (main) and `--theirs` is your commit.\n  The opposite of a merge.\n- Lockfiles, `project.pbxproj` and version catalogs have specific procedures:\n  [conflict resolution](references/conflicts.md).\n\n### 9. The description lets a reviewer review without asking\n\n```markdown\n<!-- BAD -->\nFixes the bug.\n\n<!-- GOOD (short form) -->\n## Summary\nReconnect to the gateway with exponential backoff after GATT error 133 instead of failing at once.\n\n## Ticket\nPROJ-123\n\n## Testing\n- Unit: BackoffPolicyTest (new), ConnectionManagerTest (updated)\n- Manual: Pixel 8 Android 16, iPhone 15 iOS 26; power-cycled gateway 10 times, reconnected every time\n```\n\n- Every user-visible change has Android and iOS screenshots or recordings, before and after.\n- Call out explicitly: new permissions, manifest or `Info.plist` keys, entitlements, `minSdk`,\n  `targetSdk` or deployment target changes, database migrations, analytics or telemetry changes.\n- Full template: [MR template](references/mr-template.md).\n\n### 10. Review comments are labelled and actionable\n\n```text\n# BAD\nThis is wrong.\nWhy would you do it like this?\n\n# GOOD\nissue: `onCharacteristicChanged` runs on a binder thread; `_state.value = ...` here races with the\ncollector. Use `_state.update {}` or hop to the main dispatcher.\n\nnit (non-blocking): `deviceList` -> `devices` to match the repository naming.\n\nquestion: Is the 10 s timeout from the firmware spec, or a guess? If a guess, add a constant with a comment.\n```\n\n- Labels: `issue:` (blocking), `suggestion:`, `question:`, `nit:` (never blocks), `praise:`, `todo:`.\n- Comment on the code, not the person. Explain why, and propose a fix or a suggestion block.\n- See [reviewer etiquette](references/mr-template.md#reviewer-etiquette) for both sides of a review.\n\n## Anti-patterns\n\n- \"WIP\", \"fix\", \"changes\" as the final commit or squash title.\n- Force-pushing with plain `--force`, or rewriting `main`, `develop` or a colleague's branch.\n- Rebasing and force-pushing during review without saying so, so reviewers lose their place.\n- One MR containing a feature, a dependency bump and a mass reformat.\n- Hand-merging `pubspec.lock`, `Podfile.lock`, `Package.resolved` or generated code.\n- Resolving `project.pbxproj` conflicts by taking one side wholesale and losing the other side's files.\n- Marking every reviewer thread resolved without replying or changing anything.\n- Screenshots for one platform only on a cross-platform UI change.\n- Descriptions that repeat the diff file by file instead of explaining why.\n- Merging with a red pipeline \"because it is flaky\".\n\n## Checklist\n\n- [ ] Branch follows `<type>/<initials>/<TICKET>-<short-title>` and is based on current main\n- [ ] Every commit is a valid Conventional Commit; breaking changes use `!` and a `BREAKING CHANGE:` footer\n- [ ] MR has one concern and is reviewable in one sitting (or is split or stacked)\n- [ ] Stacked MRs target their parent and say \"Depends on\"\n- [ ] Self-reviewed: no debug logs, commented-out code, secrets, personal data or unrelated files\n- [ ] Build, lint and tests pass for every affected platform after the last rebase or conflict fix\n- [ ] Description has Summary, Ticket, Changes, screenshots (Android and iOS), Testing, Risk and rollback\n- [ ] Permissions, manifest, `Info.plist`, entitlements, SDK levels and migrations are called out\n- [ ] Review fixes are fixup commits; history is cleaned before merge\n- [ ] Force pushes use `--force-with-lease`, on the author's own branch only\n- [ ] Every review thread has a reply; resolved by the agreed party\n- [ ] Squash or rebase-merge chosen deliberately; final commit title is a valid Conventional Commit\n\n## Output format\n\nWhen asked to write an MR or PR description, produce the title and the filled template:\n\n~~~markdown\nTitle: fix(ble): retry GATT connect with exponential backoff (PROJ-123)\n\n## Summary\n<one or two sentences: what changes for the user or the codebase, and why>\n\n## Ticket\nPROJ-123\n\n## Changes\n- <grouped by intent, not by file>\n\n## Screenshots / recordings\n| Android | iOS |\n|---|---|\n| TODO | TODO |\n\n## Testing\n- <tests added or updated, devices and OS versions, manual steps>\n\n## Risk and rollback\n- Risk: <what could break, who is affected>\n- Rollback: <revert MR | disable flag X | server config>\n\n## Checklist\n- [x] <items verified from the diff>\n- [ ] <items the author must confirm>\n~~~\n\nWhen asked to check MR hygiene, produce findings in this shape:\n\n~~~markdown\n## MR hygiene: <branch name>\n\nTarget: main. Commits: 5. Changed: 612 lines in 14 files (180 in lockfiles and generated code).\n\n### Findings\n1. [Must fix] Commit \"wip\" (a1b2c3d) -- not a Conventional Commit.\n   Fix: `git commit --fixup=<target>` or reword before merge.\n2. [Must fix] Description has no iOS screenshots for a shared UI change.\n3. [Should fix] Gradle wrapper bump mixed with feature code -- split into its own MR.\n4. [Note] `Info.plist` gains `NSBluetoothAlwaysUsageDescription` -- call it out under Risk.\n\n### Suggested commands\n```bash\ngit rebase -i --autosquash origin/main\ngit push --force-with-lease\n```\n\n### Verdict\nNot ready -- 2 Must fix.\n~~~\n\nSeverity: Must fix blocks requesting review; Should fix is expected before merge; Note is informational.\n\n## Related skills\n\n- `mobile-code-review` -- reviewing the code inside the MR\n- `mobile-ci-cd` -- pipelines, required checks, release branches and signing\n- `mobile-security` -- when the diff touches secrets, permissions, network or storage\n- `kotlin-android`, `swift-ios`, `dart-flutter` -- build and test commands per platform\n",
    "references/conflicts.md":
        "# Conflict resolution for mobile projects\n\nLoad this when a rebase or merge stops on conflicts. The goal is a result that contains both sides'\nintent and builds, not a result with no conflict markers.\n\n## General procedure\n\n1. See what conflicted and why.\n\n```bash\ngit status\ngit diff --name-only --diff-filter=U            # only the conflicted files\ngit log --oneline --merge -- <file>             # during a merge: commits on both sides touching the file\n```\n\n2. Know which side is which.\n\n| Operation | `--ours` / HEAD | `--theirs` |\n|---|---|---|\n| `git merge origin/main` | your branch | main |\n| `git rebase origin/main` | main (plus your already-replayed commits) | the commit being replayed |\n\n3. Resolve per file using the rules below. Never `git checkout --ours .` or `--theirs .` for the\n   whole tree.\n4. Check nothing is left behind:\n\n```bash\ngit diff --check                                # reports leftover conflict markers\ngit add <file>\ngit rebase --continue                           # or: git merge --continue\n```\n\n5. Rebuild and retest every affected platform before pushing. A clean merge can still be broken.\n\n```bash\n./gradlew assembleDebug testDebugUnitTest lint\nxcodebuild -scheme App -destination 'platform=iOS Simulator,name=<simulator name>' build test\nflutter analyze && flutter test\n```\n\n6. If the conflict is large or unclear, `git rebase --abort` and ask the other author, rather than guess.\n\n## Source files (Kotlin, Java, Swift, Dart)\n\n- Read both versions and the commit messages that produced them. Merge the intent, not the lines.\n- Watch for semantic conflicts that git does not flag: a function renamed on main and called by its old\n  name in your branch, a new required parameter, a changed default. The build catches most; tests catch\n  the rest.\n- Imports: keep the union, then let the formatter or IDE remove unused ones.\n\n## Lockfiles\n\nNever hand-merge a lockfile. Resolve the manifest, take main's lockfile, regenerate.\n\n| Lockfile | Manifest to resolve first | Regenerate with |\n|---|---|---|\n| `pubspec.lock` | `pubspec.yaml` | `flutter pub get` (or `dart pub get`) |\n| `Podfile.lock` | `Podfile` | `pod install` (not `pod update`, which bumps everything) |\n| `Package.resolved` | Xcode project or `Package.swift` | `xcodebuild -resolvePackageDependencies`, or resolve packages in Xcode |\n| `gradle.lockfile` (dependency locking) | `libs.versions.toml`, `build.gradle.kts` | the project's lock task, for example `./gradlew dependencies --write-locks` per affected module |\n\n```bash\n# during a rebase, --ours is main's version\ngit checkout --ours pubspec.lock\nflutter pub get\ngit add pubspec.lock\n```\n\nReview the regenerated lockfile diff: only the packages your branch intended to change should move.\n\n## Generated code\n\nDo not hand-merge generated output. Resolve the sources, then regenerate.\n\n- Dart `*.g.dart`, `*.freezed.dart`, mocks: `dart run build_runner build --delete-conflicting-outputs`.\n- Flutter localizations: `flutter gen-l10n` after merging the `.arb` files.\n- Protobuf or OpenAPI generated clients: rerun the generator from the merged schema.\n- Room exported schemas (`schemas/<Database>/<version>.json`): a conflict here usually means both\n  branches bumped the database to the same version. This is a real conflict: renumber one branch's\n  migration to the next version, then rebuild so the schema is exported again.\n\nIf generated files should not be in git at all, raise it as a separate MR.\n\n## Xcode `project.pbxproj`\n\n- Each added file appears in several sections (file reference, build file, group children, build\n  phase). Keep both sides' entries in every section; dropping one side silently removes files from\n  the target.\n- Remove only the conflict markers and true duplicates (same object ID listed twice).\n- Validate the file and then build:\n\n```bash\nplutil -lint App.xcodeproj/project.pbxproj\nxcodebuild -scheme App -destination 'platform=iOS Simulator,name=<simulator name>' build\n```\n\n- Open the project in Xcode and confirm the files from both sides appear in the right targets.\n- If the file is badly mangled, abort, take main's version and re-add your files in Xcode.\n- Prevention: synchronized folder groups (Xcode 16 and later) stop most per-file entries; project\n  generators (XcodeGen, Tuist) avoid committing the project file altogether.\n- Do not set `merge=union` for `*.pbxproj` in `.gitattributes`; it produces duplicate or dangling\n  entries that only fail later.\n\n## Gradle version catalog (`libs.versions.toml`)\n\n- New entries from both sides: keep both.\n- Same alias, different versions: choose deliberately, usually the newer one, and check compatibility\n  (Kotlin and Compose compiler, AGP and Gradle, BOM-managed libraries).\n- Same library added under two different aliases: keep one alias and update usages.\n- Keep each table sorted alphabetically; it makes future conflicts rare and trivial.\n- Run a Gradle sync and a full build after resolving; a catalog mistake fails at configuration time.\n\n## Android resources and manifest\n\n- `strings.xml` and translations: keep both sides' keys; make sure no key was renamed on one side.\n- `AndroidManifest.xml`: keep both sides' components and permissions, remove duplicates, and recheck\n  `android:exported` on every component either side added.\n- Navigation graphs and XML layouts: resolve by hand and open the editor preview to confirm.\n\n## iOS resources\n\n- `Localizable.xcstrings` is JSON: keep both sides' keys, then confirm it still parses by building.\n- `Info.plist` and entitlements: keep both sides' keys and call out the result in the MR description.\n- Asset catalogs (`Contents.json`): keep both entries; duplicated asset names fail the build.\n\n## After resolving\n\n- [ ] No conflict markers (`git diff --check`)\n- [ ] Lockfiles and generated code regenerated, not hand-edited\n- [ ] Build and tests green for every affected platform\n- [ ] `git range-diff origin/main @{u} HEAD` reviewed before `git push --force-with-lease`\n- [ ] Non-trivial resolutions mentioned in an MR comment so reviewers can check them\n",
    "references/git-commands.md":
        "# Git commands for the MR workflow\n\nLoad this for exact command sequences. Everything here assumes the remote is `origin` and the\ntarget branch is `main`; substitute the project's names. Commands that rewrite history are for the\nauthor's own, unshared branch only, and the agent runs them only after the user agrees.\n\n## One-time setup\n\n```bash\ngit config --global push.autoSetupRemote true   # first push of a new branch sets upstream\ngit config --global rebase.autoSquash true      # fixup! commits are folded automatically in rebase -i\ngit config --global rerere.enabled true         # remember conflict resolutions across rebases\ngit config --global pull.rebase true            # pulling your own branch rebases instead of merging\n```\n\n## Start a branch\n\n```bash\ngit fetch origin\ngit switch -c feature/AH/PROJ-123-ble-reconnect-backoff origin/main\n```\n\n## Inspect before opening the MR\n\n```bash\ngit log --oneline origin/main..HEAD             # commits that will be in the MR\ngit diff --stat --merge-base origin/main        # size and touched files\ngit diff --merge-base origin/main               # full diff, as the reviewer will see it\ngit diff --check --merge-base origin/main       # whitespace errors and leftover conflict markers\n```\n\n## Commit and amend\n\n```bash\ngit add -p                                      # stage hunk by hunk, keeps commits focused\ngit commit -m \"fix(ble): retry GATT connect with exponential backoff\" -m \"Refs: PROJ-123\"\ngit commit --amend                              # last commit only, before it is pushed or reviewed\n```\n\n## Review fixes as fixups\n\n```bash\ngit commit --fixup=a1b2c3d                      # content fix for commit a1b2c3d\ngit commit --fixup=reword:a1b2c3d               # message-only fix for commit a1b2c3d\ngit rebase -i --autosquash origin/main          # after approval: folds fixups into their targets\n```\n\n## Update a branch with main\n\n```bash\ngit fetch origin\ngit rebase origin/main                          # own branch only\ngit range-diff origin/main @{u} HEAD            # compare pushed commits with rebased commits\ngit push --force-with-lease                     # refuses if someone else pushed since your fetch\n```\n\n- `git range-diff` shows per-commit differences between the old and new series. Use it before every\n  force push to confirm the rebase did not drop or alter anything unintended.\n- `--force-with-lease` protects against overwriting commits you have not fetched. Run it right after\n  your own `fetch`, not after an automated background fetch that may have updated the lease.\n\nFor shared branches, merge instead of rebasing:\n\n```bash\ngit switch release/3.2\ngit merge --no-ff origin/main\ngit push\n```\n\n## Open the MR from the command line\n\n```bash\n# GitLab push options: create the MR on push\ngit push -o merge_request.create -o merge_request.target=main\n\n# GitHub CLI\ngh pr create --base main --title \"fix(ble): retry GATT connect with exponential backoff (PROJ-123)\" \\\n  --body-file pr-description.md\ngh pr create --draft --base main --fill\n\n# GitLab CLI\nglab mr create --target-branch main --draft\n```\n\n## Stacked MRs\n\nCreate the stack:\n\n```bash\ngit switch -c feature/AH/PROJ-123-pairing-api origin/main\n# ...commits, push, MR 1 targets main\ngit switch -c feature/AH/PROJ-124-pairing-ui\n# ...commits, push, MR 2 targets feature/AH/PROJ-123-pairing-api\n```\n\nParent changed during review (new fixups on PROJ-123), update the child:\n\n```bash\ngit switch feature/AH/PROJ-124-pairing-ui\ngit rebase feature/AH/PROJ-123-pairing-api\ngit push --force-with-lease\n```\n\nOr rebase the whole stack at once from the top branch; intermediate branches move with it:\n\n```bash\ngit switch feature/AH/PROJ-124-pairing-ui\ngit rebase --update-refs origin/main\ngit push --force-with-lease origin feature/AH/PROJ-123-pairing-api feature/AH/PROJ-124-pairing-ui\n```\n\nParent was squash-merged into main. The child still contains the parent's original commits, which\nmain no longer has. Replay only the child's own commits:\n\n```bash\ngit fetch origin\n# local feature/AH/PROJ-123-pairing-api still points at the parent's pre-merge tip\ngit rebase --onto origin/main feature/AH/PROJ-123-pairing-api feature/AH/PROJ-124-pairing-ui\ngit push --force-with-lease\n```\n\nThen retarget MR 2 to `main` in the MR settings. Both GitLab and GitHub can retarget dependent MRs\nautomatically when the parent branch is deleted on merge; check the MR target afterwards anyway.\n\n## Undo and recover\n\n```bash\ngit rebase --abort                              # back to before the rebase started\ngit merge --abort                               # back to before the merge started\ngit reflog                                      # find the commit you had before a bad rebase\ngit switch -c rescue/PROJ-123 HEAD@{2}          # recover it on a new branch, nothing is lost\ngit revert <sha>                                # undo a merged commit on a shared branch\ngit revert -m 1 <merge-sha>                     # undo a merged MR that landed as a merge commit\n```\n\nPrefer recovery onto a new branch over moving the current branch, so nothing is overwritten.\n\n## Commands the agent does not run without explicit approval\n\n- `git push --force-with-lease` or any push that rewrites remote history.\n- `git rebase` of a branch that has an open MR under review.\n- Merging an MR, deleting a branch, or changing branch protection.\n- Plain `git push --force` is never suggested.\n",
    "references/mr-template.md":
        "# MR template and reviewer etiquette\n\nLoad this when writing or improving an MR or PR description, or when reviewing or answering review\ncomments. Copy the template into `.gitlab/merge_request_templates/Default.md` (GitLab) or\n`.github/pull_request_template.md` (GitHub) to make it the project default.\n\n## Title\n\n- Same rules as a commit subject: `<type>(<scope>): <imperative summary> (<TICKET>)`.\n- It becomes the squash commit, so it must stand alone in `git log --oneline`.\n- Draft work: prefix `Draft:` on GitLab, or open as a draft PR on GitHub (`gh pr create --draft`).\n\n```text\n# BAD\nPROJ-123\nFixes\nFeature/AH/PROJ-123 pairing\n\n# GOOD\nfeat(onboarding): add QR pairing screen (PROJ-123)\n```\n\n## Full template\n\n~~~markdown\n## Summary\n<!-- One or two sentences: what changes for the user or the codebase, and why now. -->\n\n## Ticket\n<!-- PROJ-123. Add \"Closes #123\" if the tracker should close it on merge. -->\n<!-- Stacked: \"Depends on !122\" (GitLab) or \"Depends on #122\" (GitHub). -->\n\n## Changes\n<!-- Grouped by intent, not by file. Mention what reviewers should look at first. -->\n- \n- \n\n## Screenshots / recordings\n<!-- Required for any user-visible change. Before and after. Light and dark if theming is touched. -->\n| | Android | iOS |\n|---|---|---|\n| Before | | |\n| After | | |\n\n## Testing\n<!-- What proves this works. -->\n- Unit tests:\n- UI / instrumentation tests:\n- Manual: device, OS version, steps, result\n- Not tested (and why):\n\n## Risk and rollback\n- Risk: <!-- what could break, which users, which app versions -->\n- Migration: <!-- database, preferences, keychain, payload format; none if none -->\n- Rollback: <!-- revert this MR | turn off flag X | server-side switch -->\n\n## Platform and compliance notes\n<!-- Delete lines that do not apply. -->\n- New permissions / manifest entries:\n- `Info.plist` keys / entitlements / background modes:\n- `minSdk` / `targetSdk` / `compileSdk` / deployment target:\n- Privacy manifest (`PrivacyInfo.xcprivacy`) / data safety form impact:\n- Telemetry or analytics payload changes (personal data?):\n- New third-party dependency (license, size, maintenance):\n\n## Checklist\n- [ ] Self-reviewed the full diff\n- [ ] Commits follow Conventional Commits; breaking changes marked\n- [ ] Build, lint and tests pass locally for Android and iOS (or the affected platform)\n- [ ] New logic in domain and data layers has unit tests\n- [ ] No secrets, tokens, keys or personal data in code, logs or fixtures\n- [ ] Strings localized; accessibility labels added for new controls\n- [ ] Screenshots for both platforms attached\n- [ ] Docs, changelog or release notes updated if needed\n~~~\n\n## Filling it in well\n\n- Summary is about behaviour, not files. \"Users can pair by scanning a QR code\" beats\n  \"Added PairingFragment.kt\".\n- Changes lists decisions a reviewer might question: \"Kept the old pairing flow behind\n  `legacy_pairing` flag until 3.3\".\n- Testing names real devices and OS versions. \"Tested on emulator\" alone is weak for BLE, camera,\n  background work or push notifications.\n- Risk is never \"none\" for a change that touches persistence, networking, BLE or auth. Say what was\n  considered.\n- Remove empty sections rather than leaving template comments in the final description.\n\n## Reviewer etiquette\n\n### When reviewing\n\n- Respond within one working day, even if only to say when a full review will happen.\n- Read the description and ticket first; review against intent.\n- Review the whole change before commenting, so early comments are not invalidated by later code.\n- Label every comment so the author knows what blocks:\n\n| Label | Meaning | Blocks merge |\n|---|---|---|\n| `issue:` | A defect or a broken requirement | Yes |\n| `suggestion:` | A better approach, with reason | Only if marked `(blocking)` |\n| `question:` | Need information to judge | Until answered |\n| `nit:` | Preference or polish | Never |\n| `praise:` | Something done well, say why | Never |\n| `todo:` | Small required follow-up | Yes |\n\n- Propose the fix. Both GitLab and GitHub support suggestion blocks the author can apply in one click:\n\n~~~markdown\nsuggestion: keep the scan timeout in one place.\n```suggestion\nprivate const val SCAN_TIMEOUT_MS = 10_000L\n```\n~~~\n\n- Comment on the code, not the person: \"this call can run on a binder thread\", not \"you forgot threading\".\n- Out-of-scope problems: mention once, suggest a ticket, do not block.\n- Approve when remaining items are nits. Do not hold an MR hostage for preferences.\n\n### When being reviewed\n\n- Reply to every thread. A silent resolve looks like the comment was ignored.\n- Short replies that point to the change: \"Done in a1b2c3d\", \"Moved to `BackoffPolicy`, see fixup e4f5a6b\".\n- Disagree with reasons and evidence, once. If still unresolved, agree on a decision maker or a\n  follow-up ticket, not a long thread.\n- Push fixes as fixup commits during review so reviewers can see what changed since their last pass.\n- Re-request review after a round of fixes; do not assume reviewers watch the MR.\n\n### Resolving threads\n\n- Blocking threads (`issue:`, `todo:`, `question:`) are resolved by the reviewer who opened them,\n  after checking the fix.\n- Nits and applied suggestions can be resolved by the author with a reply.\n- Turn on \"all threads must be resolved\" (GitLab) or \"require conversation resolution\" (GitHub branch\n  protection) so the rule is enforced, not remembered.\n",
  },
  "mobile-mvc": {
    "SKILL.md":
        "---\nname: mobile-mvc\ndescription: \"Applies and repairs controller-based mobile architectures: UIKit MVC in Swift (Massive View Controller, child view controllers, coordinators for navigation, separate UITableViewDataSource and UICollectionViewDiffableDataSource objects, moving networking and formatting out of the view controller), legacy Android Activity-as-controller and MVP in Java and Kotlin (Contract interface, presenter, view interface, attach and detach to avoid leaks), and the Flutter controller pattern in Dart (ChangeNotifier or ValueNotifier controllers separated from widgets, owned and disposed by State). Plans step-by-step strangler migrations from MVC or MVP to MVVM one screen at a time, with characterization tests first. Use when working on UIViewController, Activity, Fragment or presenter code, splitting a god class, reviewing legacy screens, deciding whether MVC is still fine, or migrating a legacy screen to a ViewModel.\"\nlicense: MIT\nmetadata:\n  author: Ahmad Hussain\n  version: \"1.0.0\"\n  pack: mobile-dev-kit\n---\n\n# Mobile MVC and MVP\n\nKeep controller-based screens small, testable and leak-free, and move them to MVVM safely when that pays\noff. Covers UIKit MVC, legacy Android Activity-as-controller and MVP, and Flutter controllers.\n\n## When to use\n\n- A `UIViewController`, `Activity` or `Fragment` has grown past a few hundred lines and mixes\n  networking, formatting, navigation and view code.\n- Adding a feature to a legacy MVC or MVP screen without rewriting it.\n- Reviewing presenter code for leaks, or a Flutter widget whose `State` holds all the logic.\n- Deciding whether a screen should stay MVC or move to MVVM.\n- Planning or executing a screen-by-screen migration to ViewModels.\n\nNot for: designing new screens in MVVM or MVI from scratch (use `mobile-mvvm`, `mobile-mvi`).\n\n## Workflow\n\n1. Identify the platform and pattern actually in use, and load the matching reference:\n   [UIKit](references/uikit.md), [Android legacy and MVP](references/android-legacy.md),\n   [Flutter controllers](references/flutter.md).\n2. Map the controller's responsibilities. For each method, tag it: view setup, layout, data source,\n   networking, persistence, formatting, validation, navigation, lifecycle.\n3. Decide: keep, slim down, or migrate (see \"When MVC is still fine\" below). Prefer the smallest change\n   that fixes the actual pain.\n4. Before moving code, add characterization tests around behaviour that will move (formatting,\n   validation, state transitions). If logic is untestable in place, extract it as a pure function first.\n5. Extract one responsibility at a time, in this order: formatting, networking and persistence behind a\n   service, data source objects, navigation into a coordinator or navigator, child controllers for\n   independent regions.\n6. Fix lifecycle on the way: presenters detach, observers are removed, tasks are cancelled, controllers\n   are disposed.\n7. If migrating, follow [migration to MVVM](references/migration-to-mvvm.md): one screen per MR,\n   ViewModel introduced behind the existing controller, state moved field by field.\n8. Build, run the tests, and exercise the screen on device, including rotation (Android) and\n   background and foreground.\n\n## When MVC is still fine\n\n- Static or nearly static screens: settings lists, about pages, legal text, simple forms.\n- Thin wrappers around system controllers (pickers, mail compose, share sheets, web views).\n- Screens with no business rules and no state that must survive configuration change.\n- Code scheduled for deletion. Do not migrate what is about to be removed.\n- A team or codebase where MVC is consistent and screens stay small. Consistency beats a half-migrated mix.\n\nMigrate when: logic needs unit tests, state is lost on rotation or process death, the same logic is\nduplicated across screens, or the controller cannot be changed without regressions.\n\n## Rules\n\n### 1. The controller owns views, not networking or persistence\n\nSwift:\n```swift\n// BAD: view controller builds requests and decodes JSON\noverride func viewDidLoad() {\n    super.viewDidLoad()\n    URLSession.shared.dataTask(with: URL(string: \"https://api.example.com/devices\")!) { data, _, _ in\n        let devices = try! JSONDecoder().decode([Device].self, from: data!)\n        DispatchQueue.main.async { self.devices = devices; self.tableView.reloadData() }\n    }.resume()\n}\n\n// GOOD: injected service, the controller only renders\nfinal class DevicesViewController: UIViewController {\n    private let service: DeviceService\n    init(service: DeviceService) {\n        self.service = service\n        super.init(nibName: nil, bundle: nil)\n    }\n    required init?(coder: NSCoder) { fatalError(\"init(coder:) is not supported\") }\n}\n```\n\nJava:\n```java\n// BAD: Activity opens sockets and parses responses\nnew Thread(() -> {\n    String json = httpGet(\"https://api.example.com/devices\");\n    runOnUiThread(() -> adapter.submit(parse(json)));\n}).start();\n\n// GOOD: presenter asks a repository; the Activity only renders\npresenter.loadDevices();\n```\n\n### 2. Navigation is decided outside the screen\n\nSwift:\n```swift\n// BAD: the screen knows the next screen and how to build it\nlet detail = DeviceDetailViewController(service: DeviceServiceImpl(), device: device)\nnavigationController?.pushViewController(detail, animated: true)\n\n// GOOD: the screen reports intent; a coordinator decides\nvar onSelectDevice: ((Device.ID) -> Void)?\nfunc tableView(_ tableView: UITableView, didSelectRowAt indexPath: IndexPath) {\n    guard let id = dataSource.itemIdentifier(for: indexPath) else { return }\n    onSelectDevice?(id)\n}\n```\n\nKotlin:\n```kotlin\n// BAD: presenter starts Activities\nclass DevicesPresenter(private val activity: Activity) {\n    fun onDeviceClicked(id: String) = activity.startActivity(Intent(activity, DetailActivity::class.java))\n}\n\n// GOOD: presenter calls a view method; the Activity or a navigator performs it\ninterface DevicesView { fun openDetail(deviceId: String) }\n```\n\n### 3. Data sources and delegates are separate objects\n\nSwift:\n```swift\n// BAD: index-based data source in the controller, reloadData on every change\nextension DevicesViewController: UITableViewDataSource {\n    func tableView(_ t: UITableView, numberOfRowsInSection s: Int) -> Int { devices.count }\n    func tableView(_ t: UITableView, cellForRowAt i: IndexPath) -> UITableViewCell { ... }\n}\n\n// GOOD: diffable data source keyed by stable ids, owned by the controller, configured once\nprivate lazy var dataSource = UITableViewDiffableDataSource<Section, Device.ID>(tableView: tableView) {\n    [weak self] tableView, indexPath, id in\n    self?.cellProvider.cell(in: tableView, at: indexPath, for: id)\n}\n```\n\nKotlin:\n```kotlin\n// BAD: notifyDataSetChanged on every update\nadapter.items = devices; adapter.notifyDataSetChanged()\n\n// GOOD: ListAdapter with DiffUtil, stable ids in areItemsTheSame\nadapter.submitList(devices)\n```\n\n### 4. Formatting lives in formatters, not in cells or controllers\n\nSwift:\n```swift\n// BAD: new formatter per cell, logic untestable\ncell.detail.text = { let f = DateFormatter(); f.dateFormat = \"dd.MM.yyyy\"; return f.string(from: d.lastSeen) }()\n\n// GOOD: a testable row model built outside the cell\nstruct DeviceRow { let title: String; let lastSeen: String }\nextension DeviceRow {\n    init(_ d: Device) {\n        title = d.name\n        lastSeen = d.lastSeen.formatted(date: .abbreviated, time: .shortened)\n    }\n}\n```\n\nDart:\n```dart\n// BAD: formatting inside build\nText('\${(device.battery * 100).round()}% - \${device.lastSeen.toLocal()}')\n\n// GOOD: controller or row model exposes display strings, tested without widgets\nText(row.batteryLabel)\n```\n\n### 5. A presenter never outlives its view\n\nJava:\n```java\n// BAD: presenter keeps the Activity after onDestroy; async callback touches a dead view\n@Override public void onDevicesLoaded(List<Device> devices) { view.showDevices(devices); }\n\n// GOOD: attach and detach with the lifecycle; callbacks check the view\npublic void attach(DevicesContract.View view) { this.view = view; }\npublic void detach() { this.view = null; cancelPending(); }\n\n@Override public void onDevicesLoaded(List<Device> devices) {\n    if (view != null) view.showDevices(devices);\n}\n// Activity: presenter.detach() in onDestroy()\n```\n\n- `WeakReference<View>` hides the leak but keeps the work running. Cancel the work too.\n\n### 6. Presenters and controllers are free of framework types\n\nKotlin:\n```kotlin\n// BAD: presenter needs Context and a Looper, so it cannot run in a plain JUnit test\nclass LoginPresenter(private val context: Context) {\n    fun onLogin() = Toast.makeText(context, context.getString(R.string.error), Toast.LENGTH_SHORT).show()\n}\n\n// GOOD: presenter depends on interfaces; the view decides how to show an error\nclass LoginPresenter(private val auth: AuthRepository, private val scope: CoroutineScope) {\n    private var view: LoginContract.View? = null\n    fun onLogin(email: String, password: String) {\n        scope.launch {\n            val result = auth.login(email, password)\n            view?.render(if (result.isSuccess) LoginState.Done else LoginState.Error(LoginError.InvalidCredentials))\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\nSwift: controllers' helpers (formatters, validators, services) import `Foundation`, not `UIKit`.\n\n### 7. Flutter controllers are owned by State and disposed\n\nDart:\n```dart\n// BAD: controller created in build, logic in State, never disposed\n@override\nWidget build(BuildContext context) {\n  final controller = PairingController(repo);\n  return ListenableBuilder(listenable: controller, builder: (_, __) => Text(controller.status));\n}\n\n// GOOD\nclass _PairingScreenState extends State<PairingScreen> {\n  late final PairingController _controller = PairingController(widget.repo);\n\n  @override\n  void dispose() {\n    _controller.dispose();\n    super.dispose();\n  }\n\n  @override\n  Widget build(BuildContext context) => ListenableBuilder(\n        listenable: _controller,\n        builder: (context, _) => Text(_controller.status),\n      );\n}\n```\n\n### 8. Async results check that the screen is still there\n\nSwift:\n```swift\n// BAD: retains the controller and updates it after it was popped\nloadTask = Task { self.render(try await service.devices()) }\n\n// GOOD: weak self, capture only the service, cancel on disappear\nloadTask = Task { [weak self, service] in\n    do {\n        let devices = try await service.devices()\n        self?.render(devices)\n    } catch {\n        self?.showError(error)\n    }\n}\noverride func viewDidDisappear(_ animated: Bool) {\n    super.viewDidDisappear(animated)\n    loadTask?.cancel()\n}\n```\n\nDart:\n```dart\n// BAD\nawait _controller.pair();\nNavigator.of(context).pop();\n\n// GOOD\nawait _controller.pair();\nif (!mounted) return;\nNavigator.of(context).pop();\n```\n\n### 9. Dependencies come in through the initializer or constructor\n\nSwift:\n```swift\n// BAD: hidden singletons, impossible to fake\nlet devices = try await APIClient.shared.devices()\n\n// GOOD: storyboard screens can still use init injection via a custom init?(coder:service:)\nlet vc = storyboard.instantiateViewController(identifier: \"Devices\") { coder in\n    DevicesViewController(coder: coder, service: service)\n}\n```\n\nKotlin:\n```kotlin\n// BAD\nprivate val repo = DeviceRepository.getInstance()\n\n// GOOD: presenter constructed with its dependencies (Hilt or a manual factory)\nclass DevicesPresenter @Inject constructor(private val repo: DeviceRepository)\n```\n\n### 10. Migrate one screen at a time, tests first\n\n```text\n# BAD\nrefactor: migrate whole app to MVVM (214 files)\n\n# GOOD: one screen, three small MRs\ntest(devices): characterization tests for DevicesPresenter\nrefactor(devices): add DevicesViewModel behind DevicesActivity\nrefactor(devices): move device list state to DevicesViewModel, remove presenter\n```\n\nDetails and code: [migration to MVVM](references/migration-to-mvvm.md).\n\n## Anti-patterns\n\n- Massive View Controller: networking, parsing, formatting, data source, delegate and navigation in one\n  class with a dozen `extension`s that only hide the size.\n- \"Manager\" or \"Helper\" singletons that every controller calls, replacing one god class with a global one.\n- Presenter holding `Activity` or `Context` and never detached; work keeps running after `onDestroy`.\n- Using `AsyncTask` (deprecated) or Loaders (deprecated) in maintained code.\n- Child view controllers added as subviews without `addChild` and `didMove(toParent:)`, so appearance\n  callbacks and trait changes do not propagate.\n- Coordinators holding strong references to the view controllers they push and never releasing them.\n- Flutter `State` classes with hundreds of lines of business logic and `setState` calls from async code.\n- Big-bang migration of every screen in one MR, or migrating screens that work and are not changing.\n- Introducing a ViewModel but leaving the old state in the controller too, so there are two sources of truth.\n\n## Checklist\n\n- [ ] Controller code is view setup, binding and lifecycle only\n- [ ] Networking and persistence behind injected services or repositories\n- [ ] Formatting and validation in testable types, not in cells, widgets or controllers\n- [ ] Table and collection data sources are separate objects, diffable or DiffUtil based\n- [ ] Navigation goes through a coordinator, navigator or view callback\n- [ ] Child view controllers use the full containment calls\n- [ ] Presenters attach and detach with the lifecycle and cancel pending work\n- [ ] Presenters and helpers do not import `android.*` or `UIKit`\n- [ ] Flutter controllers created in `initState` or a `late final` field and disposed in `dispose`\n- [ ] Async callbacks check `mounted`, a non-null view or `[weak self]` before updating UI\n- [ ] Characterization tests exist before logic is moved\n- [ ] Migration is one screen per MR, with one source of truth for each piece of state\n\n## Related skills\n\n- `mobile-mvvm` -- target architecture when migrating\n- `mobile-mvi` -- alternative target for complex, event-heavy screens\n- `mobile-design-patterns` -- coordinator, adapter, strategy and facade patterns used here\n- `swift-ios`, `java-android`, `kotlin-android`, `dart-flutter` -- language rules for the code being moved\n- `mobile-code-review` -- reviewing the refactoring MRs\n",
    "references/android-legacy.md":
        "# Android legacy: Activity as controller and MVP\n\nLoad this when the screen is a Java or Kotlin `Activity` or `Fragment` with logic in it, or uses an MVP\npresenter. New Android screens should use MVVM with Compose; this reference is for maintaining and\nmigrating what exists.\n\n## Symptoms of a god Activity\n\n- `onCreate` longer than one screen; network calls, SQL and string formatting inline.\n- State in fields that is lost on rotation, or saved field by field in `onSaveInstanceState`.\n- `AsyncTask`, raw `Thread`, or `Handler` callbacks that touch views after `onDestroy`.\n- `onActivityResult` with a switch over request codes for camera, permissions and other screens.\n\n## MVP contract\n\nOne contract per screen. The view interface is what the presenter may ask of the UI; the presenter\ninterface is what the UI reports.\n\nJava:\n```java\npublic interface DevicesContract {\n    interface View {\n        void showLoading(boolean loading);\n        void showDevices(List<DeviceRow> rows);\n        void showError(@StringRes int message);\n        void openDetail(String deviceId);\n    }\n\n    interface Presenter {\n        void attach(View view);\n        void detach();\n        void loadDevices();\n        void onDeviceClicked(String deviceId);\n    }\n}\n```\n\n`@StringRes` is an annotation from `androidx.annotation`, which is acceptable in a presenter; avoid\nanything that needs a `Context`.\n\n## Presenter with lifecycle-safe callbacks\n\nJava:\n```java\npublic final class DevicesPresenter implements DevicesContract.Presenter {\n    private final DeviceRepository repository;\n    @Nullable private DevicesContract.View view;\n    @Nullable private Cancellable pending;\n\n    public DevicesPresenter(DeviceRepository repository) {\n        this.repository = repository;\n    }\n\n    @Override public void attach(DevicesContract.View view) { this.view = view; }\n\n    @Override public void detach() {\n        view = null;\n        if (pending != null) pending.cancel();\n    }\n\n    @Override public void loadDevices() {\n        if (view != null) view.showLoading(true);\n        pending = repository.loadDevices(new Callback<List<Device>>() {\n            @Override public void onSuccess(List<Device> devices) {\n                if (view == null) return;\n                view.showLoading(false);\n                view.showDevices(DeviceRow.from(devices));\n            }\n            @Override public void onError(Throwable error) {\n                if (view == null) return;\n                view.showLoading(false);\n                view.showError(R.string.devices_error);\n            }\n        });\n    }\n\n    @Override public void onDeviceClicked(String deviceId) {\n        if (view != null) view.openDetail(deviceId);\n    }\n}\n```\n\n`Cancellable` and `Callback` stand for whatever the project's repository returns. The point is that\ndetach both drops the view and cancels the work.\n\nCallbacks from a repository may arrive on a background thread. Either the repository posts to the main\nthread, or the presenter does; never call view methods from a worker thread.\n\nKotlin presenter with coroutines:\n```kotlin\nclass DevicesPresenter(\n    private val repository: DeviceRepository,\n    mainDispatcher: CoroutineDispatcher,\n) : DevicesContract.Presenter {\n    private val scope = CoroutineScope(SupervisorJob() + mainDispatcher)\n    private var view: DevicesContract.View? = null\n\n    override fun attach(view: DevicesContract.View) { this.view = view }\n\n    override fun detach() {\n        view = null\n        scope.coroutineContext.cancelChildren()\n    }\n\n    override fun loadDevices() {\n        scope.launch {\n            view?.showLoading(true)\n            val result = runCatchingNonCancellable { repository.devices() }\n            view?.showLoading(false)\n            result.onSuccess { view?.showDevices(DeviceRow.from(it)) }\n                .onFailure { view?.showError(R.string.devices_error) }\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n`runCatchingNonCancellable` stands for a project helper that rethrows `CancellationException`; plain\n`runCatching` around suspend calls swallows cancellation.\n\n## Wiring in the Activity and Fragment\n\nJava Activity:\n```java\n@Override protected void onCreate(@Nullable Bundle savedInstanceState) {\n    super.onCreate(savedInstanceState);\n    binding = ActivityDevicesBinding.inflate(getLayoutInflater());\n    setContentView(binding.getRoot());\n    presenter.attach(this);\n    presenter.loadDevices();\n}\n\n@Override protected void onDestroy() {\n    presenter.detach();\n    super.onDestroy();\n}\n```\n\nFragment: attach in `onViewCreated`, detach in `onDestroyView`, and clear the view binding there too.\nThe fragment instance outlives its view, so `onDestroy` is too late.\n\nLimitations to state in reviews: an MVP presenter created in `onCreate` is recreated on rotation, so\nin-flight work and loaded data are lost. This is the most common reason to migrate the screen to a\nViewModel (see [migration to MVVM](migration-to-mvvm.md)).\n\n## Deprecated APIs to replace while touching legacy screens\n\n| Legacy | Replacement |\n|---|---|\n| `AsyncTask` | Coroutines in a ViewModel, or an executor owned by a lifecycle-aware component |\n| Loaders (`LoaderManager`) | ViewModel with `StateFlow` or `LiveData` |\n| `startActivityForResult` / `onActivityResult` | `registerForActivityResult` with an `ActivityResultContract` |\n| `requestPermissions` / `onRequestPermissionsResult` | `ActivityResultContracts.RequestPermission` |\n| `onBackPressed()` override | `OnBackPressedCallback` on `onBackPressedDispatcher` |\n| `Handler()` with no Looper | `Handler(Looper.getMainLooper())`, or coroutines |\n| Kotlin synthetics (`kotlin-android-extensions`) | View Binding |\n| `ProgressDialog` | An inline progress indicator in the layout |\n\n- Back handling: with predictive back enabled (the default when targeting Android 16), `onBackPressed()`\n  is not called. Legacy screens that override it must move to `OnBackPressedCallback`.\n- Orientation: when targeting Android 16, large-screen devices ignore orientation locks and\n  resizability restrictions. A legacy Activity that locked portrait to avoid losing state on rotation\n  will now lose it on tablets and foldables. Fix the state handling, do not rely on the lock.\n\n## Testing presenters\n\n```kotlin\n@Test fun `shows devices after load`() = runTest {\n    val view = mockk<DevicesContract.View>(relaxed = true)\n    val presenter = DevicesPresenter(FakeDeviceRepository(listOf(device)), StandardTestDispatcher(testScheduler))\n    presenter.attach(view)\n\n    presenter.loadDevices()\n    advanceUntilIdle()\n\n    verifyOrder {\n        view.showLoading(true)\n        view.showLoading(false)\n        view.showDevices(DeviceRow.from(listOf(device)))\n    }\n}\n\n@Test fun `no view calls after detach`() = runTest {\n    val view = mockk<DevicesContract.View>(relaxed = true)\n    val presenter = DevicesPresenter(SlowDeviceRepository(), StandardTestDispatcher(testScheduler))\n    presenter.attach(view)\n    presenter.loadDevices()\n    presenter.detach()\n    advanceUntilIdle()\n\n    verify(exactly = 0) { view.showDevices(any()) }\n}\n```\n\nThe second test is the leak guard: it fails if detach does not cancel the work.\n",
    "references/flutter.md":
        "# Flutter controller pattern\n\nLoad this when a Flutter screen keeps its logic in a `State` class, or uses `ChangeNotifier` or\n`ValueNotifier` controllers. The model is the framework's own controllers (`TextEditingController`,\n`ScrollController`): a plain object that holds state and logic, notifies listeners, and is owned and\ndisposed by a `State`.\n\n## Before: logic in State\n\n```dart\n// BAD: networking, validation and formatting in the State; untestable without pumping widgets\nclass _PairingScreenState extends State<PairingScreen> {\n  bool _loading = false;\n  String? _error;\n\n  Future<void> _pair(String code) async {\n    if (code.length != 8) {\n      setState(() => _error = 'Invalid code');\n      return;\n    }\n    setState(() => _loading = true);\n    final response = await http.post(Uri.parse('https://api.example.com/pair'), body: code);\n    setState(() => _loading = false);\n    if (response.statusCode != 200) setState(() => _error = 'Pairing failed');\n  }\n}\n```\n\n## After: controller plus thin widget\n\n```dart\nenum PairingStatus { idle, pairing, paired, failed }\n\nclass PairingController extends ChangeNotifier {\n  PairingController(this._repository);\n\n  final PairingRepository _repository;\n  PairingStatus _status = PairingStatus.idle;\n  String? _errorKey;\n  bool _disposed = false;\n\n  PairingStatus get status => _status;\n  String? get errorKey => _errorKey;\n  bool get canSubmit => _status != PairingStatus.pairing;\n\n  static bool isValidCode(String code) => RegExp(r'^[A-Z0-9]{8}\$').hasMatch(code);\n\n  Future<void> pair(String code) async {\n    if (!isValidCode(code)) {\n      _set(PairingStatus.failed, 'invalidCode');\n      return;\n    }\n    _set(PairingStatus.pairing, null);\n    try {\n      await _repository.pair(code);\n      _set(PairingStatus.paired, null);\n    } on PairingException {\n      _set(PairingStatus.failed, 'pairingFailed');\n    }\n  }\n\n  void _set(PairingStatus status, String? errorKey) {\n    if (_disposed) return;\n    _status = status;\n    _errorKey = errorKey;\n    notifyListeners();\n  }\n\n  @override\n  void dispose() {\n    _disposed = true;\n    super.dispose();\n  }\n}\n```\n\n- State is private; the widget reads getters and calls methods.\n- The controller does not import `material.dart` widgets or hold a `BuildContext`. It exposes error keys\n  or enums; the widget maps them to localized strings.\n- `notifyListeners()` after `dispose()` throws, which is why async methods check `_disposed`.\n\n```dart\nclass _PairingScreenState extends State<PairingScreen> {\n  late final PairingController _controller = PairingController(widget.repository);\n  final _codeField = TextEditingController();\n\n  @override\n  void dispose() {\n    _controller.dispose();\n    _codeField.dispose();\n    super.dispose();\n  }\n\n  Future<void> _submit() async {\n    await _controller.pair(_codeField.text);\n    if (!mounted) return;\n    if (_controller.status == PairingStatus.paired) Navigator.of(context).pop(true);\n  }\n\n  @override\n  Widget build(BuildContext context) {\n    return ListenableBuilder(\n      listenable: _controller,\n      builder: (context, child) => Column(\n        children: [\n          child!,\n          if (_controller.errorKey != null) Text(localizeError(context, _controller.errorKey!)),\n          FilledButton(\n            onPressed: _controller.canSubmit ? _submit : null,\n            child: const Text('Pair'),\n          ),\n        ],\n      ),\n      child: TextField(controller: _codeField),\n    );\n  }\n}\n```\n\n- `ListenableBuilder` rebuilds only its builder. Static subtrees go in `child` so they are not rebuilt.\n- Navigation and snack bars stay in the widget, after a `mounted` check.\n\n## ValueNotifier for a single value\n\n```dart\n// BAD: whole screen rebuilds for a counter\nsetState(() => _secondsLeft--);\n\n// GOOD\nfinal _secondsLeft = ValueNotifier<int>(60);\n\nValueListenableBuilder<int>(\n  valueListenable: _secondsLeft,\n  builder: (context, value, _) => Text('\$value s'),\n)\n// dispose: _secondsLeft.dispose();\n```\n\n`ValueNotifier` notifies only when the new value is not `==` to the old one. Mutating a list inside a\n`ValueNotifier<List<T>>` does not notify; assign a new list.\n\n## Ownership rules\n\n- Whoever creates a controller disposes it. A widget that receives a controller from its parent never\n  disposes it.\n- Optional external controller, like `TextField`:\n\n```dart\nclass DeviceList extends StatefulWidget {\n  const DeviceList({super.key, this.controller});\n  final DeviceListController? controller;\n  @override\n  State<DeviceList> createState() => _DeviceListState();\n}\n\nclass _DeviceListState extends State<DeviceList> {\n  DeviceListController? _internal;\n  DeviceListController get _effective => widget.controller ?? (_internal ??= DeviceListController());\n\n  @override\n  void didUpdateWidget(DeviceList oldWidget) {\n    super.didUpdateWidget(oldWidget);\n    if (widget.controller != null && _internal != null) {\n      _internal!.dispose();\n      _internal = null;\n    }\n  }\n\n  @override\n  void dispose() {\n    _internal?.dispose();\n    super.dispose();\n  }\n\n  @override\n  Widget build(BuildContext context) => ListenableBuilder(\n        listenable: _effective,\n        builder: (context, _) => Text('\${_effective.count} devices'),\n      );\n}\n```\n\n- Never create a controller in `build`. It is recreated on every rebuild and never disposed.\n- Manual `addListener` calls have a matching `removeListener` in `dispose`, and in `didUpdateWidget`\n  when the listened object changes.\n\n## Testing controllers\n\nControllers are plain Dart, so they are tested with `test`, not `testWidgets`.\n\n```dart\ntest('invalid code fails without calling the repository', () async {\n  final repo = FakePairingRepository();\n  final controller = PairingController(repo);\n  var notifications = 0;\n  controller.addListener(() => notifications++);\n\n  await controller.pair('abc');\n\n  expect(controller.status, PairingStatus.failed);\n  expect(controller.errorKey, 'invalidCode');\n  expect(repo.calls, 0);\n  expect(notifications, 1);\n});\n```\n\n## When to go further\n\nA `ChangeNotifier` controller with an injected repository is already a small ViewModel. When several\nwidgets need it, or it must survive navigation, provide it through the project's state management\n(provider, Riverpod, Bloc) instead of passing it by hand. See `mobile-mvvm` and `mobile-mvi`.\n",
    "references/migration-to-mvvm.md":
        "# Migrating MVC or MVP screens to MVVM\n\nLoad this when moving an existing controller-based screen to a ViewModel. The approach is a strangler:\nthe old screen keeps working while state and logic move behind it, one screen and one slice at a time.\nEach step is a separate, shippable MR.\n\n## Choose the screen\n\n- Start with the screen that changes most often or produces the most bugs, not the easiest one.\n- Skip screens that are stable, simple or scheduled for removal (see \"When MVC is still fine\" in the\n  skill).\n- One screen per migration. Shared base classes (`BaseActivity`, `BaseViewController`) are migrated last.\n\n## Steps\n\n1. Characterization tests. Pin current behaviour before touching it: formatting output, validation\n   results, state after each user action, error messages. If the logic cannot be tested in place,\n   extract it into a pure function with no other change, and test that.\n2. Extract pure logic. Formatting, validation and mapping move into plain types with unit tests. The\n   controller calls them. Behaviour is unchanged.\n3. Introduce the ViewModel behind the existing controller. Empty or nearly empty, injected, created by\n   the platform's mechanism. The controller holds a reference; nothing reads from it yet.\n4. Move state one piece at a time. For each field: add it to the ViewModel's UI state, make the\n   controller render from the ViewModel, delete the controller's field. Never keep both.\n5. Move actions. Controller event handlers call ViewModel methods instead of services.\n6. Move navigation. The ViewModel exposes navigation as state or a one-off event; the controller or a\n   coordinator performs it.\n7. Delete the presenter, the contract and dead code. Remove the old tests only after the new ones cover\n   the same behaviour.\n8. Optionally replace the view layer later (XML to Compose, UIKit to SwiftUI) as its own project. The\n   ViewModel does not change for that.\n\nEach step keeps the app releasable. If a step cannot be shipped alone, it is too big.\n\n## Android: MVP or god Activity to ViewModel\n\nStep 3, ViewModel behind the Activity:\n\n```kotlin\ndata class DevicesUiState(\n    val loading: Boolean = false,\n    val rows: List<DeviceRow> = emptyList(),\n    val error: DevicesError? = null,\n)\n\n@HiltViewModel\nclass DevicesViewModel @Inject constructor(\n    private val repository: DeviceRepository,\n) : ViewModel() {\n    private val _state = MutableStateFlow(DevicesUiState())\n    val state: StateFlow<DevicesUiState> = _state.asStateFlow()\n\n    fun load() {\n        viewModelScope.launch {\n            _state.update { it.copy(loading = true, error = null) }\n            try {\n                val rows = DeviceRow.from(repository.devices())\n                _state.update { it.copy(loading = false, rows = rows) }\n            } catch (e: IOException) {\n                _state.update { it.copy(loading = false, error = DevicesError.Network) }\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\nStep 4, the Kotlin Activity renders from the ViewModel:\n\n```kotlin\n@AndroidEntryPoint\nclass DevicesActivity : AppCompatActivity() {\n    private val viewModel: DevicesViewModel by viewModels()\n\n    override fun onCreate(savedInstanceState: Bundle?) {\n        super.onCreate(savedInstanceState)\n        setContentView(binding.root)\n        if (savedInstanceState == null) viewModel.load()\n        lifecycleScope.launch {\n            repeatOnLifecycle(Lifecycle.State.STARTED) {\n                viewModel.state.collect(::render)\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\nThe same screen in a Java Activity, while the Activity itself is not yet converted:\n\n```java\n// Kotlin side: val stateLiveData = state.asLiveData()  (lifecycle-livedata-ktx)\nDevicesViewModel viewModel = new ViewModelProvider(this).get(DevicesViewModel.class);\nviewModel.getStateLiveData().observe(this, this::render);\n```\n\nNotes:\n- The ViewModel survives rotation, so the \"load in onCreate\" call is guarded or moved into the\n  ViewModel's `init`. The rotation bug that MVP had is gone after step 4.\n- State that must survive process death (form input, selected tab) goes in `SavedStateHandle`.\n- During the migration a Java Activity can host a Kotlin ViewModel; convert the Activity to Kotlin\n  separately.\n- Do not wrap the old presenter inside a ViewModel to \"make it survive rotation\". The presenter still\n  holds a view reference that is now retained across Activity instances: a leak.\n\n## iOS: UIKit MVC to a ViewModel\n\nStep 3, an observable ViewModel the view controller owns:\n\n```swift\n@MainActor @Observable\nfinal class DevicesViewModel {\n    private(set) var rows: [DeviceRow] = []\n    private(set) var isLoading = false\n    private(set) var error: DevicesError?\n    private let service: DeviceService\n\n    init(service: DeviceService) { self.service = service }\n\n    func load() async {\n        isLoading = true\n        defer { isLoading = false }\n        do {\n            rows = try await service.devices().map(DeviceRow.init)\n            error = nil\n        } catch is CancellationError {\n        } catch {\n            self.error = .network\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\nStep 4, binding the view controller. Pick the option that fits the deployment target:\n\n- iOS 26 and later: UIKit tracks `@Observable` properties read in `updateProperties()` and re-runs it\n  when they change.\n\n```swift\noverride func updateProperties() {\n    super.updateProperties()\n    apply(viewModel.rows)\n    rootView.spinner.isHidden = !viewModel.isLoading\n}\n```\n\n- Earlier deployment targets: keep the ViewModel as an `ObservableObject` with `@Published` state and\n  bind with Combine, or render explicitly after each ViewModel call.\n\n```swift\nviewModel.\$state\n    .receive(on: DispatchQueue.main)\n    .sink { [weak self] state in self?.render(state) }\n    .store(in: &cancellables)\n```\n\nNotes:\n- The ViewModel does not import UIKit and does not hold the view controller.\n- Navigation leaves through closures to a coordinator, as in [UIKit](uikit.md).\n- A migrated screen can later be rendered with SwiftUI inside a `UIHostingController` while the\n  coordinator and ViewModel stay the same.\n\n## Flutter: State logic to controller to ViewModel\n\n1. Move logic from the `State` into a `ChangeNotifier` controller, as in [Flutter controllers](flutter.md).\n2. Inject the repository instead of constructing clients inside the controller.\n3. When the project uses provider, Riverpod or Bloc, expose the controller through it so widgets stop\n   owning it. At that point it is a ViewModel; follow `mobile-mvvm` or `mobile-mvi`.\n\n## MR plan for one screen\n\n```text\ntest(devices): characterization tests for device list formatting and states\nrefactor(devices): extract DeviceRow formatting from DevicesActivity\nrefactor(devices): add DevicesViewModel behind DevicesActivity\nrefactor(devices): render device list from DevicesViewModel state\nrefactor(devices): route device selection through DevicesViewModel\nrefactor(devices): remove DevicesPresenter and DevicesContract\n```\n\n## Definition of done per screen\n\n- [ ] Characterization tests passed before and after every step\n- [ ] Each piece of state has one owner: the ViewModel\n- [ ] Controller or Activity contains only view binding, rendering and lifecycle\n- [ ] Rotation and process death tested (Android); background and foreground tested (iOS)\n- [ ] Old presenter, contract and dead code removed\n- [ ] No behaviour change shipped inside a refactor MR\n",
    "references/uikit.md":
        "# UIKit MVC reference\n\nLoad this when the screen is a `UIViewController` written in Swift. Assumes Swift 6 language mode, where\n`UIViewController` and `UIView` are `@MainActor`.\n\n## Where each responsibility goes\n\n| Found in the view controller | Move it to |\n|---|---|\n| `URLSession`, request building, JSON decoding | A service or repository protocol, injected through `init` |\n| Core Data, SwiftData, file or Keychain access | A store type behind a protocol |\n| Date, number, measurement formatting | Row or display models built from domain models |\n| Input validation | A pure validator type with unit tests |\n| `UITableViewDataSource`, `UICollectionViewDataSource` | A diffable data source plus a cell provider |\n| `pushViewController`, `present`, building the next screen | A coordinator |\n| Large view hierarchy built in `viewDidLoad` | A custom `UIView` subclass returned from `loadView()` |\n| Independent regions (header, map, list) | Child view controllers |\n\nWhat stays: owning the root view, binding data to views, forwarding user actions, lifecycle hooks.\n\n## Custom root view\n\n```swift\n// BAD: 150 lines of constraints in viewDidLoad\noverride func viewDidLoad() {\n    super.viewDidLoad()\n    let stack = UIStackView()\n    // ...\n}\n\n// GOOD: the view owns its layout; the controller binds\nfinal class DevicesView: UIView {\n    let tableView = UITableView(frame: .zero, style: .insetGrouped)\n    let emptyLabel = UILabel()\n    // layout in init\n}\n\nfinal class DevicesViewController: UIViewController {\n    private var rootView: DevicesView { view as! DevicesView }\n    override func loadView() { view = DevicesView() }\n}\n```\n\nThe forced cast is safe here because `loadView()` sets the type in the same class.\n\n## Child view controllers\n\nEvery containment step is required. Skipping `addChild` or `didMove(toParent:)` breaks appearance\ncallbacks, trait propagation and safe-area handling.\n\n```swift\n// BAD\nheaderContainer.addSubview(header.view)\n\n// GOOD\nfunc embed(_ child: UIViewController, in container: UIView) {\n    addChild(child)\n    child.view.translatesAutoresizingMaskIntoConstraints = false\n    container.addSubview(child.view)\n    NSLayoutConstraint.activate([\n        child.view.topAnchor.constraint(equalTo: container.topAnchor),\n        child.view.bottomAnchor.constraint(equalTo: container.bottomAnchor),\n        child.view.leadingAnchor.constraint(equalTo: container.leadingAnchor),\n        child.view.trailingAnchor.constraint(equalTo: container.trailingAnchor),\n    ])\n    child.didMove(toParent: self)\n}\n\nfunc remove(_ child: UIViewController) {\n    child.willMove(toParent: nil)\n    child.view.removeFromSuperview()\n    child.removeFromParent()\n}\n```\n\n- Children talk to the parent through closures or a delegate, not by reaching into `parent`.\n- Each child has its own dependencies and can be tested and previewed alone.\n\n## Coordinators\n\nThe coordinator builds screens, injects their dependencies and performs navigation. Screens expose\nintent through closures or a delegate.\n\n```swift\n@MainActor\nprotocol Coordinator: AnyObject {\n    func start()\n}\n\n@MainActor\nfinal class DevicesCoordinator: Coordinator {\n    private let navigationController: UINavigationController\n    private let service: DeviceService\n    private var children: [Coordinator] = []\n\n    init(navigationController: UINavigationController, service: DeviceService) {\n        self.navigationController = navigationController\n        self.service = service\n    }\n\n    func start() {\n        let list = DevicesViewController(service: service)\n        list.onSelectDevice = { [weak self] id in self?.showDetail(id) }\n        navigationController.pushViewController(list, animated: false)\n    }\n\n    private func showDetail(_ id: Device.ID) {\n        let detail = DeviceDetailViewController(deviceID: id, service: service)\n        navigationController.pushViewController(detail, animated: true)\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- Closures from screens to coordinators capture the coordinator `[weak self]`.\n- The coordinator holds the navigation controller, not the screens. Screens are owned by UIKit.\n- Child coordinators are kept in `children` and removed when their flow finishes. Detect a user-driven\n  back navigation with `UINavigationControllerDelegate.navigationController(_:didShow:animated:)`,\n  or with a callback from the screen's `viewDidDisappear` when `isMovingFromParent` is true.\n- Deep links and push notifications enter through the app coordinator, which routes to the right flow.\n\n## Diffable data sources and cell registration\n\n```swift\n// BAD: model array and index paths drift apart; full reload loses animation and selection\ndevices.remove(at: indexPath.row)\ntableView.reloadData()\n\n// GOOD: identifiers in the snapshot, models looked up by id\nenum Section { case main }\n\nprivate lazy var dataSource = makeDataSource()\n\nprivate func makeDataSource() -> UICollectionViewDiffableDataSource<Section, Device.ID> {\n    let registration = UICollectionView.CellRegistration<UICollectionViewListCell, Device.ID> {\n        [weak self] cell, _, id in\n        guard let row = self?.rows[id] else { return }\n        var content = cell.defaultContentConfiguration()\n        content.text = row.title\n        content.secondaryText = row.lastSeen\n        cell.contentConfiguration = content\n    }\n    return UICollectionViewDiffableDataSource(collectionView: collectionView) { collectionView, indexPath, id in\n        collectionView.dequeueConfiguredReusableCell(using: registration, for: indexPath, item: id)\n    }\n}\n\nprivate func apply(_ devices: [Device]) {\n    rows = Dictionary(uniqueKeysWithValues: devices.map { (\$0.id, DeviceRow(\$0)) })\n    var snapshot = NSDiffableDataSourceSnapshot<Section, Device.ID>()\n    snapshot.appendSections([.main])\n    snapshot.appendItems(devices.map(\\.id))\n    dataSource.apply(snapshot, animatingDifferences: true)\n}\n```\n\n- Use stable identifiers as item identifiers, not whole models; duplicate identifiers crash at apply time.\n- When content changes but identity does not, use `snapshot.reconfigureItems(_:)` instead of a reload.\n- Create cell registrations once, outside the cell provider closure.\n- For a large or reused data source, move `makeDataSource` and `apply` into a dedicated\n  `DevicesListAdapter` object the controller owns.\n\n## Async work in a view controller\n\n```swift\n// BAD: Task lives on after the screen is popped and retains it\noverride func viewDidLoad() {\n    super.viewDidLoad()\n    Task { self.apply(try await service.devices()) }\n}\n\n// GOOD\nprivate var loadTask: Task<Void, Never>?\n\noverride func viewWillAppear(_ animated: Bool) {\n    super.viewWillAppear(animated)\n    loadTask = Task { [weak self, service] in\n        do {\n            let devices = try await service.devices()\n            self?.apply(devices)\n        } catch is CancellationError {\n        } catch {\n            self?.showError(error)\n        }\n    }\n}\n\noverride func viewDidDisappear(_ animated: Bool) {\n    super.viewDidDisappear(animated)\n    loadTask?.cancel()\n}\n```\n\n- Notification observers: store the token from block-based `addObserver(forName:object:queue:using:)`\n  and remove it, or iterate `NotificationCenter.default.notifications(named:)` inside a stored task.\n- Delegates are `weak`.\n\n## Testing\n\n- Unit-test row models, validators, services and coordinators without UIKit rendering.\n- For controller tests, call `loadViewIfNeeded()` and drive it through its public API with fake services.\n- Coordinators are tested by giving them a `UINavigationController` and asserting on `viewControllers`.\n",
  },
  "mobile-mvi": {
    "SKILL.md":
        "---\nname: mobile-mvi\ndescription: \"Designs, implements, reviews and tests mobile screens with MVI (Model-View-Intent) on Android, iOS and Flutter. Covers intents as a sealed type, one immutable State, a pure reducer (State, Action) -> State, and side effects run outside the reducer with results fed back as actions. Kotlin with sealed interfaces, MutableStateFlow.update and Channel effects in a ViewModel store with Jetpack Compose; Swift with enums with associated values, an @MainActor @Observable Store and SwiftUI, with The Composable Architecture (TCA) as an option; Dart with sealed classes, Bloc (on<Event>, emit, bloc_concurrency transformers) and Cubit. Includes pure reducer tests, effect tests with fakes, Turbine, Swift Testing and bloc_test. Use for screens with many interacting events, debounced search, racing requests, editors, wizards or live device dashboards, when adopting or reviewing Bloc, TCA or Redux-style code, or when choosing between MVI and MVVM.\"\nlicense: MIT\nmetadata:\n  author: Ahmad Hussain\n  version: \"1.0.0\"\n  pack: mobile-dev-kit\n---\n\n# MVI for mobile apps\n\nBuild screens where every change goes through one loop: the UI sends an intent, a pure reducer computes\nthe next state, effects do the IO outside the reducer and report back as new actions. State transitions\nbecome plain functions that are trivial to test, replay and reason about under concurrency.\n\n## When to use\n\n- A screen has many events that interact: typing, retry, refresh, pagination, selection, live updates.\n- Requests race each other (debounced search, rapid toggles, BLE or socket updates arriving mid-request).\n- State transitions must be auditable or tested exhaustively (payments, onboarding wizards, editors).\n- The codebase already uses Bloc, TCA, Orbit, Mavericks or a hand-rolled store, and new code must fit.\n- Reviewing an MVI store for impure reducers, lost effects or oversized state.\n\nNot for: simple read-only or CRUD screens; MVVM with a single `UiState` is enough there\n(use `mobile-mvvm`). Do not convert a whole app to MVI to fix one complex screen.\n\n## The loop\n\n```\n          +----------------------- State -----------------------+\n          v                                                      |\n   UI renders State --- Intent ---> Store --- (State, Action) ---> Reducer (pure)\n                                      |                              ^\n                                      | effect for this action       | Result action\n                                      v                              |\n                                   Effect runner (IO, timers, navigation)\n```\n\n| Part | What it is | Kotlin | Swift | Dart |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Intent | What the user did | `sealed interface SearchIntent` | `enum SearchAction` cases | `sealed class SearchEvent` |\n| Result | What an effect produced | `sealed interface SearchResult` | more `SearchAction` cases | internal result classes |\n| State | Everything the screen renders | `data class` | `struct: Equatable` | `@immutable` class or `Equatable` |\n| Reducer | `(State, Action) -> State`, no IO | top-level or `object` function | `static func` | top-level function |\n| Effect | IO, timers, navigation, analytics | coroutine in `viewModelScope` | `Task` owned by the store | code in a Bloc handler |\n| Store | Holds state, runs the loop | `ViewModel` + `StateFlow` | `@MainActor @Observable` class | `Bloc` |\n\n## Workflow\n\n1. Confirm the screen needs MVI (see When to use). If not, use `mobile-mvvm`.\n2. Load the reference for the platform: [Kotlin and Compose](references/kotlin.md),\n   [Swift and SwiftUI](references/swift.md), [Dart with Bloc](references/dart.md).\n3. Write the `State`: the minimum the UI renders. Derive the rest with computed properties.\n4. List intents from the UI as a sealed type, named after what happened (`QueryChanged`, `RetryClicked`).\n5. List results the effects can produce (`Loaded`, `Failed`), each carrying what the reducer needs\n   to reject stale data (for example the query it was for).\n6. Write the reducer and its tests first. It is pure, so the tests need no mocks or dispatchers.\n7. Write the store: apply the reducer, then start the effect for the action with an explicit\n   concurrency policy (cancel previous, drop new, or queue).\n8. Decide how one-off effects reach the UI: state field first, effect channel only if needed.\n9. Build the UI: render `State`, send intents, never mutate state directly.\n10. Write effect tests with fakes and virtual time. Run the existing test suite and report failures.\n\n## Rules\n\n### 1. Intents are one sealed type named after what happened\n\nKotlin:\n```kotlin\n// BAD: commands named after implementation, open to anything\nfun setLoading(value: Boolean)\nfun callSearchApi(query: String)\n\n// GOOD\nsealed interface SearchIntent {\n    data class QueryChanged(val query: String) : SearchIntent\n    data object RetryClicked : SearchIntent\n    data class ResultClicked(val id: String) : SearchIntent\n}\n```\n\nSwift:\n```swift\nenum SearchAction: Equatable, Sendable {\n    case queryChanged(String)                  // from the view\n    case retryTapped\n    case resultTapped(Repo.ID)\n    case detailsDismissed\n    case loaded(query: String, [Repo])         // from effects\n    case failed(query: String)\n}\n```\n\nDart:\n```dart\nsealed class SearchEvent { const SearchEvent(); }\nfinal class QueryChanged extends SearchEvent { const QueryChanged(this.query); final String query; }\nfinal class RetryPressed extends SearchEvent { const RetryPressed(); }\n```\n\n- Exhaustive `when` / `switch` over a sealed type makes the compiler flag every unhandled intent.\n- Kotlin can split public intents and internal results into two sealed sub-interfaces of one `SearchAction`,\n  so the UI cannot send a result. Swift enums cannot; mark effect-only cases with a comment.\n\n### 2. One immutable State; derive instead of duplicating\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: flags that can contradict each other, list duplicated for filtering\ndata class SearchState(\n    val isLoading: Boolean, val isError: Boolean, val isEmpty: Boolean,\n    val items: List<Repo>, val filteredItems: List<Repo>,\n)\n\n// GOOD\ndata class SearchState(\n    val query: String = \"\",\n    val items: List<Repo> = emptyList(),\n    val isLoading: Boolean = false,\n    val hasError: Boolean = false,\n) {\n    val showEmpty: Boolean get() = query.isNotBlank() && !isLoading && !hasError && items.isEmpty()\n}\n```\n\n- When states are mutually exclusive (loading, content, error), model them as a sealed type or enum\n  instead of booleans; keep shared fields like `query` outside it.\n\n### 3. The reducer is pure\n\nSame state and action in, same state out. No IO, no clock, no random ids, no logging, no launching work.\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: IO and a launched coroutine inside the reducer\nfun reduce(state: SearchState, intent: SearchIntent): SearchState = when (intent) {\n    is SearchIntent.QueryChanged -> {\n        scope.launch { repository.search(intent.query) }\n        state.copy(query = intent.query, isLoading = true)\n    }\n    ...\n}\n\n// GOOD: only computes the next state\nfun reduce(state: SearchState, action: SearchAction): SearchState = when (action) {\n    is SearchIntent.QueryChanged ->\n        state.copy(query = action.query, isLoading = action.query.isNotBlank(), hasError = false)\n    is SearchResult.Loaded ->\n        if (action.query != state.query) state else state.copy(items = action.items, isLoading = false)\n    ...\n}\n```\n\n```swift\n// GOOD: a static function on a value type; `inout` is equally pure for structs\nenum SearchReducer {\n    static func reduce(_ state: SearchState, _ action: SearchAction) -> SearchState {\n        var state = state\n        switch action {\n        case .queryChanged(let query):\n            state.query = query\n            state.isLoading = !query.isEmpty\n        case let .loaded(query, items):\n            guard query == state.query else { return state }\n            state.items = items\n            state.isLoading = false\n        // ...\n        }\n        return state\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- Kotlin: `MutableStateFlow.update` may run its lambda more than once under contention. A pure reducer\n  makes that harmless; one with side effects would run them twice.\n- Values the reducer needs from the outside world (current time, generated id) arrive inside the action.\n\n### 4. Effects run outside the reducer and report back as actions\n\n```kotlin\nfun onIntent(intent: SearchIntent) {\n    dispatch(intent)                                   // 1. reduce\n    when (intent) {                                    // 2. start the effect for this intent\n        is SearchIntent.QueryChanged -> search(intent.query, debounce = true)\n        SearchIntent.RetryClicked -> search(state.value.query, debounce = false)\n        is SearchIntent.ResultClicked -> _effects.trySend(SearchEffect.OpenDetails(intent.id))\n    }\n}\n\nprivate fun dispatch(action: SearchAction) = _state.update { reduce(it, action) }\n\nprivate fun search(query: String, debounce: Boolean) {\n    searchJob?.cancel()\n    if (query.isBlank()) return\n    searchJob = viewModelScope.launch {\n        if (debounce) delay(300)\n        val result = try {\n            SearchResult.Loaded(query, repository.search(query))\n        } catch (e: IOException) {\n            SearchResult.Failed(query)\n        }\n        dispatch(result)                               // 3. result goes back through the reducer\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- The effect never writes state directly. Every change is visible as an action in logs and tests.\n- Swift: the store keeps a `Task` per effect and calls `send(.loaded(...))` when it finishes.\n- Dart (Bloc): the handler awaits the repository, then `emit(reduce(state, result))`.\n\n### 5. Effect concurrency is a decision, not an accident\n\n| Policy | Use for | Kotlin | Swift | Dart (Bloc) |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Cancel previous | Search, filters, latest-wins loads | `job?.cancel()` before `launch` | `task?.cancel()` before `Task {}` | `restartable()` |\n| Drop new while busy | Submit, pay, pull to refresh | `if (job?.isActive == true) return` | `guard task == nil` | `droppable()` |\n| Queue | Ordered writes, BLE command sequences | `Channel` + one consumer, or `Mutex` | `AsyncStream` + one consumer | `sequential()` |\n\n- Results carry their input (query, page, request id); the reducer drops results that no longer match.\n  Cancellation alone is not enough: a result can arrive between completion and cancel.\n- Dart: after an `await` in a `restartable()` handler, check `emit.isDone` before doing more IO;\n  cancellation stops `emit`, not your `Future`.\n\n### 6. One-off effects: state first, channel when it is truly fire-and-forget\n\n```kotlin\n// GOOD (preferred): navigation as state, cleared by an intent after the UI acts\ndata class SearchState(val openDetailsId: String? = null, ...)\n\n// ACCEPTABLE: effect channel, buffered, collected with the lifecycle on Main.immediate\nprivate val _effects = Channel<SearchEffect>(Channel.BUFFERED)\nval effects: Flow<SearchEffect> = _effects.receiveAsFlow()\n\n// BAD: SharedFlow without replay loses effects while the UI is stopped or rotating\nval effects = MutableSharedFlow<SearchEffect>()\n```\n\n```swift\n// GOOD: SwiftUI navigation driven by state\n.navigationDestination(item: Binding(\n    get: { store.state.selectedRepo },\n    set: { if \$0 == nil { store.send(.detailsDismissed) } }\n)) { id in\n    RepoDetailView(id: id)\n}\n```\n\n```dart\n// GOOD: Bloc state field plus BlocListener with listenWhen\nBlocListener<SearchBloc, SearchState>(\n  listenWhen: (previous, current) => previous.selectedRepoId != current.selectedRepoId,\n  listener: (context, state) { /* navigate, then add(const DetailsOpened()) */ },\n  child: ...,\n)\n```\n\n- A `Channel` keeps effects while nobody collects, but an effect can still be lost if the collector is\n  cancelled between receive and handling. Collect on `Dispatchers.Main.immediate` inside\n  `repeatOnLifecycle(STARTED)`; if losing one is unacceptable, use state.\n\n### 7. The store is the only writer; the UI only renders and sends\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: UI writes state and calls store internals, bypassing the reducer\nviewModel.mutableState.value = state.copy(isLoading = true)\nviewModel.searchNow(\"x\")\n\n// GOOD: one entry point\n@Composable fun SearchScreen(state: SearchState, onIntent: (SearchIntent) -> Unit, modifier: Modifier = Modifier)\n```\n\n- Swift: `private(set) var state` on the store; the view calls `store.send(_:)`.\n- Dart: widgets call `context.read<SearchBloc>().add(...)`; they never call `emit`.\n- Text fields: reduce the text change synchronously on the main thread, otherwise the cursor jumps.\n\n### 8. Keep state lean and render slices\n\nEvery action produces a new state and every observer compares it.\n\n- `StateFlow` and Bloc skip equal states using `equals` / `==`; on large lists that comparison is O(n)\n  per action. Keep big collections out of state that changes on every keystroke, or page them.\n- Compose: pass each child only the fields it reads, not the whole `State`; with strong skipping,\n  unchanged arguments let children skip.\n- SwiftUI: `@Observable` tracks `store.state` as one property, so any change re-evaluates every reader.\n  Pass child views plain values, or split rarely changing parts into separate store properties.\n- Flutter: `BlocSelector` or `context.select` for slices; `buildWhen` on `BlocBuilder`.\n- High-frequency streams (BLE notifications, sensor data) are sampled or throttled before they become\n  actions; one action per packet will flood the reducer and the UI.\n\n### 9. Test the reducer as a pure function, the store with fakes\n\n```kotlin\n@Test fun `stale result for an older query is ignored`() {\n    val state = SearchState(query = \"kotlin\", isLoading = true)\n    assertEquals(state, reduce(state, SearchResult.Loaded(\"kot\", listOf(repo))))\n}\n```\n\n```swift\n@Test func queryChangeStartsLoading() {\n    let next = SearchReducer.reduce(SearchState(hasError: true), .queryChanged(\"swift\"))\n    #expect(next == SearchState(query: \"swift\", isLoading: true))\n}\n```\n\n```dart\ntest('failure for an old query is ignored', () {\n  const state = SearchState(query: 'flutter', status: SearchStatus.loading);\n  expect(reduce(state, const SearchFailed('flut')), state);\n});\n```\n\n- Reducer tests: one test per transition, including the stale-result and no-op cases. No mocks.\n- Store tests: fake repository, virtual time (`runTest`), Turbine for state and effect sequences,\n  `bloc_test` for Blocs, `await store.send(...)?.value` for Swift stores.\n- Assert whole states, not single fields, so unexpected changes fail the test.\n\n### 10. Use the platform's established MVI tools when the team has them\n\n- Swift: The Composable Architecture (TCA) gives reducers, effects, dependency injection and an exhaustive\n  test store. Worth it for large apps that want enforced structure; it is a significant dependency with its\n  own learning curve and API changes between versions. Follow its current documentation and do not mix\n  it with hand-rolled stores inside one feature.\n- Dart: `Bloc` for event-driven screens (named events, transformers, event traceability in `onTransition`);\n  `Cubit` for simple screens, with methods instead of events and `emit` directly.\n\n```dart\n// Cubit: fine for a counter-sized screen\nclass FilterCubit extends Cubit<bool> {\n  FilterCubit() : super(false);\n  void toggle() => emit(!state);\n}\n```\n\n## Anti-patterns\n\n- Giant state: one `State` for a whole tab or app. Each screen owns its state; share data through\n  repositories.\n- Reducers doing IO, logging, reading the clock or generating ids.\n- Effects writing state directly instead of returning a result action.\n- Losing effects: `SharedFlow(replay = 0)`, collecting outside the lifecycle, or `trySend` on a full\n  `RENDEZVOUS` channel without checking the result.\n- State explosion: a sealed state per combination (`LoadingWithError`, `RefreshingEmpty`). Use a few\n  orthogonal fields plus one sealed sub-state for truly exclusive cases.\n- Over-engineering: intents, reducer, effects and a store for a settings toggle or a static detail page.\n- Booleans that can contradict each other (`isLoading` and `hasError` both true with no meaning defined).\n- One action per BLE packet or scroll pixel, flooding reducer and UI.\n- A generic \"base MVI\" framework written in-house before two screens need it.\n- Tests that mock the reducer or assert on private functions instead of states.\n\n## Checklist\n\n- [ ] The screen really needs MVI (interacting events, races or auditable transitions)\n- [ ] Intents are one sealed type, named after user actions; results carry their input\n- [ ] One immutable `State`, minimal, derived values computed\n- [ ] Reducer is pure: no IO, clock, random, logging or launching\n- [ ] Effects run outside the reducer and report back through actions\n- [ ] Concurrency policy chosen per effect (cancel previous, drop, queue); stale results ignored\n- [ ] One-off effects modelled as state, or a buffered channel collected with the lifecycle\n- [ ] UI renders state and sends intents only; children receive slices\n- [ ] High-frequency inputs throttled before they become actions\n- [ ] Reducer tests per transition; store tests with fakes and virtual time; existing tests pass\n\n## Related skills\n\n- `mobile-mvvm` -- the simpler default for screens that do not need a reducer\n- `mobile-mvc` -- legacy UIKit and Activity-centric screens\n- `mobile-design-patterns` -- state, command and observer patterns behind MVI\n- `kotlin-android`, `swift-ios`, `dart-flutter` -- language rules for each platform\n- `mobile-code-review` -- reviewing stores, reducers and effects\n",
    "references/dart.md":
        "# MVI with Dart, Flutter and Bloc\n\nLoad this when building or reviewing a Bloc or Cubit feature. Assumes Dart 3, Flutter stable, `bloc`,\n`flutter_bloc`, `bloc_concurrency`, `equatable`, and `bloc_test` in tests.\n\nBloc maps onto MVI directly: events are intents, the handler runs effects, `emit` publishes the next state.\nTo keep the reducer pure, handlers call a top-level `reduce` function and do IO only around it.\n\nThe feature: repository search with debounce, latest-query-wins, retry, and navigation to details\ndriven by state.\n\n## Contract\n\n```dart\nclass Repo extends Equatable {\n  const Repo({required this.id, required this.name});\n\n  final String id;\n  final String name;\n\n  @override\n  List<Object?> get props => [id, name];\n}\n\nclass SearchException implements Exception {}\n\nabstract interface class RepoRepository {\n  Future<List<Repo>> search(String query);\n}\n```\n\n## Actions and state\n\n```dart\nsealed class SearchAction {\n  const SearchAction();\n}\n\n/// Events: sent by the UI.\nsealed class SearchEvent extends SearchAction {\n  const SearchEvent();\n}\n\nfinal class QueryChanged extends SearchEvent {\n  const QueryChanged(this.query);\n  final String query;\n}\n\nfinal class RetryPressed extends SearchEvent {\n  const RetryPressed();\n}\n\nfinal class ResultTapped extends SearchEvent {\n  const ResultTapped(this.id);\n  final String id;\n}\n\nfinal class DetailsOpened extends SearchEvent {\n  const DetailsOpened();\n}\n\n/// Results: produced inside the Bloc by effects. Not events, so the UI cannot add them.\nsealed class SearchResult extends SearchAction {\n  const SearchResult();\n}\n\nfinal class SearchSucceeded extends SearchResult {\n  const SearchSucceeded(this.query, this.items);\n  final String query;\n  final List<Repo> items;\n}\n\nfinal class SearchFailed extends SearchResult {\n  const SearchFailed(this.query);\n  final String query;\n}\n\nenum SearchStatus { idle, loading, success, failure }\n\nfinal class SearchState extends Equatable {\n  const SearchState({\n    this.query = '',\n    this.items = const [],\n    this.status = SearchStatus.idle,\n    this.selectedRepoId,\n  });\n\n  final String query;\n  final List<Repo> items;\n  final SearchStatus status;\n  final String? selectedRepoId;\n\n  bool get showEmpty => status == SearchStatus.success && items.isEmpty;\n\n  SearchState copyWith({\n    String? query,\n    List<Repo>? items,\n    SearchStatus? status,\n    String? Function()? selectedRepoId,\n  }) =>\n      SearchState(\n        query: query ?? this.query,\n        items: items ?? this.items,\n        status: status ?? this.status,\n        selectedRepoId: selectedRepoId != null ? selectedRepoId() : this.selectedRepoId,\n      );\n\n  @override\n  List<Object?> get props => [query, items, status, selectedRepoId];\n}\n```\n\n- `Equatable` gives value equality. Bloc skips emitting a state equal to the current one, and\n  `bloc_test` compares emitted states with `==`.\n\n## Reducer\n\n```dart\nSearchState reduce(SearchState state, SearchAction action) => switch (action) {\n      QueryChanged(:final query) when query.trim().isEmpty => SearchState(query: query),\n      QueryChanged(:final query) => state.copyWith(query: query, status: SearchStatus.loading),\n      RetryPressed() => state.copyWith(status: SearchStatus.loading),\n      ResultTapped(:final id) => state.copyWith(selectedRepoId: () => id),\n      DetailsOpened() => state.copyWith(selectedRepoId: () => null),\n      SearchSucceeded(:final query, :final items) => query != state.query\n          ? state\n          : state.copyWith(items: items, status: SearchStatus.success),\n      SearchFailed(:final query) =>\n        query != state.query ? state : state.copyWith(status: SearchStatus.failure),\n    };\n```\n\n- A switch expression over the sealed `SearchAction` is exhaustive; a new event or result does not\n  compile until handled.\n\n## Bloc (store and effects)\n\n```dart\nclass SearchBloc extends Bloc<SearchEvent, SearchState> {\n  SearchBloc(this._repository, {this.debounce = const Duration(milliseconds: 300)})\n      : super(const SearchState()) {\n    on<QueryChanged>(_onQueryChanged, transformer: restartable());\n    on<RetryPressed>(_onRetry, transformer: droppable());\n    on<ResultTapped>((event, emit) => emit(reduce(state, event)));\n    on<DetailsOpened>((event, emit) => emit(reduce(state, event)));\n  }\n\n  final RepoRepository _repository;\n  final Duration debounce;\n\n  Future<void> _onQueryChanged(QueryChanged event, Emitter<SearchState> emit) async {\n    emit(reduce(state, event));\n    if (event.query.trim().isEmpty) return;\n    await Future<void>.delayed(debounce);\n    if (emit.isDone) return; // a newer QueryChanged cancelled this handler\n    await _search(event.query, emit);\n  }\n\n  Future<void> _onRetry(RetryPressed event, Emitter<SearchState> emit) async {\n    emit(reduce(state, event));\n    await _search(state.query, emit);\n  }\n\n  Future<void> _search(String query, Emitter<SearchState> emit) async {\n    try {\n      final items = await _repository.search(query);\n      emit(reduce(state, SearchSucceeded(query, items)));\n    } on SearchException {\n      emit(reduce(state, SearchFailed(query)));\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\nNotes:\n- `restartable()` cancels the previous `QueryChanged` handler. Cancelling stops its `emit` calls but not\n  its `Future`, so the handler checks `emit.isDone` before starting the request.\n- `droppable()` ignores retries while one is running.\n- A result for an old query (for example a retry that finishes after the user typed) is dropped by the\n  reducer's query check.\n- Handlers never build state inline; every state comes from `reduce`, so the reducer tests cover it.\n\n## Screen\n\n```dart\nclass SearchPage extends StatelessWidget {\n  const SearchPage({super.key});\n\n  @override\n  Widget build(BuildContext context) => BlocProvider(\n        create: (context) => SearchBloc(context.read<RepoRepository>()),\n        child: const SearchView(),\n      );\n}\n\nclass SearchView extends StatelessWidget {\n  const SearchView({super.key});\n\n  @override\n  Widget build(BuildContext context) {\n    return BlocListener<SearchBloc, SearchState>(\n      listenWhen: (previous, current) => previous.selectedRepoId != current.selectedRepoId,\n      listener: (context, state) {\n        final id = state.selectedRepoId;\n        if (id == null) return;\n        context.read<SearchBloc>().add(const DetailsOpened());\n        Navigator.of(context).push(MaterialPageRoute<void>(builder: (_) => RepoDetailPage(id: id)));\n      },\n      child: Scaffold(\n        appBar: AppBar(\n          title: TextField(\n            onChanged: (query) => context.read<SearchBloc>().add(QueryChanged(query)),\n            decoration: InputDecoration(hintText: context.l10n.searchHint), // app's AppLocalizations\n          ),\n        ),\n        body: BlocBuilder<SearchBloc, SearchState>(\n          builder: (context, state) => switch (state.status) {\n            SearchStatus.idle => const SizedBox.shrink(),\n            SearchStatus.loading when state.items.isEmpty => const Center(child: CircularProgressIndicator()),\n            SearchStatus.failure => RetryView(\n                onRetry: () => context.read<SearchBloc>().add(const RetryPressed()),\n              ),\n            _ when state.showEmpty => const EmptyResultsView(),\n            _ => ListView.builder(\n                itemCount: state.items.length,\n                itemBuilder: (context, index) {\n                  final repo = state.items[index];\n                  return ListTile(\n                    key: ValueKey(repo.id),\n                    title: Text(repo.name),\n                    onTap: () => context.read<SearchBloc>().add(ResultTapped(repo.id)),\n                  );\n                },\n              ),\n          },\n        ),\n      ),\n    );\n  }\n}\n```\n\n- The text field owns its text; the Bloc receives every change as an event. Do not feed `state.query`\n  back into a controller on every build, or the cursor jumps.\n- `BlocListener` performs navigation once per selection and acknowledges it with `DetailsOpened`.\n- For large screens, use `BlocSelector` or `context.select` so widgets rebuild only for their slice.\n\n## Reducer tests (pure)\n\n```dart\nvoid main() {\n  const repo = Repo(id: '1', name: 'flutter');\n\n  group('reduce', () {\n    test('query change starts loading and clears failure', () {\n      expect(\n        reduce(const SearchState(status: SearchStatus.failure), const QueryChanged('flu')),\n        const SearchState(query: 'flu', status: SearchStatus.loading),\n      );\n    });\n\n    test('blank query resets the screen', () {\n      expect(reduce(const SearchState(query: 'f', items: [repo]), const QueryChanged('')), const SearchState());\n    });\n\n    test('stale success is ignored', () {\n      const state = SearchState(query: 'flutter', status: SearchStatus.loading);\n      expect(reduce(state, const SearchSucceeded('flut', [repo])), state);\n    });\n\n    test('failure for the current query shows the error', () {\n      const state = SearchState(query: 'flutter', status: SearchStatus.loading);\n      expect(\n        reduce(state, const SearchFailed('flutter')),\n        const SearchState(query: 'flutter', status: SearchStatus.failure),\n      );\n    });\n  });\n}\n```\n\n## Bloc and effect tests\n\n```dart\nclass FakeRepoRepository implements RepoRepository {\n  final queries = <String>[];\n  Map<String, List<Repo>> results = {};\n  bool failNext = false;\n\n  @override\n  Future<List<Repo>> search(String query) async {\n    queries.add(query);\n    if (failNext) {\n      failNext = false;\n      throw SearchException();\n    }\n    return results[query] ?? const [];\n  }\n}\n\nvoid main() {\n  const repo = Repo(id: '1', name: 'flutter');\n  late FakeRepoRepository repository;\n\n  setUp(() => repository = FakeRepoRepository()..results = {'flutter': [repo]});\n\n  blocTest<SearchBloc, SearchState>(\n    'typing quickly searches only the last query',\n    build: () => SearchBloc(repository, debounce: const Duration(milliseconds: 10)),\n    act: (bloc) => bloc\n      ..add(const QueryChanged('flu'))\n      ..add(const QueryChanged('flutter')),\n    wait: const Duration(milliseconds: 50),\n    expect: () => const [\n      SearchState(query: 'flu', status: SearchStatus.loading),\n      SearchState(query: 'flutter', status: SearchStatus.loading),\n      SearchState(query: 'flutter', status: SearchStatus.success, items: [repo]),\n    ],\n    verify: (_) => expect(repository.queries, ['flutter']),\n  );\n\n  blocTest<SearchBloc, SearchState>(\n    'failure then retry recovers',\n    build: () => SearchBloc(repository..failNext = true, debounce: Duration.zero),\n    act: (bloc) async {\n      bloc.add(const QueryChanged('flutter'));\n      await Future<void>.delayed(const Duration(milliseconds: 10));\n      bloc.add(const RetryPressed());\n    },\n    wait: const Duration(milliseconds: 10),\n    expect: () => const [\n      SearchState(query: 'flutter', status: SearchStatus.loading),\n      SearchState(query: 'flutter', status: SearchStatus.failure),\n      SearchState(query: 'flutter', status: SearchStatus.loading),\n      SearchState(query: 'flutter', status: SearchStatus.success, items: [repo]),\n    ],\n  );\n\n  blocTest<SearchBloc, SearchState>(\n    'tapping a result selects it for navigation',\n    build: () => SearchBloc(repository),\n    act: (bloc) => bloc.add(const ResultTapped('1')),\n    expect: () => const [SearchState(selectedRepoId: '1')],\n  );\n}\n```\n\n- `blocTest` runs `build`, `act`, waits, then compares every emitted state in order.\n- Short real waits are needed because the debounce uses a real `Future.delayed`. Keep the injected\n  debounce small in tests; for heavier time logic, wrap the test in `fakeAsync` and elapse time manually.\n- Fakes over mocks: the fake records queries, so the test verifies behaviour without interaction mocks.\n\n## Cubit instead of Bloc\n\nA `Cubit` exposes methods and calls `emit` directly. It suits screens without debounce, cancellation or\nevent ordering needs:\n\n```dart\nclass FavoritesFilterCubit extends Cubit<FavoritesFilterState> {\n  FavoritesFilterCubit() : super(const FavoritesFilterState());\n\n  void toggleOnlineOnly() => emit(state.copyWith(onlineOnly: !state.onlineOnly));\n}\n```\n\n- Switch a Cubit to a Bloc when you need event transformers (`restartable`, `droppable`, `sequential`)\n  or a log of named events (`onTransition`, a `BlocObserver`).\n- Keep the reducer-style discipline in a Cubit too: compute the next state in a pure function, do IO\n  around it, and test the function without the Cubit.\n",
    "references/kotlin.md":
        "# MVI with Kotlin, Jetpack Compose and StateFlow\n\nLoad this when building or reviewing an MVI store in Kotlin. Assumes Kotlin 2.x, AndroidX Lifecycle 2.8\nor newer, Hilt, and Turbine in tests. No MVI library is required; the store is a plain `ViewModel`.\n\nThe feature: repository search with a 300 ms debounce, latest-query-wins, retry after failure, and\nopening a result's details as a one-off effect.\n\n## Contract\n\n```kotlin\ndata class Repo(val id: String, val name: String)\n\ninterface RepoRepository {\n    suspend fun search(query: String): List<Repo> // throws IOException on network failure\n}\n```\n\n## Actions, state and effects\n\n```kotlin\nsealed interface SearchAction\n\n/** Sent by the UI. */\nsealed interface SearchIntent : SearchAction {\n    data class QueryChanged(val query: String) : SearchIntent\n    data object RetryClicked : SearchIntent\n    data class ResultClicked(val id: String) : SearchIntent\n}\n\n/** Produced by effects only. Carries the query so stale results can be rejected. */\nsealed interface SearchResult : SearchAction {\n    data class Loaded(val query: String, val items: List<Repo>) : SearchResult\n    data class Failed(val query: String) : SearchResult\n}\n\ndata class SearchState(\n    val query: String = \"\",\n    val items: List<Repo> = emptyList(),\n    val isLoading: Boolean = false,\n    val hasError: Boolean = false,\n) {\n    val showEmpty: Boolean get() = query.isNotBlank() && !isLoading && !hasError && items.isEmpty()\n}\n\nsealed interface SearchEffect {\n    data class OpenDetails(val id: String) : SearchEffect\n}\n```\n\n- `onIntent` accepts `SearchIntent` only, so the UI cannot fake a `SearchResult`.\n\n## Reducer\n\n```kotlin\nfun reduce(state: SearchState, action: SearchAction): SearchState = when (action) {\n    is SearchIntent.QueryChanged ->\n        if (action.query.isBlank()) {\n            SearchState(query = action.query)\n        } else {\n            state.copy(query = action.query, isLoading = true, hasError = false)\n        }\n\n    SearchIntent.RetryClicked -> state.copy(isLoading = true, hasError = false)\n\n    is SearchIntent.ResultClicked -> state\n\n    is SearchResult.Loaded ->\n        if (action.query != state.query) state\n        else state.copy(items = action.items, isLoading = false, hasError = false)\n\n    is SearchResult.Failed ->\n        if (action.query != state.query) state\n        else state.copy(isLoading = false, hasError = true)\n}\n```\n\n- Exhaustive `when` without `else`: adding an action breaks the build until it is handled.\n- Previous items stay visible while a new query loads; the UI shows a progress bar over them.\n\n## Store\n\n```kotlin\n@HiltViewModel\nclass SearchViewModel @Inject constructor(\n    private val repository: RepoRepository,\n) : ViewModel() {\n\n    private val _state = MutableStateFlow(SearchState())\n    val state: StateFlow<SearchState> = _state.asStateFlow()\n\n    private val _effects = Channel<SearchEffect>(Channel.BUFFERED)\n    val effects: Flow<SearchEffect> = _effects.receiveAsFlow()\n\n    private var searchJob: Job? = null\n\n    fun onIntent(intent: SearchIntent) {\n        dispatch(intent)\n        when (intent) {\n            is SearchIntent.QueryChanged -> search(intent.query, debounce = true)\n            SearchIntent.RetryClicked -> search(_state.value.query, debounce = false)\n            is SearchIntent.ResultClicked -> _effects.trySend(SearchEffect.OpenDetails(intent.id))\n        }\n    }\n\n    private fun dispatch(action: SearchAction) {\n        _state.update { reduce(it, action) }\n    }\n\n    private fun search(query: String, debounce: Boolean) {\n        searchJob?.cancel()\n        if (query.isBlank()) return\n        searchJob = viewModelScope.launch {\n            if (debounce) delay(DEBOUNCE_MS)\n            val result = try {\n                SearchResult.Loaded(query, repository.search(query))\n            } catch (e: IOException) {\n                SearchResult.Failed(query)\n            }\n            dispatch(result)\n        }\n    }\n\n    companion object {\n        const val DEBOUNCE_MS = 300L\n    }\n}\n```\n\nNotes:\n- Concurrency policy: cancel previous. The reducer's query check covers the window where a result\n  completes just before the cancel.\n- `CancellationException` is not caught: only `IOException` becomes a result.\n- `ResultClicked` could also navigate directly from the UI. It goes through the store here because real\n  screens usually record it (recent searches, analytics) before navigating.\n\n## Screen\n\n```kotlin\n@Composable\nfun SearchRoute(\n    onOpenDetails: (String) -> Unit,\n    viewModel: SearchViewModel = hiltViewModel(),\n) {\n    val state by viewModel.state.collectAsStateWithLifecycle()\n    val lifecycleOwner = LocalLifecycleOwner.current // androidx.lifecycle.compose.LocalLifecycleOwner\n    val currentOnOpenDetails by rememberUpdatedState(onOpenDetails)\n\n    LaunchedEffect(viewModel, lifecycleOwner) {\n        lifecycleOwner.repeatOnLifecycle(Lifecycle.State.STARTED) {\n            withContext(Dispatchers.Main.immediate) {\n                viewModel.effects.collect { effect ->\n                    when (effect) {\n                        is SearchEffect.OpenDetails -> currentOnOpenDetails(effect.id)\n                    }\n                }\n            }\n        }\n    }\n\n    SearchScreen(state = state, onIntent = viewModel::onIntent)\n}\n\n@Composable\nfun SearchScreen(\n    state: SearchState,\n    onIntent: (SearchIntent) -> Unit,\n    modifier: Modifier = Modifier,\n) {\n    Column(modifier) {\n        OutlinedTextField(\n            value = state.query,\n            onValueChange = { onIntent(SearchIntent.QueryChanged(it)) },\n            label = { Text(stringResource(R.string.search_label)) },\n            singleLine = true,\n        )\n        if (state.isLoading) LinearProgressIndicator(Modifier.fillMaxWidth())\n        if (state.hasError) {\n            ErrorRow(onRetry = { onIntent(SearchIntent.RetryClicked) })\n        }\n        if (state.showEmpty) Text(stringResource(R.string.search_empty))\n        LazyColumn {\n            items(state.items, key = { it.id }) { repo ->\n                RepoRow(repo = repo, onClick = { onIntent(SearchIntent.ResultClicked(repo.id)) })\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- `rememberUpdatedState` keeps the long-lived effect collector from calling a stale `onOpenDetails`.\n- The text field round trip is synchronous (`update` on the main thread), so the cursor does not jump.\n- `SearchScreen` takes state and one callback, so previews pass any `SearchState`.\n\n## Reducer tests (pure, no coroutines)\n\n```kotlin\nclass SearchReducerTest {\n    private val repo = Repo(\"1\", \"kotlinx.coroutines\")\n\n    @Test\n    fun `query change starts loading and clears error`() {\n        val next = reduce(SearchState(query = \"ko\", hasError = true), SearchIntent.QueryChanged(\"kot\"))\n        assertEquals(SearchState(query = \"kot\", isLoading = true), next)\n    }\n\n    @Test\n    fun `blank query resets results`() {\n        val state = SearchState(query = \"k\", items = listOf(repo))\n        assertEquals(SearchState(query = \"\"), reduce(state, SearchIntent.QueryChanged(\"\")))\n    }\n\n    @Test\n    fun `loaded result for current query shows items`() {\n        val state = SearchState(query = \"kotlin\", isLoading = true)\n        val next = reduce(state, SearchResult.Loaded(\"kotlin\", listOf(repo)))\n        assertEquals(SearchState(query = \"kotlin\", items = listOf(repo)), next)\n    }\n\n    @Test\n    fun `stale result for an older query is ignored`() {\n        val state = SearchState(query = \"kotlin\", isLoading = true)\n        assertEquals(state, reduce(state, SearchResult.Loaded(\"kot\", listOf(repo))))\n    }\n\n    @Test\n    fun `failure shows error and stops loading`() {\n        val state = SearchState(query = \"kotlin\", isLoading = true)\n        assertEquals(SearchState(query = \"kotlin\", hasError = true), reduce(state, SearchResult.Failed(\"kotlin\")))\n    }\n}\n```\n\n## Store and effect tests\n\n```kotlin\nclass FakeRepoRepository : RepoRepository {\n    val queries = mutableListOf<String>()\n    var failNext = false\n    var results: Map<String, List<Repo>> = emptyMap()\n\n    override suspend fun search(query: String): List<Repo> {\n        queries += query\n        if (failNext) {\n            failNext = false\n            throw IOException(\"offline\")\n        }\n        return results[query].orEmpty()\n    }\n}\n\nclass SearchViewModelTest {\n    // StandardTestDispatcher, not Unconfined: each launched effect runs as its own step,\n    // so Turbine sees every intermediate state instead of a conflated one.\n    @get:Rule val mainDispatcherRule = MainDispatcherRule(StandardTestDispatcher())\n\n    private val repo = Repo(\"1\", \"kotlin\")\n    private val repository = FakeRepoRepository().apply { results = mapOf(\"kotlin\" to listOf(repo)) }\n\n    @Test\n    fun `typing quickly searches only the last query`() = runTest {\n        val viewModel = SearchViewModel(repository)\n\n        viewModel.onIntent(SearchIntent.QueryChanged(\"k\"))\n        viewModel.onIntent(SearchIntent.QueryChanged(\"ko\"))\n        viewModel.onIntent(SearchIntent.QueryChanged(\"kotlin\"))\n        advanceUntilIdle()\n\n        assertEquals(listOf(\"kotlin\"), repository.queries)\n        assertEquals(SearchState(query = \"kotlin\", items = listOf(repo)), viewModel.state.value)\n    }\n\n    @Test\n    fun `failure then retry recovers`() = runTest {\n        repository.failNext = true\n        val viewModel = SearchViewModel(repository)\n\n        viewModel.state.test {\n            assertEquals(SearchState(), awaitItem())\n\n            viewModel.onIntent(SearchIntent.QueryChanged(\"kotlin\"))\n            assertEquals(SearchState(query = \"kotlin\", isLoading = true), awaitItem())\n            assertEquals(SearchState(query = \"kotlin\", hasError = true), awaitItem())\n\n            viewModel.onIntent(SearchIntent.RetryClicked)\n            assertEquals(SearchState(query = \"kotlin\", isLoading = true), awaitItem())\n            assertEquals(SearchState(query = \"kotlin\", items = listOf(repo)), awaitItem())\n        }\n    }\n\n    @Test\n    fun `clicking a result emits OpenDetails`() = runTest {\n        val viewModel = SearchViewModel(repository)\n\n        viewModel.effects.test {\n            viewModel.onIntent(SearchIntent.ResultClicked(\"42\"))\n            assertEquals(SearchEffect.OpenDetails(\"42\"), awaitItem())\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- `MainDispatcherRule` is the same rule as in the `mobile-mvvm` Kotlin reference. Because `Dispatchers.Main`\n  is a `TestDispatcher`, `runTest` shares its scheduler: `delay(300)` is skipped in virtual time and\n  `advanceUntilIdle()` drives `viewModelScope`.\n- `StateFlow` conflates. With an unconfined Main dispatcher and a fake that never suspends, the loading\n  state of a retry can be overwritten before the collector sees it; the standard dispatcher avoids that.\n- Assert whole `SearchState` values. A stray field change fails the test instead of slipping through.\n- Effects are tested on their own flow; they never appear in state assertions.\n",
    "references/swift.md":
        "# MVI with Swift, SwiftUI and a small Store\n\nLoad this when building or reviewing an MVI-style feature in Swift. Assumes Swift 6 language mode,\niOS 17 or newer, and Swift Testing. The store below is hand-rolled and dependency-free; see the end of\nthis file for when to use The Composable Architecture (TCA) instead.\n\nThe feature: repository search with debounce, latest-query-wins, retry, and navigation to details\ndriven by state.\n\n## Contract\n\n```swift\nstruct Repo: Identifiable, Equatable, Sendable {\n    let id: String\n    let name: String\n}\n\nprotocol SearchClient: Sendable {\n    func search(_ query: String) async throws -> [Repo]\n}\n```\n\n## Actions and state\n\n```swift\nenum SearchAction: Equatable, Sendable {\n    // From the view\n    case queryChanged(String)\n    case retryTapped\n    case resultTapped(Repo.ID)\n    case detailsDismissed\n    // From effects only\n    case loaded(query: String, [Repo])\n    case failed(query: String)\n}\n\nstruct SearchState: Equatable {\n    var query = \"\"\n    var items: [Repo] = []\n    var isLoading = false\n    var hasError = false\n    var selectedRepo: Repo.ID?\n\n    var showEmpty: Bool { !query.isEmpty && !isLoading && !hasError && items.isEmpty }\n}\n```\n\n- Navigation is state (`selectedRepo`), so it survives view re-creation and is visible in tests.\n\n## Reducer\n\n```swift\nenum SearchReducer {\n    static func reduce(_ state: SearchState, _ action: SearchAction) -> SearchState {\n        var state = state\n        switch action {\n        case .queryChanged(let query):\n            state.query = query\n            state.hasError = false\n            state.isLoading = !query.isEmpty\n            if query.isEmpty { state.items = [] }\n\n        case .retryTapped:\n            state.isLoading = true\n            state.hasError = false\n\n        case .resultTapped(let id):\n            state.selectedRepo = id\n\n        case .detailsDismissed:\n            state.selectedRepo = nil\n\n        case let .loaded(query, items):\n            guard query == state.query else { return state }\n            state.items = items\n            state.isLoading = false\n\n        case .failed(let query):\n            guard query == state.query else { return state }\n            state.isLoading = false\n            state.hasError = true\n        }\n        return state\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- `switch` without `default`: a new action is a compile error until handled.\n- `var state = state` mutates a local copy of a value type; the function stays pure.\n\n## Store\n\n```swift\n@MainActor\n@Observable\nfinal class SearchStore {\n    private(set) var state = SearchState()\n\n    @ObservationIgnored private let client: any SearchClient\n    @ObservationIgnored private let debounce: Duration\n    @ObservationIgnored private var searchTask: Task<Void, Never>?\n\n    init(client: any SearchClient, debounce: Duration = .milliseconds(300)) {\n        self.client = client\n        self.debounce = debounce\n    }\n\n    /// Reduces the action, then starts its effect. Returns the effect's task so tests can await it.\n    @discardableResult\n    func send(_ action: SearchAction) -> Task<Void, Never>? {\n        state = SearchReducer.reduce(state, action)\n        switch action {\n        case .queryChanged(let query):\n            return search(query, after: debounce)\n        case .retryTapped:\n            return search(state.query, after: .zero)\n        case .resultTapped, .detailsDismissed, .loaded, .failed:\n            return nil\n        }\n    }\n\n    private func search(_ query: String, after delay: Duration) -> Task<Void, Never>? {\n        searchTask?.cancel()\n        guard !query.isEmpty else { return nil }\n        let task = Task { [client] in\n            do {\n                try await Task.sleep(for: delay)\n                let items = try await client.search(query)\n                guard !Task.isCancelled else { return }\n                send(.loaded(query: query, items))\n            } catch {\n                guard !Task.isCancelled else { return }\n                send(.failed(query: query))\n            }\n        }\n        searchTask = task\n        return task\n    }\n}\n```\n\nNotes:\n- The store is `@MainActor`, and the `Task` inherits it, so `send` from the effect is a main-actor call.\n- `Task.sleep` throws when cancelled, which is the debounce. The `Task.isCancelled` checks stop a cancelled\n  request from reporting `failed` (for example `URLError(.cancelled)`) after a newer query started.\n- Effects never assign `state`; they call `send` with a result action.\n- For time-heavy logic, inject `any Clock<Duration>` instead of a `Duration` and sleep on the clock.\n\n## View\n\n```swift\nstruct SearchView: View {\n    @State private var store: SearchStore\n\n    init(client: any SearchClient) {\n        _store = State(initialValue: SearchStore(client: client))\n    }\n\n    var body: some View {\n        NavigationStack {\n            List(store.state.items) { repo in\n                Button(repo.name) { store.send(.resultTapped(repo.id)) }\n            }\n            .overlay {\n                if store.state.isLoading {\n                    ProgressView()\n                } else if store.state.hasError {\n                    ContentUnavailableView {\n                        Label(\"Search failed\", systemImage: \"wifi.exclamationmark\")\n                    } actions: {\n                        Button(\"Retry\") { store.send(.retryTapped) }\n                    }\n                } else if store.state.showEmpty {\n                    ContentUnavailableView.search(text: store.state.query)\n                }\n            }\n            .searchable(text: Binding(\n                get: { store.state.query },\n                set: { store.send(.queryChanged(\$0)) }\n            ))\n            .navigationDestination(item: Binding(\n                get: { store.state.selectedRepo },\n                set: { if \$0 == nil { store.send(.detailsDismissed) } }\n            )) { id in\n                RepoDetailView(id: id)\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- Bindings route writes through `send`; the view never assigns state.\n- Every reader of `store.state` re-evaluates on any state change. Large screens pass child views plain\n  values (`RepoRow(repo: repo)`) so SwiftUI can skip unchanged children.\n\n## Reducer tests (pure)\n\n```swift\nimport Testing\n@testable import Search\n\nextension Repo {\n    static let swiftRepo = Repo(id: \"1\", name: \"swift\")\n}\n\nstruct SearchReducerTests {\n    @Test func queryChangeStartsLoadingAndClearsError() {\n        let next = SearchReducer.reduce(SearchState(hasError: true), .queryChanged(\"swift\"))\n        #expect(next == SearchState(query: \"swift\", isLoading: true))\n    }\n\n    @Test func emptyQueryClearsResults() {\n        let state = SearchState(query: \"s\", items: [.swiftRepo])\n        #expect(SearchReducer.reduce(state, .queryChanged(\"\")) == SearchState())\n    }\n\n    @Test func staleResultIsIgnored() {\n        let state = SearchState(query: \"swift\", isLoading: true)\n        #expect(SearchReducer.reduce(state, .loaded(query: \"sw\", [.swiftRepo])) == state)\n    }\n\n    @Test func failureShowsError() {\n        let state = SearchState(query: \"swift\", isLoading: true)\n        #expect(SearchReducer.reduce(state, .failed(query: \"swift\")) == SearchState(query: \"swift\", hasError: true))\n    }\n\n    @Test func tappingResultSelectsIt() {\n        let next = SearchReducer.reduce(SearchState(), .resultTapped(\"1\"))\n        #expect(next.selectedRepo == \"1\")\n    }\n}\n```\n\n## Store and effect tests\n\n```swift\nactor FakeSearchClient: SearchClient {\n    private(set) var queries: [String] = []\n    private let results: [String: [Repo]]\n    private let error: (any Error)?\n\n    init(results: [String: [Repo]] = [:], error: (any Error)? = nil) {\n        self.results = results\n        self.error = error\n    }\n\n    func search(_ query: String) async throws -> [Repo] {\n        queries.append(query)\n        if let error { throw error }\n        return results[query] ?? []\n    }\n}\n\n@MainActor\nstruct SearchStoreTests {\n    @Test func searchLoadsResults() async {\n        let store = SearchStore(client: FakeSearchClient(results: [\"swift\": [.swiftRepo]]), debounce: .zero)\n\n        await store.send(.queryChanged(\"swift\"))?.value\n\n        #expect(store.state == SearchState(query: \"swift\", items: [.swiftRepo]))\n    }\n\n    @Test func newQueryCancelsPreviousSearch() async {\n        let client = FakeSearchClient()\n        let store = SearchStore(client: client, debounce: .milliseconds(20))\n\n        let first = store.send(.queryChanged(\"s\"))\n        let second = store.send(.queryChanged(\"sw\"))\n        await first?.value\n        await second?.value\n\n        #expect(await client.queries == [\"sw\"])\n    }\n\n    @Test func failureShowsError() async {\n        let client = FakeSearchClient(error: URLError(.notConnectedToInternet))\n        let store = SearchStore(client: client, debounce: .zero)\n\n        await store.send(.queryChanged(\"swift\"))?.value\n\n        #expect(store.state == SearchState(query: \"swift\", hasError: true))\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- `send` returns the effect task, so tests `await` it instead of sleeping.\n- The cancellation test waits a short real delay (20 ms). If the suite grows time-dependent tests,\n  inject a clock and drive it from the test.\n- The fake is an `actor`, so recording queries is data-race free under Swift 6 checking.\n\n## The Composable Architecture (TCA)\n\nConsider TCA instead of a hand-rolled store when:\n- Several teams build features that must compose (parent and child features, shared dependencies).\n- Exhaustive testing of every state change and effect is a requirement.\n- The team accepts a large third-party dependency and its migration cost between major versions.\n\nWhen the project uses TCA, follow its current documentation for reducers, effects, dependencies and its\ntest store. Keep the same principles: pure state changes, effects returned rather than performed inline,\nresults sent back as actions. Do not mix TCA features and hand-rolled stores inside one feature.\n",
  },
  "mobile-mvvm": {
    "SKILL.md":
        "---\nname: mobile-mvvm\ndescription: \"Designs, implements and tests mobile screens with MVVM (Model-View-ViewModel) on Android, iOS and Flutter. Covers Kotlin with Jetpack Compose, ViewModel, StateFlow, stateIn with WhileSubscribed, collectAsStateWithLifecycle, SavedStateHandle and Hilt; legacy Java with LiveData, XML and ViewBinding; Swift with SwiftUI, @Observable @MainActor view models owned with @State, and SceneStorage; Dart with Riverpod Notifier and AsyncNotifier, or ChangeNotifier with provider. Enforces layers (UI, ViewModel, optional use cases, repository, data sources), one immutable UiState per screen, unidirectional data flow, one-off events as state, lifecycle-aware collection, dependency injection and ViewModel tests with runTest, Turbine, Swift Testing and ProviderContainer. Use when building a screen, adding or reviewing a ViewModel, fixing state, lifecycle or process-death bugs, migrating LiveData to StateFlow, or choosing between MVVM, MVI and MVC.\"\nlicense: MIT\nmetadata:\n  author: Ahmad Hussain\n  version: \"1.0.0\"\n  pack: mobile-dev-kit\n---\n\n# MVVM for mobile apps\n\nBuild screens where the UI is a plain function of one immutable state object, the ViewModel owns that state\nand the screen-scoped work, and data comes from repositories that the ViewModel receives by injection.\nThe result survives rotation and process death, renders the same state the same way every time, and is\ntestable without a device.\n\n## When to use\n\n- A new screen or feature needs a ViewModel, state holder or presentation layer.\n- An existing screen has state bugs: lost input on rotation or process death, double loads, stale UI,\n  snackbars shown twice or never.\n- Migrating Java + LiveData + XML screens to Kotlin + StateFlow + Compose, `ObservableObject` to\n  `@Observable`, or `setState` screens to Riverpod or provider.\n- Reviewing a ViewModel for layer leaks, lifecycle issues or untestable code.\n- Choosing between MVVM, MVI and MVC for a screen or a codebase.\n\nNot for: screens with many interacting events, strict event ordering or debounced concurrent requests\n(use `mobile-mvi`), or legacy UIKit and Activity-centric code you only maintain (use `mobile-mvc`).\n\n## When to choose MVVM vs MVI vs MVC\n\n| | MVVM | MVI | MVC |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| State | One `UiState` per screen (or a few observable properties) | One immutable `State`, changed only by a reducer | Spread across controller and views |\n| How UI talks to logic | Calls named ViewModel functions (`onRefresh()`) | Sends one sealed `Intent` type | Controller handles view callbacks directly |\n| Side effects | Inside ViewModel functions | Explicit effects outside a pure reducer | Inside the controller |\n| Boilerplate | Low | Medium to high | Lowest at first, grows fast |\n| Testing | ViewModel tests with fakes | Pure reducer tests plus effect tests | Hard, logic bound to views |\n| Good fit | Most screens: lists, details, forms, settings | Search with debounce, editors, wizards, chat, real-time device dashboards | Prototypes, small UIKit or Activity code already in that style |\n| Platform fit | Android Architecture guidance, SwiftUI `@Observable`, Riverpod, provider | Compose + `StateFlow` store, TCA, Bloc | UIKit `UIViewController`, legacy Activities |\n\nRules of thumb:\n- Default to MVVM with a single `UiState`. It already gives unidirectional data flow.\n- Move a screen to MVI when ViewModel functions start racing each other or the state transitions need\n  to be auditable and tested as pure functions.\n- Do not mix patterns inside one screen. Mixing across screens is fine.\n\n## Layers\n\n```\nUI (Composable, SwiftUI View, Widget)  renders UiState, forwards user events\n   | events                ^ UiState\n   v                       |\nViewModel                  owns screen state and screen-scoped work, maps domain data to UiState\n   |\n   v\nUse cases (optional)       business rules reused by several ViewModels, or too complex for one\n   |\n   v\nRepository                 single source of truth, merges sources, maps DTOs and entities to domain models\n   |\n   v\nData sources               network, database, BLE, files, platform APIs\n```\n\n- Dependencies point down only. Lower layers never import UI or ViewModel types.\n- Use cases are optional. Add one when the logic is shared or non-trivial, not as a pass-through.\n- The ViewModel lives as long as the screen (navigation destination), not as long as the view instance.\n\n## Workflow\n\n1. Confirm MVVM fits the screen using the table above.\n2. Load the reference for the platform before writing code:\n   [Kotlin and Compose](references/kotlin.md), [Java with LiveData and ViewBinding](references/java.md),\n   [Swift and SwiftUI](references/swift.md), [Dart with Riverpod or provider](references/dart.md).\n3. Write the `UiState`: everything the screen renders, with defaults. Derive values (filtered lists,\n   button enabled flags) instead of storing them twice.\n4. List the user events and name one ViewModel function per event (`onRefresh`, `onFavoriteClick`).\n5. Define the repository interface in domain types, plus a fake for tests and previews.\n6. Implement the ViewModel: combine sources into `UiState`, handle events, map errors to state.\n7. Wire DI and ownership (Hilt, `@State`, Riverpod provider, `ChangeNotifierProvider`).\n8. Build the UI as a stateless function of `UiState` plus callbacks. Preview it with fake states.\n9. Save small user input that must survive process death (filters, query, selected id).\n10. Write ViewModel tests with fakes, run the existing test suite and report failures.\n\n## Rules\n\n### 1. One immutable UiState per screen, exposed read-only\n\nSeveral independent mutable properties drift out of sync and let the UI write state.\n\nKotlin:\n```kotlin\n// BAD: public mutable state, three flows that can disagree\nval devices = MutableStateFlow<List<Device>>(emptyList())\nval isLoading = MutableStateFlow(false)\nval error = MutableStateFlow<String?>(null)\n\n// GOOD\ndata class DeviceListUiState(\n    val devices: List<Device> = emptyList(),\n    val isLoading: Boolean = false,\n    val userMessage: UserMessage? = null,\n)\n\nprivate val _uiState = MutableStateFlow(DeviceListUiState())\nval uiState: StateFlow<DeviceListUiState> = _uiState.asStateFlow()\n\nfun onRefresh() = _uiState.update { it.copy(isLoading = true) }\n```\n\nSwift:\n```swift\n// BAD: the view can write any of these\n@Observable final class DeviceListModel { var devices: [Device] = []; var isLoading = false }\n\n// GOOD\n@MainActor @Observable\nfinal class DeviceListModel {\n    private(set) var state = DeviceListState()\n}\n```\n\nDart:\n```dart\n// BAD: mutable fields, callers mutate the list in place\nclass DeviceListController extends ChangeNotifier { List<Device> devices = []; }\n\n// GOOD: immutable state, replaced as a whole\nclass DeviceListNotifier extends Notifier<DeviceListState> {\n  @override\n  DeviceListState build() => const DeviceListState();\n}\n```\n\nJava:\n```java\n// BAD\npublic final MutableLiveData<List<Device>> devices = new MutableLiveData<>();\n\n// GOOD\nprivate final MediatorLiveData<DeviceListUiState> uiState = new MediatorLiveData<>();\npublic LiveData<DeviceListUiState> getUiState() { return uiState; }\n```\n\n- Swift: separate `private(set)` properties also work and `@Observable` tracks them individually.\n\n### 2. Events go up, state comes down\n\nThe UI calls a named function for each user event and renders whatever state comes back.\nComposables, Views and Widgets below the screen level receive state and lambdas, not the ViewModel.\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: leaf composable owns the ViewModel, cannot be previewed or reused\n@Composable fun DeviceRow(id: String, vm: DeviceListViewModel = hiltViewModel()) { ... }\n\n// GOOD\n@Composable fun DeviceRow(device: Device, onFavoriteClick: () -> Unit, modifier: Modifier = Modifier) { ... }\n```\n\n```swift\n// BAD: view mutates model internals, no place for validation or side effects\nToggle(\"Online only\", isOn: \$model.onlineOnly)\n\n// GOOD: the change goes through the model\nToggle(\"Online only\", isOn: Binding(get: { model.state.onlineOnly }, set: { model.setOnlineOnly(\$0) }))\n```\n\n- A direct `@Bindable` binding to a model property is acceptable for plain form fields with no logic.\n\n### 3. The ViewModel knows no UI framework types\n\n- No `Context`, `Activity`, `View`, `NavController`, `UIViewController` or `BuildContext` in a ViewModel.\n- No resolved strings. Expose an enum or id; the UI maps it to localized text.\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: leaks the Activity, breaks on locale change, needs Robolectric to test\nclass DeviceListViewModel(private val context: Context) : ViewModel() {\n    fun error() = context.getString(R.string.refresh_failed)\n}\n\n// GOOD\nenum class UserMessage { RefreshFailed }\n// UI: UserMessage.RefreshFailed -> stringResource(R.string.refresh_failed)\n```\n\n- Flutter: notifier methods never take a `BuildContext`. Set state (`saved: true`) and let the widget\n  navigate from `ref.listen`.\n\n### 4. Expose observed data as hot state, started by the UI\n\nKotlin: convert repository flows once with `stateIn` and `WhileSubscribed(5_000)`. Collection stops five\nseconds after the last subscriber leaves (survives rotation, stops in the background).\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: loads in init, ignores lifecycle, hard to test the initial state\ninit { viewModelScope.launch { _uiState.value = DeviceListUiState(devices = repo.load()) } }\n\n// GOOD\nval uiState: StateFlow<DeviceListUiState> = repository.observeDevices()\n    .map { DeviceListUiState(devices = it) }\n    .stateIn(viewModelScope, SharingStarted.WhileSubscribed(5_000), DeviceListUiState(isLoading = true))\n```\n\nSwift: start loading from `.task`, which cancels when the view disappears, not from `init`.\nDart: load in `AsyncNotifier.build`; use `autoDispose` providers for screen state.\n\n### 5. Collect state with the lifecycle\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: keeps collecting while the app is in the background\nval state by viewModel.uiState.collectAsState()\n\n// GOOD\nval state by viewModel.uiState.collectAsStateWithLifecycle()\n```\n\n```java\n// BAD: Fragment lifecycle outlives its view, observers pile up after back navigation\nviewModel.getUiState().observe(this, this::render);\n\n// GOOD\nviewModel.getUiState().observe(getViewLifecycleOwner(), this::render);\n```\n\n- Fragments with flows: `viewLifecycleOwner.lifecycleScope` plus `repeatOnLifecycle(Lifecycle.State.STARTED)`.\n- SwiftUI: async work in `.task` / `.task(id:)`; `@Observable` reads in `body` are tracked automatically.\n- Flutter: `ref.watch` in `build`, `ref.read` in callbacks, `ref.listen` for side effects.\n\n### 6. One-off events are state that the UI acknowledges\n\nSnackbars, navigation after save and dialogs are modelled as nullable state. The UI performs the effect,\nthen tells the ViewModel it is done. Nothing is lost if the UI is not collecting at that moment.\n\nKotlin:\n```kotlin\n// BAD: SharedFlow without replay drops events when nobody collects (rotation, background)\nprivate val _events = MutableSharedFlow<String>()\n\n// GOOD\nfun onUserMessageShown() = userMessage.update { null }\n\n// UI\nstate.userMessage?.let { message ->\n    val text = message.toText()\n    LaunchedEffect(message) {\n        snackbarHostState.showSnackbar(text)\n        viewModel.onUserMessageShown()\n    }\n}\n```\n\nSwift:\n```swift\n.alert(model.state.userMessage?.title ?? \"\", isPresented: Binding(\n    get: { model.state.userMessage != nil },\n    set: { if !\$0 { model.userMessageShown() } }\n)) { Button(\"OK\", role: .cancel) {} }\n```\n\nDart:\n```dart\nref.listen(deviceListProvider.select((s) => s.value?.userMessage), (_, message) {\n  if (message == null) return;\n  ScaffoldMessenger.of(context).showSnackBar(SnackBar(content: Text(message.text(context))));\n  ref.read(deviceListProvider.notifier).userMessageShown();\n});\n```\n\nChannel trade-offs, if the team still prefers an event stream:\n- `Channel(Channel.BUFFERED)` + `receiveAsFlow()` keeps events while no one collects, but an event can\n  still be lost if the collector is cancelled between receiving and handling it.\n- Collect it in `repeatOnLifecycle(STARTED)` on `Dispatchers.Main.immediate` to shrink that window.\n- Never use `SingleLiveEvent` in new code; it supports one observer and hides the same race.\n\n### 7. Dependencies come in through the constructor\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: hidden dependency, cannot be faked\nclass DeviceListViewModel : ViewModel() { private val repo = DeviceRepositoryImpl(ApiClient.instance) }\n\n// GOOD\n@HiltViewModel\nclass DeviceListViewModel @Inject constructor(\n    private val repository: DeviceRepository,\n    private val savedStateHandle: SavedStateHandle,\n) : ViewModel()\n```\n\n- Swift: the owning view creates the model once with its dependencies:\n  `_model = State(initialValue: DeviceListModel(repository: repository))` in the view's `init`.\n- Dart: notifiers read dependencies with `ref.watch(deviceRepositoryProvider)`; tests override that provider.\n- Screen state is never held by a `@Singleton`, a global or an app-wide provider.\n- Navigation arguments reach the ViewModel through `SavedStateHandle` or assisted injection, not a setter\n  called from the UI after creation.\n\n### 8. Small user input survives process death\n\nRotation is handled by the ViewModel. Process death is not: the ViewModel is recreated empty.\n\n| Platform | ViewModel-owned input | View-only state |\n|---|---|---|\n| Android | `SavedStateHandle` (`getStateFlow`, `set`) | `rememberSaveable` |\n| iOS | Restore from the view on appear | `@SceneStorage` |\n| Flutter | Pass restored values in from the widget | `RestorationMixin`, `RestorableInt`, `RestorableTextEditingController` |\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: filter resets after process death\nprivate val onlineOnly = MutableStateFlow(false)\n\n// GOOD\nprivate val onlineOnly = savedStateHandle.getStateFlow(KEY_ONLINE_ONLY, false)\nfun onToggleOnlineOnly() { savedStateHandle[KEY_ONLINE_ONLY] = !onlineOnly.value }\n```\n\n- Save ids, queries, filters and draft text. Never save lists, images or anything the repository can reload.\n- Saved state is not a secure store. Never put tokens or personal data in it.\n\n### 9. The repository is the single source of truth\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: ViewModel talks to Retrofit and Room, maps DTOs, decides caching\nclass DeviceListViewModel(private val api: DeviceApi, private val dao: DeviceDao) : ViewModel()\n\n// GOOD: ViewModel sees domain models only\nclass DeviceListViewModel(private val repository: DeviceRepository) : ViewModel()\n```\n\n- Repositories expose domain models, not network DTOs or database entities.\n- Data that changes over time is observed (`Flow`, `AsyncSequence`, `Stream`), so writes elsewhere update\n  the screen without a manual reload.\n\n### 10. Errors become state; cancellation is not an error\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: runCatching swallows CancellationException; catch-all hides bugs\nrunCatching { repository.refresh() }\n\n// GOOD\ntry {\n    repository.refresh()\n} catch (e: IOException) {\n    userMessage.value = UserMessage.RefreshFailed\n}\n```\n\n```swift\ndo {\n    state.devices = try await repository.fetchDevices()\n} catch is CancellationError {\n    return\n} catch {\n    state.userMessage = .loadFailed\n}\n```\n\n- Dart: catch specific types (`on DeviceException`). In `AsyncNotifier`, `AsyncValue.guard` turns a thrown\n  error into `AsyncError` for the screen to render.\n\n### 11. Test the ViewModel with fakes and virtual time\n\n```kotlin\n@get:Rule val mainDispatcherRule = MainDispatcherRule()\n\n@Test fun `refresh failure shows message until acknowledged`() = runTest {\n    repository.refreshError = IOException(\"offline\")\n    val viewModel = DeviceListViewModel(repository, SavedStateHandle())\n    viewModel.uiState.test {\n        viewModel.onRefresh()\n        advanceUntilIdle()\n        assertEquals(UserMessage.RefreshFailed, expectMostRecentItem().userMessage)\n        viewModel.onUserMessageShown()\n        assertNull(awaitItem().userMessage)\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- Swift: a `@MainActor` Swift Testing suite awaits the model's async functions directly and uses `#expect`.\n- Dart: a `ProviderContainer` with the repository provider overridden by a fake; `container.listen` keeps\n  `autoDispose` providers alive. Full examples are in each reference file.\n- Fakes over mocks for repositories. Mocks only to verify an interaction that has no visible state.\n- Test state transitions and messages, not private functions.\n- No `Thread.sleep`, `Task.sleep` or real timers in ViewModel tests.\n\n## Anti-patterns\n\n- A ViewModel per composable or per row. One per screen or navigation destination.\n- Sharing one ViewModel across unrelated screens as a global state bag. Share a repository instead.\n- `LiveData` or `ObservableObject` in new code without a reason (interop with legacy screens is a reason).\n- UI state copied into local `remember` or `@State` and then edited there, so two sources of truth exist.\n- Business logic in composables, `body` or `build` (sorting, filtering, validation).\n- Use cases that only forward one repository call.\n- Calling ViewModel functions from the composable body or `body` instead of an effect or event handler.\n- Hardcoded `Dispatchers.IO` in the ViewModel. Repositories own their dispatchers.\n- `@ObservedObject var model = Model()`: a new model on every parent redraw. `@State var model = Model()`\n  is correct, but its initializer still runs on every parent redraw (only the first instance is kept),\n  so keep `init` cheap and start work in `.task`.\n\n## Checklist\n\n- [ ] MVVM chosen deliberately (not a screen that needs `mobile-mvi`)\n- [ ] One immutable `UiState` per screen, exposed read-only\n- [ ] Derived values computed, not stored twice\n- [ ] UI below screen level receives state and callbacks, not the ViewModel\n- [ ] No `Context`, `View`, `NavController`, `BuildContext` or resolved strings in the ViewModel\n- [ ] Repository flows converted with `stateIn(viewModelScope, WhileSubscribed(5_000), initial)`\n- [ ] Collected with `collectAsStateWithLifecycle`, `getViewLifecycleOwner()`, `.task` or `ref.watch`\n- [ ] One-off events modelled as state and acknowledged by the UI\n- [ ] Dependencies injected by constructor; screen state not held in singletons\n- [ ] Filters, query, selected id and draft input survive process death; no secrets in saved state\n- [ ] Repository is the source of truth; DTOs mapped at the data layer\n- [ ] Errors mapped to user-facing state; cancellation rethrown or ignored, never reported\n- [ ] ViewModel tests with fakes and virtual time; existing tests pass\n\n## Related skills\n\n- `mobile-mvi` -- when the screen needs a reducer, explicit intents and effects\n- `mobile-mvc` -- maintaining UIKit or Activity-centric screens\n- `mobile-design-patterns` -- repository, factory, adapter and other building blocks used here\n- `kotlin-android`, `java-android`, `swift-ios`, `dart-flutter` -- language rules for each platform\n- `mobile-code-review` -- reviewing the resulting ViewModels and screens\n",
    "references/dart.md":
        "# MVVM with Dart and Flutter (Riverpod, or provider)\n\nLoad this when building or reviewing a Flutter screen and its state holder. Assumes Dart 3, Flutter\nstable and Riverpod 3. In Riverpod the \"ViewModel\" is a `Notifier` or `AsyncNotifier`; with the simpler\n`provider` package it is a `ChangeNotifier`.\n\nThe feature: a device list loaded from a repository, pull to refresh, an \"online only\" filter, favorites\nwith optimistic update and rollback, and a snackbar when something fails.\n\n## Domain and data contract\n\n```dart\n@immutable\nclass Device {\n  const Device({required this.id, required this.name, required this.isOnline, this.isFavorite = false});\n\n  final String id;\n  final String name;\n  final bool isOnline;\n  final bool isFavorite;\n\n  Device copyWith({bool? isFavorite}) =>\n      Device(id: id, name: name, isOnline: isOnline, isFavorite: isFavorite ?? this.isFavorite);\n}\n\nclass DeviceException implements Exception {}\n\nabstract interface class DeviceRepository {\n  Future<List<Device>> fetchDevices();\n  Future<void> setFavorite(String id, {required bool favorite});\n}\n\nfinal deviceRepositoryProvider = Provider<DeviceRepository>(\n  (ref) => HttpDeviceRepository(ref.watch(httpClientProvider)), // data layer\n);\n```\n\n- Use `freezed` or `equatable` for value equality and `copyWith` in real code; it is written by hand\n  here to keep the example dependency-free.\n\n## State\n\n```dart\nenum UserMessage { refreshFailed, favoriteFailed }\n\n@immutable\nclass DeviceListState {\n  const DeviceListState({this.devices = const [], this.onlineOnly = false, this.userMessage});\n\n  final List<Device> devices;\n  final bool onlineOnly;\n  final UserMessage? userMessage;\n\n  List<Device> get visibleDevices => onlineOnly ? devices.where((d) => d.isOnline).toList() : devices;\n\n  DeviceListState copyWith({\n    List<Device>? devices,\n    bool? onlineOnly,\n    ValueGetter<UserMessage?>? userMessage, // a getter, so callers can set the field back to null\n  }) =>\n      DeviceListState(\n        devices: devices ?? this.devices,\n        onlineOnly: onlineOnly ?? this.onlineOnly,\n        userMessage: userMessage != null ? userMessage() : this.userMessage,\n      );\n}\n```\n\n## Notifier (the ViewModel)\n\n```dart\nfinal deviceListProvider =\n    AsyncNotifierProvider.autoDispose<DeviceListNotifier, DeviceListState>(DeviceListNotifier.new);\n\nclass DeviceListNotifier extends AsyncNotifier<DeviceListState> {\n  DeviceRepository get _repository => ref.read(deviceRepositoryProvider);\n\n  @override\n  Future<DeviceListState> build() async {\n    final devices = await ref.watch(deviceRepositoryProvider).fetchDevices();\n    return DeviceListState(devices: devices);\n  }\n\n  Future<void> refresh() async {\n    try {\n      final devices = await _repository.fetchDevices();\n      if (!ref.mounted) return;\n      _update((s) => s.copyWith(devices: devices));\n    } on DeviceException {\n      if (!ref.mounted) return;\n      _update((s) => s.copyWith(userMessage: () => UserMessage.refreshFailed));\n    }\n  }\n\n  void setOnlineOnly(bool value) => _update((s) => s.copyWith(onlineOnly: value));\n\n  Future<void> toggleFavorite(String id) async {\n    final current = state.value;\n    if (current == null) return;\n    final newValue = !current.devices.firstWhere((d) => d.id == id).isFavorite;\n    _update((s) => s.copyWith(devices: _withFavorite(s.devices, id, newValue)));\n    try {\n      await _repository.setFavorite(id, favorite: newValue);\n    } on DeviceException {\n      if (!ref.mounted) return;\n      _update((s) => s.copyWith(\n            devices: _withFavorite(s.devices, id, !newValue),\n            userMessage: () => UserMessage.favoriteFailed,\n          ));\n    }\n  }\n\n  void userMessageShown() => _update((s) => s.copyWith(userMessage: () => null));\n\n  void _update(DeviceListState Function(DeviceListState) transform) {\n    final current = state.value;\n    if (current != null) state = AsyncData(transform(current));\n  }\n}\n\nList<Device> _withFavorite(List<Device> devices, String id, bool favorite) => [\n      for (final d in devices) d.id == id ? d.copyWith(isFavorite: favorite) : d,\n    ];\n```\n\nNotes:\n- `build` loads the first time the screen watches the provider; `autoDispose` frees it when the screen leaves.\n- `ref.watch` in `build` for dependencies, `ref.read` in methods.\n- After every `await`, check `ref.mounted` before touching `state`; the screen may be gone.\n- `_update` always transforms the latest state, so a filter toggled during a refresh is not overwritten.\n- A failed refresh keeps the data and shows a message instead of replacing the list with an error page.\n- With code generation (`@riverpod` annotations) the provider is generated; the class body is the same.\n\n## Screen\n\n```dart\nclass DeviceListScreen extends ConsumerWidget {\n  const DeviceListScreen({super.key});\n\n  @override\n  Widget build(BuildContext context, WidgetRef ref) {\n    ref.listen(deviceListProvider.select((s) => s.value?.userMessage), (_, message) {\n      if (message == null) return;\n      ScaffoldMessenger.of(context).showSnackBar(SnackBar(content: Text(message.text(context))));\n      ref.read(deviceListProvider.notifier).userMessageShown();\n    });\n\n    final asyncState = ref.watch(deviceListProvider);\n\n    return Scaffold(\n      appBar: AppBar(\n        title: Text(context.l10n.devicesTitle), // context.l10n: the app's AppLocalizations extension\n        actions: [\n          Switch(\n            value: asyncState.value?.onlineOnly ?? false,\n            onChanged: (value) => ref.read(deviceListProvider.notifier).setOnlineOnly(value),\n          ),\n        ],\n      ),\n      body: switch (asyncState) {\n        AsyncData(:final value) => RefreshIndicator(\n            onRefresh: () => ref.read(deviceListProvider.notifier).refresh(),\n            child: ListView.builder(\n              itemCount: value.visibleDevices.length,\n              itemBuilder: (context, index) {\n                final device = value.visibleDevices[index];\n                return DeviceTile(\n                  key: ValueKey(device.id),\n                  device: device,\n                  onFavoriteTap: () => ref.read(deviceListProvider.notifier).toggleFavorite(device.id),\n                );\n              },\n            ),\n          ),\n        AsyncError() => ErrorView(onRetry: () => ref.invalidate(deviceListProvider)),\n        _ => const Center(child: CircularProgressIndicator()),\n      },\n    );\n  }\n}\n\nextension on UserMessage {\n  String text(BuildContext context) => switch (this) {\n        UserMessage.refreshFailed => context.l10n.devicesRefreshFailed,\n        UserMessage.favoriteFailed => context.l10n.devicesFavoriteFailed,\n      };\n}\n```\n\n- `ref.listen` performs the one-off effect and acknowledges it; `ref.watch` only renders.\n- `DeviceTile` is a separate `StatelessWidget` with a `const` constructor, not a helper method.\n\n## Saved state\n\nRiverpod state is lost on process death. For small widget-owned input, use Flutter state restoration\n(requires `restorationScopeId` on `MaterialApp`):\n\n```dart\nclass _SearchFieldState extends State<SearchField> with RestorationMixin {\n  final _query = RestorableTextEditingController();\n\n  @override\n  String? get restorationId => 'device_search';\n\n  @override\n  void restoreState(RestorationBucket? oldBucket, bool initialRestore) {\n    registerForRestoration(_query, 'query');\n  }\n\n  @override\n  void dispose() {\n    _query.dispose();\n    super.dispose();\n  }\n\n  @override\n  Widget build(BuildContext context) => TextField(controller: _query.value);\n}\n```\n\n## Tests\n\n```dart\nclass FakeDeviceRepository implements DeviceRepository {\n  FakeDeviceRepository(this.devices);\n\n  List<Device> devices;\n  Exception? fetchError;\n  Exception? favoriteError;\n\n  @override\n  Future<List<Device>> fetchDevices() async {\n    if (fetchError case final error?) throw error;\n    return devices;\n  }\n\n  @override\n  Future<void> setFavorite(String id, {required bool favorite}) async {\n    if (favoriteError case final error?) throw error;\n  }\n}\n\nvoid main() {\n  const online = Device(id: '1', name: 'Gateway', isOnline: true);\n  const offline = Device(id: '2', name: 'Sensor', isOnline: false);\n\n  late FakeDeviceRepository repository;\n  late ProviderContainer container;\n\n  setUp(() {\n    repository = FakeDeviceRepository([online, offline]);\n    container = ProviderContainer(overrides: [deviceRepositoryProvider.overrideWithValue(repository)]);\n    addTearDown(container.dispose);\n    container.listen(deviceListProvider, (_, _) {}); // keeps the autoDispose provider alive\n  });\n\n  DeviceListNotifier notifier() => container.read(deviceListProvider.notifier);\n  DeviceListState current() => container.read(deviceListProvider).value!;\n\n  test('loads devices', () async {\n    final state = await container.read(deviceListProvider.future);\n    expect(state.devices.map((d) => d.id), ['1', '2']);\n  });\n\n  test('online only hides offline devices', () async {\n    await container.read(deviceListProvider.future);\n    notifier().setOnlineOnly(true);\n    expect(current().visibleDevices.map((d) => d.id), ['1']);\n  });\n\n  test('refresh failure keeps data and shows message until acknowledged', () async {\n    await container.read(deviceListProvider.future);\n    repository.fetchError = DeviceException();\n\n    await notifier().refresh();\n\n    expect(current().devices, hasLength(2));\n    expect(current().userMessage, UserMessage.refreshFailed);\n    notifier().userMessageShown();\n    expect(current().userMessage, isNull);\n  });\n\n  test('favorite failure rolls back', () async {\n    await container.read(deviceListProvider.future);\n    repository.favoriteError = DeviceException();\n\n    await notifier().toggleFavorite('1');\n\n    expect(current().devices.first.isFavorite, isFalse);\n    expect(current().userMessage, UserMessage.favoriteFailed);\n  });\n}\n```\n\n- Override the repository provider, not the notifier; the notifier is the unit under test.\n- `container.listen` keeps an `autoDispose` provider alive between reads in a test.\n\n## Simpler option: ChangeNotifier with provider\n\nFine for small apps or teams new to Flutter. Same layering, more manual work.\n\n```dart\nclass DeviceListController extends ChangeNotifier {\n  DeviceListController(this._repository);\n\n  final DeviceRepository _repository;\n  DeviceListState _state = const DeviceListState();\n  bool _isLoading = false;\n  bool _disposed = false;\n\n  DeviceListState get state => _state;\n  bool get isLoading => _isLoading;\n\n  Future<void> load() async {\n    _isLoading = true;\n    notifyListeners();\n    try {\n      final devices = await _repository.fetchDevices();\n      _state = _state.copyWith(devices: devices);\n    } on DeviceException {\n      _state = _state.copyWith(userMessage: () => UserMessage.refreshFailed);\n    } finally {\n      _isLoading = false;\n      if (!_disposed) notifyListeners();\n    }\n  }\n\n  @override\n  void dispose() {\n    _disposed = true;\n    super.dispose();\n  }\n}\n\n// Wiring\nChangeNotifierProvider(\n  create: (context) => DeviceListController(context.read<DeviceRepository>())..load(),\n  child: const DeviceListScreen(),\n);\n\n// In build: context.watch<DeviceListController>().state\n// In callbacks: context.read<DeviceListController>().load()\n```\n\n- `notifyListeners()` after `dispose()` throws, so async methods guard it.\n- Test it as a plain Dart object: construct with a fake, call `load()`, assert `state`.\n",
    "references/java.md":
        "# MVVM with Java, LiveData, XML and ViewBinding (legacy)\n\nLoad this when maintaining Java screens. New screens should be Kotlin + Compose (see\n[the Kotlin reference](kotlin.md)); this file shows how to keep legacy code correct until it is migrated.\n\nThe feature is the same device list: observe devices, refresh, \"online only\" filter that survives process\ndeath, and a message when refresh fails.\n\n## Contract\n\n```java\npublic interface DeviceRepository {\n    LiveData<List<Device>> observeDevices();          // for example a Room DAO returning LiveData\n\n    /** Callbacks are delivered on the main thread. */\n    Cancellable refresh(Callback callback);\n\n    interface Callback {\n        void onSuccess();\n        void onError(@NonNull IOException error);\n    }\n\n    interface Cancellable {\n        void cancel();\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- The repository runs IO on its own `ExecutorService` and posts results to the main thread, so the\n  ViewModel only uses `setValue` and never races on read-modify-write.\n\n## UI state\n\n```java\npublic enum UserMessage { REFRESH_FAILED }\n\npublic final class DeviceListUiState {\n    public final List<Device> devices;\n    public final boolean onlineOnly;\n    public final boolean isRefreshing;\n    @Nullable public final UserMessage userMessage;\n\n    public DeviceListUiState(List<Device> devices, boolean onlineOnly, boolean isRefreshing,\n                             @Nullable UserMessage userMessage) {\n        this.devices = Collections.unmodifiableList(new ArrayList<>(devices));\n        this.onlineOnly = onlineOnly;\n        this.isRefreshing = isRefreshing;\n        this.userMessage = userMessage;\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- Immutable fields and an unmodifiable list. Override `equals` and `hashCode` if the state is compared\n  (tests, `distinctUntilChanged`).\n\n## ViewModel\n\n```java\n@HiltViewModel\npublic final class DeviceListViewModel extends ViewModel {\n\n    static final String KEY_ONLINE_ONLY = \"online_only\";\n\n    private final DeviceRepository repository;\n    private final SavedStateHandle savedStateHandle;\n    private final MediatorLiveData<DeviceListUiState> uiState = new MediatorLiveData<>();\n\n    private List<Device> allDevices = Collections.emptyList();\n    private boolean onlineOnly;\n    private boolean isRefreshing;\n    @Nullable private UserMessage userMessage;\n    @Nullable private DeviceRepository.Cancellable inFlight;\n\n    @Inject\n    public DeviceListViewModel(DeviceRepository repository, SavedStateHandle savedStateHandle) {\n        this.repository = repository;\n        this.savedStateHandle = savedStateHandle;\n        uiState.addSource(repository.observeDevices(), devices -> {\n            allDevices = devices;\n            publish();\n        });\n        uiState.addSource(savedStateHandle.getLiveData(KEY_ONLINE_ONLY, false), value -> {\n            onlineOnly = value;\n            publish();\n        });\n    }\n\n    public LiveData<DeviceListUiState> getUiState() {\n        return uiState;\n    }\n\n    public void refresh() {\n        if (isRefreshing) return;\n        isRefreshing = true;\n        publish();\n        inFlight = repository.refresh(new DeviceRepository.Callback() {\n            @Override public void onSuccess() {\n                isRefreshing = false;\n                publish();\n            }\n\n            @Override public void onError(@NonNull IOException error) {\n                isRefreshing = false;\n                userMessage = UserMessage.REFRESH_FAILED;\n                publish();\n            }\n        });\n    }\n\n    public void setOnlineOnly(boolean value) {\n        if (value != onlineOnly) savedStateHandle.set(KEY_ONLINE_ONLY, value);\n    }\n\n    public void userMessageShown() {\n        userMessage = null;\n        publish();\n    }\n\n    private void publish() {\n        List<Device> visible = new ArrayList<>();\n        for (Device device : allDevices) {\n            if (!onlineOnly || device.isOnline()) visible.add(device);\n        }\n        uiState.setValue(new DeviceListUiState(visible, onlineOnly, isRefreshing, userMessage));\n    }\n\n    @Override\n    protected void onCleared() {\n        if (inFlight != null) inFlight.cancel();\n    }\n}\n```\n\nNotes:\n- `MediatorLiveData` observes its sources only while it has an active observer, so it follows the\n  Fragment's view lifecycle automatically.\n- The anonymous callback holds the ViewModel, not the Fragment, and is cancelled in `onCleared()`.\n- No `SingleLiveEvent`: the message is state, cleared by `userMessageShown()`.\n\n## Fragment\n\n```java\n@AndroidEntryPoint\npublic final class DeviceListFragment extends Fragment {\n\n    @Nullable private FragmentDeviceListBinding binding;\n    private DeviceListViewModel viewModel;\n    private final DeviceAdapter adapter = new DeviceAdapter(); // ListAdapter with a DiffUtil.ItemCallback\n\n    @Override\n    public View onCreateView(@NonNull LayoutInflater inflater, @Nullable ViewGroup container,\n                             @Nullable Bundle savedInstanceState) {\n        binding = FragmentDeviceListBinding.inflate(inflater, container, false);\n        return binding.getRoot();\n    }\n\n    @Override\n    public void onViewCreated(@NonNull View view, @Nullable Bundle savedInstanceState) {\n        viewModel = new ViewModelProvider(this).get(DeviceListViewModel.class);\n        binding.list.setAdapter(adapter);\n        binding.swipeRefresh.setOnRefreshListener(viewModel::refresh);\n        binding.onlineOnly.setOnCheckedChangeListener((button, checked) -> viewModel.setOnlineOnly(checked));\n        viewModel.getUiState().observe(getViewLifecycleOwner(), this::render);\n    }\n\n    private void render(DeviceListUiState state) {\n        adapter.submitList(state.devices);\n        binding.swipeRefresh.setRefreshing(state.isRefreshing);\n        if (binding.onlineOnly.isChecked() != state.onlineOnly) {\n            binding.onlineOnly.setChecked(state.onlineOnly);\n        }\n        if (state.userMessage == UserMessage.REFRESH_FAILED) {\n            Snackbar.make(binding.getRoot(), R.string.devices_refresh_failed, Snackbar.LENGTH_SHORT).show();\n            viewModel.userMessageShown();\n        }\n    }\n\n    @Override\n    public void onDestroyView() {\n        binding = null;\n        super.onDestroyView();\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- `getViewLifecycleOwner()`, not `this`, so observers do not pile up after back navigation.\n- `render` is idempotent: it sets every view from state, guarding listeners that would echo back.\n- The binding is cleared in `onDestroyView` to avoid leaking the view hierarchy.\n\n## Tests\n\n```java\npublic class DeviceListViewModelTest {\n\n    @Rule public InstantTaskExecutorRule instantTaskExecutorRule = new InstantTaskExecutorRule();\n\n    private final FakeDeviceRepository repository = new FakeDeviceRepository();\n    private final Observer<DeviceListUiState> observer = state -> { };\n    private DeviceListViewModel viewModel;\n\n    @Before\n    public void setUp() {\n        viewModel = new DeviceListViewModel(repository, new SavedStateHandle());\n        viewModel.getUiState().observeForever(observer); // MediatorLiveData needs an active observer\n    }\n\n    @After\n    public void tearDown() {\n        viewModel.getUiState().removeObserver(observer);\n    }\n\n    @Test\n    public void refreshFailureShowsMessageUntilAcknowledged() {\n        repository.nextRefreshError = new IOException(\"offline\");\n\n        viewModel.refresh();\n\n        DeviceListUiState state = viewModel.getUiState().getValue();\n        assertEquals(UserMessage.REFRESH_FAILED, state.userMessage);\n        assertFalse(state.isRefreshing);\n\n        viewModel.userMessageShown();\n        assertNull(viewModel.getUiState().getValue().userMessage);\n    }\n\n    @Test\n    public void onlineOnlyHidesOfflineDevices() {\n        repository.devices.setValue(Arrays.asList(Devices.ONLINE, Devices.OFFLINE));\n\n        viewModel.setOnlineOnly(true);\n\n        assertEquals(Collections.singletonList(Devices.ONLINE), viewModel.getUiState().getValue().devices);\n    }\n}\n\nfinal class FakeDeviceRepository implements DeviceRepository {\n    final MutableLiveData<List<Device>> devices = new MutableLiveData<>(Collections.emptyList());\n    @Nullable IOException nextRefreshError;\n\n    @Override public LiveData<List<Device>> observeDevices() { return devices; }\n\n    @Override public Cancellable refresh(Callback callback) {\n        if (nextRefreshError != null) callback.onError(nextRefreshError); else callback.onSuccess();\n        return () -> { };\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- `Devices.ONLINE` and `Devices.OFFLINE` are test fixtures (one online, one offline `Device`).\n- `InstantTaskExecutorRule` (from `androidx.arch.core:core-testing`) runs LiveData work synchronously.\n- The fake calls back synchronously, matching the \"main thread\" contract without real executors.\n\n## Migration path to Kotlin\n\n1. Convert the state class to a Kotlin `data class`; Java callers keep working.\n2. Convert the ViewModel; expose `StateFlow` and, for remaining Java Fragments, `uiState.asLiveData()`.\n3. Replace the Fragment with a Compose screen hosted in the same navigation graph.\n4. Delete the `LiveData` bridge when no Java observer is left.\n",
    "references/kotlin.md":
        "# MVVM with Kotlin, Jetpack Compose and Hilt\n\nLoad this when building or reviewing a Kotlin ViewModel and Compose screen. Assumes Kotlin 2.x,\nCompose with strong skipping, AndroidX Lifecycle 2.8 or newer, Hilt and Turbine in tests.\n\nThe feature: a device list that observes devices from a repository, supports pull to refresh,\nan \"online only\" filter that survives process death, favorites, and a snackbar when refresh fails.\n\n## Domain and data contract\n\n```kotlin\ndata class Device(\n    val id: String,\n    val name: String,\n    val isOnline: Boolean,\n    val isFavorite: Boolean,\n)\n\ninterface DeviceRepository {\n    fun observeDevices(): Flow<List<Device>>\n    suspend fun refresh()\n    suspend fun setFavorite(id: String, favorite: Boolean)\n}\n```\n\n- The repository implementation owns its dispatcher, caching and DTO mapping. The ViewModel never sees them.\n\n## UI state\n\n```kotlin\nenum class UserMessage { RefreshFailed, FavoriteFailed }\n\ndata class DeviceListUiState(\n    val devices: List<Device> = emptyList(),\n    val onlineOnly: Boolean = false,\n    val isLoading: Boolean = false,\n    val isRefreshing: Boolean = false,\n    val userMessage: UserMessage? = null,\n) {\n    val isEmpty: Boolean get() = !isLoading && devices.isEmpty()\n}\n```\n\n- `List<Device>` is unstable for the Compose compiler. With strong skipping it is compared by instance,\n  which is fine because each state copy is a new object only when something changed. Use\n  `kotlinx.collections.immutable` if profiling shows extra recompositions.\n\n## ViewModel\n\n```kotlin\n@HiltViewModel\nclass DeviceListViewModel @Inject constructor(\n    private val repository: DeviceRepository,\n    private val savedStateHandle: SavedStateHandle,\n) : ViewModel() {\n\n    private val onlineOnly = savedStateHandle.getStateFlow(KEY_ONLINE_ONLY, false)\n    private val isRefreshing = MutableStateFlow(false)\n    private val userMessage = MutableStateFlow<UserMessage?>(null)\n\n    val uiState: StateFlow<DeviceListUiState> = combine(\n        repository.observeDevices(),\n        onlineOnly,\n        isRefreshing,\n        userMessage,\n    ) { devices, onlineOnly, refreshing, message ->\n        DeviceListUiState(\n            devices = if (onlineOnly) devices.filter { it.isOnline } else devices,\n            onlineOnly = onlineOnly,\n            isRefreshing = refreshing,\n            userMessage = message,\n        )\n    }.stateIn(\n        scope = viewModelScope,\n        started = SharingStarted.WhileSubscribed(5_000),\n        initialValue = DeviceListUiState(isLoading = true),\n    )\n\n    fun onRefresh() {\n        if (isRefreshing.value) return\n        viewModelScope.launch {\n            isRefreshing.value = true\n            try {\n                repository.refresh()\n            } catch (e: IOException) {\n                userMessage.value = UserMessage.RefreshFailed\n            } finally {\n                isRefreshing.value = false\n            }\n        }\n    }\n\n    fun onToggleOnlineOnly() {\n        savedStateHandle[KEY_ONLINE_ONLY] = !onlineOnly.value\n    }\n\n    fun onFavoriteClick(device: Device) {\n        viewModelScope.launch {\n            try {\n                repository.setFavorite(device.id, !device.isFavorite)\n            } catch (e: IOException) {\n                userMessage.value = UserMessage.FavoriteFailed\n            }\n        }\n    }\n\n    fun onUserMessageShown() {\n        userMessage.value = null\n    }\n\n    companion object {\n        internal const val KEY_ONLINE_ONLY = \"online_only\"\n    }\n}\n```\n\nNotes:\n- Local UI flags (`isRefreshing`, `userMessage`) are small private flows combined with repository data,\n  so there is still exactly one public state.\n- The favorite toggle writes to the repository; the observed flow updates the list. No manual copy of the list.\n- `IOException` stands in for the domain error type the repository throws. Never catch `Exception`\n  around suspend calls without rethrowing `CancellationException`.\n\n## Passing navigation arguments\n\n- With type-safe Navigation Compose routes, read them from `SavedStateHandle` (`savedStateHandle.toRoute<T>()`).\n- With Navigation 3, where the app holds the back stack and the key, pass the key into the ViewModel with\n  Hilt assisted injection (`@AssistedInject`, `@AssistedFactory`, `@HiltViewModel(assistedFactory = ...)`)\n  and create it with `hiltViewModel<VM, VM.Factory> { factory -> factory.create(key) }`.\n- Never pass arguments through a setter the UI calls after creation; it runs again on recomposition and\n  is skipped after process death.\n\n## Screen\n\n```kotlin\n@Composable\nfun DeviceListRoute(\n    onDeviceClick: (String) -> Unit,\n    viewModel: DeviceListViewModel = hiltViewModel(),\n) {\n    val uiState by viewModel.uiState.collectAsStateWithLifecycle()\n    DeviceListScreen(\n        state = uiState,\n        onRefresh = viewModel::onRefresh,\n        onToggleOnlineOnly = viewModel::onToggleOnlineOnly,\n        onFavoriteClick = viewModel::onFavoriteClick,\n        onDeviceClick = onDeviceClick,\n        onUserMessageShown = viewModel::onUserMessageShown,\n    )\n}\n\n@Composable\nfun DeviceListScreen(\n    state: DeviceListUiState,\n    onRefresh: () -> Unit,\n    onToggleOnlineOnly: () -> Unit,\n    onFavoriteClick: (Device) -> Unit,\n    onDeviceClick: (String) -> Unit,\n    onUserMessageShown: () -> Unit,\n    modifier: Modifier = Modifier,\n) {\n    val snackbarHostState = remember { SnackbarHostState() }\n\n    state.userMessage?.let { message ->\n        val text = message.toText()\n        LaunchedEffect(message) {\n            snackbarHostState.showSnackbar(text)\n            onUserMessageShown()\n        }\n    }\n\n    Scaffold(modifier = modifier, snackbarHost = { SnackbarHost(snackbarHostState) }) { padding ->\n        PullToRefreshBox(\n            isRefreshing = state.isRefreshing,\n            onRefresh = onRefresh,\n            modifier = Modifier.padding(padding),\n        ) {\n            when {\n                state.isLoading -> CircularProgressIndicator()\n                state.isEmpty -> Text(stringResource(R.string.devices_empty))\n                else -> LazyColumn {\n                    item {\n                        FilterRow(checked = state.onlineOnly, onCheckedChange = { onToggleOnlineOnly() })\n                    }\n                    items(state.devices, key = { it.id }) { device ->\n                        DeviceRow(\n                            device = device,\n                            onClick = { onDeviceClick(device.id) },\n                            onFavoriteClick = { onFavoriteClick(device) },\n                        )\n                    }\n                }\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}\n\n@Composable\nprivate fun UserMessage.toText(): String = when (this) {\n    UserMessage.RefreshFailed -> stringResource(R.string.devices_refresh_failed)\n    UserMessage.FavoriteFailed -> stringResource(R.string.devices_favorite_failed)\n}\n```\n\n- `DeviceListScreen` has no ViewModel parameter, so previews and screenshot tests pass fake states.\n- The snackbar is cleared only after it was shown. Rotation while it is visible shows it again instead\n  of losing it.\n- Navigation on click needs no ViewModel round trip when there is no logic; the route receives a lambda.\n\n## Tests\n\n```kotlin\nclass MainDispatcherRule(\n    private val dispatcher: TestDispatcher = UnconfinedTestDispatcher(),\n) : TestWatcher() {\n    override fun starting(description: Description) = Dispatchers.setMain(dispatcher)\n    override fun finished(description: Description) = Dispatchers.resetMain()\n}\n\nclass FakeDeviceRepository : DeviceRepository {\n    val devices = MutableStateFlow<List<Device>>(emptyList())\n    var refreshError: IOException? = null\n\n    override fun observeDevices(): Flow<List<Device>> = devices\n    override suspend fun refresh() {\n        refreshError?.let { throw it }\n    }\n    override suspend fun setFavorite(id: String, favorite: Boolean) {\n        devices.update { list -> list.map { if (it.id == id) it.copy(isFavorite = favorite) else it } }\n    }\n}\n\nclass DeviceListViewModelTest {\n    @get:Rule val mainDispatcherRule = MainDispatcherRule()\n\n    private val repository = FakeDeviceRepository()\n    private val online = Device(\"1\", \"Gateway\", isOnline = true, isFavorite = false)\n    private val offline = Device(\"2\", \"Sensor\", isOnline = false, isFavorite = false)\n\n    @Test\n    fun `online only filter hides offline devices and is saved`() = runTest {\n        val handle = SavedStateHandle()\n        val viewModel = DeviceListViewModel(repository, handle)\n        backgroundScope.launch(UnconfinedTestDispatcher(testScheduler)) { viewModel.uiState.collect() }\n        repository.devices.value = listOf(online, offline)\n\n        viewModel.onToggleOnlineOnly()\n\n        assertEquals(listOf(online), viewModel.uiState.value.devices)\n        assertEquals(true, handle.get<Boolean>(DeviceListViewModel.KEY_ONLINE_ONLY))\n    }\n\n    @Test\n    fun `filter is restored after process death`() = runTest {\n        val handle = SavedStateHandle(mapOf(DeviceListViewModel.KEY_ONLINE_ONLY to true))\n        val viewModel = DeviceListViewModel(repository, handle)\n        backgroundScope.launch(UnconfinedTestDispatcher(testScheduler)) { viewModel.uiState.collect() }\n        repository.devices.value = listOf(online, offline)\n\n        assertTrue(viewModel.uiState.value.onlineOnly)\n        assertEquals(listOf(online), viewModel.uiState.value.devices)\n    }\n\n    @Test\n    fun `refresh failure shows message until acknowledged`() = runTest {\n        repository.refreshError = IOException(\"offline\")\n        val viewModel = DeviceListViewModel(repository, SavedStateHandle())\n\n        viewModel.uiState.test {\n            viewModel.onRefresh()\n            advanceUntilIdle()\n            val failed = expectMostRecentItem()\n            assertEquals(UserMessage.RefreshFailed, failed.userMessage)\n            assertFalse(failed.isRefreshing)\n\n            viewModel.onUserMessageShown()\n            assertNull(awaitItem().userMessage)\n        }\n    }\n\n    @Test\n    fun `favorite click updates the observed list`() = runTest {\n        val viewModel = DeviceListViewModel(repository, SavedStateHandle())\n        backgroundScope.launch(UnconfinedTestDispatcher(testScheduler)) { viewModel.uiState.collect() }\n        repository.devices.value = listOf(online)\n\n        viewModel.onFavoriteClick(online)\n\n        assertTrue(viewModel.uiState.value.devices.single().isFavorite)\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- `stateIn` with `WhileSubscribed` does nothing until someone collects. Either collect in `backgroundScope`\n  and read `.value`, or use Turbine's `test {}`.\n- When `Dispatchers.Main` is a `TestDispatcher`, `runTest` shares its scheduler, so `advanceUntilIdle()`\n  also drives `viewModelScope`.\n- `SavedStateHandle()` and `SavedStateHandle(map)` work in plain JVM tests; no Robolectric needed.\n",
    "references/swift.md":
        "# MVVM with Swift, SwiftUI and Observation\n\nLoad this when building or reviewing a SwiftUI screen and its view model. Assumes Swift 6 language mode,\niOS 17 or newer (`@Observable`), and Swift Testing.\n\nThe feature: a device list loaded from a repository, pull to refresh, an \"online only\" filter restored\nper scene, favorites with optimistic update and rollback, and an alert when something fails.\n\n## Domain and data contract\n\n```swift\nstruct Device: Identifiable, Equatable, Sendable {\n    let id: String\n    var name: String\n    var isOnline: Bool\n    var isFavorite: Bool\n}\n\nprotocol DeviceRepository: Sendable {\n    func fetchDevices() async throws -> [Device]\n    func setFavorite(_ favorite: Bool, for id: Device.ID) async throws\n}\n```\n\n- The protocol is the seam for fakes. The real implementation owns `URLSession`, decoding and caching.\n\n## State\n\n```swift\nenum UserMessage: Equatable {\n    case loadFailed\n    case favoriteFailed\n\n    var title: String {\n        switch self {\n        case .loadFailed: String(localized: \"Could not load devices\")\n        case .favoriteFailed: String(localized: \"Could not update favorite\")\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nstruct DeviceListState: Equatable {\n    var devices: [Device] = []\n    var onlineOnly = false\n    var isLoading = false\n    var userMessage: UserMessage?\n\n    var visibleDevices: [Device] {\n        onlineOnly ? devices.filter(\\.isOnline) : devices\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- `visibleDevices` is derived, so it can never disagree with `devices` and `onlineOnly`.\n\n## View model\n\n```swift\n@MainActor\n@Observable\nfinal class DeviceListModel {\n    private(set) var state = DeviceListState()\n\n    @ObservationIgnored private let repository: any DeviceRepository\n\n    init(repository: any DeviceRepository) {\n        self.repository = repository\n    }\n\n    func load() async {\n        state.isLoading = true\n        defer { state.isLoading = false }\n        do {\n            state.devices = try await repository.fetchDevices()\n        } catch is CancellationError {\n            return\n        } catch {\n            state.userMessage = .loadFailed\n        }\n    }\n\n    func setOnlineOnly(_ value: Bool) {\n        state.onlineOnly = value\n    }\n\n    func toggleFavorite(_ id: Device.ID) async {\n        guard let index = state.devices.firstIndex(where: { \$0.id == id }) else { return }\n        let newValue = !state.devices[index].isFavorite\n        state.devices[index].isFavorite = newValue\n        do {\n            try await repository.setFavorite(newValue, for: id)\n        } catch {\n            if let i = state.devices.firstIndex(where: { \$0.id == id }) {\n                state.devices[i].isFavorite = !newValue\n            }\n            state.userMessage = .favoriteFailed\n        }\n    }\n\n    func userMessageShown() {\n        state.userMessage = nil\n    }\n}\n```\n\nNotes:\n- `@MainActor` makes every state mutation main-thread safe; repository calls suspend off it.\n- `@ObservationIgnored` on dependencies, so reading them does not register view dependencies.\n- After `await`, the array may have changed. The rollback looks the index up again instead of reusing it.\n- `URLSession` reports cancellation as `URLError(.cancelled)`, not `CancellationError`. Map it in the\n  repository or check `Task.isCancelled` before reporting an error.\n\n## View\n\n```swift\nstruct DeviceListView: View {\n    @State private var model: DeviceListModel\n    @SceneStorage(\"deviceList.onlineOnly\") private var storedOnlineOnly = false\n\n    init(repository: any DeviceRepository) {\n        _model = State(initialValue: DeviceListModel(repository: repository))\n    }\n\n    var body: some View {\n        List(model.state.visibleDevices) { device in\n            DeviceRow(device: device) {\n                Task { await model.toggleFavorite(device.id) }\n            }\n        }\n        .overlay {\n            if model.state.isLoading && model.state.devices.isEmpty {\n                ProgressView()\n            }\n        }\n        .refreshable { await model.load() }\n        .toolbar {\n            Toggle(\"Online only\", isOn: Binding(\n                get: { model.state.onlineOnly },\n                set: { model.setOnlineOnly(\$0) }\n            ))\n        }\n        .alert(model.state.userMessage?.title ?? \"\", isPresented: Binding(\n            get: { model.state.userMessage != nil },\n            set: { if !\$0 { model.userMessageShown() } }\n        )) {\n            Button(\"OK\", role: .cancel) {}\n        }\n        .task {\n            model.setOnlineOnly(storedOnlineOnly)\n            await model.load()\n        }\n        .onChange(of: model.state.onlineOnly) { _, newValue in\n            storedOnlineOnly = newValue\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nstruct DeviceRow: View {\n    let device: Device\n    let onFavoriteTap: () -> Void\n\n    var body: some View {\n        HStack {\n            Text(device.name)\n            Spacer()\n            Button(action: onFavoriteTap) {\n                Image(systemName: device.isFavorite ? \"star.fill\" : \"star\")\n            }\n            .accessibilityLabel(device.isFavorite ? \"Remove favorite\" : \"Add favorite\")\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- `@State` owns the model; the view that creates it is the only owner. Child views get values and closures,\n  or the model as a plain property (`@Bindable` when they need bindings).\n- `State(initialValue:)` in `init` is evaluated on every parent redraw but only the first instance is kept.\n  Keep the model `init` cheap; start work in `.task`.\n- `.task` cancels `load()` when the view disappears.\n- `@SceneStorage` restores the filter per window after the app is terminated in the background.\n  It is not encrypted; store only non-sensitive UI values.\n- Dependencies can also come from a custom `EnvironmentValues` entry instead of the initializer.\n\n## Tests (Swift Testing)\n\n```swift\nimport Testing\n@testable import Devices\n\nstruct FakeDeviceRepository: DeviceRepository {\n    var devices: [Device] = []\n    var loadError: (any Error)?\n    var favoriteError: (any Error)?\n\n    func fetchDevices() async throws -> [Device] {\n        if let loadError { throw loadError }\n        return devices\n    }\n\n    func setFavorite(_ favorite: Bool, for id: Device.ID) async throws {\n        if let favoriteError { throw favoriteError }\n    }\n}\n\nextension Device {\n    static let online = Device(id: \"1\", name: \"Gateway\", isOnline: true, isFavorite: false)\n    static let offline = Device(id: \"2\", name: \"Sensor\", isOnline: false, isFavorite: false)\n}\n\n@MainActor\nstruct DeviceListModelTests {\n    @Test func loadPublishesDevices() async {\n        let model = DeviceListModel(repository: FakeDeviceRepository(devices: [.online, .offline]))\n\n        await model.load()\n\n        #expect(model.state.devices == [.online, .offline])\n        #expect(model.state.isLoading == false)\n    }\n\n    @Test func onlineOnlyFiltersDevices() async {\n        let model = DeviceListModel(repository: FakeDeviceRepository(devices: [.online, .offline]))\n        await model.load()\n\n        model.setOnlineOnly(true)\n\n        #expect(model.state.visibleDevices == [.online])\n    }\n\n    @Test func loadFailureShowsMessageUntilAcknowledged() async {\n        let model = DeviceListModel(repository: FakeDeviceRepository(loadError: URLError(.notConnectedToInternet)))\n\n        await model.load()\n        #expect(model.state.userMessage == .loadFailed)\n\n        model.userMessageShown()\n        #expect(model.state.userMessage == nil)\n    }\n\n    @Test func favoriteFailureRollsBack() async throws {\n        let repository = FakeDeviceRepository(devices: [.online], favoriteError: URLError(.timedOut))\n        let model = DeviceListModel(repository: repository)\n        await model.load()\n\n        await model.toggleFavorite(Device.online.id)\n\n        let device = try #require(model.state.devices.first)\n        #expect(device.isFavorite == false)\n        #expect(model.state.userMessage == .favoriteFailed)\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- The suite is `@MainActor` because the model is. Tests `await` the model's async functions directly;\n  no sleeps, no expectations with timeouts.\n- `Error` is `Sendable`, so a struct fake holding `(any Error)?` is `Sendable` without annotations.\n- For time-based logic (debounce, polling), inject `any Clock<Duration>` into the model.\n",
  },
  "mobile-security": {
    "SKILL.md":
        "---\nname: mobile-security\ndescription: \"Audits Android, iOS and Flutter apps (Kotlin, Java, Swift, Dart) against OWASP MASVS v2 (STORAGE, CRYPTO, AUTH, NETWORK, PLATFORM, CODE, RESILIENCE, PRIVACY) and OWASP Mobile Top 10 2024. Finds hardcoded secrets in BuildConfig, Info.plist and dart-define, insecure storage (Android Keystore, Tink, DataStore, deprecated EncryptedSharedPreferences, Keychain accessibility, flutter_secure_storage), cleartext traffic, ATS and network_security_config gaps, pinning risks, exported components, deep links, App Links and Universal Links, WebView bridges, PII in logs, BiometricPrompt without CryptoObject, LocalAuthentication misuse, R8, Play Integrity, App Attest, vulnerable dependencies and SBOM. Covers MQTT and BLE IoT companion apps plus GDPR, Cyber Resilience Act and EN 18031 notes. Reports findings with MASVS IDs, severity and file:line evidence. Use for security audits, pentests, compliance reviews or changes to auth, crypto, storage, network or IPC.\"\nlicense: MIT\nmetadata:\n  author: Ahmad Hussain\n  version: \"1.0.0\"\n  pack: mobile-dev-kit\n---\n\n# Mobile security audit\n\nAudit a mobile app the way a mobile pentester and a compliance reviewer would together: find what an\nattacker on the network, a malicious app on the device, or someone holding an unlocked or rooted phone can\nactually exploit, map it to an OWASP MASVS v2 control, and show the smallest fix that closes it.\n\n## When to use\n\n- The user asks for a security audit, security review, MASVS or OWASP Mobile Top 10 check, or pentest prep.\n- A change touches credentials, tokens, crypto, local storage, networking, TLS, pinning, IPC, deep links,\n  WebViews, biometrics, logging or third-party SDKs.\n- An IoT companion app talks to devices over BLE, MQTT or HTTPS and handles device identifiers.\n- Preparing evidence for GDPR, Cyber Resilience Act, RED Delegated Act (EN 18031) or ETSI EN 303 645 reviews.\n\nNot for: general code quality or concurrency review (use `mobile-code-review`), or CI pipeline setup\n(use `mobile-ci-cd`, though this skill flags missing dependency scanning and SBOM steps).\n\n## Workflow\n\n1. Define scope and threat model before reading code.\n   - What is sensitive: credentials, tokens, keys, health or location data, device identifiers, payment data.\n   - Which attackers matter: network attacker (public Wi-Fi), malicious app on the same device, lost or\n     stolen unlocked device, user with a rooted or jailbroken device, reverse engineer of the binary.\n   - Which backends and devices are under the developer's control (relevant for pinning and attestation).\n2. Inventory the attack surface and load the matching references:\n   [Android](references/android.md), [iOS](references/ios.md), [Flutter](references/flutter.md),\n   [MASVS controls and Top 10 map](references/masvs-map.md),\n   [EU compliance notes](references/eu-compliance.md) for EU-market or IoT products.\n   - Android: merged release manifest, network security config, backup rules, release build type, catalogs.\n   - iOS: `Info.plist`, entitlements, `PrivacyInfo.xcprivacy`, `.xcconfig`, lock files. Flutter: both of these.\n3. Run the quick-scan patterns from the references. Each hit is a candidate, not a finding.\n4. Walk the MASVS categories in order: STORAGE, CRYPTO, AUTH, NETWORK, PLATFORM, CODE, RESILIENCE, PRIVACY.\n   Read the full code path for every candidate, not just the matching line.\n5. Judge the release build: check flavor manifests, `debug-overrides`, `#if DEBUG` and `kDebugMode` guards.\n   A debug-only issue is Low at most, unless it can ship.\n6. Verify each finding: reachable in release? Mitigated elsewhere (server check, interceptor, OS default)?\n   If static review cannot confirm it, list it under \"Needs dynamic verification\", not as a finding.\n7. Rate severity, assign one MASVS v2 control ID and the matching Mobile Top 10 2024 item, and write a fix.\n   Only use control IDs listed in [MASVS controls](references/masvs-map.md).\n8. Report using the output format, including what was not checked. Do not edit files unless asked.\n\n## Severity levels\n\n| Severity | Definition | Examples |\n|---|---|---|\n| Critical | Exploitable remotely or at scale with no special access; compromises accounts, keys or many users' data | Backend admin key in the binary, trust-all `TrustManager`, cleartext login, MQTT credentials over port 1883 |\n| High | Exploitable with realistic preconditions (same network, malicious app installed, brief physical access); exposes sensitive data or bypasses auth | Exported component leaking tokens, unvalidated deep link loading any URL in a WebView with a JS bridge, boolean-only biometric gate on secrets |\n| Medium | Needs strong preconditions (root, jailbreak, backup extraction) or weakens defense in depth | Tokens in plaintext app storage, sensitive screens without screenshot protection, `allowBackup` with tokens |\n| Low | Hardening gap with little direct impact | No obfuscation, verbose non-sensitive logs, missing CT awareness, debug-only issue |\n\nRate by impact and likelihood in this app's threat model. A finding without a realistic attacker is not\nHigh because a checklist says so.\n\n## Rules\n\n### 1. No secrets in the app package (MASVS-CRYPTO-2, MASVS-AUTH-1, M1)\n\nEverything in an APK, IPA or Flutter bundle is readable: `BuildConfig`, `strings.xml`, `Info.plist`,\n`.xcconfig`, assets, `--dart-define` values. Obfuscation does not change that.\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: ends up as a constant in the APK\nbuildConfigField(\"String\", \"PAYMENTS_SECRET\", \"\\\"sk_live_...\\\"\")\n\n// GOOD: app gets a short-lived, user-scoped token from your backend after login\nval token = authRepository.sessionToken()\n```\n\n```swift\n// BAD: Info.plist and xcconfig values ship in plain text\nlet key = Bundle.main.object(forInfoDictionaryKey: \"BROKER_PASSWORD\") as? String\n```\n\n- Restricted public identifiers (OAuth client ID, Maps key locked to package and signing cert) are fine.\n  Anything that grants privilege on its own (server keys, signing keys, shared broker passwords) is not.\n- Check git history and CI config too; a secret ever committed must be rotated, not just deleted.\n\n### 2. Sensitive data at rest uses platform key stores (MASVS-STORAGE-1, MASVS-CRYPTO-2, M9)\n\n`androidx.security:security-crypto` (`EncryptedSharedPreferences`, `EncryptedFile`, `MasterKey`) is\ndeprecated. Do not add it; plan migration for existing users. Replacement: an Android Keystore key, either\ndirectly with `Cipher` or via Tink AEAD, with ciphertext stored in DataStore or a file.\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: plaintext token in SharedPreferences or DataStore\nprefs.edit { putString(\"refresh_token\", token) }\n\n// GOOD: Tink AEAD, keyset wrapped by an Android Keystore key (setup in references/android.md)\nval ciphertext = aead.encrypt(token.encodeToByteArray(), \"refresh_token\".encodeToByteArray())\ndataStore.edit { it[REFRESH_TOKEN] = Base64.encodeToString(ciphertext, Base64.NO_WRAP) }\n```\n\n```swift\n// BAD\nUserDefaults.standard.set(token, forKey: \"refreshToken\")\n\n// GOOD: Keychain, not migrated to other devices or backups\nlet query: [String: Any] = [\n    kSecClass as String: kSecClassGenericPassword,\n    kSecAttrService as String: \"com.example.auth\",\n    kSecAttrAccount as String: \"refreshToken\",\n    kSecValueData as String: Data(token.utf8),\n    kSecAttrAccessible as String: kSecAttrAccessibleAfterFirstUnlockThisDeviceOnly,\n]\nlet status = SecItemAdd(query as CFDictionary, nil)\n```\n\n```dart\n// BAD\nawait prefs.setString('refresh_token', token);\n\n// GOOD\nconst storage = FlutterSecureStorage(\n  iOptions: IOSOptions(accessibility: KeychainAccessibility.first_unlock_this_device),\n);\nawait storage.write(key: 'refresh_token', value: token);\n```\n\n- Pick the strictest Keychain accessibility class the feature allows; background refresh needs `AfterFirstUnlock`.\n- Never roll custom crypto: no ECB, no static IVs, no keys derived from device IDs, no `Random` for secrets.\n\n### 3. Sensitive data does not leak through side channels (MASVS-STORAGE-2, MASVS-PLATFORM-3, M9)\n\nLogs, backups, screenshots, the app switcher snapshot, clipboard and keyboard caches all leak.\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: Log.d survives in release unless stripped\nLog.d(\"Auth\", \"login ok token=\$token user=\$email\")\n\n// GOOD: no secrets or personal data in logs; strip debug logs in release with R8 rules\nLog.d(\"Auth\", \"login ok\")\n```\n\n```swift\n// BAD: explicitly public in the unified log\nlogger.info(\"token \\(token, privacy: .public)\")\n\n// GOOD\nlogger.info(\"login ok for user \\(userId, privacy: .private(mask: .hash))\")\n```\n\n- Android: `window.setFlags(FLAG_SECURE, FLAG_SECURE)` on screens with credentials, OTPs, payment or health data.\n- iOS has no API to block screenshots; cover sensitive content when `scenePhase` leaves `.active`.\n- Android backup: exclude token and keyset files via `dataExtractionRules` and `fullBackupContent`, or disable\n  backup. Keystore keys never leave the device, so restored ciphertext cannot be decrypted; handle that path.\n\n### 4. All traffic uses TLS with platform validation (MASVS-NETWORK-1, M5)\n\n```xml\n<!-- BAD -->\n<application android:usesCleartextTraffic=\"true\" ... >\n\n<!-- GOOD: res/xml/network_security_config.xml -->\n<network-security-config>\n    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted=\"false\" />\n</network-security-config>\n```\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: disables certificate and hostname validation for everything\nOkHttpClient.Builder()\n    .sslSocketFactory(trustAllFactory, trustAllManager)\n    .hostnameVerifier { _, _ -> true }\n\n// GOOD: platform defaults\nOkHttpClient.Builder().build()\n```\n\n- iOS: no `NSAllowsArbitraryLoads`; no `.useCredential` for a server trust that skipped `SecTrustEvaluateWithError`.\n- Dart: no `badCertificateCallback` returning `true`. `dart:io` does not apply ATS or the Android network config.\n\nIoT and MQTT: TLS always, including on \"internal\" networks. Never cleartext brokers.\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD\nMqttClient(\"tcp://broker.example.com:1883\", clientId, MemoryPersistence())\n\n// GOOD: TLS, per-device or per-user credentials, broker-side topic ACLs\nMqttClient(\"ssl://broker.example.com:8883\", clientId, MemoryPersistence())\n```\n\n- BLE: CRC16 or CRC32 detects corruption, not tampering. Commands that change device state need an\n  authenticated channel (LE Secure Connections pairing plus app-layer authentication, or a MAC/AEAD).\n\n### 5. Pinning only with a rotation plan (MASVS-NETWORK-2, M5)\n\nDefault: standard CA validation, which Certificate Transparency enforcement strengthens where the platform\napplies it. Pin only when the threat model requires it (high-value targets, controlled backends, IoT\nprovisioning endpoints) and the team owns key rotation.\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: one pin, no backup; the app stops connecting when the server key rotates\n.add(\"api.example.com\", \"sha256/<current-spki>\")\n\n// GOOD: SPKI pins for current key and a pre-generated backup key, with monitoring and a release plan\nCertificatePinner.Builder()\n    .add(\"api.example.com\", \"sha256/<current-spki>\")\n    .add(\"api.example.com\", \"sha256/<backup-spki>\")\n    .build()\n```\n\n- Android `<pin-set>` needs an `expiration` and a backup pin; iOS uses `NSPinnedDomains`. Never pin\n  third-party endpoints you do not control (analytics, CDNs, identity providers).\n- Pinning is bypassable on a rooted device; it protects users from network attackers, not the app from its user.\n\n### 6. Biometrics unlock a key, not a boolean (MASVS-AUTH-2, MASVS-AUTH-3, M3)\n\nA success callback alone can be hooked on a rooted or jailbroken device. Bind the secret to authentication.\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: boolean gate\nbiometricPrompt.authenticate(promptInfo)\n// onAuthenticationSucceeded { showVault() }\n\n// GOOD: Keystore key with setUserAuthenticationRequired(true); only an authenticated Cipher decrypts\nbiometricPrompt.authenticate(promptInfo, BiometricPrompt.CryptoObject(cipher))\n// onAuthenticationSucceeded { result -> result.cryptoObject?.cipher?.doFinal(encryptedToken) }\n```\n\n```swift\n// BAD: boolean gate\nif try await LAContext().evaluatePolicy(.deviceOwnerAuthenticationWithBiometrics, localizedReason: \"Unlock\") {\n    showVault()\n}\n\n// GOOD: Keychain item protected by access control; the OS releases it only after biometry\nlet access = SecAccessControlCreateWithFlags(\n    nil, kSecAttrAccessibleWhenPasscodeSetThisDeviceOnly, .biometryCurrentSet, nil)\n// add with kSecAttrAccessControl: access; read with kSecUseAuthenticationContext off the main thread\n```\n\n- `BIOMETRIC_STRONG` for crypto-bound auth. `setInvalidatedByBiometricEnrollment(true)` and `.biometryCurrentSet`\n  invalidate the key on new enrollment. iOS requires `NSFaceIDUsageDescription` in `Info.plist`.\n\n### 7. Sessions are controlled by the server (MASVS-AUTH-1, M3)\n\n- OAuth / OIDC uses the system browser (Custom Tabs, `ASWebAuthenticationSession`) with PKCE, not an\n  embedded WebView. Passkeys via Credential Manager or `AuthenticationServices` where supported.\n- Short-lived access tokens, refresh tokens in secure storage, server-side revocation on logout.\n- Authorization is enforced by the backend. A hidden button or a local `isAdmin` flag is not access control.\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: role decided on the client\nif (prefs.getBoolean(\"is_admin\", false)) showAdminPanel()\n\n// GOOD: UI hint only; every admin API call is authorized server-side\nif (session.claims.roles.contains(\"admin\")) showAdminPanel()\n```\n\n### 8. IPC entry points are minimal and validate input (MASVS-PLATFORM-1, MASVS-CODE-4, M4, M8)\n\n```xml\n<!-- BAD: internal screen reachable by any app -->\n<activity android:name=\".DebugMenuActivity\" android:exported=\"true\" />\n\n<!-- GOOD -->\n<activity android:name=\".DebugMenuActivity\" android:exported=\"false\" />\n```\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: deep link parameter goes straight into a privileged action\nwebView.loadUrl(intent.data?.getQueryParameter(\"url\").orEmpty())\n\n// GOOD: allowlist scheme and host\nval target = intent.data?.getQueryParameter(\"url\")?.toUri()\nif (target?.scheme == \"https\" && target.host in trustedHosts) webView.loadUrl(target.toString())\n```\n\n- iOS `onOpenURL` / `application(_:continue:)` and Flutter routers validate the same way before routing.\n- Custom URL schemes can be claimed by any app. Use verified App Links (`android:autoVerify=\"true\"` plus\n  `assetlinks.json`) and Universal Links (Associated Domains) for anything carrying tokens or codes.\n- Never launch an `Intent` taken from another app's extras (intent redirection).\n- `PendingIntent` uses `FLAG_IMMUTABLE` unless mutability is required, with an explicit target component.\n- Content providers: `exported=\"false\"` or signature-level permissions; no raw path joins on `openFile`.\n\n### 9. WebViews load trusted content and expose minimal bridges (MASVS-PLATFORM-2, M4)\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: JS bridge on any page the WebView navigates to\nwebView.settings.javaScriptEnabled = true\nwebView.addJavascriptInterface(TokenBridge(session), \"Android\")\nwebView.loadUrl(urlFromServerOrDeepLink)\n\n// GOOD: origin-scoped message listener, navigation restricted to an allowlist\nif (WebViewFeature.isFeatureSupported(WebViewFeature.WEB_MESSAGE_LISTENER)) {\n    WebViewCompat.addWebMessageListener(webView, \"bridge\", setOf(\"https://app.example.com\"), listener)\n}\n```\n\n- iOS: every `WKScriptMessageHandler` checks `message.frameInfo.securityOrigin` and `isMainFrame`.\n- Keep `allowFileAccess`, `allowFileAccessFromFileURLs` and `allowUniversalAccessFromFileURLs` off.\n  Serve local content through `WebViewAssetLoader`.\n- External links open in the browser, not inside the app WebView.\n\n### 10. Dependencies are known and scanned (MASVS-CODE-3, M2)\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: dynamic version, unreproducible and unscannable\nimplementation(\"com.example:sdk:+\")\n\n// GOOD\nimplementation(libs.example.sdk)\n```\n\n- Commit lock files (`Package.resolved`, `pubspec.lock`, `Podfile.lock`); scan them in CI (OSV-Scanner,\n  OWASP Dependency-Check, Dependabot or Renovate) and fail on Critical.\n- Generate a CycloneDX or SPDX SBOM per release and archive it with the build.\n- MASVS-CODE-1: no unjustified support for unpatched OS versions. MASVS-CODE-2: a forced-update path\n  exists (Play In-App Updates immediate flow, or a server-driven minimum version).\n\n### 11. Obfuscation and attestation raise cost; the server decides (MASVS-RESILIENCE-1 to 4, M7)\n\nR8, Swift symbol stripping and Flutter `--obfuscate` slow reverse engineering. None is a security boundary.\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: local check is the only control, patched out in minutes\nif (RootChecker.isRooted()) finish()\n\n// GOOD: attestation token sent with the sensitive request; backend verifies and decides\nval token = integrityTokenProvider.request(\n    StandardIntegrityTokenRequest.builder().setRequestHash(requestHash).build()).await()\napi.transfer(request, integrityToken = token.token())\n```\n\n- Android: Play Integrity API, verdict decrypted and checked on the server. SafetyNet Attestation is gone.\n- iOS: App Attest (`DCAppAttestService`) with server-side attestation and assertion verification;\n  DeviceCheck for per-device bits.\n- Release builds: `isMinifyEnabled = true`, `android:debuggable` not set, no `get-task-allow` in distribution.\n\n### 12. Collect the minimum and treat device identifiers as personal data (MASVS-PRIVACY-1 to 4, M6)\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: telemetry carries direct identifiers it does not need\nTelemetry(mac = device.macAddress, serial = device.serial, email = user.email, rssi = rssi)\n\n// GOOD: only what the feature needs, pseudonymous ID rotated per installation\nTelemetry(installId = installId, firmware = device.firmwareVersion, rssi = rssi)\n```\n\n- MAC addresses, serials, IMEI, advertising IDs and IP addresses are personal data under GDPR when linkable\n  to a person; pseudonymised data is still personal data.\n- Request runtime permissions at point of use; prefer coarse location, `neverForLocation` for BLE scans\n  on Android 12+, and the photo picker over broad media access.\n- iOS: `PrivacyInfo.xcprivacy` declares collected data and required-reason APIs, consistent with the\n  App Store privacy labels. Android: the Play Data safety form matches what the code does.\n\n## Anti-patterns\n\n- Reporting checklist items without an attacker and a path. \"No root detection\" is not a finding by itself.\n- Auditing the debug build or a single file and calling the app secure.\n- Recommending pinning by default without a rotation, backup-pin and monitoring plan.\n- Treating R8, obfuscation, root or jailbreak detection as a fix for a server-side authorization flaw.\n- \"Encrypting\" with a key hardcoded next to the ciphertext, or moving a secret to native code and calling it hidden.\n- Suggesting `EncryptedSharedPreferences` for new code, or CRC as protection against an attacker.\n- Inventing MASVS control IDs, CVE numbers or legal deadlines. If unsure, say so.\n- Dumping real secrets found during the audit into the report. Show the location and a redacted prefix only.\n\n## Checklist\n\n- [ ] Threat model and data classification written down before findings\n- [ ] Release manifest, `Info.plist`, entitlements, network config and build types reviewed\n- [ ] No privileged secrets in code, resources, `BuildConfig`, `Info.plist`, `--dart-define` or git history\n- [ ] Tokens and keys in Keystore / Keychain-backed storage; no new `security-crypto` usage\n- [ ] Keychain accessibility class and Android backup rules checked\n- [ ] No secrets or personal data in release logs; `FLAG_SECURE` or snapshot cover on sensitive screens\n- [ ] Cleartext disabled; no trust-all managers, hostname verifiers or `badCertificateCallback`\n- [ ] MQTT and other IoT channels use TLS; BLE commands authenticated, not only CRC-checked\n- [ ] Pinning, if present, has backup pins, expiry and a rotation plan\n- [ ] Biometric auth bound to a `CryptoObject` or Keychain access control\n- [ ] OAuth via system browser with PKCE; authorization enforced server-side\n- [ ] Every exported component, deep link and App / Universal Link validated\n- [ ] WebView bridges origin-scoped; file access off; untrusted URLs not loaded\n- [ ] Dependencies pinned and scanned in CI, SBOM per release, attestation verified server-side if used\n- [ ] Telemetry minimised; device identifiers handled as personal data\n- [ ] \"Not checked\" section lists everything outside static review\n\n## Output format\n\n~~~markdown\n## Security audit: <app / module / MR>\n\nScope: <files, modules, commit>. Platforms: <Android | iOS | Flutter>. Build reviewed: <release variant>.\nThreat model: <assets, attackers considered>.\n\n### Findings\n\n1. [Critical] MASVS-NETWORK-1 / M5 -- Trust-all TrustManager in production client\n   Evidence: app/src/main/java/com/example/net/HttpModule.kt:42\n   Impact: any network attacker can read and modify all API traffic, including login credentials.\n   Fix:\n   ```kotlin\n   OkHttpClient.Builder().build() // remove custom sslSocketFactory and hostnameVerifier\n   ```\n\n2. [High] MASVS-AUTH-2 / M3 -- Biometric unlock is a boolean gate\n   Evidence: ios/App/Vault/VaultUnlock.swift:88\n   Impact: ...\n   Fix: ...\n\n### Needs dynamic verification\n- <items that static review could not confirm, for example \"pinning behaviour on proxy interception\">\n\n### Summary\n\n| MASVS category | Critical | High | Medium | Low | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| STORAGE | 0 | 0 | 1 | 0 | |\n| CRYPTO | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 | |\n| AUTH | 0 | 1 | 0 | 0 | |\n| NETWORK | 1 | 0 | 0 | 0 | |\n| PLATFORM | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 | |\n| CODE | 0 | 0 | 0 | 1 | |\n| RESILIENCE | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 | |\n| PRIVACY | 0 | 0 | 1 | 0 | |\n\n### Not checked\n- Backend authorization and API security\n- Runtime behaviour (proxy interception, Frida, rooted / jailbroken device testing)\n- Compiled binary (only source reviewed)\n- <modules, flavors or SDKs out of scope>\n\n### Compliance notes (if EU market or IoT)\n- <GDPR / CRA / RED DA EN 18031 points, see references/eu-compliance.md>\n~~~\n\nRules for the report:\n- One root cause is one finding; list extra locations under Evidence.\n- Every Critical and High has a concrete fix snippet or config change.\n- Redact any real secret found: show the file, line and first four characters only, and say \"rotate\".\n\n## Related skills\n\n- `mobile-code-review` -- general correctness, concurrency and lifecycle review\n- `mobile-ci-cd` -- wiring dependency scanning, SBOM generation and signing into the pipeline\n- `kotlin-android`, `java-android`, `swift-ios`, `dart-flutter` -- platform idioms behind the fixes\n- `mobile-merge-request` -- describing security fixes and permission or entitlement changes in the MR\n",
    "references/android.md":
        "# Android security reference\n\nLoad this for Kotlin, Java, `AndroidManifest.xml`, `res/xml/*` and Gradle files. Always audit the merged\nrelease manifest (`app/build/intermediates/merged_manifests/release/...` or the APK Analyzer view), since\nlibraries add components and permissions.\n\n## Quick scan\n\n| Pattern | Why | Usual severity |\n|---|---|---|\n| `buildConfigField(` with key, secret, password, token | Secret in the APK | Critical if privileged |\n| `usesCleartextTraffic=\"true\"`, `cleartextTrafficPermitted=\"true\"` | Cleartext HTTP | High, Critical for credentials |\n| `<certificates src=\"user\"` outside `debug-overrides` | Trusts user-installed CAs in release | High |\n| `X509TrustManager` with empty `checkServerTrusted` | Trust-all | Critical |\n| `hostnameVerifier { _, _ -> true }`, `ALLOW_ALL_HOSTNAME_VERIFIER` | Hostname check disabled | Critical |\n| `tcp://` broker URI, port `1883` | Cleartext MQTT | Critical |\n| `EncryptedSharedPreferences`, `EncryptedFile`, `MasterKey` | Deprecated library | Low (migrate), not a vulnerability by itself |\n| `getSharedPreferences(` / DataStore keys named token, password, pin | Plaintext secrets | Medium |\n| `MODE_WORLD_READABLE`, `MODE_WORLD_WRITEABLE` | Other apps read the file | High (throws on modern targets) |\n| `getExternalStorageDirectory`, `getExternalFilesDir` with sensitive data | Shared or removable storage | Medium |\n| `android:exported=\"true\"` | Entry point for other apps | Review each |\n| `android:allowBackup=\"true\"` without `dataExtractionRules` | Tokens restorable to another device | Medium |\n| `android:debuggable=\"true\"` in a release manifest | Debugger and data access | High |\n| `addJavascriptInterface(` | JS bridge | High if untrusted content can load |\n| `setAllowFileAccess(true)`, `allowUniversalAccessFromFileURLs` | File theft via WebView | High |\n| `PendingIntent.FLAG_MUTABLE`, implicit base intent | Hijackable PendingIntent | High |\n| `getParcelableExtra<Intent>` then `startActivity` | Intent redirection | High |\n| `Log.` / `println(` / `Timber.` with token, password, email, mac, serial | Leak via logcat or crash reports | Medium to High |\n| `Cipher.getInstance(\"AES\")`, `\"AES/ECB/...\"`, `\"DES\"` | Weak mode or default ECB | High |\n| `MessageDigest.getInstance(\"MD5\")` / `\"SHA-1\"` for security | Broken hash | Medium |\n| `java.util.Random` for tokens, nonces, keys | Predictable | High |\n| `isMinifyEnabled = false` in release | No shrinking or obfuscation | Low |\n\n## Storage and keys\n\n`androidx.security:security-crypto` is deprecated. Existing users need a one-time migration: read with the\nold API, write with the new scheme, delete the old file, keep the old dependency only until migration ships.\n\nDirect Android Keystore key (no extra library):\n\n```kotlin\nfun getOrCreateKey(alias: String): SecretKey {\n    val ks = KeyStore.getInstance(\"AndroidKeyStore\").apply { load(null) }\n    (ks.getKey(alias, null) as? SecretKey)?.let { return it }\n    val spec = KeyGenParameterSpec.Builder(\n        alias, KeyProperties.PURPOSE_ENCRYPT or KeyProperties.PURPOSE_DECRYPT,\n    )\n        .setBlockModes(KeyProperties.BLOCK_MODE_GCM)\n        .setEncryptionPaddings(KeyProperties.ENCRYPTION_PADDING_NONE)\n        .setKeySize(256)\n        .build()\n    return KeyGenerator.getInstance(KeyProperties.KEY_ALGORITHM_AES, \"AndroidKeyStore\")\n        .apply { init(spec) }\n        .generateKey()\n}\n\n// Encrypt: let the Keystore generate the IV, store iv + ciphertext together\nval cipher = Cipher.getInstance(\"AES/GCM/NoPadding\").apply { init(Cipher.ENCRYPT_MODE, key) }\nval blob = cipher.iv + cipher.doFinal(plaintext)\n```\n\nTink AEAD (keyset stored in its own preferences file, encrypted by an Android Keystore master key):\n\n```kotlin\nAeadConfig.register()\nval aead: Aead = AndroidKeysetManager.Builder()\n    .withSharedPref(context, \"auth_keyset\", \"auth_keyset_prefs\")\n    .withKeyTemplate(KeyTemplates.get(\"AES256_GCM\"))\n    .withMasterKeyUri(\"android-keystore://auth_master_key\")\n    .build()\n    .keysetHandle\n    .getPrimitive(RegistryConfiguration.get(), Aead::class.java)\n\n// Associated data binds the ciphertext to its slot, so it cannot be swapped into another key\nval ciphertext = aead.encrypt(token.encodeToByteArray(), \"refresh_token\".encodeToByteArray())\n```\n\n- Older Tink versions use `keysetHandle.getPrimitive(Aead::class.java)`; match the pinned Tink version.\n- Build the `Aead` once (it is thread-safe) and off the main thread; keyset loading touches disk and Keystore.\n- `setIsStrongBoxBacked(true)` throws `StrongBoxUnavailableException` on devices without StrongBox; fall back.\n- Keystore operations can be slow and can fail after OS updates or lock-screen changes; handle\n  `KeyPermanentlyInvalidatedException` by re-authenticating the user, not by crashing.\n- Room / SQLite with sensitive data: encrypt the fields that matter or use an encrypted database library\n  with a Keystore-wrapped key.\n\n## Backup\n\n```xml\n<!-- AndroidManifest.xml -->\n<application\n    android:allowBackup=\"true\"\n    android:dataExtractionRules=\"@xml/data_extraction_rules\"\n    android:fullBackupContent=\"@xml/backup_rules\" ... >\n```\n\n```xml\n<!-- res/xml/data_extraction_rules.xml (API 31+) -->\n<data-extraction-rules>\n    <cloud-backup>\n        <exclude domain=\"sharedpref\" path=\"auth_keyset_prefs.xml\" />\n        <exclude domain=\"file\" path=\"datastore/session.preferences_pb\" />\n    </cloud-backup>\n    <device-transfer>\n        <exclude domain=\"sharedpref\" path=\"auth_keyset_prefs.xml\" />\n        <exclude domain=\"file\" path=\"datastore/session.preferences_pb\" />\n    </device-transfer>\n</data-extraction-rules>\n```\n\n`fullBackupContent` covers API 30 and below. Restored Tink keysets whose Keystore master key did not\ntravel cannot be decrypted; exclude them rather than crash on restore.\n\n## Network\n\n```xml\n<!-- res/xml/network_security_config.xml -->\n<network-security-config>\n    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted=\"false\">\n        <trust-anchors>\n            <certificates src=\"system\" />\n        </trust-anchors>\n    </base-config>\n\n    <!-- Optional pinning, only with a rotation plan -->\n    <domain-config>\n        <domain includeSubdomains=\"true\">api.example.com</domain>\n        <pin-set expiration=\"2027-06-30\">\n            <pin digest=\"SHA-256\">CURRENT_SPKI_BASE64=</pin>\n            <pin digest=\"SHA-256\">BACKUP_SPKI_BASE64=</pin>\n        </pin-set>\n    </domain-config>\n\n    <!-- Debug builds only: allow a proxy CA for testing -->\n    <debug-overrides>\n        <trust-anchors>\n            <certificates src=\"user\" />\n        </trust-anchors>\n    </debug-overrides>\n</network-security-config>\n```\n\n- After the `pin-set` expiration date, pins are no longer enforced; the app keeps working on normal validation.\n- OkHttp `CertificatePinner` pins apply in addition to platform validation; same backup-pin rules apply.\n- MQTT (Paho, HiveMQ client): `ssl://` / TLS on 8883, hostname verification on, credentials per device or\n  per user, never a shared broker password compiled into the app. Prefer client certificates or short-lived\n  tokens issued by the backend.\n\n## IPC\n\n- Since Android 12 every component with an intent filter must declare `android:exported`. Audit each `true`.\n- Exported components that need to be reachable only by your own apps use a `signature` protection level\n  permission.\n- Read extras defensively: `IntentCompat.getParcelableExtra(intent, key, Foo::class.java)`, validate every field.\n- Never forward an `Intent` received from another app to `startActivity` or `sendBroadcast`.\n- Runtime receivers: `ContextCompat.registerReceiver(..., ContextCompat.RECEIVER_NOT_EXPORTED)` unless other\n  apps must send to it.\n- App Links: `<intent-filter android:autoVerify=\"true\">` with `https` scheme and a matching\n  `/.well-known/assetlinks.json` listing the release signing certificate fingerprint.\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: mutable, implicit PendingIntent\nPendingIntent.getActivity(ctx, 0, Intent(\"com.example.OPEN\"), PendingIntent.FLAG_MUTABLE)\n\n// GOOD\nPendingIntent.getActivity(\n    ctx, 0, Intent(ctx, DetailActivity::class.java), PendingIntent.FLAG_IMMUTABLE,\n)\n```\n\n## WebView\n\n- `javaScriptEnabled` only when needed. No `addJavascriptInterface` on a WebView that can reach\n  third-party or user-controlled URLs; every `@JavascriptInterface` method is callable by any frame.\n- Prefer `WebViewCompat.addWebMessageListener` with an explicit allowed-origin set.\n- `shouldOverrideUrlLoading` allowlists hosts and sends everything else to the browser.\n- Local content through `WebViewAssetLoader` over `https://appassets.androidplatform.net`, with file access off.\n- No `onReceivedSslError { handler.proceed() }`.\n\n## Biometrics\n\n```kotlin\nval spec = KeyGenParameterSpec.Builder(\"vault\", KeyProperties.PURPOSE_ENCRYPT or KeyProperties.PURPOSE_DECRYPT)\n    .setBlockModes(KeyProperties.BLOCK_MODE_GCM)\n    .setEncryptionPaddings(KeyProperties.ENCRYPTION_PADDING_NONE)\n    .setUserAuthenticationRequired(true)\n    .setUserAuthenticationParameters(0, KeyProperties.AUTH_BIOMETRIC_STRONG) // API 30+\n    .setInvalidatedByBiometricEnrollment(true)\n    .build()\n\nval promptInfo = BiometricPrompt.PromptInfo.Builder()\n    .setTitle(getString(R.string.unlock_title))\n    .setAllowedAuthenticators(BiometricManager.Authenticators.BIOMETRIC_STRONG)\n    .setNegativeButtonText(getString(R.string.cancel))\n    .build()\n\nbiometricPrompt.authenticate(promptInfo, BiometricPrompt.CryptoObject(cipher))\n```\n\n- Timeout `0` means every use of the key needs a fresh authentication.\n- Decrypt or sign inside `onAuthenticationSucceeded` with `result.cryptoObject`; the GATT or network call\n  that follows must not run on the assumption that a flag was set.\n\n## UI and side channels\n\n- `FLAG_SECURE` on windows showing credentials, OTPs, payment or health data. In Compose apps set it on the\n  Activity window or the dialog window.\n- `Activity.setRecentsScreenshotEnabled(false)` (API 33+) keeps the recents thumbnail blank.\n- Clipboard: mark copied secrets sensitive with `ClipDescription.EXTRA_IS_SENSITIVE` (API 33+).\n- Notifications with sensitive content use `VISIBILITY_PRIVATE` or a public version.\n\n## Release build\n\n```kotlin\nandroid {\n    buildTypes {\n        release {\n            isMinifyEnabled = true\n            isShrinkResources = true\n            proguardFiles(getDefaultProguardFile(\"proguard-android-optimize.txt\"), \"proguard-rules.pro\")\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n```proguard\n# Strip debug and verbose logging from release\n-assumenosideeffects class android.util.Log {\n    public static int v(...);\n    public static int d(...);\n}\n```\n\n- Stripping log calls does not remove string building done before the call in all cases; do not log\n  secrets in the first place.\n- Play Integrity standard request: prepare a token provider once, request a token per sensitive action with\n  a `requestHash` of the action, send it to the backend, decrypt and evaluate the verdict there.\n- SBOM: the CycloneDX Gradle plugin (`org.cyclonedx.bom`) generates a CycloneDX SBOM from resolved\n  dependencies. Gradle dependency verification (`gradle/verification-metadata.xml`) and dependency locking\n  protect against tampered or drifting artifacts.\n",
    "references/eu-compliance.md":
        "# EU compliance notes for mobile and IoT companion apps\n\nLoad this when the app is placed on the EU market, processes personal data of people in the EU, or\ncontrols a connected (radio) device. These notes help an engineering audit produce useful evidence.\nThey are not legal advice: flag legal questions for the user's compliance or legal team, and check the\ncurrent Official Journal text and harmonised-standard citations before quoting dates or clauses.\n\n## GDPR (Regulation (EU) 2016/679)\n\n- Personal data includes online identifiers (Art. 4(1), Recital 30). Treat MAC addresses, device serials,\n  IMEI, advertising IDs, `identifierForVendor`, push tokens and IP addresses as personal data when they\n  can be linked to a person, which is the normal case for a device owned by one user.\n- Pseudonymised data is still personal data. Only anonymised data is out of scope, and hashing a MAC\n  address is not anonymisation.\n- Data minimisation (Art. 5(1)(c)) and data protection by design and by default (Art. 25) apply to code:\n  collect only the fields a feature needs, keep optional telemetry off until the user opts in where consent\n  is the legal basis, and set retention.\n- Security of processing (Art. 32): encryption in transit and at rest, access control, logging without\n  personal data.\n- User rights need a working path: data export and account and data deletion, including data held by\n  the device cloud.\n\nAudit checks:\n- [ ] Telemetry payloads list every field and its purpose; direct identifiers removed or justified\n- [ ] Third-party SDKs (analytics, crash reporting, ads) reviewed for what they collect and where they send it\n- [ ] Logs, crash reports and analytics events contain no identifiers or location unless justified\n- [ ] Deletion removes data in the app, the backend and the device pairing records\n\n## Cyber Resilience Act (Regulation (EU) 2024/2847)\n\nApplies to products with digital elements made available on the EU market, which includes software such\nas mobile apps supplied in the course of a commercial activity, and connected hardware. Obligations phase in:\n\n| Date | What applies |\n|---|---|\n| 10 December 2024 | Regulation entered into force |\n| 11 September 2026 | Reporting of actively exploited vulnerabilities and severe incidents (Art. 14) |\n| 11 December 2027 | All remaining obligations, including essential requirements and conformity assessment |\n\nReporting already applies to products on the market, including those placed before December 2027.\n\nWhat it means for a mobile or companion-app codebase:\n- Secure by default configuration, no known exploitable vulnerabilities at release, protection of\n  confidentiality and integrity of data, minimised attack surface, security logging where relevant.\n- Vulnerability handling: a coordinated vulnerability disclosure policy and contact, a process to triage\n  and fix reported issues, and security updates delivered for the declared support period.\n- SBOM: identify and document components, at least top-level dependencies, in a commonly used\n  machine-readable format (CycloneDX or SPDX). Generate it per release from lock files.\n- Reporting: actively exploited vulnerabilities and severe incidents are reported to the authorities\n  (CSIRT / ENISA single reporting platform) on short deadlines; the team needs a ready process.\n- Technical documentation and a risk assessment are part of conformity; audit findings and fixes feed it.\n\nAudit checks:\n- [ ] SBOM generated per release and archived with the build\n- [ ] Dependency scanning in CI with a policy for fixing known vulnerabilities\n- [ ] Forced-update or minimum-version mechanism exists (MASVS-CODE-2)\n- [ ] `SECURITY.md` or equivalent disclosure contact exists for the product\n- [ ] Support period and update channel documented\n\n## RED Delegated Act (Regulation (EU) 2022/30) and EN 18031\n\n- Applies from 1 August 2025 to radio equipment (Wi-Fi, BLE, cellular devices) placed on the EU market,\n  under the essential requirements of RED Article 3(3)(d) network protection, (e) personal data and\n  privacy protection and (f) protection from fraud.\n- EN 18031-1, -2 and -3 are the harmonised standards for these three articles. Their citation in the\n  Official Journal carries restrictions; where a restriction applies, presumption of conformity may not\n  hold and a notified body route may be needed.\n- The companion app is not itself radio equipment, but it often implements the device's security\n  mechanisms: authentication to the device, access control, secure update delivery, secure communication,\n  storage of credentials and user-initiated deletion. Those app features are assessed as part of the device.\n\nMechanism areas EN 18031 covers that usually touch the app:\n- Access control and authentication: no universal default passwords, per-device credentials, brute-force\n  protection on pairing or PIN entry.\n- Secure update: firmware images delivered over TLS and verified on the device (signature), not only by CRC.\n- Secure storage and communication: keys and credentials in Keystore / Keychain, TLS to cloud and brokers,\n  authenticated and encrypted BLE commands.\n- Logging, deletion and user notification: user can reset and delete data from the device and app.\n\n## ETSI EN 303 645 (consumer IoT baseline)\n\nThirteen provisions, several directly checkable in a companion app audit:\n- No universal default passwords.\n- Implement a means to manage reports of vulnerabilities.\n- Keep software updated.\n- Securely store sensitive security parameters.\n- Communicate securely.\n- Minimise exposed attack surfaces.\n- Ensure software integrity.\n- Ensure that personal data is secure.\n- Make systems resilient to outages.\n- Examine system telemetry data.\n- Make it easy for users to delete user data.\n- Make installation and maintenance of devices easy.\n- Validate input data.\n\n## Mapping to MASVS v2\n\n| Requirement area | MASVS controls |\n|---|---|\n| Secure storage of credentials and keys | MASVS-STORAGE-1, MASVS-CRYPTO-2 |\n| Secure communication (cloud, MQTT, BLE) | MASVS-NETWORK-1, MASVS-NETWORK-2 |\n| Authentication and access control | MASVS-AUTH-1, MASVS-AUTH-2, MASVS-AUTH-3 |\n| Input validation (deep links, device payloads) | MASVS-CODE-4, MASVS-PLATFORM-1 |\n| Known vulnerabilities, SBOM, updates | MASVS-CODE-3, MASVS-CODE-2 |\n| Data minimisation, transparency, deletion | MASVS-PRIVACY-1 to MASVS-PRIVACY-4 |\n\nIn the report, put compliance observations in the \"Compliance notes\" section, linked to the technical\nfinding that supports them. Do not state that a product \"is compliant\"; state what the audit found.\n",
    "references/flutter.md":
        "# Flutter security reference\n\nLoad this when the project has `pubspec.yaml`. A Flutter app is also an Android app and an iOS app: audit\n`android/` with [Android](android.md) and `ios/` with [iOS](ios.md) as well. Most platform findings\n(manifest, ATS, entitlements, backup, `FLAG_SECURE`) live there, not in Dart.\n\n## Quick scan\n\n| Pattern | Why | Usual severity |\n|---|---|---|\n| `--dart-define` / `String.fromEnvironment` holding secrets | Compiled into the binary | Critical if privileged |\n| `.env` files bundled as assets (`flutter_dotenv` and similar) | Plain text in the app bundle | Critical if privileged |\n| `SharedPreferences` / `Hive` box (unencrypted) with tokens | Plaintext storage | Medium |\n| `badCertificateCallback = (...) => true` | Trust-all | Critical |\n| `http://` base URLs, `Uri.http(` | Cleartext | High |\n| `MqttServerClient` with `secure` not set, port 1883 | Cleartext MQTT | Critical |\n| `print(` / `debugPrint(` / logger calls with token, email, mac, serial | Leak via device logs in release | Medium |\n| `JavaScriptMode.unrestricted` plus `addJavaScriptChannel` on untrusted URLs | Bridge exposed to any page | High |\n| `launchUrl(Uri.parse(param))` with deep link input | Open redirect, scheme abuse | Medium |\n| `local_auth` `authenticate()` result as the only gate | Hookable boolean | High for secrets |\n| Packages with `any` or wide version ranges, `pubspec.lock` not committed for the app | Unreproducible builds | Low to Medium |\n\n## Secrets and configuration\n\n```dart\n// BAD: anyone can extract this from libapp.so or App.framework\nconst brokerPassword = String.fromEnvironment('BROKER_PASSWORD');\n\n// GOOD: the backend issues a short-lived, per-user credential after login\nfinal creds = await authApi.mqttCredentials();\n```\n\n- `--dart-define` and `--dart-define-from-file` are build configuration, not secret storage.\n- `--obfuscate --split-debug-info=<dir>` renames Dart symbols in release builds. It does not hide string\n  literals and is not a security control. Keep the symbol files private for crash symbolication.\n\n## Secure storage\n\n```dart\nconst storage = FlutterSecureStorage(\n  iOptions: IOSOptions(accessibility: KeychainAccessibility.first_unlock_this_device),\n);\nawait storage.write(key: 'refresh_token', value: token);\n```\n\n- iOS backend is the Keychain; choose the accessibility class per [iOS](ios.md).\n- Android backend uses an Android Keystore-protected key. Check the pinned plugin version and its changelog:\n  older Android options were built on the deprecated `androidx.security:security-crypto` library, and\n  major versions have changed the Android storage format, which needs a tested migration path.\n- Exclude the plugin's Android preferences file from backup, or restored data will fail to decrypt.\n- Large or structured sensitive data: encrypt with a key kept in secure storage, not in Dart constants.\n\n## Network\n\n```dart\n// BAD\nfinal client = HttpClient()..badCertificateCallback = (cert, host, port) => true;\n\n// GOOD: default validation\nfinal client = HttpClient();\n```\n\nPinning with `dart:io` (only with a rotation plan):\n\n```dart\nSecurityContext pinnedContext(List<int> caPem) {\n  final context = SecurityContext(withTrustedRoots: false);\n  context.setTrustedCertificatesBytes(caPem); // pinned CA, ship current and backup\n  return context;\n}\n\nfinal client = HttpClient(context: pinnedContext(caBytes));\n```\n\n- Dio on mobile uses `IOHttpClientAdapter`; apply the same `HttpClient` configuration through its\n  `createHttpClient` callback rather than disabling checks.\n- `network_security_config.xml` and ATS govern platform networking (WebViews, native plugins). `dart:io`\n  `HttpClient` uses its own TLS stack and does not consult them, so Dart code must enforce `https` itself.\n- MQTT (`mqtt_client`): set `secure = true`, port 8883, and keep certificate validation on.\n\n```dart\n// BAD\nfinal client = MqttServerClient.withPort('broker.example.com', clientId, 1883);\n\n// GOOD\nfinal client = MqttServerClient.withPort('broker.example.com', clientId, 8883)\n  ..secure = true;\n```\n\n## Local authentication\n\n- `local_auth` returns a boolean. For secrets, store them behind platform key protection instead: Keychain\n  access control on iOS and a `CryptoObject`-bound Keystore key on Android, exposed through a platform\n  channel or a plugin that implements the crypto binding.\n- Do not treat `canCheckBiometrics` or `isDeviceSupported` as proof that the user authenticated.\n\n## WebView (`webview_flutter`)\n\n```dart\ncontroller\n  ..setJavaScriptMode(JavaScriptMode.unrestricted)\n  ..setNavigationDelegate(NavigationDelegate(\n    onNavigationRequest: (request) {\n      final uri = Uri.parse(request.url);\n      return uri.scheme == 'https' && trustedHosts.contains(uri.host)\n          ? NavigationDecision.navigate\n          : NavigationDecision.prevent;\n    },\n  ))\n  ..addJavaScriptChannel('Bridge', onMessageReceived: _onBridgeMessage)\n  ..loadRequest(Uri.parse('https://app.example.com/help'));\n```\n\n- JavaScript channels are reachable by every page the controller loads; the navigation allowlist is the\n  control that makes the bridge safe.\n- Validate every message received on a channel as untrusted input.\n\n## Deep links\n\n- Routes from `go_router` or another router receive path and query parameters from other apps. Validate\n  them before triggering actions (pairing a device, opening a URL, changing settings).\n- Use verified App Links and Universal Links for anything carrying codes or tokens; configure them in the\n  native projects.\n\n## Logging\n\n- `print` and `debugPrint` reach logcat and the device console in release builds.\n- Route logs through one logger that drops debug output and redacts identifiers when `kReleaseMode` is true.\n\n```dart\n// BAD\ndebugPrint('connected to \${device.mac} with token \$token');\n\n// GOOD\nappLog.info('connected to device ref=\${device.pseudonymousId}'); // project logger, redacts in release\n```\n\n## Supply chain\n\n- Commit `pubspec.lock` for apps. Review new packages for maintenance status, publisher and native code.\n- Scan `pubspec.lock` together with the Android and iOS lock files; plugins pull native dependencies\n  (Gradle artifacts, CocoaPods or SwiftPM packages) that also belong in the SBOM.\n",
    "references/ios.md":
        "# iOS security reference\n\nLoad this for Swift, Objective-C, `Info.plist`, `.entitlements`, `.xcconfig` and `PrivacyInfo.xcprivacy`.\nCall out every `Info.plist` and entitlement change explicitly in the report; they affect App Review,\nsigning and provisioning.\n\n## Quick scan\n\n| Pattern | Why | Usual severity |\n|---|---|---|\n| Keys like `API_SECRET`, `PASSWORD`, `TOKEN` in `Info.plist` or `.xcconfig` | Secret in the IPA | Critical if privileged |\n| `NSAllowsArbitraryLoads` = YES | ATS disabled globally | High, needs App Review justification |\n| `NSExceptionAllowsInsecureHTTPLoads`, `NSExceptionMinimumTLSVersion` lowered | Cleartext or weak TLS for a domain | High |\n| `.useCredential` with `serverTrust` and no `SecTrustEvaluateWithError` | Trust-all | Critical |\n| `UserDefaults` / `@AppStorage` with token, password, PIN | Plaintext plist | Medium |\n| `kSecAttrAccessibleAlways` / `kSecAttrAccessibleAlwaysThisDeviceOnly` | Deprecated, readable while locked | Medium |\n| `evaluatePolicy(` result used as the only gate | Hookable boolean | High for secrets or payments |\n| `print(`, `NSLog(`, `privacy: .public` with personal data | Leak via device logs | Medium |\n| `WKScriptMessageHandler` without origin check | Any frame calls native code | High |\n| `loadFileURL(_:allowingReadAccessTo:)` with a broad directory | Web content reads app files | Medium |\n| `open(url)` or routing from `onOpenURL` without validation | Deep link abuse | Medium to High |\n| `CC_MD5`, `Insecure.MD5`, `Insecure.SHA1` for security | Broken hash | Medium |\n| `get-task-allow` = true in a distribution entitlement | Debuggable release | High |\n| CocoaMQTT / other MQTT clients with SSL disabled or port 1883 | Cleartext MQTT | Critical |\n\n## Keychain accessibility classes\n\n| Class | Readable when | Migrates to new device | Use for |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `kSecAttrAccessibleWhenPasscodeSetThisDeviceOnly` | Unlocked, only while a passcode is set | No | Highest-value secrets, biometric-bound items |\n| `kSecAttrAccessibleWhenUnlockedThisDeviceOnly` | Unlocked | No | Secrets used only in the foreground |\n| `kSecAttrAccessibleWhenUnlocked` | Unlocked | Yes | Default; foreground secrets that may migrate |\n| `kSecAttrAccessibleAfterFirstUnlockThisDeviceOnly` | After first unlock until reboot | No | Tokens needed by background refresh, BLE restoration, push handling |\n| `kSecAttrAccessibleAfterFirstUnlock` | After first unlock until reboot | Yes | Background secrets that may migrate |\n\n- `ThisDeviceOnly` items are not restored onto a different device from a backup.\n- Keychain items can survive app deletion. Clear stale credentials on first launch after install\n  (detect with a flag in `UserDefaults`, which is removed on uninstall).\n- Shared access across your apps uses a Keychain access group entitlement; keep it as narrow as possible.\n\n## Biometric-bound Keychain item\n\n```swift\nfunc storeVaultKey(_ secret: Data) throws {\n    var error: Unmanaged<CFError>?\n    guard let access = SecAccessControlCreateWithFlags(\n        nil, kSecAttrAccessibleWhenPasscodeSetThisDeviceOnly, .biometryCurrentSet, &error\n    ) else { throw error!.takeRetainedValue() as Error }\n\n    let query: [String: Any] = [\n        kSecClass as String: kSecClassGenericPassword,\n        kSecAttrService as String: \"com.example.vault\",\n        kSecAttrAccount as String: \"vaultKey\",\n        kSecAttrAccessControl as String: access,\n        kSecValueData as String: secret,\n    ]\n    let status = SecItemAdd(query as CFDictionary, nil)\n    guard status == errSecSuccess else { throw KeychainError(status: status) }\n}\n\n// Blocks while the system prompt is shown: never call on the main thread.\nfunc readVaultKey() throws -> Data {\n    let context = LAContext()\n    context.localizedReason = String(localized: \"Unlock your vault\")\n    let query: [String: Any] = [\n        kSecClass as String: kSecClassGenericPassword,\n        kSecAttrService as String: \"com.example.vault\",\n        kSecAttrAccount as String: \"vaultKey\",\n        kSecReturnData as String: true,\n        kSecUseAuthenticationContext as String: context,\n    ]\n    var result: CFTypeRef?\n    let status = SecItemCopyMatching(query as CFDictionary, &result)\n    guard status == errSecSuccess, let data = result as? Data else { throw KeychainError(status: status) }\n    return data\n}\n```\n\n- `.biometryCurrentSet` invalidates the item when enrolled biometrics change; `.biometryAny` does not.\n- `.userPresence` allows passcode fallback; choose deliberately and document it.\n- For signing use cases, create a Secure Enclave key (`kSecAttrTokenIDSecureEnclave`) with the same access\n  control and have the server verify signatures.\n- `NSFaceIDUsageDescription` is required in `Info.plist` when Face ID is used.\n\n## Files and data protection\n\n```swift\ntry data.write(to: url, options: [.atomic, .completeFileProtection])\n```\n\n- Default file protection is `completeUntilFirstUserAuthentication`. Use `.complete` for sensitive files\n  that are not needed in the background.\n- Exclude caches and regenerable data from backup with `isExcludedFromBackup` on `URLResourceValues`.\n- Core Data / SwiftData stores inherit file protection; set it on the store file when it holds sensitive data.\n\n## Network\n\n```xml\n<!-- Info.plist: ATS on by default; pin only domains you control -->\n<key>NSAppTransportSecurity</key>\n<dict>\n    <key>NSPinnedDomains</key>\n    <dict>\n        <key>api.example.com</key>\n        <dict>\n            <key>NSIncludesSubdomains</key>\n            <true/>\n            <key>NSPinnedCAIdentities</key>\n            <array>\n                <dict>\n                    <key>SPKI-SHA256-BASE64</key>\n                    <string>CURRENT_CA_SPKI_BASE64=</string>\n                </dict>\n                <dict>\n                    <key>SPKI-SHA256-BASE64</key>\n                    <string>BACKUP_CA_SPKI_BASE64=</string>\n                </dict>\n            </array>\n        </dict>\n    </dict>\n</dict>\n```\n\n- Custom trust evaluation must call `SecTrustEvaluateWithError` and only then compare pins.\n- Apple platforms require Certificate Transparency for publicly trusted TLS certificates, so standard\n  validation already rejects many mis-issued certificates.\n- ATS exceptions in `Info.plist` need a justification at App Review; local-network IoT devices are a valid\n  case only for the specific local domain or `NSAllowsLocalNetworking`, never a global exception.\n- Local network access to IoT devices needs `NSLocalNetworkUsageDescription`.\n\n## WebView\n\n```swift\nlet config = WKWebViewConfiguration()\nconfig.defaultWebpagePreferences.allowsContentJavaScript = needsJavaScript\nconfig.userContentController.add(handler, name: \"bridge\")\n\nfunc webView(_ webView: WKWebView, decidePolicyFor action: WKNavigationAction) async -> WKNavigationActionPolicy {\n    guard let host = action.request.url?.host, trustedHosts.contains(host) else {\n        if let url = action.request.url { _ = await UIApplication.shared.open(url) }\n        return .cancel\n    }\n    return .allow\n}\n```\n\n- Every `WKScriptMessage` handler checks `frameInfo.isMainFrame` and `frameInfo.securityOrigin` before acting.\n- OAuth and SSO use `ASWebAuthenticationSession`, not a `WKWebView` the app can read.\n\n## Logging\n\n- `os.Logger` redacts dynamic interpolations by default in release logs. Never mark tokens, emails, device\n  identifiers or locations `privacy: .public`.\n- `print` and `NSLog` output reaches the device console; remove or guard them.\n- Crash reporters and analytics SDKs: check breadcrumbs and custom keys for personal data.\n\n## UI\n\n- Hide sensitive content when the scene is inactive, so the app switcher snapshot is clean:\n\n```swift\n.overlay { if scenePhase != .active { PrivacyCover() } }\n```\n\n- Secure text fields (`SecureField`) for passwords and PINs; set `textContentType` so autofill works and\n  the keyboard does not learn secrets.\n- `UIPasteboard`: use `setItems(_:options:)` with `.localOnly` and `.expirationDate` for copied secrets.\n\n## Integrity and attestation\n\n- App Attest: `DCAppAttestService.shared.isSupported`, `generateKey()`, `attestKey(_:clientDataHash:)` once,\n  then `generateAssertion(_:clientDataHash:)` per sensitive request. The server verifies the attestation,\n  stores the public key and checks assertion counters.\n- Requires the App Attest entitlement (`com.apple.developer.devicecheck.appattest-environment`); flag it as\n  an entitlement and provisioning change.\n- Jailbreak detection is advisory only; never rely on it as the sole control.\n\n## Privacy\n\n- `PrivacyInfo.xcprivacy` lists collected data types and required-reason API usage, and third-party SDKs ship\n  their own manifests. Check consistency with the App Store privacy label.\n- Bluetooth needs `NSBluetoothAlwaysUsageDescription`; ask only when the user starts a BLE feature.\n- `identifierForVendor` is still an identifier; treat it as personal data in telemetry.\n",
    "references/masvs-map.md":
        "# MASVS v2 controls and Mobile Top 10 map\n\nLoad this before assigning control IDs. Use only the IDs in this table. MASVS v2 removed the old v1\nlevels (L1, L2, R); use MAS testing profiles from the MASTG if the user asks for levels.\n\n## MASVS v2 controls\n\n| Control | Statement (short) | Typical findings |\n|---|---|---|\n| MASVS-STORAGE-1 | The app securely stores sensitive data | Tokens in SharedPreferences, UserDefaults, plain files, SQLite without protection |\n| MASVS-STORAGE-2 | The app prevents leakage of sensitive data | Secrets in logs, backups, screenshots, app switcher snapshot, clipboard, keyboard cache, crash reports |\n| MASVS-CRYPTO-1 | The app employs current strong cryptography and uses it according to industry best practices | ECB, static IV, MD5 or SHA-1 for security, `java.util.Random` for secrets, custom crypto |\n| MASVS-CRYPTO-2 | The app performs key management according to industry best practices | Hardcoded keys, keys derived from device IDs, keys outside Keystore / Keychain |\n| MASVS-AUTH-1 | The app uses secure authentication and authorization protocols and follows the relevant best practices | Client-side role checks, long-lived tokens, OAuth in embedded WebView, no PKCE, shared credentials in app |\n| MASVS-AUTH-2 | The app performs local authentication securely according to the platform best practices | Boolean-only biometric gate, weak fallback, key not invalidated on enrollment change |\n| MASVS-AUTH-3 | The app secures sensitive operations with additional authentication | Payments, device unlock commands or credential changes without step-up auth |\n| MASVS-NETWORK-1 | The app secures all network traffic according to the current best practices | Cleartext, trust-all managers, disabled hostname checks, ATS exceptions, cleartext MQTT |\n| MASVS-NETWORK-2 | The app performs identity pinning for all remote endpoints under the developer's control | Pinning missing where the threat model needs it, or pinning without backup pins |\n| MASVS-PLATFORM-1 | The app uses IPC mechanisms securely | Exported components, intent redirection, mutable PendingIntents, unvalidated deep links, providers |\n| MASVS-PLATFORM-2 | The app uses WebViews securely | JS bridges on untrusted content, file access enabled, loading arbitrary URLs |\n| MASVS-PLATFORM-3 | The app uses the user interface securely | No screenshot protection on sensitive screens, overlay or tapjacking, sensitive data in notifications |\n| MASVS-CODE-1 | The app requires an up-to-date platform version | Very old `minSdk` or deployment target without justification |\n| MASVS-CODE-2 | The app has a mechanism for enforcing app updates | No way to block a known-vulnerable version |\n| MASVS-CODE-3 | The app only uses software components without known vulnerabilities | Vulnerable or unmaintained dependencies, no scanning, dynamic versions |\n| MASVS-CODE-4 | The app validates and sanitizes all untrusted inputs | Deep link params, IPC extras, server or BLE payloads used without validation, SQL or path injection |\n| MASVS-RESILIENCE-1 | The app validates the integrity of the platform | No attestation where the threat model needs it |\n| MASVS-RESILIENCE-2 | The app implements anti-tampering mechanisms | No signature or integrity verification for high-value apps |\n| MASVS-RESILIENCE-3 | The app implements anti-static analysis mechanisms | No obfuscation in release, debug symbols shipped |\n| MASVS-RESILIENCE-4 | The app implements anti-dynamic analysis techniques | Debuggable release build, no debugger or instrumentation awareness where required |\n| MASVS-PRIVACY-1 | The app minimizes access to sensitive data and resources | Over-broad permissions, collecting identifiers the feature does not need |\n| MASVS-PRIVACY-2 | The app prevents identification of the user | Stable cross-app identifiers, linkable telemetry, fingerprinting |\n| MASVS-PRIVACY-3 | The app is transparent about data collection and usage | Privacy manifest or Data safety form inconsistent with code, undisclosed SDK collection |\n| MASVS-PRIVACY-4 | The app offers user control over their data | No account or data deletion path, no opt-out for optional telemetry |\n\nRESILIENCE controls apply only when the threat model includes the device owner or reverse engineers\n(banking, DRM, anti-cheat, high-value IoT control). Do not report them as gaps for every app.\n\n## OWASP Mobile Top 10 (2024)\n\n| ID | Risk | Main MASVS controls |\n|---|---|---|\n| M1 | Improper Credential Usage | MASVS-AUTH-1, MASVS-CRYPTO-2, MASVS-STORAGE-1 |\n| M2 | Inadequate Supply Chain Security | MASVS-CODE-3 |\n| M3 | Insecure Authentication/Authorization | MASVS-AUTH-1, MASVS-AUTH-2, MASVS-AUTH-3 |\n| M4 | Insufficient Input/Output Validation | MASVS-CODE-4, MASVS-PLATFORM-1, MASVS-PLATFORM-2 |\n| M5 | Insecure Communication | MASVS-NETWORK-1, MASVS-NETWORK-2 |\n| M6 | Inadequate Privacy Controls | MASVS-PRIVACY-1 to MASVS-PRIVACY-4, MASVS-STORAGE-2 |\n| M7 | Insufficient Binary Protections | MASVS-RESILIENCE-1 to MASVS-RESILIENCE-4 |\n| M8 | Security Misconfiguration | MASVS-PLATFORM-1, MASVS-NETWORK-1, MASVS-CODE-1 |\n| M9 | Insecure Data Storage | MASVS-STORAGE-1, MASVS-STORAGE-2 |\n| M10 | Insufficient Cryptography | MASVS-CRYPTO-1, MASVS-CRYPTO-2 |\n\nThe mapping is a guide for the report, not an official one-to-one table. Pick the single control that\nbest describes the root cause.\n",
  },
  "swift-ios": {
    "SKILL.md":
        "---\nname: swift-ios\ndescription: \"Writes and refactors idiomatic modern Swift for iOS in Swift 6 language mode with strict concurrency. Covers async/await, async let, task groups, actors and reentrancy, @MainActor, Sendable, cancellation and AsyncStream; structs vs final classes; some vs any; optionals without force unwraps; typed throws; SwiftUI with @Observable, @State, @Binding, @Bindable, @Environment, .task(id:), NavigationStack and stable ForEach identity; capture lists and retain cycles; SwiftData (@Model, @Query, ModelContext, @ModelActor, migrations); SPM modularisation and the package access modifier; Swift Testing (@Test, #expect, #require, parameterized tests) and XCTest for UI and performance tests; SwiftLint, SwiftFormat; Info.plist, entitlements and PrivacyInfo.xcprivacy. Use when writing, refactoring or migrating Swift, SwiftUI or SwiftData code, fixing Swift 6 data-race or Sendable errors, or adding tests and local packages to an Xcode project.\"\nlicense: MIT\nmetadata:\n  author: Ahmad Hussain\n  version: \"1.0.0\"\n  pack: mobile-dev-kit\n---\n\n# Swift for iOS\n\nWrite Swift the way a senior iOS engineer would in Swift 6: data-race safe by construction, value types\nfirst, no crashes on unexpected input, SwiftUI views that stay small and cheap, and code split into\npackages with tests next to it.\n\n## When to use\n\n- Writing new Swift, SwiftUI or SwiftData code, or generating a feature from a ticket.\n- Refactoring or migrating older code: completion handlers, `ObservableObject`, `NavigationView`,\n  Core Data, XCTest unit tests, Swift 5 language mode.\n- Fixing compiler errors such as \"sending risks causing data races\", \"non-Sendable type\",\n  \"main actor-isolated property can not be referenced\".\n- Adding a local Swift package, a test target, a lint config, or a privacy manifest.\n\nNot for: reviewing someone else's diff (use `mobile-code-review`), a security audit (use\n`mobile-security`), or choosing an app architecture (use `mobile-mvvm`, `mobile-mvi`).\n\n## Workflow\n\n1. Read the project settings before writing code.\n   - Swift language mode, strict concurrency level, default actor isolation, minimum iOS version.\n   - `Package.swift` files, existing lint and format configs, test framework in use.\n2. Match the deployment target. `@Observable` and SwiftData need iOS 17, `Mutex` needs iOS 18.\n   Gate newer APIs with `if #available` or ask before raising the target.\n3. Load the reference for the area being touched:\n   [Concurrency](references/concurrency.md), [SwiftUI](references/swiftui.md),\n   [SwiftData](references/swiftdata.md), [Testing](references/testing.md),\n   [Packages and tooling](references/packages.md).\n4. Model data first: value types, explicit `Sendable` where it crosses isolation, typed errors where the\n   set is closed.\n5. Decide isolation per type: `@MainActor` for UI-facing state, an `actor` for shared mutable state,\n   `nonisolated` value types for everything else.\n6. Write the code, then the tests in the same change. Swift Testing for unit tests, XCTest only for UI\n   and performance tests.\n7. Build with zero concurrency warnings, run the tests, run SwiftLint and the formatter.\n8. List every `Info.plist` key, entitlement, background mode and privacy manifest entry the change adds.\n   These have signing and App Review consequences and must be called out explicitly.\n\n## Rules\n\n### 1. Structs by default, `final class` only when identity matters\n\n```swift\n// BAD: reference semantics for plain data, shared mutation, not Sendable\nclass Reading { var value: Double; var date: Date }\n\n// GOOD\nstruct Reading: Sendable, Equatable { let value: Double; let date: Date }\n\n// GOOD: identity and lifetime matter (connection, cache, model observed by UI)\n@MainActor @Observable\nfinal class DeviceSession { var state: ConnectionState = .idle }\n```\n\n- Classes are `final` unless subclassing is designed for.\n- `let` over `var`. Mutation happens through methods with clear names, not public setters.\n\n### 2. No force unwraps, force casts or `try!` in app code\n\n```swift\n// BAD\nlet url = URL(string: base + path)!\nlet cell = tableView.dequeueReusableCell(withIdentifier: \"c\") as! DeviceCell\n\n// GOOD\nguard let url = URL(string: base + path) else { throw APIError.invalidURL(path) }\nlet name = device.name ?? String(localized: \"Unknown device\")\n```\n\n- In tests use `try #require(value)` instead of `!`, so a nil fails the test instead of crashing the run.\n- A static literal URL may use a `guard` with `preconditionFailure(\"...\")` and a message, never a bare `!`.\n\n### 3. `some` when one concrete type is fixed, `any` only when it must vary at runtime\n\n```swift\n// BAD: existential box and dynamic dispatch for no reason\nfunc render(_ items: any Collection<Reading>) -> any View\n\n// GOOD: generic, specialised by the compiler\nfunc render(_ items: some Collection<Reading>) -> some View\n\n// GOOD: heterogeneous storage really needs an existential\nvar sinks: [any TelemetrySink]\n```\n\n- Write `any` explicitly for every existential even where the compiler does not yet require it.\n- Primary associated types (`some Collection<Reading>`, `any AsyncSequence<Int, Never>`) instead of\n  type-erasing wrappers.\n\n### 4. Structured concurrency before unstructured `Task {}`\n\n```swift\n// BAD: unstructured tasks, no cancellation propagation, errors lost\nTask { profile = try await api.profile() }\nTask { devices = try await api.devices() }\n\n// GOOD: both cancelled with the parent, errors propagate\nasync let profile = api.profile()\nasync let devices = api.devices()\nstate = .loaded(try await profile, try await devices)\n```\n\n- Dynamic fan-out uses `withThrowingTaskGroup`; bound the width for large inputs.\n- `Task {}` only at an entry point from synchronous code (button action, delegate callback), and its\n  handle is stored and cancelled when the owner goes away.\n- `Task.detached` needs a written reason.\n\n### 5. UI-facing state is `@MainActor`, data crossing isolation is `Sendable`\n\n```swift\n// BAD: silences the checker, data race still possible\nfinal class TokenStore: @unchecked Sendable { var token: String? }\n\n// GOOD: an actor owns the mutable state\nactor TokenStore {\n    private var token: String?\n    func update(_ new: String?) { token = new }\n}\n```\n\n- View models: `@MainActor @Observable final class`.\n- `@unchecked Sendable` and `nonisolated(unsafe)` only with a lock guarding every field and a comment\n  explaining why an actor does not fit.\n- No `DispatchQueue.main.async` in `async` code; isolate the function or type to `@MainActor` instead.\n\n### 6. Actor state is re-checked after every `await`\n\n```swift\n// Inside an actor or a @MainActor class.\n// BAD: reentrancy, two callers both pass the check and both connect\nfunc connect() async throws {\n    guard state == .idle else { return }\n    try await transport.open()\n    state = .connected\n}\n\n// GOOD: claim the state before suspending\nfunc connect() async throws {\n    guard state == .idle else { return }\n    state = .connecting\n    do { try await transport.open(); state = .connected }\n    catch { state = .idle; throw error }\n}\n```\n\n### 7. Cancellation is checked in loops and forwarded to callback APIs\n\n```swift\n// BAD: keeps uploading after the user left the screen\nfor chunk in chunks { try await upload(chunk) }\n\n// GOOD\nfor chunk in chunks {\n    try Task.checkCancellation()\n    try await upload(chunk)\n}\n```\n\n- Wrap callback APIs that support cancelling in `withTaskCancellationHandler`. Details in\n  [Concurrency](references/concurrency.md).\n- Do not catch `CancellationError` and turn it into a user-facing error state.\n\n### 8. Callbacks are bridged once, at the edge\n\n```swift\n// BAD: continuation resumed for every discovery, traps on the second one\nfunc scan() async -> Peripheral {\n    await withCheckedContinuation { c in\n        scanner.onDiscover = { c.resume(returning: \$0) }\n    }\n}\n\n// GOOD: repeated events are a stream, cleaned up when the consumer stops\n// scanner: a Sendable wrapper around the platform delegate API\nfunc discoveries() -> AsyncStream<Peripheral> {\n    let (stream, continuation) = AsyncStream.makeStream(of: Peripheral.self)\n    scanner.onDiscover = { continuation.yield(\$0) }\n    continuation.onTermination = { [scanner] _ in scanner.stop() }\n    scanner.start()\n    return stream\n}\n```\n\n- One-shot callback: `withCheckedThrowingContinuation`, resumed exactly once on every path.\n- Repeated events: `AsyncStream` / `AsyncThrowingStream` with `onTermination`.\n\n### 9. Typed throws only for closed, local error domains\n\n```swift\n// BAD: public API promises a single error type forever, callers gain nothing\npublic func sync() async throws(SyncError)\n\n// GOOD: closed set, callers switch exhaustively\nfunc decode(_ frame: Data) throws(FrameError) -> Packet\nenum FrameError: Error { case tooShort(Int), badChecksum, unknownOpcode(UInt8) }\n```\n\n- Use `throws(E)` for parsers, validation and module-internal code. Use untyped `throws` at module and\n  network boundaries where new failure kinds will appear.\n- Typed throws does not replace `Result` in stored properties.\n\n### 10. SwiftUI: the view that creates a model owns it in `@State`\n\n```swift\n// BAD: legacy pattern, model recreated on every parent redraw\n@ObservedObject var model = DeviceListModel()\n\n// GOOD\nstruct DeviceListScreen: View {\n    @State private var model = DeviceListModel()\n    var body: some View {\n        DeviceList(model: model)\n            .task { await model.load() }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- Passed-in `@Observable` models are plain `let`/`var` properties; use `@Bindable` only to get bindings.\n- `@Binding` for a value the parent owns. `@Environment` for app-wide dependencies.\n- `.task(id: query)` for work that restarts when an input changes; it cancels the previous run.\n\n### 11. SwiftUI identity is stable and views stay small\n\n```swift\n// BAD: index identity, AnyView erasure, heavy body\nForEach(items.indices, id: \\.self) { i in AnyView(row(items[i])) }\n\n// GOOD\nForEach(items) { item in DeviceRow(device: item) }\n```\n\n- Items are `Identifiable` with an id from the data, never `UUID()` created in `body`.\n- Conditional content uses `@ViewBuilder` or `Group`, not `AnyView`.\n- Extract subviews when `body` stops fitting on a screen; no formatters, sorting or I/O inside `body`.\n\n### 12. Capture lists break cycles, and only where a cycle exists\n\n```swift\n// BAD: self -> task -> self, the loop keeps the object alive forever\nmonitor = Task { for await r in readings { self.append(r) } }\n\n// GOOD\nmonitor = Task { [weak self] in\n    for await r in readings {\n        guard let self else { return }\n        append(r)\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- Escaping closures stored by `self` (timers, observers, stored tasks) capture `[weak self]`.\n- `unowned` only when the referenced object provably outlives the closure.\n- SwiftUI views are structs: no `[weak self]` in `.task` or button actions.\n- Delegates are `weak var delegate: (any SomeDelegate)?` with an `AnyObject`-constrained protocol.\n\n### 13. SwiftData contexts stay on their actor\n\n```swift\n// BAD: ModelContext and model objects are not Sendable\nTask.detached { context.insert(entity) }\n\n// GOOD: background work in a model actor, identifiers cross the boundary\n@ModelActor\nactor ReadingImporter {\n    func importAll(_ rows: [ReadingDTO]) throws {\n        for row in rows { modelContext.insert(ReadingEntity(row)) }\n        try modelContext.save()\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- UI reads with `@Query`; writes go through `@Environment(\\.modelContext)` or a model actor.\n- Pass `PersistentIdentifier` between actors, not model instances. See [SwiftData](references/swiftdata.md).\n\n### 14. Features are local packages with explicit, minimal APIs\n\n```swift\n// BAD: public only so a sibling target can call it, now visible to the whole app\npublic func makeDecoder() -> JSONDecoder\n\n// GOOD: visible to other targets of the same Swift package, hidden from everyone else\npackage func makeDecoder() -> JSONDecoder\n```\n\n- One local package (or one target) per feature plus shared core modules; features depend on core,\n  never on each other.\n- Default to `internal`. `package` spans targets of one Swift package only; `public` is for what\n  other packages and the app target import.\n- Every dependency is declared in `Package.swift`; nothing relies on a transitive import.\n\n### 15. Swift Testing for unit tests, XCTest where it is still required\n\n```swift\n// BAD: a nil or a thrown error crashes the whole test run\nfunc testParse() { XCTAssertEqual(try! parse(frame).battery!, 87) }\n\n// GOOD: failures are reported, the run continues\n@Test func parsesBatteryLevel() throws {\n    let packet = try parse(frame)\n    let battery = try #require(packet.battery)\n    #expect(battery == 87)\n}\n```\n\n- XCTest stays for UI tests (`XCUIApplication`) and performance tests (`measure`).\n- No `sleep` in tests; inject a clock. Details in [Testing](references/testing.md).\n\n## Platform and project changes\n\nCall these out in the change summary every time, with the exact key or entitlement name.\n\n- `Info.plist` usage strings: `NSBluetoothAlwaysUsageDescription`, `NSCameraUsageDescription`,\n  `NSLocationWhenInUseUsageDescription` and others. For Bluetooth, camera and microphone a missing\n  string terminates the app on first access.\n- `UIBackgroundModes` entries such as `bluetooth-central` or `location`. App Review asks for justification.\n- Entitlements (push, App Groups, Keychain sharing, associated domains) change the provisioning profile.\n- `PrivacyInfo.xcprivacy`: required-reason APIs (for example `UserDefaults`, file timestamps, system boot\n  time, disk space) need a declared reason; collected data types and tracking domains must match reality.\n  See [Packages and tooling](references/packages.md).\n\n## Anti-patterns\n\n- Sprinkling `@MainActor`, `@unchecked Sendable` or `nonisolated(unsafe)` until the compiler is quiet.\n- `Task { }` inside `onAppear` instead of `.task`.\n- `DispatchSemaphore` or `DispatchGroup.wait()` to make async code synchronous.\n- `ObservableObject` and `@Published` in new code on iOS 17+.\n- Giant views with `AnyView` and computed `some View` properties instead of real subviews.\n- `UUID()` as the `id` of items created during rendering.\n- Singletons (`static let shared`) reached from deep inside views and models instead of injection.\n- `try?` that silently drops an error the user should see.\n- One giant app target with every file `public` \"just in case\".\n- Adding a third-party SDK without checking it ships its own privacy manifest and signature.\n\n## Checklist\n\n- [ ] Language mode, strict concurrency and deployment target checked before writing\n- [ ] Value types by default; classes are `final`\n- [ ] No `!`, `as!`, `try!` in app code; tests use `#require`\n- [ ] UI-facing types `@MainActor`; shared mutable state in actors; no unjustified `@unchecked Sendable`\n- [ ] Actor state re-validated after each `await`\n- [ ] Long loops check cancellation; continuations resume exactly once; streams have `onTermination`\n- [ ] Escaping closures stored by `self` use `[weak self]`; delegates are `weak`\n- [ ] SwiftUI models owned with `@State`; async work in `.task` / `.task(id:)`; stable `ForEach` ids\n- [ ] No `AnyView` or heavy work in `body`\n- [ ] SwiftData contexts not shared across actors; identifiers passed instead of models\n- [ ] New code in the right package with the narrowest access level\n- [ ] Swift Testing unit tests added; XCTest only for UI and performance\n- [ ] Zero warnings, tests pass, SwiftLint and formatter clean\n- [ ] `Info.plist`, entitlements, background modes and privacy manifest changes listed explicitly\n\n## Related skills\n\n- `mobile-code-review` -- review checklist that applies these rules to a diff\n- `mobile-security` -- Keychain, ATS, data protection and MASVS checks for the code written here\n- `mobile-mvvm`, `mobile-mvi` -- where view models, state and intents live\n- `mobile-design-patterns` -- repository, coordinator and dependency injection patterns in Swift\n- `mobile-ci-cd` -- Xcode Cloud, signing and test plans in the pipeline\n- `kotlin-android`, `dart-flutter` -- the equivalent rules for Android and Flutter\n",
    "references/concurrency.md":
        "# Swift concurrency reference\n\nLoad this when writing `async` code, actors, `Sendable` types, or bridging callback and delegate APIs.\nAssumes Swift 6 language mode with complete data-race checking.\n\n## Isolation map\n\n| Kind of code | Isolation | Why |\n|---|---|---|\n| View models, routers, anything SwiftUI reads | `@MainActor final class` | UI reads and writes happen on main |\n| Shared mutable state (caches, connection registry, token store) | `actor` | Serialised access without locks |\n| Plain data, DTOs, parsers, pure functions | `nonisolated` value types | Usable from any isolation |\n| Legacy class that must be shared and cannot be an actor | `final class` + `Mutex` (iOS 18+) | Synchronous access with a real lock |\n\nCheck the project's default actor isolation before adding annotations. Since Swift 6.2 a module can\ndefault to `@MainActor` (Xcode build setting \"Default Actor Isolation\", or\n`.defaultIsolation(MainActor.self)` in `Package.swift`). In such a module, mark parsers, DTOs and\nbackground services `nonisolated` explicitly.\n\n## async let vs task groups\n\n- `async let`: a fixed, small number of child tasks known at compile time.\n- `withThrowingTaskGroup`: a dynamic number of children, results collected in completion order.\n- `withDiscardingTaskGroup` / `withThrowingDiscardingTaskGroup`: many children whose results are not needed.\n\n```swift\n// Collect results keyed by input, completion order does not matter\nlet readings = try await withThrowingTaskGroup(of: (Device.ID, Reading).self) { group in\n    for id in ids {\n        group.addTask { (id, try await api.latestReading(for: id)) }\n    }\n    var result: [Device.ID: Reading] = [:]\n    for try await (id, reading) in group { result[id] = reading }\n    return result\n}\n```\n\nBound the width when the input can be large, so 500 inputs do not open 500 connections:\n\n```swift\nfunc uploadAll(_ files: [URL], maxConcurrent: Int = 4) async throws {\n    try await withThrowingTaskGroup(of: Void.self) { group in\n        var pending = files.makeIterator()\n        for _ in 0..<maxConcurrent {\n            guard let file = pending.next() else { break }\n            group.addTask { try await upload(file) }\n        }\n        while try await group.next() != nil {\n            if let file = pending.next() {\n                group.addTask { try await upload(file) }\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- When one child throws, the group cancels the remaining children and rethrows.\n- Never let a child task escape the group closure.\n\n## Actors and reentrancy\n\nEvery `await` inside an actor is a suspension point where other callers can run. Rules:\n\n- Read state, mutate it, then suspend. Not read, suspend, mutate.\n- Re-check invariants after each `await`.\n- Deduplicate concurrent requests by caching the in-flight `Task`, not only the result.\n\n```swift\nactor FirmwareCatalog {\n    private var inFlight: [String: Task<Manifest, Error>] = [:]\n\n    func manifest(for model: String) async throws -> Manifest {\n        if let task = inFlight[model] { return try await task.value }\n        let task = Task { try await api.manifest(model) }\n        inFlight[model] = task\n        defer { inFlight[model] = nil }\n        return try await task.value\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- Keep actor methods short. Long synchronous work inside an actor blocks every other caller.\n- `nonisolated` on actor members that touch only immutable `let` state of `Sendable` type.\n\n## Sendable\n\n- Non-public structs and enums whose stored properties are all `Sendable` are inferred `Sendable`.\n  Public types need an explicit conformance.\n- Classes are `Sendable` only if `final` with immutable `Sendable` stored properties, or main-actor\n  isolated, or protected by a lock.\n- Closures crossing isolation are `@Sendable` and capture only `Sendable` values.\n\n```swift\nimport Synchronization\n\n// Synchronous, thread-safe counter for code that cannot await\nfinal class PacketStats: Sendable {\n    private let dropped = Mutex(0)\n    func recordDrop() { dropped.withLock { \$0 += 1 } }\n    var droppedCount: Int { dropped.withLock { \$0 } }\n}\n```\n\n- `sending` on a parameter or result lets a non-`Sendable` value move across isolation when the caller\n  no longer uses it. Prefer making the type `Sendable`; reach for `sending` when it cannot be.\n- `@preconcurrency import SomeModule` for dependencies not yet annotated. Remove it once they are.\n\n## Swift 6.2 isolation of async functions\n\nWith the upcoming feature `NonisolatedNonsendingByDefault` (on in new Xcode 26 projects with\n\"Approachable Concurrency\"), a `nonisolated async` function runs on the caller's actor instead of\nhopping to the global executor.\n\n- To force work off the caller's actor (CPU-heavy parsing, compression), mark the function `@concurrent`.\n- Check which mode the project uses before reasoning about where an `async` function runs.\n\n```swift\n// CPU-bound work explicitly leaves the main actor\n@concurrent\nnonisolated func decodeLog(_ data: Data) async throws -> [LogEntry] {\n    try LogDecoder().decode(data)\n}\n```\n\n## Cancellation\n\nCancellation is cooperative. Nothing stops unless the code checks.\n\n- `try Task.checkCancellation()` in loops and before expensive steps.\n- `Task.isCancelled` when cleanup is needed instead of throwing.\n- `Task.sleep(for:)` and `URLSession` async APIs throw `CancellationError` on cancel.\n- Callback APIs with their own cancel method use `withTaskCancellationHandler`:\n\n```swift\nfunc fetch(_ request: LegacyRequest) async throws -> Data {\n    let operation = LegacyOperation(request) // Sendable; cancel() is thread-safe\n    return try await withTaskCancellationHandler {\n        try await withCheckedThrowingContinuation { continuation in\n            operation.start { result in continuation.resume(with: result) }\n        }\n    } onCancel: {\n        operation.cancel()\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- `onCancel` runs immediately and concurrently, possibly before `start` is called. The operation must\n  tolerate cancel-before-start.\n- The cancelled operation must still complete its callback so the continuation resumes.\n\n## Continuations\n\n- `withCheckedThrowingContinuation` in all code. The unsafe variants only after profiling proves a need.\n- Resume exactly once on every path, including error and timeout paths.\n- A checked continuation that is never resumed leaks the task and logs a warning; resuming twice traps.\n\n## AsyncStream\n\n```swift\nfunc notifications(from characteristic: CharacteristicID) -> AsyncThrowingStream<Data, Error> {\n    let (stream, continuation) = AsyncThrowingStream.makeStream(\n        of: Data.self,\n        bufferingPolicy: .bufferingNewest(64)\n    )\n    let token = transport.subscribe(characteristic) { event in\n        switch event {\n        case .value(let data): continuation.yield(data)\n        case .failed(let error): continuation.finish(throwing: error)\n        case .ended: continuation.finish()\n        }\n    }\n    continuation.onTermination = { _ in token.cancel() }\n    return stream\n}\n```\n\n- Choose a buffering policy on purpose. The default is unbounded; high-rate sensor or BLE notifications\n  should use `.bufferingNewest(n)` so memory cannot grow without limit.\n- `onTermination` releases the underlying subscription when the consumer cancels or the stream ends.\n- `AsyncStream` supports one consumer. For several consumers, give each its own stream.\n- Consume in `.task` in SwiftUI, or in a stored `Task` that is cancelled in the owner's teardown.\n\n## Time\n\n- `try await Task.sleep(for: .seconds(2))`, not `Task.sleep(nanoseconds:)`.\n- Inject `any Clock<Duration>` into types that wait, time out or debounce, so tests control time.\n\n```swift\nfinal class Reconnector {\n    private let clock: any Clock<Duration>\n    init(clock: any Clock<Duration> = ContinuousClock()) { self.clock = clock }\n\n    func backoff(attempt: Int) async throws {\n        try await clock.sleep(for: .seconds(min(1 << attempt, 30)))\n    }\n}\n```\n\n## Migrating older code\n\n- Convert completion handlers at the lowest layer first, then move up. Keep the callback version only\n  if other modules still depend on it.\n- Replace `DispatchQueue` serial queues that guard state with an `actor`.\n- Replace `DispatchGroup` fan-out with a task group.\n- Move one module at a time to Swift 6 language mode; fix warnings in Swift 5 mode with complete\n  checking first.\n- Never fix a data-race error with `@unchecked Sendable` without a lock covering every stored property.\n",
    "references/packages.md":
        "# Packages and tooling reference\n\nLoad this when creating or changing Swift packages, access levels, lint and format configuration,\n`Info.plist`, entitlements or the privacy manifest.\n\n## Layout\n\n```\nApp/                      Xcode app target: composition root, App struct, assets, entitlements\nPackages/\n  Core/                   Package.swift with targets: CoreModels, CoreNetworking, CoreBLE, CoreTesting\n  DeviceFeature/          Package.swift: DeviceFeature, DeviceFeatureTests\n  FirmwareFeature/        Package.swift: FirmwareFeature, FirmwareFeatureTests\n```\n\n- The app target is thin: it wires dependencies and hosts the root view.\n- Features depend on core modules, never on other features. Cross-feature navigation goes through\n  routes or closures provided by the app.\n- Shared fakes live in a testing target (for example `CoreTesting`) that only test targets depend on.\n\n## Package.swift\n\n```swift\n// swift-tools-version: 6.0\nimport PackageDescription\n\nlet package = Package(\n    name: \"DeviceFeature\",\n    platforms: [.iOS(.v17)],\n    products: [\n        .library(name: \"DeviceFeature\", targets: [\"DeviceFeature\"]),\n    ],\n    dependencies: [\n        .package(path: \"../Core\"),\n    ],\n    targets: [\n        .target(\n            name: \"DeviceFeature\",\n            dependencies: [\n                .product(name: \"CoreModels\", package: \"Core\"),\n                .product(name: \"CoreBLE\", package: \"Core\"),\n            ],\n            resources: [.process(\"Resources\")]\n        ),\n        .testTarget(\n            name: \"DeviceFeatureTests\",\n            dependencies: [\n                \"DeviceFeature\",\n                .product(name: \"CoreTesting\", package: \"Core\"),\n            ]\n        ),\n    ],\n    swiftLanguageModes: [.v6]\n)\n```\n\n- Declare every module the target imports. Do not rely on something being linked transitively.\n- Resources are loaded with `Bundle.module`, never `Bundle.main`, inside a package.\n- Remote dependencies use a version requirement (`from: \"2.1.0\"`), not a branch. Commit\n  `Package.resolved` for the app.\n- Opt into upcoming features per target with `swiftSettings: [.enableUpcomingFeature(\"ExistentialAny\")]`.\n- With tools version 6.2 or later, `swiftSettings: [.defaultIsolation(MainActor.self)]` makes a UI\n  module main-actor by default. Do not use it for networking, parsing or BLE transport modules.\n\n## Access control\n\n| Level | Visible to | Use for |\n|---|---|---|\n| `private` | Enclosing declaration and its extensions in the same file | Implementation details |\n| `fileprivate` | Same file | Rare; helpers shared by several types in one file |\n| `internal` (default) | Same module | Almost everything |\n| `package` | All targets in the same Swift package | Helpers shared between targets of one package |\n| `public` | Any importing module | The module's API |\n| `open` | Any importing module, subclassable | Only for classes designed for subclassing |\n\n- Start `internal`, widen only when a caller in another module needs it.\n- A `public` type needs a `public init` if other modules construct it; the memberwise init is internal.\n- Public types that cross isolation declare `Sendable` explicitly; it is not inferred for public types.\n- `@testable import` in tests only. Production code never relies on it.\n\n## SwiftLint\n\n```yaml\n# .swiftlint.yml\nincluded:\n  - App\n  - Packages\nexcluded:\n  - \"**/.build\"\nopt_in_rules:\n  - force_unwrapping\n  - implicitly_unwrapped_optional\n  - unowned_variable_capture\n  - empty_count\n  - first_where\n  - fatal_error_message\nline_length:\n  warning: 140\n  error: 200\n```\n\n- `force_cast` and `force_try` are on by default; keep them on.\n- Run `swiftlint --strict` in CI so warnings fail the build.\n- Disable a rule inline with `// swiftlint:disable:next <rule>` and a reason, never file-wide.\n\n## Formatting\n\n- Pick one formatter for the repository: SwiftFormat (`.swiftformat` config) or the `swift-format` tool\n  shipped with the Swift toolchain. Running both causes churn.\n- SwiftFormat example:\n\n```\n# .swiftformat\n--swiftversion 6.0\n--indent 4\n--maxwidth 140\n--exclude **/.build\n```\n\n- CI runs the formatter in lint mode (`swiftformat --lint .`) and fails on differences.\n- Formatting-only changes go in their own commit.\n\n## Info.plist and entitlements\n\nEvery addition here must be listed in the change summary because it affects signing, App Review or\nuser prompts.\n\n- Usage descriptions explain the user benefit in plain language:\n  `NSBluetoothAlwaysUsageDescription`, `NSCameraUsageDescription`,\n  `NSLocationWhenInUseUsageDescription`, `NSLocalNetworkUsageDescription`.\n  Accessing Bluetooth, camera or microphone without the key terminates the app.\n- `UIBackgroundModes` (`bluetooth-central`, `location`, `remote-notification`, `processing`) must match\n  real behaviour; App Review rejects unused modes.\n- Entitlements such as Push Notifications, App Groups, Keychain Sharing and Associated Domains require\n  the capability on the App ID and a regenerated provisioning profile. Flag this for whoever owns signing.\n- App Transport Security exceptions (`NSAppTransportSecurity`) need a written justification. Never add\n  `NSAllowsArbitraryLoads` for production traffic.\n\n## Privacy manifest (PrivacyInfo.xcprivacy)\n\nThe app and every SDK that uses required-reason APIs or collects data ships a `PrivacyInfo.xcprivacy`.\n\n```xml\n<key>NSPrivacyTracking</key>\n<false/>\n<key>NSPrivacyTrackingDomains</key>\n<array/>\n<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes</key>\n<array/>\n<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes</key>\n<array>\n    <dict>\n        <key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>\n        <string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryUserDefaults</string>\n        <key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>\n        <array>\n            <string>CA92.1</string>\n        </array>\n    </dict>\n</array>\n```\n\n- Required-reason API categories include `UserDefaults`, file timestamps, system boot time, disk space\n  and active keyboards. Adding code that calls one of these means adding or updating an entry with an\n  approved reason code from Apple's documentation. Do not guess a reason code.\n- Collected data types (device IDs, location, diagnostics, telemetry) must match what the app and its\n  SDKs actually send, including whether it is linked to the user and used for tracking.\n- A Swift package that needs its own manifest includes it as a resource:\n  `resources: [.copy(\"PrivacyInfo.xcprivacy\")]`.\n- Generate the privacy report from an archive in the Xcode Organizer before release and review it.\n",
    "references/swiftdata.md":
        "# SwiftData reference\n\nLoad this when adding or changing persistence with SwiftData. Requires iOS 17. For Core Data projects,\ndo not mix in SwiftData without a migration plan agreed with the team.\n\n## Models\n\n```swift\nimport SwiftData\n\n@Model\nfinal class DeviceEntity {\n    @Attribute(.unique) var serial: String\n    var name: String\n    var lastSeen: Date?\n    @Relationship(deleteRule: .cascade, inverse: \\ReadingEntity.device)\n    var readings: [ReadingEntity] = []\n\n    init(serial: String, name: String) {\n        self.serial = serial\n        self.name = name\n    }\n}\n\n@Model\nfinal class ReadingEntity {\n    var value: Double\n    var date: Date\n    var device: DeviceEntity?\n\n    init(value: Double, date: Date) {\n        self.value = value\n        self.date = date\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- `@Model` types are classes and are not `Sendable`. Keep them in the persistence and UI layers.\n- Map to plain `struct` domain models at the repository boundary if the domain layer must stay\n  framework-free or cross actors.\n- Store secrets and tokens in the Keychain, not in SwiftData. Device serials and telemetry can be\n  personal data; apply a file protection class and a retention policy.\n\n## Container\n\n```swift\n@main\nstruct GatewayApp: App {\n    var body: some Scene {\n        WindowGroup { RootView() }\n            .modelContainer(for: [DeviceEntity.self, ReadingEntity.self])\n    }\n}\n```\n\nFor tests and previews use an in-memory store:\n\n```swift\nlet container = try ModelContainer(\n    for: DeviceEntity.self, ReadingEntity.self,\n    configurations: ModelConfiguration(isStoredInMemoryOnly: true)\n)\n```\n\n## Reading\n\n```swift\nstruct DeviceList: View {\n    @Query(sort: \\DeviceEntity.name) private var devices: [DeviceEntity]\n    var body: some View { List(devices) { DeviceRow(device: \$0) } }\n}\n```\n\nFilter that depends on input: build the query in `init`.\n\n```swift\nstruct ReadingList: View {\n    @Query private var readings: [ReadingEntity]\n\n    init(serial: String) {\n        _readings = Query(\n            filter: #Predicate<ReadingEntity> { \$0.device?.serial == serial },\n            sort: \\ReadingEntity.date,\n            order: .reverse\n        )\n    }\n\n    var body: some View { List(readings) { ReadingRow(reading: \$0) } }\n}\n```\n\nOutside views, fetch with a descriptor and a limit:\n\n```swift\nvar descriptor = FetchDescriptor<ReadingEntity>(\n    sortBy: [SortDescriptor(\\.date, order: .reverse)]\n)\ndescriptor.fetchLimit = 100\nlet latest = try context.fetch(descriptor)\n```\n\n## Writing\n\n```swift\n@Environment(\\.modelContext) private var context\n\nfunc add(_ device: DeviceEntity) throws {\n    context.insert(device)\n    try context.save()\n}\n```\n\n- The main context autosaves, but call `save()` explicitly after user-visible writes so errors surface.\n- Bulk imports and sync run in a `@ModelActor`, never on the main context.\n\n```swift\n@ModelActor\nactor ReadingImporter {\n    func importBatch(_ rows: [ReadingDTO], for deviceID: PersistentIdentifier) throws {\n        guard let device = self[deviceID, as: DeviceEntity.self] else { return }\n        for row in rows {\n            let reading = ReadingEntity(value: row.value, date: row.date)\n            reading.device = device\n            modelContext.insert(reading)\n        }\n        try modelContext.save()\n    }\n}\n\n// Caller\nlet importer = ReadingImporter(modelContainer: container)\ntry await importer.importBatch(rows, for: device.persistentModelID)\n```\n\n- Pass `PersistentIdentifier` (which is `Sendable`) across actors, then re-fetch in the target context.\n- One context per actor. Never capture a `ModelContext` or `@Model` instance in a `Task.detached`.\n\n## Schema changes and migration\n\nAny change to a shipped `@Model` needs a versioned schema so existing user data migrates.\n\n```swift\nenum SchemaV1: VersionedSchema {\n    static var versionIdentifier: Schema.Version { Schema.Version(1, 0, 0) }\n    static var models: [any PersistentModel.Type] { [DeviceEntity.self] }\n\n    @Model final class DeviceEntity {\n        var serial: String\n        init(serial: String) { self.serial = serial }\n    }\n}\n\nenum SchemaV2: VersionedSchema {\n    static var versionIdentifier: Schema.Version { Schema.Version(2, 0, 0) }\n    static var models: [any PersistentModel.Type] { [DeviceEntity.self] }\n\n    @Model final class DeviceEntity {\n        var serial: String\n        var firmwareVersion: String?\n        init(serial: String) { self.serial = serial }\n    }\n}\n\nenum GatewayMigrationPlan: SchemaMigrationPlan {\n    static var schemas: [any VersionedSchema.Type] { [SchemaV1.self, SchemaV2.self] }\n    static var stages: [MigrationStage] {\n        [.lightweight(fromVersion: SchemaV1.self, toVersion: SchemaV2.self)]\n    }\n}\n\n// The rest of the app refers to the current version only\ntypealias DeviceEntity = SchemaV2.DeviceEntity\n\nlet container = try ModelContainer(\n    for: DeviceEntity.self,\n    migrationPlan: GatewayMigrationPlan.self\n)\n```\n\n- Lightweight stages cover added optional properties, renames with `@Attribute(originalName:)`, and\n  removed properties. Data transformations need a `.custom` stage.\n- Test migration on a store created by the previous release build, not only on a fresh install.\n- Never ship a change that deletes and recreates the store to \"fix\" a migration error.\n",
    "references/swiftui.md":
        "# SwiftUI reference\n\nLoad this when writing or refactoring SwiftUI views and their models. Assumes iOS 17+ and the Observation\nframework. For older targets keep `ObservableObject` and `@StateObject` and say so.\n\n## Property wrapper choice\n\n| Situation | Use |\n|---|---|\n| View creates and owns an `@Observable` model | `@State private var model = Model()` |\n| Model passed in, view only reads it | `let model: Model` (or `var`) |\n| Model passed in, view needs `\$model.field` bindings | `@Bindable var model: Model` |\n| Simple value owned by this view | `@State private var isExpanded = false` |\n| Value owned by the parent, edited here | `@Binding var isOn: Bool` |\n| App-wide dependency or model | `@Environment(Store.self) private var store` |\n| System or custom environment value | `@Environment(\\.dismiss) private var dismiss` |\n| Legacy `ObservableObject` | `@StateObject` (owner) / `@ObservedObject` (passed in) |\n\n`@State` is always `private`. A value that comes from outside is not `@State`.\n\n## Observable models\n\n```swift\n@MainActor @Observable\nfinal class DeviceListModel {\n    private(set) var devices: [Device] = []\n    private(set) var error: LoadError?\n    var filter = \"\"\n\n    @ObservationIgnored private let repository: DeviceRepository\n\n    init(repository: DeviceRepository) { self.repository = repository }\n\n    func load() async {\n        do { devices = try await repository.devices() }\n        catch is CancellationError { }\n        catch { self.error = LoadError(error) }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- Views re-render only when a property they actually read changes.\n- `@ObservationIgnored` for dependencies and caches the UI never reads.\n- Keep `init` cheap. `@State private var model = Model()` evaluates the initializer each time the\n  parent re-creates the view value; the extra instances are discarded but their init cost is paid.\n  Load data in `.task`, not in `init`.\n\n## Bindings from an observable model\n\n```swift\nstruct DeviceSettingsView: View {\n    @Bindable var settings: DeviceSettings\n    var body: some View {\n        Form {\n            Toggle(\"Auto reconnect\", isOn: \$settings.autoReconnect)\n            Stepper(\"Interval: \\(settings.interval)s\", value: \$settings.interval, in: 5...60)\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\nWhen the model comes from the environment, create the bindable locally:\n\n```swift\n@Environment(DeviceSettings.self) private var settings\nvar body: some View {\n    @Bindable var settings = settings\n    Toggle(\"Auto reconnect\", isOn: \$settings.autoReconnect)\n}\n```\n\n## Environment\n\n```swift\n// Inject an observable model\nContentView().environment(store)\n\n// Read it\n@Environment(Store.self) private var store\n```\n\nCustom values use the `@Entry` macro:\n\n```swift\n// TelemetryClient is a Sendable protocol, so the default value is safe to share\nextension EnvironmentValues {\n    @Entry var telemetryClient: any TelemetryClient = NoopTelemetryClient()\n}\n\n// Inject and read\nRootView().environment(\\.telemetryClient, LiveTelemetryClient())\n@Environment(\\.telemetryClient) private var telemetry\n```\n\n- `@Environment(Store.self)` crashes at runtime if no ancestor injected it. Inject at the root and in\n  every `#Preview`.\n- The environment is for dependencies and app-wide state, not for passing one value two levels down.\n\n## Async work\n\n```swift\n// Restarts on every query change, previous run is cancelled\n.task(id: query) {\n    try? await Task.sleep(for: .milliseconds(300))\n    guard !Task.isCancelled else { return }\n    await model.search(query)\n}\n```\n\n- `.task` starts before the view appears and is cancelled when it disappears.\n- `.refreshable { await model.reload() }` for pull to refresh.\n- Consume `AsyncSequence` values in `.task` with `for await`; the loop ends when the view disappears.\n- `.onChange(of: value) { oldValue, newValue in }` for synchronous reactions; avoid chains of `onChange`\n  that feed each other.\n\n## Navigation\n\n```swift\nenum Route: Hashable {\n    case device(Device.ID)\n    case firmware(Device.ID)\n    case settings\n}\n\nstruct RootView: View {\n    @State private var path: [Route] = []\n\n    var body: some View {\n        NavigationStack(path: \$path) {\n            DeviceList(onSelect: { path.append(.device(\$0)) })\n                .navigationDestination(for: Route.self) { route in\n                    switch route {\n                    case .device(let id): DeviceDetail(id: id)\n                    case .firmware(let id): FirmwareUpdate(id: id)\n                    case .settings: SettingsView()\n                    }\n                }\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- Routes carry identifiers, not models, so they are cheap, `Hashable` and restorable.\n- One `navigationDestination(for:)` per type, attached inside the stack, not inside lazy containers.\n- `NavigationPath` only when the stack really holds unrelated types.\n- `NavigationSplitView` for iPad and Mac layouts. `NavigationView` is deprecated.\n\n## Identity and performance\n\n```swift\n// BAD: id regenerated on every render, every row is new every time\nForEach(readings.map { RowItem(id: UUID(), reading: \$0) }) { RowView(item: \$0) }\n\n// GOOD\nForEach(readings) { reading in ReadingRow(reading: reading) } // Reading.id comes from the data\n```\n\n- `.id(value)` on a view discards its state when `value` changes. Use it only to reset on purpose.\n- Prefer `if` / `switch` inside a `@ViewBuilder` over `AnyView`. Same-type branches can use modifiers\n  (`.opacity`, `.disabled`) instead of swapping views, which keeps identity.\n- `body` does no I/O, sorting of large arrays, or formatter creation. Precompute in the model or use\n  `Text(date, format: .dateTime.hour().minute())`.\n- `LazyVStack` / `List` for long or unbounded lists; `VStack` only for short fixed content.\n- Split views at natural boundaries. A subview re-renders only when its inputs change.\n\n## Previews\n\n```swift\n#Preview {\n    DeviceListScreen()\n        .environment(Store.preview)\n}\n\n#Preview(\"Toggle states\") {\n    @Previewable @State var isOn = true\n    Toggle(\"Auto reconnect\", isOn: \$isOn)\n}\n```\n\n- Previews use in-memory or fake dependencies, never live network or BLE.\n\n## Accessibility\n\n- Meaningful images get `.accessibilityLabel`; decorative ones use `Image(decorative:)`.\n- Use text styles (`.font(.body)`), not fixed point sizes, so Dynamic Type works.\n- Combine row content with `.accessibilityElement(children: .combine)` when a row reads as one item.\n- Tap targets at least 44x44 points.\n",
    "references/testing.md":
        "# Testing reference\n\nLoad this when adding or changing tests. Swift Testing for unit and integration tests; XCTest for UI\ntests and performance tests. Both frameworks can live in the same test target.\n\n## Swift Testing basics\n\n```swift\nimport Testing\n@testable import DeviceFeature\n\n@Suite(\"Frame decoder\")\nstruct FrameDecoderTests {\n    let decoder = FrameDecoder() // fresh instance per test, no shared state\n\n    @Test func decodesBatteryLevel() throws {\n        let packet = try decoder.decode(Fixtures.batteryFrame)\n        let battery = try #require(packet.battery)\n        #expect(battery == 87)\n    }\n\n    @Test func rejectsBadChecksum() {\n        #expect(throws: FrameError.badChecksum) {\n            try decoder.decode(Fixtures.corruptedFrame)\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- `#expect` records a failure and continues. `try #require` stops the test; use it for preconditions and\n  to unwrap optionals.\n- A `struct` suite gets a new instance per test. Setup goes in `init`, teardown in `deinit` of a\n  `final class` suite only when cleanup is required.\n- `#expect(throws: SomeError.self)` checks the type; passing an `Equatable` error value checks the case.\n- Write the expectation as a plain expression. `#expect(a == b)` already reports both sides on failure.\n\n## Parameterized tests\n\n```swift\n@Test(arguments: [\n    (Data([0x01, 0x00]), Opcode.ping),\n    (Data([0x02, 0x10]), Opcode.status),\n])\nfunc decodesOpcode(frame: Data, expected: Opcode) throws {\n    #expect(try FrameDecoder().decode(frame).opcode == expected)\n}\n\n@Test(arguments: [0, 1, 19, 20])\nfunc rejectsFramesShorterThanHeader(length: Int) {\n    #expect(throws: FrameError.self) {\n        try FrameDecoder().decode(Data(count: length))\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- Each argument runs as its own test case and can be re-run alone.\n- Two argument collections produce every combination. Use `zip(inputs, outputs)` for pairs.\n- Prefer parameterized tests over a `for` loop inside one test.\n\n## Async code\n\n```swift\n@MainActor\n@Test func loadPublishesDevices() async {\n    let model = DeviceListModel(repository: FakeDeviceRepository(stubbedDevices: [.sample]))\n    await model.load()\n    #expect(model.devices == [.sample])\n}\n```\n\n- Mark tests or suites `@MainActor` when they touch main-actor types.\n- `await` the operation under test. Never `sleep` and hope.\n\nCallbacks and events that must fire use `confirmation`:\n\n```swift\n@Test func emitsConnectedEvent() async {\n    let session = DeviceSession(transport: FakeTransport())\n    await confirmation(\"connected event\") { connected in\n        session.onEvent = { event in\n            if event == .connected { connected() }\n        }\n        await session.connect()\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- The confirmation must be confirmed before the closure returns. It does not wait for later callbacks.\n- `confirmation(expectedCount: 3)` for an exact count; `expectedCount: 0` asserts something never happens.\n\n## Time and dependencies\n\n- Inject `any Clock<Duration>` and use a manual test clock (own implementation or a library) so backoff,\n  timeouts and debounce run instantly and deterministically.\n- Put protocols at boundaries (network, BLE transport, storage, keychain) and inject fakes. Avoid mocking\n  frameworks that rely on runtime tricks.\n- SwiftData tests use `ModelConfiguration(isStoredInMemoryOnly: true)`.\n\n```swift\nprotocol DeviceRepository: Sendable {\n    func devices() async throws -> [Device]\n}\n\nstruct FakeDeviceRepository: DeviceRepository {\n    var stubbedDevices: [Device] = []\n    var stubbedError: (any Error)?\n    func devices() async throws -> [Device] {\n        if let stubbedError { throw stubbedError }\n        return stubbedDevices\n    }\n}\n```\n\n## Traits and organisation\n\n```swift\nextension Tag {\n    @Tag static var ble: Self\n    @Tag static var parsing: Self\n}\n\n@Suite(.tags(.ble), .timeLimit(.minutes(1)))\nstruct ReconnectTests { /* tests */ }\n\n@Test(.disabled(\"Flaky on CI simulator, see TICKET-123\"))\nfunc scansInBackground() async { /* test body */ }\n```\n\n- `.serialized` on a suite whose tests share an external resource. Tests run in parallel by default.\n- `.enabled(if:)` for environment-dependent tests.\n- `.bug(\"https://...\")` links a known issue.\n\n## XCTest: what still needs it\n\nUI tests:\n\n```swift\nfinal class PairingUITests: XCTestCase {\n    func testPairingFlow() throws {\n        let app = XCUIApplication()\n        app.launchArguments = [\"-ui-testing\", \"-fake-ble\"]\n        app.launch()\n\n        app.buttons[\"pair-device\"].tap()\n        XCTAssertTrue(app.staticTexts[\"paired-status\"].waitForExistence(timeout: 5))\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- Query by `accessibilityIdentifier`, not by visible text that changes with localisation.\n- Launch arguments switch the app to fake transports and fixed data. UI tests never depend on real\n  devices, servers or BLE hardware.\n\nPerformance tests:\n\n```swift\nfunc testDecodePerformance() {\n    measure(metrics: [XCTClockMetric(), XCTMemoryMetric()]) {\n        _ = try? FrameDecoder().decodeAll(Fixtures.largeCapture)\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- Set a baseline in Xcode and run on a fixed device class; simulator numbers vary.\n- Async XCTest waits use `await fulfillment(of: [expectation], timeout: 2)`, not `wait(for:)`.\n\n## Running\n\n- Packages that build on macOS: `swift test`.\n- iOS-only packages and the app: `xcodebuild test -scheme <Scheme> -destination '<simulator>'` or a\n  test plan.\n- Keep fast unit tests in package test targets so they run without launching the simulator app.\n",
  },
};

